window.STUDY_CONTENT = {"readme": "# 🎯 NISM Series X-A: Investment Adviser (Level 1) — Complete Study Guide\n\n> **Everything you need to clear the exam — explained from first principles, chapter by chapter.**\n> Built to the official NISM-Series-X-A syllabus and test objectives (6 modules · 20 chapters · 150 marks).\n\n---\n\n## 📋 What this exam is actually about (read this first!)\n\nAn **Investment Adviser** is a financial doctor. A doctor examines your body and prescribes medicine. An Investment Adviser examines your **money situation** — what you earn, what you owe, what you're saving for (a house, a child's education, retirement), and how much loss you can stomach — and then prescribes the right financial \"medicines.\"\n\n**Level 1 (Series X-A)** is the *foundation* exam. It tests whether you can:\n\n1. **Plan** — build a personal financial plan, do the maths of money (time value, EMIs, ratios).\n2. **Know the market** — how India's financial system and securities markets are wired, and who regulates whom.\n3. **Know the products** — equity, bonds, derivatives, mutual funds, PMS, AIFs.\n4. **Build a portfolio** — asset allocation, Modern Portfolio Theory, and measuring whether the portfolio actually performed.\n5. **Operate legally and ethically** — KYC and onboarding, the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, ethics, and how a client complains about you.\n\n> **Level 1 vs Level 2 in one line:** Level 1 (X-A) is *markets, products, portfolios and rules*. Level 2 (X-B) is *insurance, retirement, tax and estate planning*. You must pass **both** to be a registered Investment Adviser under the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Exam Structure (know this cold — it changes how you study)\n\n| Part | What | Marks |\n|------|------|-------|\n| **Independent MCQs** | 90 questions × 1 mark | **90** |\n| **Caselets (1-mark)** | 6 caselets × 5 questions × 1 mark | **30** |\n| **Caselets (2-mark)** | 3 caselets × 5 questions × 2 marks | **30** |\n| **TOTAL** | **105 questions** | **150** |\n\n- ⏱️ **Time:** 3 hours\n- ✅ **Passing score:** 60% — i.e. **90 out of 150 marks**\n- ⚠️ **Negative marking:** 25% of the marks for that question. A wrong 1-mark MCQ costs **−0.25**; a wrong 2-mark caselet answer costs **−0.5**.\n- 💰 **Fee:** ₹3,000 (check nism.ac.in for the current fee)\n- 🧮 **Calculator:** the test machine has a spreadsheet — practise `=PV()`, `=FV()`, `=PMT()`, `=RATE()`, `=NPER()` until they're automatic.\n\n### 🎯 Where the marks actually live\n\n| Module | Chapters | Marks | Share |\n|--------|----------|-------|-------|\n| 1 · Personal Financial Planning | 1–4 | **37** | 24.7% |\n| 2 · Indian Financial Markets | 5–6 | **10** | 6.7% |\n| 3 · Investment Products | 7–10 | **30** | 20.0% |\n| 4 · Investment Through Managed Portfolio | 11–13 | **23** | 15.3% |\n| 5 · Portfolio Construction, Monitoring & Evaluation | 14–16 | **20** | 13.3% |\n| 6 · Operations, Regulation, Compliance & Ethics | 17–20 | **30** | 20.0% |\n| | | **150** | 100% |\n\n> **Read that table again.** Module 1 alone is **37 marks** — more than any other — and it is almost entirely *numerical* (time value of money, EMIs, financial ratios). Module 6 is another **30 marks** and is almost entirely *recall* (regulations, ethics, grievance routes). Those two modules together are **45% of the paper**, and they are the two easiest to score in — one with a calculator, the other with flashcards.\n>\n> Module 2 is only **10 marks** — don't over-invest a week in it.\n\n---\n\n## 📚 How to use this study guide\n\nEach chapter folder contains **three files**:\n\n| File | What it is | How to use it |\n|------|-----------|---------------|\n| `notes.md` | **Short Notes** — the whole chapter explained plainly, with every formula *and the logic behind it* | Read first, 2–3 times |\n| `flashcards.md` | **Flashcards** — question on one side, answer on the other | Revise daily; cover the answer and test yourself |\n| `questions.md` | **Question Bank — 100 MCQs**, each with a full explanation (the formula used, *and* why every wrong option is wrong) + a worked caselet | Attempt *after* reading the notes |\n\nThat's **~2,000 practice MCQs + 20 worked caselets** across the guide. Each `questions.md` is organised into three difficulty tiers so you build up gradually:\n\n- 🟢 **Tier 1 — Easy (Recall):** definitions and facts (≈Q1–35)\n- 🟡 **Tier 2 — Medium (Application):** \"which rule applies here\", comparisons (≈Q36–70)\n- 🔴 **Tier 3 — Hard (Numerical & Scenario):** multi-step calculations and exam-style scenarios (≈Q71–100)\n\nEvery answer sits inside a collapsible *\"Answer & Explanation\"* block — attempt first, then expand to see **why the right option is right, why the others are wrong, and the exact calculation behind it.**\n\n**Plus, across the whole guide:**\n\n- 📑 **10 full-length mock papers** — each built to the exact 150-mark blueprint (90 MCQs + 6 one-mark caselets + 3 two-mark caselets), auto-scored with real negative marking and a 3-hour timer.\n- 🔢 **Financial Maths Primer** — every formula in the syllabus, derived and worked.\n- 📊 **Excel/Calc TVM guide** — the spreadsheet functions you'll actually use in the exam hall.\n\n**Recommended cycle per chapter:**\n> Read `notes.md` → drill `flashcards.md` → attempt `questions.md` (Easy → Medium → Hard) → re-read the notes for anything you got wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 🗺️ Chapter Map\n\n### Module 1: Personal Financial Planning — *37 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 1 | Introduction to Personal Financial Planning | [`chapter-01`](chapter-01/) |\n| 2 | Time Value of Money | [`chapter-02`](chapter-02/) |\n| 3 | Cash Flow Management and Budgeting | [`chapter-03`](chapter-03/) |\n| 4 | Debt Management and Loans | [`chapter-04`](chapter-04/) |\n\n### Module 2: Indian Financial Markets — *10 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 5 | Introduction to Indian Financial Markets | [`chapter-05`](chapter-05/) |\n| 6 | Securities Market Segments | [`chapter-06`](chapter-06/) |\n\n### Module 3: Investment Products — *30 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 7 | Introduction to Investments | [`chapter-07`](chapter-07/) |\n| 8 | Investing in Stocks | [`chapter-08`](chapter-08/) |\n| 9 | Investing in Fixed Income Securities | [`chapter-09`](chapter-09/) |\n| 10 | Understanding Derivatives | [`chapter-10`](chapter-10/) |\n\n### Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio — *23 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 11 | Mutual Funds | [`chapter-11`](chapter-11/) |\n| 12 | Portfolio Manager | [`chapter-12`](chapter-12/) |\n| 13 | Overview of Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs) | [`chapter-13`](chapter-13/) |\n\n### Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation — *20 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 14 | Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory | [`chapter-14`](chapter-14/) |\n| 15 | Portfolio Construction Process | [`chapter-15`](chapter-15/) |\n| 16 | Portfolio Performance Measurement and Evaluation | [`chapter-16`](chapter-16/) |\n\n### Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics — *30 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 17 | Operational Aspects of Investment Management | [`chapter-17`](chapter-17/) |\n| 18 | Key Regulations | [`chapter-18`](chapter-18/) |\n| 19 | Ethical Issues | [`chapter-19`](chapter-19/) |\n| 20 | Grievance Redress Mechanism | [`chapter-20`](chapter-20/) |\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ 10-Day Study Plan\n\n| Day | Cover | Why |\n|-----|-------|-----|\n| 1 | Ch 1 + Financial Maths Primer | Sets the vocabulary for everything else |\n| 2 | Ch 2 (TVM) + Excel guide | **The single highest-value day.** TVM shows up in Ch 4, 9, 14, 16 and every caselet |\n| 3 | Ch 3–4 (Budgeting, Ratios, Loans) | Finishes the 37-mark module; heavily numerical |\n| 4 | Ch 5–6 (Markets, Segments) | Only 10 marks — read once, flashcard it, move on |\n| 5 | Ch 7–8 (Investments, Stocks) | Valuation ratios are exam favourites |\n| 6 | Ch 9–10 (Bonds, Derivatives) | Bond pricing & duration are the hardest maths in the paper |\n| 7 | Ch 11–13 (MF, PMS, AIF) | Product features + the MF/PMS/AIF comparison table |\n| 8 | Ch 14–16 (MPT, Construction, Performance) | Sharpe/Treynor/Beta/Alpha — pure formula marks |\n| 9 | Ch 17–18 (Operations, Regulations) | Big recall load — start flashcards early |\n| 10 | Ch 19–20 (Ethics, Grievances) + mock papers | Easy marks; then simulate the full 3-hour paper |\n\n> Then: **one full mock paper a day** until the exam, reviewing every wrong answer.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 The 12 Facts You Must Know Cold\n\nBurn these into memory — they recur across chapters and caselets:\n\n1. **Exam:** 150 marks · 105 questions · 3 hours · pass **90/150 (60%)** · **25% negative marking**.\n2. **FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ** — and PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ. Everything else in Module 1 is a variation of this.\n3. **Effective annual rate** with *m* compoundings: **(1 + r/m)^m − 1**. Monthly compounding always beats annual.\n4. **Real return** = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 — *not* simply nominal minus inflation.\n5. **Annuity due > ordinary annuity** by exactly one period's interest: **FV(due) = FV(ordinary) × (1 + r)**.\n6. **Solvency ratio** = Net worth ÷ Total assets. **Liquidity ratio** = Liquid assets ÷ Monthly expenses. **Savings ratio** = Savings ÷ Income.\n7. **EMI** = P × r × (1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1], where r is the *monthly* rate and n the number of months.\n8. **Regulator map:** SEBI → securities; RBI → banking, money & forex; IRDAI → insurance; PFRDA → pensions; MCA/RoC → companies.\n9. **Bond price and yield move in opposite directions.** Longer duration = bigger price swing.\n10. **Sharpe = (Rp − Rf) ÷ σ** (total risk). **Treynor = (Rp − Rf) ÷ β** (market risk). **Alpha** = actual return − CAPM-expected return.\n11. **IA net worth:** ₹5 lakh (individual) / ₹50 lakh (non-individual); an IA must do **risk profiling** and ensure **suitability**, and must **segregate advice from distribution**.\n12. **Complaint escalation:** the intermediary first → then **SCORES** (SEBI) / **RBI Ombudsman** / **Insurance Ombudsman** / **PFRDA** → then the **Securities Appellate Tribunal (SAT)**.\n\n*(Each is explained in full inside the relevant chapter — these are just your quick-recall anchors.)*\n\n---\n\n*Made with care. Good luck — you've got this! 💪*\n", "chapters": [{"num": "01", "title": "Introduction to Personal Financial Planning", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 1: Introduction to Personal Financial Planning — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 1 · Personal Financial Planning (37 marks — the biggest module in the paper).**\n> This chapter is the *map*. It doesn't have much maths, but it names every concept the rest of the syllabus expands on. Learn the vocabulary here and the other 19 chapters get easier.\n\n---\n\n## 1.1 What is Financial Planning?\n\n**Financial planning is the process of meeting your life goals through the proper management of your finances.**\n\nUnpack that sentence — every word is doing work:\n\n- **Life goals** — buying a house, funding a child's education, retiring comfortably, leaving money to heirs. Not \"getting rich\"; *specific, dated, costed* objectives.\n- **Process** — it's ongoing, not a one-time event. You plan, act, review, adjust.\n- **Management of finances** — income, expenses, savings, investments, insurance, loans and taxes are all levers.\n\n> 🧠 **The core idea:** money has no meaning on its own. ₹50 lakh is neither \"enough\" nor \"not enough\" until you attach it to a goal (a flat in Pune? 25 years of retirement?). Financial planning is the discipline of connecting **money you will have** to **money you will need**.\n\n### Investment vs. Financial Planning\n\n| | Investment advice | Financial planning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Question asked** | \"Which fund should I buy?\" | \"What are you trying to achieve, and what set of decisions gets you there?\" |\n| **Scope** | One product decision | Cash flow, insurance, debt, investment, tax, retirement, estate |\n| **Time horizon** | Often short | Whole lifetime |\n| **Success measured by** | Product return | Whether the goal was met |\n\nA product can beat its benchmark and *still* fail you — if it's illiquid when you need the money. That gap is exactly why financial planning exists.\n\n---\n\n## 1.2 Why is Financial Planning Needed?\n\nFive forces make planning necessary:\n\n1. **Goals cost money and arrive on dates you don't control.** College fees arrive when the child turns 18, not when markets are up.\n2. **Inflation quietly destroys purchasing power.** At 6% inflation, ₹1 lakh of expenses today becomes about ₹3.2 lakh in 20 years. Money kept \"safe\" in a low-interest account *loses* real value.\n3. **Income is finite and lumpy.** Most people earn for ~30–35 years but spend for ~60+ years. The earning years must fund the non-earning years.\n4. **Life is uncertain.** Death, disability, illness and job loss can wipe out a plan that had no insurance or emergency fund.\n5. **Products are complex and conflicted.** Investors face hundreds of schemes and a sales industry paid to push them.\n\n### 1.2.1 Role of the Financial Planner\n\nThe planner:\n\n- **Collects** data on income, expenses, assets, liabilities, insurance and taxes;\n- **Quantifies** goals — converting \"a good retirement\" into \"₹4.2 crore by 2050\";\n- **Assesses risk** — both the *ability* and the *willingness* to take risk;\n- **Recommends** a coherent strategy across products (never a product in isolation);\n- **Implements** or helps implement;\n- **Reviews and rebalances** as life and markets change.\n\nCritically, the planner brings **objectivity and discipline**. Investors panic-sell in crashes and chase winners in booms; a planner is the circuit-breaker between emotion and the \"confirm\" button.\n\n### 1.2.2 Financial Planning vs. a Typical Financial Advisory Service\n\n| Aspect | Typical advisory / distribution | Financial planning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Trigger** | A product is available to sell | A client goal needs funding |\n| **Focus** | Product features and returns | The client's whole balance sheet and cash flow |\n| **Coverage** | One need at a time | Integrated across all needs |\n| **Compensation** | Often commission from the manufacturer | Often a fee from the client |\n| **Duty** | May be a *suitability* standard only | **Fiduciary** — client's interest first |\n| **Relationship** | Transaction | Long-term engagement |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam favourite.** A **fiduciary** must put the client's interest *ahead of their own*. This is the defining obligation of a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser (see Chapters 18–19).\n\n---\n\n## 1.3 Scope of Financial Planning\n\nFinancial planning has **eight** components. Expect questions that give you a client situation and ask which component applies.\n\n### 1.3.1 Personal Financial Analysis\n\nThe diagnosis stage: where does the client stand *today*?\n\n- Build the **personal balance sheet** (assets, liabilities, net worth) and the **cash flow statement** (income, expenses, surplus).\n- Compute the ratios (Chapter 3) — savings, solvency, liquidity, debt-to-income.\n- Then **set goals**.\n\n**Goal-setting rules:**\n\n- **Prioritise goals.** Not everything can be funded at once. Retirement and a child's education generally outrank a car upgrade — because you can borrow for education but *nobody lends for retirement*.\n- **Ensure adequate focus on important goals.** Long-dated, expensive goals (retirement) get ignored because they feel far away. Yet they benefit most from compounding, so starting early is worth more than investing more later.\n- **Stagger the timing of goals.** If three big goals land in the same year, the plan breaks. Move a discretionary goal (a foreign holiday, a car) forward or back so the funding requirement smooths out.\n\nA good goal is **specific, quantified in future rupees (i.e. inflation-adjusted), and dated**. \"Save for my daughter's college\" is not a goal. \"₹42 lakh in March 2038 for a 4-year engineering degree\" is.\n\n### 1.3.2 Cash Flow Management and Budgeting\nTracking inflows and outflows, generating a **surplus**, and building an **emergency/contingency fund** (commonly 3–6 months of expenses). Without a surplus there is nothing to invest — this is the engine of the whole plan. *(Chapter 3.)*\n\n### 1.3.3 Insurance Planning\nProtecting the plan against catastrophic events — death, disability, illness, property loss. Insurance is about **risk transfer**, not returns. If the earning member dies uninsured, every other goal collapses.\n\n### 1.3.4 Debt Management and Counselling\nKeeping borrowing productive and serviceable — the right *purpose*, *cost*, and *maturity* of debt; repayment strategies; and rehabilitation when a borrower is over-leveraged. *(Chapter 4.)*\n\n### 1.3.5 Investment Planning and Asset Allocation\nChoosing the mix of asset classes (equity, debt, gold, real estate) that matches each goal's horizon and the client's risk profile — then selecting products within that mix. **Asset allocation, not stock selection, drives most of a portfolio's outcome.** *(Chapters 14–15.)*\n\n### 1.3.6 Tax Planning\nArranging affairs to legally minimise tax — using available exemptions, deductions and the tax treatment of different products. Note: tax efficiency is a *modifier*, never the primary reason to buy a product.\n\n### 1.3.7 Retirement Planning\nEstimating the corpus needed to fund post-retirement expenses (inflated to the retirement date) and building it through the earning years. *(Covered in depth in Level 2.)*\n\n### 1.3.8 Estate Planning\nEnsuring assets pass to intended beneficiaries smoothly — wills, nominations, trusts, joint holdings. *(Covered in depth in Level 2.)*\n\n---\n\n## 1.4 Assets, Liabilities and Net Worth\n\nThe **personal balance sheet** is a snapshot at a point in time.\n\n$$\\textbf{Net Worth} = \\textbf{Total Assets} - \\textbf{Total Liabilities}$$\n\n### Classifying assets\n\n| Type | What it is | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Liquid assets** | Convertible to cash quickly with little loss of value | Cash, savings balance, liquid funds, sweep-in FDs |\n| **Financial assets** | Claims on someone else | Deposits, bonds, shares, mutual funds, insurance cash values, PF/PPF |\n| **Physical / real assets** | Tangible property | House, land, gold, jewellery, art |\n| **Personal-use assets** | Held for use, not returns | Car, appliances, the home you live in |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap:** *personal-use assets* (car, furniture) generally **depreciate** and produce no income. Counting them heavily inflates net worth but not the ability to fund goals — which is exactly why the **financial assets ratio** exists (Chapter 3).\n\n### Classifying liabilities\n\n- **Short-term:** credit card outstanding, overdrafts, personal loans, dues within a year.\n- **Long-term:** home loan, education loan, vehicle loan.\n\nRecord liabilities at the **outstanding principal** on the statement date — not the original loan amount and not the total of remaining EMIs (that would include future interest).\n\n### Reading net worth\n\n- **Positive and growing** = the plan is working.\n- **Negative** = liabilities exceed assets — technically insolvent; debt management becomes the first priority.\n- Net worth grows in exactly two ways: (a) **saving** out of income, and (b) **appreciation** of assets held.\n\n---\n\n## 1.5 The Financial Planning Process\n\nThe six steps — a favourite for sequence questions. Learn the **order**.\n\n| Step | Name | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | Establish the **client–planner relationship** | Define scope, services, duration, compensation, and both parties' responsibilities — **in writing** |\n| **2** | Gather data on the **client's financial situation** | Quantitative (income, assets, debts, insurance, taxes) *and* qualitative (attitudes, experience, family) |\n| **3** | Define **client needs and financial goals** | Convert wishes into specific, dated, costed goals; prioritise them |\n| **4** | **Risk profiling** and asset allocation | Assess capacity + willingness to bear risk; derive the allocation |\n| **5** | **Portfolio construction** | Recommend and implement a specific plan/product set |\n| **6** | **Reviewing and rebalancing** | Monitor, report, and correct drift as life and markets change |\n\n### 1.5.1 Client–Planner Relationship\nSets the terms of engagement. The adviser must disclose **what services are provided, how they are paid, and any conflicts of interest**. Getting this in writing protects both sides — and for a registered IA it is a regulatory requirement.\n\n### 1.5.2 Client's Financial Situation\nIncomplete data produces a wrong plan. Collect income, expenses, existing investments, loans, insurance covers, tax status, employer benefits — plus family structure and dependants.\n\n### 1.5.3 Client Needs and Financial Goals\nTranslate \"I want to be comfortable\" into numbers. Apply the goal rules from 1.3.1: prioritise, focus, stagger.\n\n### 1.5.4 Risk Profiling\nTwo distinct things, often confused:\n\n- **Risk capacity (ability)** — *objective*. Driven by horizon, income stability, net worth, dependants and how essential the goal is. A 28-year-old with a stable job and no dependants has high capacity.\n- **Risk tolerance/attitude (willingness)** — *subjective*. Psychological comfort with volatility.\n\n> 🧠 **Rule:** where they conflict, plan to the **lower** of the two. High capacity but low tolerance means a client who panic-sells at the bottom, converting a paper loss into a permanent one. Also note a **risk requirement**: the return the goal actually needs.\n\n### 1.5.5 Portfolio Construction\nAsset allocation first, product selection second. Products must fit the goal's **horizon** (don't fund a 1-year goal with equity) and the client's **liquidity** needs.\n\n### 1.5.6 Reviewing and Rebalancing\nTwo separate triggers:\n\n- **Market drift** — a 60:40 equity:debt mix becomes 70:30 after a rally. Rebalancing sells the winner and buys the laggard, restoring the risk level (and enforcing \"sell high, buy low\").\n- **Life events** — marriage, a child, job change, inheritance, illness. These change the *goals*, not just the weights.\n\n---\n\n## 1.6 Financial Advisory and Execution\n\nTwo distinct activities:\n\n- **Advice** — recommending what the client should do. Value comes from the recommendation itself.\n- **Execution / distribution** — actually placing the transaction. Value comes from operational convenience.\n\nThe **conflict**: if the adviser earns commission on what they sell, the recommendation may drift toward whatever pays best. SEBI's answer is the **segregation of advice and distribution** — a registered Investment Adviser must keep advisory separate from any distribution activity, and an individual IA cannot do both. *(Chapter 18 covers the exact provisions.)*\n\n### The IA's value proposition\n\n| Adviser adds value by | Because |\n|---|---|\n| Setting the asset allocation | It drives most of the outcome |\n| Preventing behavioural mistakes | Panic-selling and return-chasing destroy more wealth than bad products |\n| Choosing **direct plans** | Lower expense ratio compounds over decades |\n| Tax-aware structuring | Post-tax return is what the client actually spends |\n| Regular review | Keeps the plan aligned with a changing life |\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Financial planning | Meeting life goals through proper management of finances |\n| Net worth | Total assets − total liabilities |\n| Steps in the process | Relationship → Data → Goals → Risk profiling → Construction → Review |\n| Scope (8 areas) | Analysis, cash flow, insurance, debt, investment, tax, retirement, estate |\n| Risk capacity | **Ability** to take risk — objective (horizon, income, net worth) |\n| Risk tolerance | **Willingness** to take risk — subjective (psychology) |\n| Which one binds? | Plan to the **lower** of capacity and tolerance |\n| Fiduciary | Must place the client's interest **ahead of** their own |\n| Goal quality | Specific + quantified in future rupees + dated + prioritised |\n| Why stagger goals | So funding requirements don't collide in one year |\n| Two ways net worth grows | Saving out of income; appreciation of assets |\n| Rebalancing triggers | Market drift and life events |\n| Advice vs execution | Recommending vs transacting — SEBI requires them segregated |\n\n> **Exam tip:** this chapter is mostly definitional, so the questions are *precise*. Watch the difference between **capacity and tolerance**, between **assets and net worth**, and the **order** of the six process steps.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 1: Introduction to Personal Financial Planning — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** Define financial planning in one sentence.\n> **A:** The process of **meeting your life goals through the proper management of your finances** — connecting money you will have to money you will need.\n\n**Q2.** What are the three things that make a goal a *proper* financial goal?\n> **A:** It is **specific**, **quantified in future (inflation-adjusted) rupees**, and **dated**. \"Save for college\" is not a goal; \"₹42 lakh in March 2038\" is.\n\n**Q3.** What is the formula for net worth?\n> **A:** **Net Worth = Total Assets − Total Liabilities.**\n\n**Q4.** Name the six steps of the financial planning process, in order.\n> **A:** 1) Establish the **client–planner relationship** → 2) Gather the **client's financial situation** → 3) Define **needs and goals** → 4) **Risk profiling** → 5) **Portfolio construction** → 6) **Review and rebalance**.\n\n**Q5.** List the eight components in the scope of financial planning.\n> **A:** Personal financial **analysis**, **cash flow**/budgeting, **insurance**, **debt** management, **investment** planning & asset allocation, **tax**, **retirement**, **estate** planning.\n\n**Q6.** What is the difference between risk *capacity* and risk *tolerance*?\n> **A:** **Capacity = ability**, and it is **objective** (horizon, income stability, net worth, dependants). **Tolerance = willingness**, and it is **subjective** (psychological comfort with volatility).\n\n**Q7.** If a client has high risk capacity but low risk tolerance, what do you plan to?\n> **A:** The **lower** of the two. A client who panics and sells at the bottom converts a paper loss into a permanent one, regardless of how much risk they could theoretically afford.\n\n**Q8.** What does \"fiduciary\" mean?\n> **A:** The adviser must place the **client's interest ahead of their own** — the defining obligation of a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser.\n\n**Q9.** Why can't you simply borrow your way through retirement?\n> **A:** You can borrow for a house or education, but **nobody lends for retirement**. That is why retirement typically outranks discretionary goals in prioritisation.\n\n**Q10.** What does \"staggering goals\" mean and why do it?\n> **A:** Deliberately spacing goals out in time so several large funding requirements **don't land in the same year** and break the plan. Discretionary goals get moved, not essential ones.\n\n**Q11.** Name the four classes of assets on a personal balance sheet.\n> **A:** **Liquid** assets, **financial** assets, **physical/real** assets, and **personal-use** assets.\n\n**Q12.** Why are personal-use assets treated carefully in a balance sheet?\n> **A:** They generally **depreciate and produce no income**, so counting them inflates net worth without improving the ability to fund goals.\n\n**Q13.** At what value do you record a home loan on the personal balance sheet?\n> **A:** The **outstanding principal** on the statement date — *not* the original loan amount, and *not* the sum of remaining EMIs (which would include future interest).\n\n**Q14.** What are the only two ways net worth grows?\n> **A:** **Saving** out of income, and **appreciation** of assets already held.\n\n**Q15.** What does a negative net worth mean?\n> **A:** Liabilities exceed assets — the person is **technically insolvent**, and debt management becomes the first priority.\n\n**Q16.** Give three differences between financial planning and typical product-led advisory.\n> **A:** Planning is triggered by a **client goal** (not a saleable product); it is **integrated** across all needs (not one need at a time); and it usually carries a **fiduciary** duty with fee-based compensation.\n\n**Q17.** What are the two triggers for reviewing and rebalancing a portfolio?\n> **A:** **Market drift** (a rally turns 60:40 into 70:30) and **life events** (marriage, child, job change, inheritance, illness).\n\n**Q18.** How does rebalancing enforce good behaviour?\n> **A:** It mechanically **sells the asset that has risen and buys the one that lagged** — \"sell high, buy low\" — while restoring the intended risk level.\n\n**Q19.** What is the difference between advice and execution?\n> **A:** **Advice** is recommending what the client should do; **execution/distribution** is placing the transaction. SEBI requires an IA to **segregate** the two.\n\n**Q20.** Why does inflation make financial planning necessary?\n> **A:** It destroys purchasing power — at 6% inflation ₹1 lakh of expenses becomes roughly **₹3.2 lakh in 20 years**, so \"safe\" low-interest money loses real value.\n\n**Q21.** Why is the mismatch between earning years and living years a planning problem?\n> **A:** People typically earn for ~30–35 years but spend for ~60+ years, so the earning years must **fund the non-earning years**.\n\n**Q22.** What is a contingency (emergency) fund and how large is it usually?\n> **A:** A pool of liquid money for income shocks and emergencies, commonly **3–6 months of expenses**.\n\n**Q23.** Which drives portfolio outcomes more — asset allocation or security selection?\n> **A:** **Asset allocation.** It explains most of the variation in portfolio returns, which is why it comes before product selection.\n\n**Q24.** Why should long-dated goals get deliberate focus?\n> **A:** They *feel* far away and get neglected, yet they benefit most from **compounding** — starting early is worth more than investing more later.\n\n**Q25.** What must be disclosed when establishing the client–planner relationship?\n> **A:** The **scope of services, duration, compensation, responsibilities of both parties, and any conflicts of interest** — in writing.\n\n**Q26.** What two kinds of data are gathered in step 2?\n> **A:** **Quantitative** (income, expenses, assets, liabilities, insurance, tax status) and **qualitative** (attitudes, risk experience, family structure, dependants).\n\n**Q27.** What is \"risk requirement\"?\n> **A:** The **return the goal actually needs** — distinct from risk capacity (ability) and risk tolerance (willingness).\n\n**Q28.** Why is tax planning never the *primary* reason to buy a product?\n> **A:** Tax efficiency is a **modifier** of return, not a substitute for suitability. A tax-efficient product that doesn't fit the goal's horizon or liquidity need is still the wrong product.\n\n**Q29.** What is the core conflict of interest in commission-based distribution?\n> **A:** The recommendation can drift toward **whatever pays the adviser most**, rather than what suits the client best.\n\n**Q30.** Name five ways an Investment Adviser adds measurable value.\n> **A:** Setting **asset allocation**; preventing **behavioural mistakes**; using **direct plans** (lower expense ratio); **tax-aware** structuring; and **regular review**.\n\n**Q31.** Can a financial plan succeed if the product beats its benchmark?\n> **A:** Not necessarily. A product can outperform and still fail the client — for example if it is **illiquid exactly when the money is needed**. Goal achievement, not product return, is the measure of success.\n\n**Q32.** Insurance planning is about what — returns or protection?\n> **A:** **Risk transfer / protection.** Insurance shields the plan from catastrophic events; it is not bought for returns.\n\n**Q33.** What is the role of the planner as a \"circuit breaker\"?\n> **A:** Investors panic-sell in crashes and chase winners in booms. The planner sits **between emotion and the confirm button**, supplying objectivity and discipline.\n\n**Q34.** Why is ₹50 lakh neither \"enough\" nor \"not enough\" on its own?\n> **A:** Because money has meaning only **relative to a goal**. Enough for a flat in a small town, nowhere near enough for 25 years of retirement.\n\n**Q35.** What four things should debt management get right?\n> **A:** The **purpose**, the **cost**, the **maturity** of the debt — and rehabilitation (counselling/rescheduling) when a borrower is over-leveraged.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 1: Introduction to Personal Financial Planning — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy/Recall), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium/Application), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard/Numerical & Scenario). Remember: the NISM exam has **25% negative marking**, so a wrong answer costs you a quarter of the marks — guess only when you can eliminate at least two options.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Financial planning is best defined as the process of:\nA) Maximising investment returns  B) Meeting life goals through the proper management of finances  C) Selecting the best-performing mutual fund  D) Minimising the tax payable each year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The standard definition ties money to **life goals**: financial planning is meeting one's life goals through the proper management of one's finances.\nA) Maximising returns is not the objective; a high-return product that is illiquid when the goal arrives still fails the client. C) Product selection is one small step inside planning, not the definition. D) Tax minimisation is one of the eight components of scope, not the whole discipline.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Net worth is calculated as:\nA) Total income − total expenses  B) Total assets − total liabilities  C) Liquid assets ÷ monthly expenses  D) Total assets + total liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth is a **balance sheet** concept: everything you own minus everything you owe.\nA) Income minus expenses is the **surplus/savings**, a cash-flow concept. C) Is the liquidity ratio. D) Adding liabilities to assets is meaningless — liabilities are a claim *against* assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which of the following is the FIRST step in the financial planning process?\nA) Risk profiling of the client  B) Constructing the portfolio  C) Establishing the client–planner relationship  D) Gathering the client's financial data\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The process begins by **defining the engagement**: scope of services, duration, compensation, responsibilities and conflicts, preferably in writing.\nD) Data gathering is step 2 — you cannot ethically collect sensitive financial data before the terms of engagement are agreed. A) Risk profiling is step 4. B) Portfolio construction is step 5.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Risk capacity refers to:\nA) The client's psychological comfort with volatility  B) The client's objective ability to bear risk  C) The return the goal requires  D) The volatility of the recommended portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk **capacity** is objective and is driven by time horizon, income stability, net worth and number of dependants.\nA) That is risk **tolerance** (willingness) — subjective. C) That is the risk **requirement**. D) That is portfolio risk, an output, not a client attribute.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which of these is NOT one of the components in the scope of financial planning?\nA) Insurance planning  B) Estate planning  C) Corporate merger advisory  D) Retirement planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Corporate merger advisory is **investment banking**, not personal financial planning.\nA), B) and D) are three of the eight components — the others being personal financial analysis, cash flow/budgeting, debt management, investment planning and tax planning.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A fiduciary is required to:\nA) Guarantee the client a minimum return  B) Place the client's interest ahead of their own  C) Disclose returns only on request  D) Recommend only products from a single manufacturer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The fiduciary standard requires the adviser to put the **client's interest first**, above the adviser's own commercial interest.\nA) No adviser may guarantee returns. C) Disclosure must be proactive, not on-request. D) Restricting to a single manufacturer is a conflict of interest, the very thing a fiduciary duty guards against.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which of the following is a liquid asset?\nA) A residential plot of land  B) Balance in a savings bank account  C) The family car  D) Unlisted equity shares of a private company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A liquid asset can be converted into cash **quickly and without material loss of value** — cash, savings balances, liquid funds, sweep-in deposits.\nA) Land takes months to sell. C) A car is a personal-use asset that depreciates. D) Unlisted shares have no ready market.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The personal balance sheet shows a client's position:\nA) Over a financial year  B) At a specific point in time  C) Only after retirement  D) Averaged over three years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A balance sheet is a **snapshot** on a stated date. The cash-flow (income and expenditure) statement is the one that covers a *period*.\nA) and D) describe flow statements or averages. C) It is prepared at any life stage.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which of these is a personal-use asset?\nA) A fixed deposit  B) An equity mutual fund  C) The car used by the family  D) A rented-out second flat\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Personal-use assets are held for use rather than for return, and typically **depreciate** — cars, appliances, furniture.\nA) and B) are financial assets. D) A rented-out flat generates income, so it is an investment (physical/real) asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** How should an outstanding home loan be recorded on the personal balance sheet?\nA) The original sanctioned loan amount  B) The total of all remaining EMIs  C) The outstanding principal on the statement date  D) The market value of the house\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only the **outstanding principal** is a liability today.\nA) The original amount ignores repayments already made. B) The sum of remaining EMIs includes **future interest**, which is not yet owed. D) The house value is an *asset*, recorded on the other side of the balance sheet.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Risk tolerance is best described as:\nA) Objective and measurable from income and horizon  B) Subjective — the client's psychological willingness to bear volatility  C) The same as risk capacity  D) Determined solely by the client's age\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tolerance is **attitudinal**: how comfortable the client actually feels when the portfolio falls.\nA) Describes risk capacity. C) The two are distinct and often conflict. D) Age influences capacity, but tolerance is psychological and varies between people of the same age.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A contingency or emergency fund is typically sized at:\nA) 1 month of expenses  B) 3–6 months of expenses  C) 3–6 months of income  D) 2 years of expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The common benchmark is **3–6 months of expenses**, held in liquid form.\nA) One month is too thin to absorb a job loss. C) The standard is measured against *expenses*, not income, because expenses are what must be met. D) Two years parks far too much in low-return liquid assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which statement about advice and execution is correct?\nA) They are the same activity  B) Advice is recommending; execution is transacting  C) Execution must always precede advice  D) Only execution requires SEBI registration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Advice** = recommending what the client should do. **Execution/distribution** = actually placing the transaction. SEBI requires an Investment Adviser to segregate the two.\nA) They are distinct and have different conflicts. C) Advice logically precedes execution. D) Investment *advice* is precisely what triggers IA registration.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which of the following increases a client's net worth?\nA) Transferring money from a savings account to a fixed deposit  B) Saving a part of income  C) Taking a personal loan  D) Repaying the interest portion of an EMI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth grows in only two ways: **saving out of income** and **appreciation of assets held**.\nA) Moving money between assets changes composition, not total net worth. C) A loan raises assets *and* liabilities equally — net worth is unchanged. D) Interest is an expense; only the *principal* portion of an EMI reduces liabilities.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** \"Staggering goals\" means:\nA) Postponing all goals indefinitely  B) Spacing goals in time so funding requirements don't collide  C) Funding only the cheapest goals  D) Investing in staggered maturities of bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If several expensive goals land in the same year the plan breaks, so **discretionary** goals are shifted earlier or later.\nA) Postponing everything is avoidance, not planning. C) Cost is not the prioritisation criterion — importance is. D) That is a bond ladder, a portfolio technique.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Which pair correctly matches the concept to its statement?\nA) Surplus — balance sheet  B) Net worth — cash flow statement  C) Net worth — balance sheet  D) Assets — income statement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Net worth** belongs to the **balance sheet** (a stock concept).\nA) Surplus (income − expenses) belongs to the cash flow statement. B) and D) reverse the two statements. Remember: balance sheet = *stocks*, cash flow = *flows*.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Inflation matters to financial planning primarily because it:\nA) Increases interest rates on deposits  B) Erodes the purchasing power of money over time  C) Reduces the tax payable on gains  D) Guarantees higher equity returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflation means the same rupees buy less later, so goals must be costed in **future** rupees.\nA) Rates may rise with inflation but that is a side effect, not the planning reason. C) Inflation does not reduce tax. D) No return is guaranteed by inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Which of the following is a qualitative input in data gathering?\nA) The client's annual salary  B) The outstanding home loan balance  C) The client's past experience of market losses  D) The premium on a term policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Qualitative** data covers attitudes, prior experience, family structure and preferences.\nA), B) and D) are all **quantitative** — hard numbers from documents.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Asset allocation is primarily the decision about:\nA) Which individual stocks to buy  B) The mix of asset classes in the portfolio  C) Which broker to use  D) When to file tax returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Asset allocation is the **mix across asset classes** (equity, debt, gold, real estate).\nA) That is security selection, a later and less impactful decision. C) and D) are operational/compliance matters, not allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which is a long-term liability?\nA) A credit card outstanding due this month  B) A 20-year home loan  C) An overdraft repayable on demand  D) An electricity bill payable next week\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Long-term liabilities run beyond a year — home, education and vehicle loans.\nA), C) and D) are all short-term liabilities due within a year.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The main purpose of insurance planning within a financial plan is:\nA) To generate the highest possible return  B) To transfer the financial impact of catastrophic risks  C) To reduce the client's tax liability  D) To increase the client's net worth quickly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Insurance is about **risk transfer** — protecting the plan from death, disability, illness or property loss.\nA) Insurance is not a return product. C) Tax relief is incidental. D) Insurance protects net worth; it does not build it.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which of the following describes the review-and-rebalance step?\nA) It happens only once, at the start  B) It is triggered by market drift and life events  C) It is required only when markets fall  D) It applies only to clients above age 60\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two triggers: **market drift** (allocation moves away from target) and **life events** (marriage, child, job change, inheritance, illness).\nA) Review is ongoing, not one-off. C) A rally distorts allocation just as a fall does. D) It applies to all clients.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which of the following is TRUE of a goal-based approach?\nA) Goals should be expressed in today's rupees only  B) All goals should be funded simultaneously and equally  C) Goals should be specific, quantified in future rupees and dated  D) Goals need not be prioritised\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A usable goal is specific, costed in **inflation-adjusted future rupees**, and has a date.\nA) Today's rupees understate the requirement. B) Resources are finite, so goals must be prioritised. D) Prioritisation is explicitly part of goal-setting.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** The financial planning process step that immediately follows \"defining client needs and goals\" is:\nA) Establishing the relationship  B) Risk profiling and asset allocation  C) Reviewing and rebalancing  D) Gathering financial data\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Order: relationship → data → goals → **risk profiling** → construction → review.\nA) and D) come earlier. C) Review is the last step.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which of these is an example of a physical/real asset?\nA) A corporate bond  B) A unit-linked insurance plan  C) Gold jewellery  D) A recurring deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Physical/real assets are **tangible** — property, land, gold, art.\nA), B) and D) are all financial assets, i.e. claims on another party.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** A client with a negative net worth is:\nA) In an ideal position for aggressive equity investing  B) Technically insolvent, with debt management as the first priority  C) Required to buy more insurance immediately  D) Unaffected, since net worth is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liabilities exceed assets, which is **technical insolvency**; reducing debt comes before building an investment portfolio.\nA) Aggressive equity while insolvent compounds the problem. C) Insurance matters but is not the first fix. D) Net worth is the central measure of financial health.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which of the following best distinguishes financial planning from product distribution?\nA) Planning always uses direct plans  B) Planning is triggered by a client's goal, distribution by a saleable product  C) Distribution is illegal in India  D) Planning is only for high-net-worth clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Distribution starts from **what can be sold**; planning starts from **what the client needs**.\nA) Direct plans are typical of advisory but not the defining difference. C) Distribution is legal and regulated. D) Planning applies at every income level.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Which of these is a short-term liability?\nA) A 15-year home loan  B) A 7-year education loan  C) A credit card outstanding  D) A 5-year car loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Credit card dues are payable within the month — a classic short-term liability.\nA), B) and D) all extend well beyond one year.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which of the following is the correct relationship?\nA) Risk requirement = the client's psychological comfort  B) Risk requirement = the return the goal needs  C) Risk requirement = total assets ÷ liabilities  D) Risk requirement = the same as risk capacity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **risk requirement** is the return the goal actually demands, given the amount and the time available.\nA) That is tolerance. C) That is a leverage-type ratio. D) Capacity is the *ability* to bear risk, a different concept.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The client–planner relationship step should ideally document:\nA) Only the adviser's fee  B) Scope, duration, compensation, responsibilities and conflicts  C) The expected portfolio return  D) The client's PAN alone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All the engagement terms are documented, in writing, so both sides know the boundaries.\nA) Fee alone is incomplete. C) Returns cannot be promised. D) PAN is collected in onboarding, not the engagement terms.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which of these best explains why retirement usually outranks a car upgrade in priority?\nA) Cars are always cheaper  B) You can borrow for most goals, but nobody lends for retirement  C) Retirement goals are always nearer in time  D) Regulations require it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Loans exist for houses, cars and education; there is **no loan for retirement**, so it must be self-funded.\nA) Cost is not the criterion. C) Retirement is usually the *farthest* goal, which is exactly why it gets neglected. D) No regulation prescribes goal priority.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In the balance sheet, the value of an equity mutual fund holding should be taken at:\nA) The amount originally invested  B) Its current market/NAV-based value  C) The amount net of exit load only  D) Zero, until redeemed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Assets are recorded at **current market value** so the balance sheet reflects reality today.\nA) Cost ignores gains and losses. C) Loads are transaction costs, not the valuation basis. D) Unrealised holdings are still assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Which of the following is TRUE about tax planning within financial planning?\nA) It is the primary reason to select any product  B) It modifies returns but does not override suitability  C) It applies only to salaried clients  D) It replaces the need for asset allocation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tax efficiency is a **modifier** of post-tax return; a tax-friendly product that doesn't fit the horizon or liquidity need is still wrong.\nA) Suitability comes first. C) It applies to all taxpayers. D) Allocation is a separate and more fundamental decision.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The main behavioural value an adviser adds is:\nA) Predicting market tops and bottoms  B) Preventing panic-selling and return-chasing  C) Guaranteeing outperformance  D) Eliminating all portfolio risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investors destroy returns by selling in crashes and buying after rallies; the adviser is the **circuit breaker**.\nA) Market timing is not reliably possible. C) No one can guarantee outperformance. D) Risk can be managed, never eliminated.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Which one of these is an output of the financial planning process rather than an input?\nA) The client's monthly income  B) The client's existing insurance covers  C) The recommended asset allocation  D) The client's outstanding loans\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **recommended asset allocation** is produced by the process.\nA), B) and D) are all data *collected* in step 2 as inputs.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Rahul, aged 27, has a stable government job, no dependants and a 30-year horizon, but he sold all his equity funds in the last 12% market correction. What should the adviser plan to?\nA) His high risk capacity, since he is young  B) His low demonstrated risk tolerance  C) The average of the two  D) The highest equity allocation available\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where capacity and tolerance conflict, plan to the **lower**. Rahul's *capacity* is high (age, stability, no dependants) but his behaviour reveals **low tolerance**. Allocating aggressively guarantees another panic-sale, converting a temporary paper loss into a permanent one.\nA) Ignores demonstrated behaviour. C) Averaging is not the rule; the binding constraint is the lower of the two. D) The worst possible fit for a proven panic-seller. The adviser can raise equity gradually as Rahul's experience grows.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A client owns: savings balance ₹2,00,000; equity funds ₹8,00,000; a house worth ₹60,00,000; a car worth ₹6,00,000. Liabilities: home loan outstanding ₹35,00,000; car loan ₹2,00,000. What is the net worth?\nA) ₹39,00,000  B) ₹76,00,000  C) ₹33,00,000  D) ₹41,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = Total assets − Total liabilities. Assets = 2,00,000 + 8,00,000 + 60,00,000 + 6,00,000 = **₹76,00,000**. Liabilities = 35,00,000 + 2,00,000 = **₹37,00,000**. Net worth = 76,00,000 − 37,00,000 = **₹39,00,000**.\nB) ₹76,00,000 is total assets only, ignoring all debt. C) ₹33,00,000 wrongly excludes the car from assets. D) ₹41,00,000 omits the car loan.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A client takes a ₹5,00,000 personal loan and deposits it in a fixed deposit. The immediate effect on net worth is:\nA) An increase of ₹5,00,000  B) A decrease of ₹5,00,000  C) No change  D) An increase equal to the FD interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Borrowing raises **assets by ₹5,00,000** (the FD) and **liabilities by ₹5,00,000** (the loan) simultaneously. Assets − liabilities is therefore unchanged.\nA) Ignores the new liability. B) Ignores the new asset. D) Interest accrues *later*, and in practice the loan rate will exceed the FD rate, so net worth will actually *fall* over time.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A client says: \"I want my son to study abroad.\" Which is the properly framed goal?\nA) \"Save aggressively for my son\"  B) \"Accumulate ₹75 lakh by June 2035 for a 2-year master's programme\"  C) \"Invest ₹20,000 a month in equity funds\"  D) \"Get the highest return possible before 2035\"\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is **specific** (2-year master's), **quantified in future rupees** (₹75 lakh), and **dated** (June 2035).\nA) is a vague intention. C) describes a *means*, not a goal — you cannot tell whether ₹20,000 a month is enough without the target. D) has a date but no amount, so success cannot be measured.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** An adviser earns a trail commission from a fund house and also charges the client a fee for the same advice. Under SEBI's framework this is problematic because:\nA) Trail commissions are illegal in all cases  B) Advice and distribution must be segregated  C) Fees may not exceed ₹1,000  D) Only banks may earn commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SEBI requires a registered Investment Adviser to **segregate advisory from distribution**; an individual IA cannot provide advice and distribution to the same client, precisely to remove this conflict.\nA) Commissions are legal for distributors, who are not acting as IAs. C) There is a fee cap framework but that is not the issue here. D) Many entities may distribute.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Which client most urgently needs a contingency fund before starting equity SIPs?\nA) A tenured professor with a 25-year horizon and 6 months of expenses in a liquid fund  B) A freelance designer with irregular income and no liquid savings  C) A salaried employee with 8 months of expenses in a sweep-in FD  D) A retiree with a large annuity and 12 months of liquid assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Irregular income plus **zero liquid savings** is the dangerous combination: any income gap forces redemption of long-term investments, possibly at a loss.\nA), C) and D) all already hold 6 months or more of liquid cover, so they can begin or continue long-term investing.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A 45-year-old client wants to fund a goal that is 9 months away. The most appropriate consideration is:\nA) Maximise equity exposure for the highest return  B) Match the product to the short horizon — capital protection and liquidity  C) Use the client's risk tolerance alone  D) Choose whichever product is most tax-efficient\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a **9-month** goal, horizon dominates: the money must be there, in full, on the date. Equity's short-run volatility can easily leave a shortfall.\nA) Nine months is far too short for equity. C) Even a high-tolerance client cannot change the fact that the goal is near. D) Tax efficiency never overrides the horizon match.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which of the following pairs a life event to the correct planning response?\nA) Birth of a child → reduce life insurance  B) Job loss → increase equity SIP amount  C) Inheritance received → review goals and rebalance allocation  D) Marriage → cancel the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An inheritance changes the client's **capacity and resources**, so goals and allocation should be revisited.\nA) A new dependant *increases* the insurance need. B) During job loss the emergency fund is drawn on and SIPs may be paused, not increased. D) Marriage adds responsibilities; the emergency fund becomes more important.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A portfolio set at 60% equity : 40% debt has drifted to 72% : 28% after a strong rally. Rebalancing to target requires:\nA) Buying more equity  B) Selling equity and buying debt  C) Selling debt and buying equity  D) Doing nothing, since equity is performing well\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity has grown beyond target, so you **sell equity and buy debt** to restore 60:40. This both resets the risk level and mechanically enforces \"sell high, buy low\".\nA) and C) push the drift further. D) Leaving it means the client is now carrying materially more risk than the plan intended — precisely when valuations are highest.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Which is the strongest argument for recommending direct plans?\nA) Direct plans always outperform in a single year  B) The lower expense ratio compounds over long horizons  C) Direct plans are guaranteed by SEBI  D) Direct plans have no market risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Direct plans carry **no distribution commission**, so the expense ratio is lower; over decades that cost saving compounds into a meaningfully larger corpus.\nA) Single-year outperformance is not guaranteed and is not the argument. C) SEBI regulates but guarantees nothing. D) Market risk is identical — only the cost differs.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A client's balance sheet is dominated by the self-occupied house (85% of assets). The key planning concern is:\nA) Net worth is too high  B) The asset base is illiquid and cannot fund goals  C) The house should be sold immediately  D) There is no concern; property is the safest asset\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A self-occupied house produces **no income and cannot be partly liquidated**. High net worth on paper coexists with an inability to fund goals — which is why the *financial assets ratio* is tracked.\nA) High net worth is not a problem in itself. C) Selling the home the family lives in is rarely the answer; building financial assets alongside is. D) Property carries market, liquidity and concentration risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which sequence correctly describes goal prioritisation for a 32-year-old with limited surplus?\nA) Foreign holiday → retirement → emergency fund  B) Emergency fund and insurance → retirement and child's education → discretionary goals  C) Retirement → foreign holiday → emergency fund  D) Discretionary goals first, since they are cheapest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Protect first** (emergency fund, insurance), then fund **essential long-term goals** (retirement, education), then **discretionary** wants.\nA) and C) place discretionary spending or long-term goals ahead of basic protection, leaving the plan exposed to a single shock. D) Cost is not the criterion — importance and the consequence of failure are.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** An adviser gathers only the client's income and existing mutual fund holdings, then recommends a portfolio. The main deficiency is:\nA) Too much data was collected  B) Liabilities, insurance and goals were not captured, so the plan may be unsuitable  C) Mutual funds should never be recommended  D) Income is irrelevant to planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Without **liabilities, insurance cover, tax status and goals**, the adviser cannot know the client's capacity, protection gaps or horizons. Incomplete data produces an unsuitable plan.\nA) The problem is too *little* data. C) Mutual funds are perfectly legitimate. D) Income is essential — it is just not sufficient.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which of these is the best example of the planner acting as a \"circuit breaker\"?\nA) Switching the client's portfolio to cash during every correction  B) Persuading a client not to redeem their entire equity SIP after a 15% fall  C) Recommending the fund with the highest one-year return  D) Increasing equity allocation after a rally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Stopping an emotional, plan-destroying decision at the worst moment is precisely the behavioural value the adviser adds.\nA) Systematically selling into every fall *is* the mistake. C) Chasing recent winners is return-chasing. D) Raising equity after a rally increases risk exactly when valuations are richest.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A client aged 55 with 5 years to retirement and a modest corpus has a high risk *requirement* but low risk *capacity*. The appropriate response is:\nA) Take maximum equity risk to close the gap  B) Reset the goal — retire later, save more, or reduce the target corpus  C) Ignore the shortfall  D) Borrow to invest in equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When the return a goal *needs* exceeds what the client can *safely* pursue, the honest answer is to change the **goal**, not to gamble: extend the working years, raise the savings rate, or lower the target.\nA) Chasing a high required return with 5 years left risks a catastrophic shortfall precisely when there is no time to recover. C) Ignoring it guarantees failure. D) Leveraging into equity near retirement is reckless.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which of the following correctly describes the difference between the balance sheet and cash flow statement?\nA) Both cover a period of time  B) The balance sheet is a snapshot; the cash flow statement covers a period  C) The cash flow statement shows net worth  D) The balance sheet shows monthly surplus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Balance sheet = **stock** at a point in time; cash flow statement = **flow** over a period.\nA) Only the cash flow statement covers a period. C) Net worth comes from the balance sheet. D) Surplus comes from the cash flow statement.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A client insists on investing an emergency fund in a small-cap equity fund \"for better returns\". The adviser should explain that:\nA) Small-cap funds are the ideal emergency vehicle  B) An emergency fund's job is availability of full value on demand, not return  C) Emergency funds are unnecessary  D) The fund will guarantee capital\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An emergency fund's defining requirement is **liquidity and certainty of value** at the moment of need — which is often exactly when markets are falling and jobs are being lost.\nA) Small-caps are the most volatile and least liquid segment. C) Emergency funds are foundational. D) No equity fund guarantees capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A client's goals are: retirement in 25 years, a car in 2 years, and a home down-payment in 4 years. Which allocation logic is correct?\nA) All three funded from the same aggressive equity portfolio  B) Longer-horizon goals can carry more equity; nearer goals need debt/liquid products  C) All three in fixed deposits  D) Nearer goals should carry the most equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Horizon drives allocation.** The 25-year retirement goal can absorb equity volatility; the 2-year and 4-year goals need capital stability.\nA) A single aggressive pot exposes near-term goals to a badly timed drawdown. C) All-FD would likely fail the 25-year goal against inflation. D) Reverses the correct logic entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which of these is a conflict of interest an Investment Adviser must disclose?\nA) The adviser has passed the NISM examination  B) The adviser's family member owns the fund house being recommended  C) The adviser uses a spreadsheet for calculations  D) The adviser has more than 50 clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **financial or family interest in a recommended product provider** is a material conflict and must be disclosed.\nA) Certification is a qualification, not a conflict. C) and D) are operational facts with no bearing on the adviser's incentives.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** The primary reason that a product beating its benchmark can still fail a client is:\nA) Benchmarks are always wrong  B) The product may not match the goal's horizon or liquidity need  C) Outperformance is taxed at a higher rate  D) SEBI prohibits benchmark comparison\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Success is measured by whether the **goal** was met. A great fund that is locked in, or volatile just when the money is needed, still fails the client.\nA) Benchmarks are imperfect but that is not the point. C) Tax does not depend on beating a benchmark. D) Benchmark comparison is required, not prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which statement about the eight components of financial planning scope is correct?\nA) They are applied strictly one at a time, in isolation  B) They are interlinked — a decision in one affects the others  C) Only investment planning matters for young clients  D) Estate planning applies only to the very wealthy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The components are **integrated**: taking a large home loan changes cash flow, insurance need, tax position and investible surplus at once.\nA) Treating them in isolation is exactly the weakness of product-led advice. C) Young clients still need protection and cash-flow discipline. D) Anyone with assets and heirs benefits from at least a will and correct nominations.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A client earns ₹1,20,000 a month and spends ₹1,15,000. The most immediate planning issue is:\nA) The portfolio is wrongly allocated  B) There is almost no surplus to fund any goal  C) The client needs more insurance riders  D) The client should buy a house\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A surplus of only ₹5,000 (about **4% of income**) means the *engine* of the plan is barely running. Cash-flow management must come before any allocation discussion.\nA) Allocation is irrelevant when there is nothing meaningful to allocate. C) Insurance matters but is not the binding constraint. D) Adding an EMI to an already stretched budget makes it worse.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which best describes why long-dated goals are frequently under-funded?\nA) They are cheaper than short-term goals  B) They feel distant, so they lose out to immediate wants — despite benefiting most from compounding  C) Regulations discourage long-term saving  D) Compounding does not apply beyond 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Psychological discounting of the distant future means retirement loses to today's spending, yet the long horizon is exactly what makes compounding powerful.\nA) They are usually the *most* expensive. C) Tax rules generally *encourage* long-term saving. D) Compounding grows stronger with time, not weaker.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** An adviser recommends the same model portfolio to every client regardless of circumstances. This violates:\nA) Nothing — model portfolios are standard practice  B) The suitability principle, since recommendations must fit the individual client  C) Only the record-keeping rules  D) The requirement to file tax returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Suitability** requires the recommendation to reflect the client's goals, horizon, risk profile and circumstances.\nA) Model portfolios may be a starting point but cannot replace client-specific assessment. C) Record-keeping is separate. D) Irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A client's assets are ₹40 lakh and liabilities ₹52 lakh. Which statement is correct?\nA) Net worth is ₹92 lakh  B) Net worth is −₹12 lakh and debt reduction is the priority  C) Net worth is ₹12 lakh  D) Net worth cannot be negative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth = 40 − 52 = **−₹12 lakh**. The client is technically insolvent, so **debt management comes first**.\nA) Adds instead of subtracting. C) Ignores the sign — the answer is negative. D) Net worth certainly can be negative, which is the whole warning here.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which of these correctly ranks the *impact* on long-run portfolio outcomes?\nA) Security selection > asset allocation > market timing  B) Asset allocation has the largest impact  C) Broker choice > asset allocation  D) Timing the market reliably beats all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Empirical work consistently finds **asset allocation** explains most of the variation in portfolio returns over time.\nA) Reverses the order. C) Broker choice affects costs marginally. D) Consistent market timing is not reliably achievable.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A 60-year-old retiree needs ₹40,000 a month from a ₹90 lakh corpus. The adviser's first check should be:\nA) Whether the corpus can sustain the withdrawal after inflation  B) Which small-cap fund has the best 3-year record  C) Whether the client wants a new car  D) The client's credit score\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The core question is **sustainability**: ₹4.8 lakh a year is a 5.3% withdrawal rate on ₹90 lakh, and that requirement will *rise* with inflation while the corpus must last decades.\nB) Chasing small-cap returns with a drawdown portfolio is dangerous. C) A discretionary want, not the first check. D) Credit score matters for borrowing, not for withdrawal sustainability.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which action best demonstrates fiduciary conduct?\nA) Recommending the product with the highest commission because it also performs adequately  B) Recommending a lower-cost direct plan that pays the adviser nothing extra  C) Not disclosing a family stake in a recommended product  D) Guaranteeing 12% returns to retain a client\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Choosing the option that is **best for the client even though it earns the adviser less** is fiduciary conduct in its purest form.\nA) Letting commission drive the recommendation is the conflict itself. C) Non-disclosure of a material interest is a breach. D) Guaranteeing returns is both false and prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client wants to fund a wedding in 18 months and retirement in 22 years from one lump sum. The correct approach is:\nA) Invest the whole sum in equity  B) Split by goal — short-horizon money in liquid/debt, long-horizon money in growth assets  C) Invest the whole sum in a savings account  D) Fund the wedding by borrowing and invest everything in equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Goal-bucketing**: each goal gets an allocation matched to *its own* horizon.\nA) Risks an 18-month shortfall. C) Wastes 22 years of compounding on the retirement portion. D) Adds unnecessary interest cost and leverage risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which of the following is an example of the \"risk requirement\" driving a plan revision?\nA) A client's income rises, so capacity rises  B) A client feels uneasy about volatility  C) The corpus needed implies a 16% annual return, which is unrealistic, so the goal is reset  D) The adviser changes broker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — When the **return the goal demands** is implausible, the plan (not the portfolio) must change — save more, extend the horizon, or reduce the target.\nA) That is capacity. B) That is tolerance. D) Operational, not a planning trigger.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A client holds ₹15 lakh in a savings account \"to be safe\" while paying 11% on a ₹10 lakh personal loan. The adviser should point out:\nA) The savings balance is earning more than the loan costs  B) Using idle low-yield cash to repay high-cost debt gives a certain, tax-free 11% return  C) The loan should be increased  D) Nothing — the arrangement is optimal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Repaying an 11% loan with cash earning ~3% is a **guaranteed 8% improvement**, with no market risk. Paying off debt is a risk-free, tax-free \"return\" equal to the interest saved.\nA) Savings rates are far below 11%. C) More debt worsens it. D) It is clearly sub-optimal — though an emergency buffer should still be retained.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which of these best reflects the integrated nature of financial planning?\nA) Buying a term policy has no effect on the investment plan  B) A large home loan EMI reduces investible surplus, raises the insurance need and changes the tax position  C) Tax planning is entirely separate from investment planning  D) Estate planning affects nothing else\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — One decision ripples across **cash flow, protection and tax** simultaneously — the essence of integration.\nA) The premium consumes surplus and the cover protects the plan. C) Product tax treatment directly affects post-tax returns. D) Estate decisions interact with ownership, nomination and tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** An adviser tells a client \"equity always gives 15% over the long term\". This is inappropriate because:\nA) Equity returns are lower than debt  B) Past returns are not guaranteed and stating them as certain misleads the client  C) Equity is prohibited for retail investors  D) 15% is below inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Presenting a historical average as a **guarantee** is misleading. Returns vary by period, and the client may make decisions on a false premise.\nA) Equity has historically outperformed debt over long periods, but not with certainty. C) Equity is freely available to retail investors. D) 15% would comfortably exceed typical Indian inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** In which situation is rebalancing MOST clearly required?\nA) Equity has moved from 60% to 61% of the portfolio  B) A 60:40 portfolio has become 78:22 after a two-year rally  C) The client received a routine account statement  D) A fund changed its logo\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **18-percentage-point drift** means the client is carrying materially more risk than the plan intended.\nA) A 1-point drift is inside any reasonable tolerance band and rebalancing would only incur cost and tax. C) and D) are not portfolio events at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A client asks the adviser to simply \"pick the best fund\". The most professional response is:\nA) Name the top-performing fund of the last year  B) Explain that the right fund depends on the goal, horizon and risk profile, and begin the planning process  C) Refuse to help  D) Recommend the fund paying the highest commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — There is no context-free \"best fund\". Redirecting to **goal, horizon and risk profile** is exactly what distinguishes planning from product-pushing.\nA) Chasing last year's winner is return-chasing. C) Refusal helps no one. D) A direct conflict of interest.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client has assets of ₹1,10,00,000 and liabilities of ₹42,00,000. During the year she saves ₹6,00,000 and her assets appreciate by ₹9,00,000, while she repays ₹3,00,000 of loan principal. What is her closing net worth?\nA) ₹68,00,000  B) ₹83,00,000  C) ₹86,00,000  D) ₹80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Work both sides of the balance sheet.\n\n**Opening net worth** = 1,10,00,000 − 42,00,000 = **₹68,00,000**.\n\n**Closing assets** = 1,10,00,000 + 6,00,000 (saved) + 9,00,000 (appreciation) − 3,00,000 (cash used to repay principal) = **₹1,22,00,000**.\n**Closing liabilities** = 42,00,000 − 3,00,000 = **₹39,00,000**.\n**Closing net worth** = 1,22,00,000 − 39,00,000 = **₹83,00,000**.\n\nShortcut: net worth grows only by **savings + appreciation** = 6,00,000 + 9,00,000 = ₹15,00,000, so 68,00,000 + 15,00,000 = **₹83,00,000**.\n\nA) ₹68,00,000 is the *opening* figure, ignoring the year's growth. C) ₹86,00,000 wrongly adds the ₹3,00,000 repayment as an increase. D) ₹80,00,000 omits part of the appreciation. **Key principle: repaying principal is net-worth neutral** — it swaps ₹3,00,000 of assets for ₹3,00,000 less liability.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A client's goal needs ₹50,00,000 in today's terms, 15 years away, with inflation at 6% p.a. What is the goal value in future rupees (nearest lakh)?\nA) ₹95,00,000  B) ₹1,19,80,000  C) ₹80,00,000  D) ₹1,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = Present cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ = ₹50,00,000 × (1.06)¹⁵. (1.06)¹⁵ ≈ **2.3966**. So ₹50,00,000 × 2.3966 ≈ **₹1,19,83,000 ≈ ₹1.20 crore**.\nA) ₹95,00,000 roughly applies simple interest (50 × 1.9). C) ₹80,00,000 badly understates the compounding. D) ₹1,50,00,000 assumes a 3× multiple, too high for 6% over 15 years. **This is why goals must be stated in future rupees — the real requirement is 2.4× the sticker price today.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A client's monthly income is ₹1,50,000 and monthly expenses ₹1,05,000. What is the savings ratio?\nA) 70%  B) 30%  C) 43%  D) 15%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Savings ratio = Savings ÷ Income. Savings = 1,50,000 − 1,05,000 = **₹45,000**. Ratio = 45,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **0.30 = 30%**.\nA) 70% is the *expenses* ratio (1,05,000 ÷ 1,50,000). C) 43% wrongly divides savings by expenses (45,000 ÷ 1,05,000). D) 15% halves the correct answer. Note savings and expenses ratios must sum to 100%.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A client holds: cash ₹50,000; liquid fund ₹2,50,000; equity funds ₹12,00,000; PPF ₹8,00,000; house ₹75,00,000; car ₹8,00,000. Monthly expenses are ₹75,000. What is the liquidity ratio (in months)?\nA) 4.0 months  B) 3.3 months  C) 0.67 months  D) 28.0 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Liquidity ratio = **Liquid assets ÷ Monthly expenses**. Liquid assets = cash ₹50,000 + liquid fund ₹2,50,000 = **₹3,00,000**. Ratio = 3,00,000 ÷ 75,000 = **4.0 months**.\nB) 3.3 months uses only ₹2,50,000. C) 0.67 uses cash alone. D) 28 months wrongly counts equity and PPF as liquid — equity is volatile and PPF is locked in, so neither qualifies. The client has 4 months of cover: acceptable, though the low end of the 3–6 month norm.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Using Q74's data, what is the client's total asset figure and the financial assets ratio?\nA) ₹1,06,00,000 and 21.7%  B) ₹98,00,000 and 23.5%  C) ₹1,06,00,000 and 78.3%  D) ₹1,06,00,000 and 11.3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total assets = 50,000 + 2,50,000 + 12,00,000 + 8,00,000 + 75,00,000 + 8,00,000 = **₹1,06,00,000**. Financial assets = cash + liquid fund + equity + PPF = 50,000 + 2,50,000 + 12,00,000 + 8,00,000 = **₹23,00,000**. Ratio = 23,00,000 ÷ 1,06,00,000 = **21.7%**.\nB) Miscounts total assets. C) 78.3% is the *physical* asset share (house + car). D) 11.3% counts only equity. **Interpretation: with 78% of wealth in a house and a car, this client is asset-rich but cannot fund goals — the classic illiquidity trap.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client's goal requires ₹1,00,00,000 in 12 years. He can invest a lump sum of ₹35,00,000 today. What annual return does the goal require?\nA) 6.0%  B) 9.15%  C) 12.5%  D) 8.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the **risk requirement**. FV = PV × (1+r)ⁿ, so r = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 = (1,00,00,000 ÷ 35,00,000)^(1/12) − 1 = (2.857)^(0.08333) − 1. ln(2.857) ≈ 1.0498; ÷ 12 = 0.08748; e^0.08748 ≈ 1.0914. So r ≈ **9.15% p.a.**\nA) 6% would give only ₹70.4 lakh. C) 12.5% would overshoot to about ₹1.44 crore. D) 8% gives about ₹88 lakh — short of the goal. In Excel: `=RATE(12,0,-3500000,10000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A 30-year-old plans to retire at 60. His current annual expenses are ₹6,00,000. At 6% inflation, what will his annual expenses be in the first year of retirement?\nA) ₹18,00,000  B) ₹34,46,000  C) ₹24,00,000  D) ₹12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Years to retirement = 60 − 30 = **30**. Future expenses = ₹6,00,000 × (1.06)³⁰. (1.06)³⁰ ≈ **5.7435**. So ₹6,00,000 × 5.7435 ≈ **₹34,46,000**.\nA) ₹18,00,000 assumes only a 3× rise. C) ₹24,00,000 assumes 4×. D) ₹12,00,000 assumes expenses merely double. **The lesson: over 30 years at 6%, costs rise nearly 5.75×** — which is why retirement corpora look shockingly large.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A client has a home loan of ₹30,00,000 at 9%, a car loan of ₹5,00,000 at 11%, and a credit card balance of ₹1,50,000 at 42%. She has ₹2,00,000 spare. Under the *avalanche* method she should repay:\nA) The car loan, as it is mid-sized  B) The credit card first, as it has the highest rate  C) The home loan, as it is largest  D) Split equally across all three\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **avalanche** method attacks the **highest interest rate first**, which minimises total interest paid. At 42%, the card is by far the costliest.\nA) and C) ignore the rate. D) Splitting dilutes the benefit. *(Contrast: the **snowball** method repays the smallest balance first for psychological momentum. Here both happen to point at the card, since it is both costliest and smallest.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A client wants ₹20,00,000 in 6 years. She invests ₹10,00,000 today at 9% p.a. Will she reach the goal, and what is the gap?\nA) Yes, with a surplus of ₹2.4 lakh  B) No — she reaches about ₹16.77 lakh, a shortfall of about ₹3.23 lakh  C) No — she reaches ₹12 lakh  D) Yes, exactly ₹20 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FV = 10,00,000 × (1.09)⁶. (1.09)⁶ ≈ **1.6771**. FV ≈ **₹16,77,100**. Shortfall = 20,00,000 − 16,77,100 = **₹3,22,900**.\nA) Overstates growth. C) ₹12 lakh implies simple interest of about 3.3%. D) Would need roughly 12.2% a year. **The adviser's options: invest more now, add a monthly SIP, extend the horizon, or reduce the target.**\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A client's portfolio target is 50% equity, 30% debt, 20% gold on a ₹40,00,000 portfolio. Current values: equity ₹24,00,000, debt ₹10,00,000, gold ₹6,00,000. What trade rebalances it?\nA) Sell ₹4,00,000 equity; buy ₹2,00,000 debt and ₹2,00,000 gold  B) Buy ₹4,00,000 equity  C) Sell ₹2,00,000 gold  D) No action needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total = ₹40,00,000. Targets: equity 50% = **₹20,00,000**; debt 30% = **₹12,00,000**; gold 20% = **₹8,00,000**. Current vs target: equity 24 vs 20 → **sell ₹4,00,000**; debt 10 vs 12 → **buy ₹2,00,000**; gold 6 vs 8 → **buy ₹2,00,000**.\nB) Increases the existing overweight. C) Gold is *under*weight, not over. D) Equity is 60% against a 50% target — a 10-point drift that clearly warrants action.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client aged 40 will need ₹80,00,000 in 20 years. Assuming 10% p.a., what lump sum is needed today (nearest ₹1,000)?\nA) ₹11,89,000  B) ₹40,00,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹18,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = FV ÷ (1+r)ⁿ = 80,00,000 ÷ (1.10)²⁰. (1.10)²⁰ ≈ **6.7275**. PV = 80,00,000 ÷ 6.7275 ≈ **₹11,89,150 ≈ ₹11,89,000**.\nB) ₹40,00,000 halves the target arbitrarily. C) ₹8,00,000 divides by 10. D) ₹18,50,000 corresponds to roughly 7.6%. In Excel: `=PV(10%,20,0,-8000000)`. **Note how powerful 20 years is: under ₹12 lakh becomes ₹80 lakh.**\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Two clients, both aged 35 with identical incomes: A has 3 dependants and an unstable job; B is single with a government job. Which statement is correct?\nA) They must have identical portfolios since age and income match  B) B has higher risk capacity because of income stability and no dependants  C) A has higher risk capacity because of more dependants  D) Risk capacity depends only on age\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk **capacity** depends on horizon, **income stability**, net worth and **dependants**. B's secure income and absence of dependants both raise capacity.\nA) Identical age and income do not make identical circumstances. C) More dependants *reduce* capacity — more claims on the same income. D) Age is only one of several drivers.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A ₹25,00,000 portfolio generated a 14% return in a year in which inflation was 6%. What is the approximate real return?\nA) 8.00%  B) 7.55%  C) 20.00%  D) 6.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 = (1.14 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.07547 − 1 = **7.55%**.\nA) 8% is the crude subtraction (14 − 6), which always slightly *overstates* the real return. C) 20% adds them. D) 6% is the inflation rate itself. **Use the exact formula — the exam tests precisely this distinction.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A client has monthly income ₹2,00,000, total EMIs ₹70,000, total assets ₹1,50,00,000 and total liabilities ₹60,00,000. What are the debt-to-income ratio and the solvency ratio?\nA) 35% and 1.50  B) 35% and 0.60  C) 35% and 0.40  D) 40% and 0.60\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Debt-to-income = EMIs ÷ Income = 70,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **35%**. Solvency ratio = Net worth ÷ Total assets = (1,50,00,000 − 60,00,000) ÷ 1,50,00,000 = 90,00,000 ÷ 1,50,00,000 = **0.60**.\nA) 1.50 divides assets by liabilities — not the solvency ratio. C) 0.40 is the **leverage** ratio (liabilities ÷ assets), the complement of solvency. D) Miscomputes the EMI share (uses ₹80,000). **Note leverage + solvency = 1**, a useful self-check: 0.40 + 0.60 = 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A client can save ₹25,000 a month for 10 years at 11% p.a. (monthly compounding). Approximately what corpus results?\nA) ₹30,00,000  B) ₹54,80,000  C) ₹43,00,000  D) ₹66,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Ordinary annuity FV = PMT × [((1+i)ⁿ − 1) ÷ i], with i = 11% ÷ 12 = **0.0091667** and n = 120. (1.0091667)¹²⁰ ≈ **2.9853**. So FV = 25,000 × [(2.9853 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667] = 25,000 × (1.9853 ÷ 0.0091667) = 25,000 × **216.58** ≈ **₹54,14,500 ≈ ₹54.8 lakh** (small rounding differences).\nA) ₹30,00,000 is just the total invested (₹25,000 × 120), ignoring all returns. C) ₹43,00,000 understates the compounding. D) ₹66,00,000 overstates it. In Excel: `=FV(11%/12,120,-25000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Continuing Q85: how much of that corpus is the client's own contribution, and how much is return?\nA) ₹30,00,000 contributed; about ₹24,80,000 return  B) ₹25,00,000 contributed; ₹29,80,000 return  C) ₹54,80,000 contributed; nil return  D) ₹12,00,000 contributed; ₹42,80,000 return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total contributed = ₹25,000 × 120 months = **₹30,00,000**. Corpus ≈ ₹54,80,000, so the return component ≈ 54,80,000 − 30,00,000 = **₹24,80,000**.\nB) Uses one month's contribution. C) Ignores returns entirely. D) Understates contributions. **Teaching point: over 10 years roughly 45% of the corpus came from returns — and that share rises steeply with longer horizons.**\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** An adviser is paid a fee by the client *and* a commission by a product manufacturer for the same recommendation, without disclosure. This is:\nA) Acceptable if returns are good  B) A breach of the fiduciary duty and disclosure obligations  C) Required by SEBI  D) Acceptable if the client does not ask\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Undisclosed dual compensation creates a **material conflict of interest** and breaches both the fiduciary duty and disclosure obligations; SEBI additionally requires segregation of advisory and distribution.\nA) Good outcomes do not cure a conflict — the process was compromised. C) SEBI requires the opposite. D) Disclosure is proactive; silence is not consent.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client aged 50 has a ₹60,00,000 corpus and needs ₹1,50,00,000 at 60. What return is required, and what should the adviser conclude?\nA) 9.6% — comfortably achievable with a debt portfolio  B) About 9.6% — demanding but feasible with a growth-tilted portfolio, else the goal must be reset  C) 25% — easily achievable  D) 4% — very conservative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = (1,50,00,000 ÷ 60,00,000)^(1/10) − 1 = (2.5)^(0.1) − 1. ln(2.5) ≈ 0.9163; ÷ 10 = 0.09163; e^0.09163 ≈ 1.0959. So r ≈ **9.6% p.a.**\nA) A pure debt portfolio is unlikely to deliver 9.6% consistently. C) The required return is nowhere near 25%. D) 4% would reach only about ₹88.8 lakh. **The professional answer: 9.6% over 10 years needs real equity exposure — but the client is 50, so if capacity or tolerance won't support it, reset the goal (save more, retire later, or lower the target) rather than over-reach.**\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A client's expenses are ₹90,000 a month and he holds ₹1,80,000 in liquid assets. His job is contract-based, renewed annually. What should the adviser advise?\nA) Nothing — 2 months of cover is fine  B) Build the contingency fund toward at least 6 months, given the income insecurity  C) Invest the ₹1,80,000 in mid-cap equity  D) Reduce insurance cover to save money\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liquidity ratio = 1,80,000 ÷ 90,000 = **2 months**, below the 3–6 month norm. With **contract-based, annually renewed income**, the risk of a gap is high, so the target should sit at the *upper* end — about **₹5,40,000**.\nA) Two months is thin for insecure income. C) Moving the emergency fund into mid-caps defeats its purpose. D) Insecure income makes protection *more* important.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A client compares two options for a 3-year goal: Option A returns 12% but can fall 25% in a bad year; Option B returns 7% with negligible volatility. Which is more appropriate and why?\nA) Option A — higher return always wins  B) Option B — a 3-year horizon cannot absorb a 25% drawdown near the goal date  C) Option A, because 3 years is long term  D) Neither; the client should not invest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With only **3 years**, there is no time to recover from a large drawdown. If the fall happens in year 3, the goal simply fails.\nA) Return cannot be assessed without the horizon and the consequence of failure. C) Three years is *not* long term for volatile assets — equity generally needs 5–7 years or more. D) Not investing guarantees inflation erosion; Option B is the sound middle path.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A client's total assets are ₹80,00,000, of which ₹64,00,000 is a self-occupied house. Liabilities are ₹20,00,000. Compute the solvency ratio and comment.\nA) 0.75 — strong on paper, but wealth is concentrated and illiquid  B) 0.25 — insolvent  C) 1.25 — impossible  D) 0.80 — fully liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = 80,00,000 − 20,00,000 = **₹60,00,000**. Solvency ratio = 60,00,000 ÷ 80,00,000 = **0.75**, which is healthy.\nB) 0.25 is the leverage ratio (20 ÷ 80). C) Solvency cannot exceed 1 unless liabilities are negative. D) The portfolio is anything but liquid. **The nuance the exam wants: a strong solvency ratio can coexist with poor goal-funding ability when 80% of assets sit in a self-occupied house.**\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Which combination correctly describes a client who should NOT be advised into a 100% equity portfolio?\nA) Age 25, 30-year horizon, stable income, high tolerance  B) Age 58, retiring in 2 years, sole corpus, low tolerance  C) Age 30, 20-year goal, secure job  D) Age 35, 25-year retirement goal, comfortable with volatility\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short horizon (**2 years**), the corpus is the **only** one, and **low tolerance** — every factor argues against full equity. A market fall just before retirement would be unrecoverable.\nA), C) and D) all combine long horizons with adequate capacity, so meaningful equity is defensible.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client will receive a ₹40,00,000 inheritance. Goals: clear a ₹12,00,000 loan at 13%, build a ₹4,50,000 emergency fund, and invest the rest for retirement in 20 years. What is the most sensible sequence?\nA) Invest all ₹40,00,000 in equity, keep paying the loan  B) Repay the 13% loan, fund the emergency corpus, invest the remaining ₹23,50,000 for retirement  C) Keep it all in a savings account  D) Repay the loan only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Repaying a **13%** loan is a **guaranteed, tax-free 13% return** — very hard to beat with certainty. Then secure the **₹4,50,000** emergency buffer. The remaining ₹40,00,000 − 12,00,000 − 4,50,000 = **₹23,50,000** goes to the 20-year retirement goal, where equity is appropriate.\nA) Carrying 13% debt while chasing uncertain returns is poor risk-adjusted logic. C) Guarantees inflation erosion over 20 years. D) Leaves the client without a buffer and wastes the compounding opportunity.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A goal needs ₹30,00,000 in 8 years. The client invests ₹18,000 a month at 10% p.a. (monthly compounding). Does she reach it?\nA) Yes, with a large surplus  B) She reaches about ₹26.4 lakh — a shortfall of roughly ₹3.6 lakh  C) She reaches exactly ₹30,00,000  D) She reaches about ₹17.3 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — i = 10% ÷ 12 = **0.008333**, n = 96. (1.008333)⁹⁶ ≈ **2.2182**. FV = 18,000 × [(2.2182 − 1) ÷ 0.008333] = 18,000 × (1.2182 ÷ 0.008333) = 18,000 × **146.19** ≈ **₹26,31,000**. Shortfall ≈ **₹3.69 lakh**.\nA) There is no surplus. C) She would need about ₹20,500 a month. D) ₹17.3 lakh is roughly the un-compounded total (₹18,000 × 96 = ₹17.28 lakh) — which is exactly the *contribution*, not the corpus. In Excel: `=FV(10%/12,96,-18000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Continuing Q94: what monthly investment would exactly meet the ₹30,00,000 goal?\nA) ₹18,000  B) About ₹20,520  C) About ₹25,000  D) About ₹31,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PMT = FV ÷ annuity factor = 30,00,000 ÷ **146.19** ≈ **₹20,522**.\nA) ₹18,000 is the shortfall case from Q94. C) ₹25,000 would overshoot to roughly ₹36.5 lakh. D) ₹31,250 is simply ₹30,00,000 ÷ 96, ignoring all returns. In Excel: `=PMT(10%/12,96,0,3000000)`. **A ₹2,500 a month increase closes the entire gap — the kind of concrete, actionable answer a planner is paid for.**\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** An adviser prepares a plan without asking about the client's existing life insurance. Six months later the sole earner dies with no cover, and the family's goals collapse. The principal failure was in which step?\nA) Step 1 — establishing the relationship  B) Step 2 — gathering the client's financial situation  C) Step 5 — portfolio construction  D) Step 6 — review and rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Data gathering** must capture existing insurance cover; without it the protection gap is invisible and the plan is built on incomplete facts.\nA) The engagement terms were not the failure. C) The portfolio may have been fine — the *protection* was missing. D) Review comes later and cannot repair a foundational data gap. **Insurance planning is one of the eight components precisely because an uninsured death destroys every other goal.**\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A client's portfolio target is 70:30 equity:debt with a ±5 percentage-point tolerance band. Current allocation is 73:27. The correct action is:\nA) Rebalance immediately  B) No action — the drift is inside the band  C) Move to 100% debt  D) Increase equity to 80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The drift is **3 percentage points**, inside the ±5-point band. Rebalancing anyway would incur **transaction costs and capital gains tax** for no meaningful change in risk.\nA) Premature — bands exist exactly to prevent over-trading. C) An extreme move unrelated to the plan. D) Deliberately pushes the drift beyond the band.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client earns ₹18,00,000 a year, spends ₹13,50,000 and pays ₹2,40,000 in EMIs (included in expenses). What is the savings ratio, and is the debt burden reasonable?\nA) 25% savings; debt-to-income 13.3% — both healthy  B) 33% savings; debt-to-income 20% — both healthy  C) 25% savings; debt-to-income 40% — debt is excessive  D) 15% savings; debt-to-income 13.3% — savings too low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 18,00,000 − 13,50,000 = **₹4,50,000**; savings ratio = 4,50,000 ÷ 18,00,000 = **25%**. Debt-to-income = 2,40,000 ÷ 18,00,000 = **13.3%**, comfortably under the ~35–40% guideline.\nB) Miscomputes both. C) Overstates the EMI burden. D) Understates the savings ratio. **A 25% savings rate with a 13% debt burden is a genuinely strong position — the client can afford to invest toward long-term goals.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client insists on a goal requiring a 22% annual return over 7 years. The adviser's most professional response is:\nA) Promise to deliver 22% using derivatives  B) Explain that 22% sustained is unrealistic, and reset the goal by saving more, extending the horizon, or lowering the target  C) Recommend the riskiest available fund and hope  D) Tell the client the goal is impossible and end the engagement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When the **risk requirement** is implausible, the honest professional response is to change the **plan variables** the client controls: contribution, time, or target.\nA) Promising returns is prohibited and false. C) Hoping is not a strategy, and the downside falls entirely on the client. D) Correct diagnosis but poor advice — the planner's job is to offer workable alternatives, not to walk away.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Rank these actions in the correct order for a 29-year-old with ₹6,00,000 of savings, no insurance, ₹3,00,000 of credit card debt at 40%, and a 30-year retirement goal.\nA) Start equity SIP → buy insurance → repay card  B) Repay the 40% card debt → build emergency fund and buy term cover → start the retirement SIP  C) Buy insurance → start SIP → repay card  D) Build emergency fund → start SIP → ignore the card\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Clear the 40% debt first** — no investment reliably beats a certain 40% cost. Then **protect** (emergency fund and term cover) so a single shock cannot derail the plan. Only then **invest** the balance for the 30-year retirement goal, where equity is appropriate.\nA) and C) leave 40% interest compounding against the client while chasing ~12% returns. D) Ignoring 40% debt is the single most expensive mistake available here. **The universal sequence: expensive debt → protection → investment.**\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Read the scenario, then attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** In the real exam a 2-mark caselet like this is worth 10 marks, and a wrong answer costs −0.5.\n\n### Case: The Sharma Household\n\nAnil Sharma, aged 38, is a marketing manager earning **₹2,40,000 a month** (net). His wife Meera, 35, is a homemaker. They have one daughter, Aditi, aged 6.\n\n**Monthly expenses:** ₹1,55,000 (this includes a home loan EMI of ₹42,000 and a car loan EMI of ₹13,000).\n\n**Assets:** savings account ₹3,20,000; liquid fund ₹1,80,000; equity mutual funds ₹14,00,000; EPF ₹11,00,000; self-occupied house (market value) ₹95,00,000; car ₹7,00,000; gold jewellery ₹6,00,000.\n\n**Liabilities:** home loan outstanding ₹48,00,000; car loan outstanding ₹3,50,000; credit card outstanding ₹1,20,000 (interest 38% p.a.).\n\n**Insurance:** an employer group life cover of ₹25,00,000 only. No individual term cover, no health cover beyond the employer policy.\n\n**Goals:** (i) Aditi's undergraduate education in 12 years, costing **₹25,00,000 in today's terms**; (ii) retirement at 60.\n\nAssume **inflation of 6% p.a.** unless stated otherwise.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is the Sharma household's net worth?\nA) ₹1,38,00,000  B) ₹85,30,000  C) ₹52,70,000  D) ₹1,25,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total assets** = 3,20,000 + 1,80,000 + 14,00,000 + 11,00,000 + 95,00,000 + 7,00,000 + 6,00,000 = **₹1,38,00,000**. **Total liabilities** = 48,00,000 + 3,50,000 + 1,20,000 = **₹52,70,000**. **Net worth = 1,38,00,000 − 52,70,000 = ₹85,30,000.**\nA) ₹1,38,00,000 is total assets only. C) ₹52,70,000 is total liabilities. D) ₹1,25,00,000 does not correspond to any correct combination. *(Always compute both sides before subtracting — the distractors here are exactly the two subtotals.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What is the household's liquidity ratio, and how does it compare with the norm?\nA) 3.23 months — adequate  B) 2.06 months — below the 3–6 month norm  C) 12.26 months — excessive  D) 1.16 months — critically low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Liquid assets** = savings ₹3,20,000 + liquid fund ₹1,80,000 = **₹5,00,000**. Monthly expenses = **₹1,55,000**. Liquidity ratio = 5,00,000 ÷ 1,55,000 = **3.23 months**, which just meets the lower bound of the 3–6 month norm.\nB) 2.06 wrongly uses ₹3,20,000 alone. C) 12.26 incorrectly counts equity funds as liquid — they are volatile and not an emergency asset. D) 1.16 uses ₹1,80,000 alone. **Verdict: adequate but thin.** With a sole earner and a young child, pushing toward 5–6 months (≈₹8–9 lakh) is advisable.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** What will Aditi's education cost in future rupees, 12 years from now?\nA) ₹41,00,000  B) ₹50,29,000  C) ₹30,00,000  D) ₹25,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = Present cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ = ₹25,00,000 × (1.06)¹². (1.06)¹² ≈ **2.01220**. So ₹25,00,000 × 2.0122 = **₹50,30,500 ≈ ₹50.29 lakh**.\nA) ₹41,00,000 understates the compounding (roughly 1.64×). C) ₹30,00,000 applies a token 20% uplift. D) ₹25,00,000 ignores inflation entirely — the most common and most damaging error. **At 6%, costs double in about 12 years** (the \"rule of 72\": 72 ÷ 6 = 12) — a shortcut worth memorising.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Ignoring existing investments, what monthly SIP at 11% p.a. (monthly compounding) is needed to accumulate the QC3 amount in 12 years?\nA) About ₹17,300  B) About ₹34,900  C) About ₹28,000  D) About ₹42,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Target **₹50,30,500**; i = 11% ÷ 12 = **0.0091667**; n = **144**. (1.0091667)¹⁴⁴ ≈ **3.7275**. Annuity factor = (3.7275 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 2.7275 ÷ 0.0091667 ≈ **297.55**. PMT = 50,30,500 ÷ 297.55 ≈ **₹16,907 ≈ ₹17,300** (allowing for rounding).\nB) ₹34,900 roughly doubles the requirement. C) ₹28,000 assumes a far lower return. D) ₹42,000 is close to 50,30,500 ÷ 120, ignoring returns. In Excel: `=PMT(11%/12,144,0,5030500)`. **Affordability check: the household surplus is ₹2,40,000 − ₹1,55,000 = ₹85,000 a month, so ₹17,300 is comfortably affordable.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Which is the single most urgent gap in the Sharma household's financial plan?\nA) The equity allocation is too low  B) The absence of individual term life and health cover, with a sole earner and a dependent child  C) The gold holding is too small  D) The car loan should be prepaid first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Anil is the **sole earner** supporting a homemaker spouse and a 6-year-old, carrying **₹52.70 lakh of liabilities**, and is covered only by an **employer group policy of ₹25,00,000** — which lapses the day he changes or loses his job. If he dies, ₹25 lakh does not even cover the ₹48 lakh home loan, let alone education and retirement. Every other goal in the plan is contingent on his survival.\nA) Allocation matters, but it is a return question, not a survival one. C) Gold weighting is a minor optimisation. D) The **credit card at 38%** — not the car loan — is the debt to clear first, and even that ranks below securing protection. **Priority order here: term cover + health cover → clear the 38% card → top up the emergency fund → then fund education and retirement.**\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "02", "title": "Time Value of Money", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 2: Time Value of Money — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 1 · Personal Financial Planning (37 marks).**\n> ⭐ **This is the single most important chapter in the syllabus.** Its formulas reappear in Chapter 4 (EMIs), Chapter 9 (bond pricing), Chapter 14 (expected returns), Chapter 16 (CAGR, annualising) and in almost every caselet. Master this and you have effectively pre-answered a large slice of the paper.\n\n---\n\n## 2.1 The Core Idea\n\n**A rupee today is worth more than a rupee tomorrow.**\n\nThree reasons:\n\n1. **Opportunity cost** — money in hand can be invested and earn a return.\n2. **Inflation** — future rupees buy less.\n3. **Risk/uncertainty** — a promised future rupee might never arrive.\n\nBecause of this, you can never simply add or compare cash flows that occur at different dates. You must first move them all to a **common point in time**.\n\n> 🧠 **The one mental model:** think of a **time line**. Money moved *forward* in time is **compounded** (multiplied by (1+r)ⁿ). Money moved *backward* is **discounted** (divided by (1+r)ⁿ). That's the entire chapter.\n\n```\n        discount ←────────────────────── compound\n   |--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|\n   0        1        2        3        4        5   (periods)\n   PV                                            FV\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.2 The Five Variables\n\nEvery TVM problem has **five** variables. Give me any four and I can solve for the fifth.\n\n| Symbol | Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **PV** | Present Value | Value today |\n| **FV** | Future Value | Value at a future date |\n| **r** (or `RATE`) | Rate per period | Interest/return **per period** |\n| **n** (or `NPER`) | Number of periods | Total compounding periods |\n| **PMT** | Payment | The equal cash flow each period |\n\n> ⚠️ **The #1 exam error:** mixing an *annual* rate with a *monthly* number of periods. **r and n must always use the same period.** For monthly problems: `r = annual rate ÷ 12` and `n = years × 12`.\n\n### Sign convention (matters in Excel)\nMoney you **pay out** is **negative**; money you **receive** is **positive**. If a spreadsheet returns `#NUM!`, it is almost always because every value was entered with the same sign.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.1 & 2.2.2 Future Value and Present Value\n\n### Future Value of a lump sum\n\n$$\\boxed{FV = PV \\times (1 + r)^n}$$\n\n**Example:** ₹1,00,000 invested for 10 years at 9%.\nFV = 1,00,000 × (1.09)¹⁰ = 1,00,000 × 2.3674 = **₹2,36,740**.\n\n### Present Value of a lump sum\n\n$$\\boxed{PV = \\frac{FV}{(1 + r)^n}}$$\n\n**Example:** how much do I need today to have ₹50,00,000 in 15 years at 10%?\nPV = 50,00,000 ÷ (1.10)¹⁵ = 50,00,000 ÷ 4.1772 = **₹11,96,970**.\n\n> These two are the **same equation rearranged**. Learn one and you know both.\n\n### Simple vs compound interest\n\n| | Simple interest | Compound interest |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Interest earned on** | Principal only | Principal **+ accumulated interest** |\n| **Formula** | FV = PV × (1 + r×n) | FV = PV × (1+r)ⁿ |\n| **Growth shape** | Straight line | Accelerating curve |\n\n**₹1,00,000 at 10% for 20 years:** simple → ₹3,00,000; compound → **₹6,72,750**. The gap *is* the value of compounding.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.3 Rate of Return\n\nRearranging FV = PV(1+r)ⁿ:\n\n$$\\boxed{r = \\left(\\frac{FV}{PV}\\right)^{1/n} - 1}$$\n\nThis is the **CAGR** (Compound Annual Growth Rate) — the single smoothed annual rate that takes PV to FV.\n\n**Example:** ₹4,00,000 grows to ₹10,00,000 in 8 years.\nr = (10,00,000 ÷ 4,00,000)^(1/8) − 1 = (2.5)^0.125 − 1 = 1.1214 − 1 = **12.14% p.a.**\n\n> Excel: `=RATE(8,0,-400000,1000000)`.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.5 Number of Periods\n\n$$\\boxed{n = \\frac{\\ln(FV / PV)}{\\ln(1 + r)}}$$\n\n**Example:** how long for ₹5,00,000 to become ₹15,00,000 at 11%?\nn = ln(3) ÷ ln(1.11) = 1.0986 ÷ 0.10436 = **10.53 years**.\n\n### 🔑 The Rule of 72 (memorise this)\n\n$$\\textbf{Years to double} \\approx \\frac{72}{\\text{annual rate (\\%)}}$$\n\n| Rate | Doubling time |\n|---|---|\n| 6% | 12 years |\n| 8% | 9 years |\n| 9% | 8 years |\n| 12% | 6 years |\n\nUse it in reverse too: if money doubled in 6 years, the rate was ≈ 72 ÷ 6 = **12%**. It's an approximation, but exam-fast and usually within a few months.\n\n---\n\n## 2.3 Compounding Frequency\n\nInterest may be credited more than once a year. More frequent compounding = **more interest**, because interest starts earning interest sooner.\n\n$$\\boxed{FV = PV \\times \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{m}\\right)^{m \\times n}}$$\n\nwhere **m** = compoundings per year (annual 1, half-yearly 2, quarterly 4, monthly 12, daily 365).\n\n### Effective Annual Rate (EAR)\n\nThe rate that, compounded **once a year**, gives the same result:\n\n$$\\boxed{EAR = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{m}\\right)^{m} - 1}$$\n\n**₹1,00,000 at a nominal 12% for 1 year:**\n\n| Frequency | m | EAR | Value after 1 year |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Annual | 1 | 12.000% | ₹1,12,000 |\n| Half-yearly | 2 | 12.360% | ₹1,12,360 |\n| Quarterly | 4 | 12.551% | ₹1,12,551 |\n| Monthly | 12 | 12.683% | ₹1,12,683 |\n| Daily | 365 | 12.747% | ₹1,12,747 |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap:** the **nominal** (stated) rate is the same 12% throughout — only the **effective** rate changes. Always compare products on **EAR**, never on the headline rate.\n>\n> Note the gains shrink as m rises: annual→monthly adds 0.68 percentage points; monthly→daily adds only 0.06. The limit (continuous compounding) is e^r − 1 = 12.75%.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.6 Annuities\n\nAn **annuity** is a series of **equal** cash flows at **regular** intervals — a SIP, an EMI, a pension, rent.\n\n### Ordinary annuity (payment at the END of each period)\n\nThis is the default. Most EMIs and SIPs are treated this way.\n\n$$\\boxed{FV_{\\text{ordinary}} = PMT \\times \\frac{(1+r)^n - 1}{r}}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{ordinary}} = PMT \\times \\frac{1 - (1+r)^{-n}}{r}}$$\n\n**Example (FV):** ₹10,000 a month for 15 years at 12% p.a. compounded monthly.\nr = 0.12 ÷ 12 = **0.01**; n = 15 × 12 = **180**.\n(1.01)¹⁸⁰ = **5.9958**.\nFV = 10,000 × [(5.9958 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 10,000 × **499.58** = **₹49,95,800**.\n\n*(Total invested = ₹18,00,000. Returns contributed ₹31,95,800 — nearly two-thirds of the corpus.)*\n\n### Annuity due (payment at the BEGINNING of each period)\n\nEach payment sits invested for **one extra period**, so simply multiply by (1+r):\n\n$$\\boxed{FV_{\\text{due}} = FV_{\\text{ordinary}} \\times (1 + r)}$$\n$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{due}} = PV_{\\text{ordinary}} \\times (1 + r)}$$\n\nUsing the example above: FV_due = 49,95,800 × 1.01 = **₹50,45,758** — about ₹50,000 more, purely from timing.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook:** \"**Due** means you pay **first**\" → money works longer → the value is **always higher**. In Excel this is the `type` argument: **0 = ordinary (end)**, **1 = due (beginning)**.\n\n### 2.2.4 Solving for PMT\n\nRearranging the FV annuity formula:\n\n$$\\boxed{PMT = \\frac{FV \\times r}{(1+r)^n - 1}}$$\n\n**Example:** to accumulate ₹1,00,00,000 in 20 years at 11% p.a. (monthly):\nr = 0.0091667, n = 240, (1.0091667)²⁴⁰ = **8.9006**.\nPMT = (1,00,00,000 × 0.0091667) ÷ (8.9006 − 1) = 91,667 ÷ 7.9006 = **₹11,602 a month**.\n\nAnd from the **PV** side (this is the **EMI** formula — see Chapter 4):\n\n$$\\boxed{PMT = \\frac{PV \\times r}{1 - (1+r)^{-n}} = PV \\times \\frac{r(1+r)^n}{(1+r)^n - 1}}$$\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.7 Perpetuity\n\nA perpetuity is an annuity that **never ends**.\n\n$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{perpetuity}} = \\frac{PMT}{r}}$$\n\n**Example:** ₹60,000 a year forever, discount rate 8% → PV = 60,000 ÷ 0.08 = **₹7,50,000**.\n\n### Growing perpetuity\n\nIf the payment grows at a constant rate **g** (and g < r):\n\n$$\\boxed{PV = \\frac{PMT_1}{r - g}}$$\n\n**Example:** ₹60,000 next year growing 4% a year forever, discounted at 8%:\nPV = 60,000 ÷ (0.08 − 0.04) = **₹15,00,000**.\n\n> ⚠️ If **g ≥ r** the formula breaks (the value is infinite) — a favourite trick question.\n\n### Growing annuity (finite)\n\n$$PV = \\frac{PMT_1}{r-g}\\left[1 - \\left(\\frac{1+g}{1+r}\\right)^n\\right]$$\n\nUseful for retirement income that must rise with inflation.\n\n---\n\n## 2.4 Real vs Nominal Return\n\nThe **nominal** return is what the statement shows. The **real** return is what your money will actually buy.\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Real return} = \\frac{1 + \\text{nominal}}{1 + \\text{inflation}} - 1}$$\n\n**Example:** nominal 9%, inflation 6%.\nReal = (1.09 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.02830 − 1 = **2.83%**.\n\n> ⚠️ The rough answer (9 − 6 = 3%) **overstates** it. The exam frequently offers the crude subtraction as a distractor — use the exact formula.\n\n**Why it matters:** an FD at 7% with inflation at 6% delivers a real return of only (1.07 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **0.94%**. Before tax. After 30% tax the nominal becomes 4.9%, and the real return is (1.049 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **−1.04%** — the \"safe\" investment is quietly *losing* purchasing power.\n\n### Inflating a goal\n\n$$\\text{Future cost} = \\text{Today's cost} \\times (1 + \\text{inflation})^n$$\n\nAlways cost goals in **future rupees** before computing what to invest.\n\n---\n\n## 2.5 Putting It Together — the standard goal calculation\n\nEvery goal caselet follows the same four steps:\n\n1. **Inflate the goal** to its future cost: `Cost × (1 + i)ⁿ`\n2. **Grow what you already have**: `Existing × (1 + r)ⁿ`\n3. **Find the gap**: step 1 − step 2\n4. **Solve for the SIP** that fills the gap: `PMT = Gap × r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]`\n\n**Worked example.** Goal: education costing ₹20,00,000 today, needed in 10 years. Inflation 7%. Existing earmarked corpus ₹5,00,000. Expected return 11% p.a. (monthly compounding).\n\n1. Future cost = 20,00,000 × (1.07)¹⁰ = 20,00,000 × 1.9672 = **₹39,34,400**\n2. Existing grows = 5,00,000 × (1.11)¹⁰ = 5,00,000 × 2.8394 = **₹14,19,700**\n3. Gap = 39,34,400 − 14,19,700 = **₹25,14,700**\n4. r = 0.0091667, n = 120, (1.0091667)¹²⁰ = 2.9853\n PMT = (25,14,700 × 0.0091667) ÷ 1.9853 = 23,051 ÷ 1.9853 = **₹11,611 a month**\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Excel / Calc Functions (the test machine has a spreadsheet!)\n\n| Need | Function |\n|---|---|\n| Future value | `=FV(rate, nper, pmt, [pv], [type])` |\n| Present value | `=PV(rate, nper, pmt, [fv], [type])` |\n| Payment | `=PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])` |\n| Rate | `=RATE(nper, pmt, pv, [fv], [type])` |\n| Periods | `=NPER(rate, pmt, pv, [fv], [type])` |\n| Effective rate | `=EFFECT(nominal_rate, npery)` |\n| Nominal rate | `=NOMINAL(effect_rate, npery)` |\n\n**Rules:** use the **per-period** rate; enter outflows as **negative**; `type` = 0 (end) or 1 (beginning).\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Need | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| FV of lump sum | PV × (1+r)ⁿ |\n| PV of lump sum | FV ÷ (1+r)ⁿ |\n| Rate / CAGR | (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 |\n| Periods | ln(FV÷PV) ÷ ln(1+r) |\n| Rule of 72 | Years to double ≈ 72 ÷ rate% |\n| Non-annual compounding | PV × (1 + r/m)^(m×n) |\n| Effective annual rate | (1 + r/m)^m − 1 |\n| FV of ordinary annuity | PMT × [((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r] |\n| PV of ordinary annuity | PMT × [(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r] |\n| Annuity **due** | Ordinary × (1+r) |\n| PMT from FV | FV × r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] |\n| PMT from PV (EMI) | PV × r × (1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] |\n| Perpetuity | PMT ÷ r |\n| Growing perpetuity | PMT₁ ÷ (r − g), needs g < r |\n| Real return | [(1+nominal) ÷ (1+inflation)] − 1 |\n| Future cost of a goal | Cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ |\n\n### The five traps that cost marks\n\n1. **Period mismatch** — annual rate with monthly periods. Convert *both*.\n2. **Ordinary vs due** — \"beginning of month\" means multiply by (1+r).\n3. **Real return by subtraction** — use the division formula.\n4. **Forgetting to inflate the goal** — today's cost is never the target.\n5. **Nominal vs effective** — compare products on EAR.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Module 1 is 37 marks and this chapter underpins most of it. If you have limited revision time, spend it here.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 2: Time Value of Money — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **These formulas must be automatic — they reappear in Chapters 4, 9, 14 and 16.**\n\n**Q1.** Why is a rupee today worth more than a rupee tomorrow?\n> **A:** Three reasons — **opportunity cost** (it can be invested today), **inflation** (future rupees buy less), and **risk** (a future rupee may never arrive).\n\n**Q2.** Name the five TVM variables.\n> **A:** **PV, FV, r (rate per period), n (number of periods), PMT.** Give any four and the fifth is solvable.\n\n**Q3.** State the future value of a lump sum.\n> **A:** **FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ**\n\n**Q4.** State the present value of a lump sum.\n> **A:** **PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ** — the same equation rearranged.\n\n**Q5.** What is the #1 mistake in TVM calculations?\n> **A:** **Period mismatch** — using an annual rate with a monthly number of periods. For monthly problems: r = annual ÷ 12 and n = years × 12.\n\n**Q6.** State the formula for the rate of return (CAGR).\n> **A:** **r = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1**\n\n**Q7.** State the formula for the number of periods.\n> **A:** **n = ln(FV ÷ PV) ÷ ln(1 + r)**\n\n**Q8.** State the Rule of 72 and use it: money doubled in 6 years — what was the rate?\n> **A:** **Years to double ≈ 72 ÷ rate%.** Reversed: 72 ÷ 6 = **12% p.a.**\n\n**Q9.** How long does money take to double at 8%? At 9%? At 6%?\n> **A:** **9 years** at 8%; **8 years** at 9%; **12 years** at 6%.\n\n**Q10.** Give the formula for compounding m times a year.\n> **A:** **FV = PV × (1 + r/m)^(m×n)** — m = 1 annual, 2 half-yearly, 4 quarterly, 12 monthly, 365 daily.\n\n**Q11.** State the Effective Annual Rate formula.\n> **A:** **EAR = (1 + r/m)^m − 1** — the rate that, compounded once a year, gives the same result.\n\n**Q12.** A nominal 12% compounded monthly gives what EAR?\n> **A:** (1 + 0.12/12)¹² − 1 = (1.01)¹² − 1 = **12.683%**.\n\n**Q13.** As compounding frequency rises, what happens to the nominal rate and to the effective rate?\n> **A:** The **nominal rate stays the same**; only the **effective rate rises** — and by shrinking increments (annual→monthly adds 0.68pp; monthly→daily only 0.06pp).\n\n**Q14.** What is an annuity?\n> **A:** A series of **equal** cash flows at **regular** intervals — a SIP, an EMI, a pension, rent.\n\n**Q15.** State the FV of an ordinary annuity.\n> **A:** **FV = PMT × [((1 + r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]**\n\n**Q16.** State the PV of an ordinary annuity.\n> **A:** **PV = PMT × [(1 − (1 + r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r]**\n\n**Q17.** What is the difference between an ordinary annuity and an annuity due?\n> **A:** **Ordinary** pays at the **end** of each period; **due** pays at the **beginning**. Each due payment is invested one extra period.\n\n**Q18.** How do you convert an ordinary annuity value into an annuity due value?\n> **A:** **Multiply by (1 + r).** This works for both PV and FV. The due value is **always higher**.\n\n**Q19.** In Excel, what does the `type` argument do?\n> **A:** `type = 0` → ordinary annuity (end of period, the default). `type = 1` → annuity due (beginning of period).\n\n**Q20.** State the formula for PMT when you know the target FV.\n> **A:** **PMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1]**\n\n**Q21.** State the formula for PMT when you know the PV (i.e. the EMI formula).\n> **A:** **PMT = PV × r × (1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]**\n\n**Q22.** State the present value of a perpetuity.\n> **A:** **PV = PMT ÷ r**\n\n**Q23.** State the present value of a growing perpetuity, and its one condition.\n> **A:** **PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g)**, valid only when **g < r**. If g ≥ r the value is infinite and the formula breaks.\n\n**Q24.** ₹60,000 a year forever at an 8% discount rate — what is it worth today?\n> **A:** 60,000 ÷ 0.08 = **₹7,50,000**.\n\n**Q25.** State the real return formula.\n> **A:** **Real = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1** — *not* nominal minus inflation.\n\n**Q26.** Nominal 9%, inflation 6%. What is the exact real return, and what does the crude method give?\n> **A:** Exact = (1.09 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **2.83%**. Crude subtraction gives 3%, which **overstates** it.\n\n**Q27.** An FD yields 7%, inflation is 6%, tax is 30%. What is the post-tax real return?\n> **A:** Post-tax nominal = 7% × 0.70 = 4.9%. Real = (1.049 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **−1.04%** — the \"safe\" option loses purchasing power.\n\n**Q28.** How do you convert today's goal cost into the amount actually needed?\n> **A:** **Future cost = Today's cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ.** Never target today's price.\n\n**Q29.** State the four steps of the standard goal calculation.\n> **A:** 1) **Inflate** the goal cost. 2) **Grow** the existing earmarked corpus. 3) Find the **gap**. 4) Solve for the **SIP** that fills the gap.\n\n**Q30.** Difference between simple and compound interest, with the 20-year example?\n> **A:** Simple earns on principal only (**FV = PV(1 + r×n)**); compound earns on principal *plus* accumulated interest. ₹1,00,000 at 10% for 20 years: simple **₹3,00,000**, compound **₹6,72,750**.\n\n**Q31.** What is the Excel sign convention, and what does `#NUM!` usually mean?\n> **A:** Money **paid out is negative**, money **received is positive**. `#NUM!` almost always means every value was entered with the **same sign**.\n\n**Q32.** ₹10,000 a month for 15 years at 12% p.a. (monthly) — what is the corpus, and how much was contributed?\n> **A:** r = 0.01, n = 180, (1.01)¹⁸⁰ = 5.9958. FV = 10,000 × 499.58 = **₹49,95,800**. Contributed = **₹18,00,000**; returns supplied nearly two-thirds.\n\n**Q33.** Which Excel functions solve for each variable?\n> **A:** `=FV()`, `=PV()`, `=PMT()`, `=RATE()`, `=NPER()`, plus `=EFFECT()` and `=NOMINAL()` for rate conversion.\n\n**Q34.** ₹4,00,000 grows to ₹10,00,000 in 8 years. What is the CAGR?\n> **A:** (10,00,000 ÷ 4,00,000)^(1/8) − 1 = 2.5^0.125 − 1 = **12.14% p.a.**\n\n**Q35.** Why must you always compare deposit products on EAR rather than the headline rate?\n> **A:** Two products can quote the same **nominal** rate but differ in **compounding frequency**, so their actual returns differ. Only the **effective** rate is comparable.\n\n**Q36.** State the finite growing-annuity PV formula and where it's used.\n> **A:** **PV = [PMT₁ ÷ (r − g)] × [1 − ((1+g)/(1+r))ⁿ]** — used for retirement income that must rise with inflation.\n\n**Q37.** What are the five classic traps in this chapter?\n> **A:** 1) Period mismatch. 2) Ordinary vs due confusion. 3) Real return by subtraction. 4) Forgetting to inflate the goal. 5) Comparing nominal instead of effective rates.\n\n**Q38.** In one sentence, what is the whole chapter?\n> **A:** Cash flows at different dates cannot be compared directly — move them along the time line by **compounding forward** ((1+r)ⁿ) or **discounting back** (÷(1+r)ⁿ).\n", "questions": "# Chapter 2: Time Value of Money — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> ⭐ **The most exam-critical bank in the guide.** Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). Keep a spreadsheet open and check each answer with `=FV()`, `=PV()`, `=PMT()`, `=RATE()`. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The time value of money concept states that:\nA) All rupees are worth the same regardless of timing  B) A rupee today is worth more than a rupee tomorrow  C) A rupee tomorrow is worth more than a rupee today  D) Money loses value only when inflation is above 5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Money available today can be invested (opportunity cost), buys more (inflation), and is certain (risk).\nA) Ignores all three reasons. C) Reverses the principle. D) Inflation is only one of three reasons, and it applies at any positive rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The formula for the future value of a lump sum is:\nA) FV = PV × (1 + r × n)  B) FV = PV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ  C) FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ  D) FV = PV × r × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Compounding multiplies the present value by **(1 + r)ⁿ**.\nA) Is the **simple interest** formula. B) Is present value (discounting). D) Computes only the interest under simple interest, not the future value.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which of these is NOT one of the five TVM variables?\nA) PMT  B) NPER  C) Inflation  D) RATE\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The five variables are **PV, FV, RATE, NPER and PMT**. Inflation is used *around* TVM (to inflate goals or compute real returns) but is not one of the five.\nA), B) and D) are all core variables.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In an ordinary annuity, payments occur:\nA) At the beginning of each period  B) At the end of each period  C) Only once  D) At irregular intervals\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **ordinary annuity** pays at the **end** of each period; it is the default assumption.\nA) Describes an annuity **due**. C) A single payment is a lump sum, not an annuity. D) Annuities require *regular* intervals and *equal* amounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The relationship between an annuity due and an ordinary annuity is:\nA) Due = Ordinary ÷ (1 + r)  B) Due = Ordinary × (1 + r)  C) They are always equal  D) Due = Ordinary × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each payment in an annuity due sits invested **one extra period**, so multiply by **(1 + r)**.\nA) Divides, which would make it smaller. C) They are never equal for r > 0. D) Multiplying by n has no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The present value of a perpetuity is:\nA) PMT × r  B) PMT ÷ r  C) PMT × (1 + r)  D) PMT ÷ n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A perpetuity pays forever, so **PV = PMT ÷ r**.\nA) Multiplies instead of dividing. C) Is the annuity-due adjustment. D) Perpetuities have no finite n.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The Rule of 72 estimates:\nA) The effective annual rate  B) The number of years for money to double  C) The real rate of return  D) The EMI on a loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Years to double ≈ 72 ÷ annual rate (%)**.\nA) EAR uses (1 + r/m)^m − 1. C) Real return uses the ratio formula. D) EMIs use the annuity PMT formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** At 9% per annum, money approximately doubles in:\nA) 6 years  B) 8 years  C) 12 years  D) 9 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 72 ÷ 9 = **8 years**.\nA) 6 years corresponds to 12%. C) 12 years corresponds to 6%. D) 9 years corresponds to 8% — note the easy-to-confuse pair: 8% → 9 years, 9% → 8 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which compounding frequency produces the highest effective annual rate for a given nominal rate?\nA) Annual  B) Half-yearly  C) Quarterly  D) Daily\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The more often interest is credited, the sooner it starts earning interest itself, so **daily** gives the highest EAR.\nA), B) and C) all compound less frequently. For a 12% nominal: annual 12.000%, half-yearly 12.360%, quarterly 12.551%, daily 12.747%.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The effective annual rate formula is:\nA) (1 + r × m) − 1  B) (1 + r/m)^m − 1  C) (1 + r)^m − 1  D) r ÷ m\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **EAR = (1 + r/m)^m − 1**: divide the nominal rate by the frequency, compound it m times, subtract 1.\nA) Uses simple interest. C) Fails to divide the nominal rate by m, overstating the result badly. D) Gives the periodic rate, not the annual effective rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** The real rate of return is calculated as:\nA) Nominal − inflation  B) [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1  C) Nominal × inflation  D) Nominal ÷ inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The exact formula divides the growth factors.\nA) The crude subtraction **overstates** the real return and is the most common distractor. C) and D) have no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** In Excel, money paid out should be entered as:\nA) A positive number  B) A negative number  C) Zero  D) Either, it makes no difference\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The sign convention is **outflows negative, inflows positive**.\nA) Would misstate the direction of the cash flow. C) Zero would remove the cash flow entirely. D) It matters greatly — same-sign entries typically produce `#NUM!`.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which Excel function solves for the periodic payment?\nA) `=FV()`  B) `=PV()`  C) `=PMT()`  D) `=NPER()`\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — `=PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])`.\nA) Solves for future value. B) Solves for present value. D) Solves for the number of periods.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Simple interest differs from compound interest because simple interest:\nA) Is earned on principal plus accumulated interest  B) Is earned on the principal only  C) Grows exponentially  D) Always produces a higher value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Simple interest is computed on the **original principal only**, giving straight-line growth.\nA) and C) describe compound interest. D) Compound interest always produces more for n > 1 and r > 0.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** The FV of an ordinary annuity is:\nA) PMT × [((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]  B) PMT ÷ r  C) PMT × (1+r)ⁿ  D) PMT × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The annuity factor is **[((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]**.\nB) Is a perpetuity. C) Compounds a single payment. D) Ignores interest completely — that is just the total contributed.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Which of the following is an annuity?\nA) A one-time bonus of ₹5,00,000  B) A monthly SIP of ₹10,000 for 10 years  C) Irregular dividend payments  D) A lump-sum inheritance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An annuity requires **equal amounts at regular intervals** — a SIP fits exactly.\nA) and D) are lump sums. C) Dividends vary in amount and timing, so they are not an annuity.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** For a growing perpetuity, the formula PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g) requires:\nA) g > r  B) g < r  C) g = r  D) g = 0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Growth must be **less than** the discount rate, otherwise the denominator is zero or negative and the value is infinite/meaningless.\nA) and C) break the formula. D) g = 0 is allowed but reduces it to an ordinary perpetuity — it is not a *requirement*.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** When converting an annual rate to a monthly rate for TVM purposes, you should:\nA) Multiply the annual rate by 12  B) Divide the annual rate by 12  C) Leave it unchanged  D) Divide by 365\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Use **r ÷ 12** for the monthly periodic rate, together with n = years × 12.\nA) Multiplying inflates the rate twelve-fold. C) Mixing an annual rate with monthly periods is the classic error. D) 365 applies to daily compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Discounting means:\nA) Moving a cash flow forward in time  B) Moving a cash flow backward in time to find its present value  C) Reducing the interest rate  D) Applying a sales rebate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Discounting divides by (1+r)ⁿ to bring a future amount **back** to today.\nA) That is compounding. C) and D) confuse the financial meaning of \"discount\" with everyday usage.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which statement about the nominal rate is correct when compounding frequency increases?\nA) The nominal rate rises  B) The nominal rate stays the same while the effective rate rises  C) Both fall  D) The effective rate stays the same\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **stated/nominal** rate is unchanged; only the **effective** rate rises.\nA) and C) misstate the direction. D) The effective rate is precisely what changes.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** ₹1,00,000 at 10% simple interest for 20 years grows to:\nA) ₹6,72,750  B) ₹3,00,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹1,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FV = PV × (1 + r×n) = 1,00,000 × (1 + 0.10 × 20) = 1,00,000 × 3 = **₹3,00,000**.\nA) ₹6,72,750 is the **compound** result. C) ₹2,00,000 would be 10 years of simple interest. D) ₹1,10,000 is one year only.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The PV of an ordinary annuity formula is:\nA) PMT × [(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r]  B) PMT × [((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]  C) PMT ÷ (r − g)  D) PMT × (1+r)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The PV annuity factor is **[(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r]**.\nB) Is the **FV** annuity factor. C) Is a growing perpetuity. D) Is the annuity-due adjustment.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** In the Excel `type` argument, `type = 1` means:\nA) Ordinary annuity  B) Annuity due (payment at the beginning)  C) Simple interest  D) Continuous compounding\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — `type = 1` shifts payments to the **beginning** of each period.\nA) `type = 0` is the ordinary annuity default. C) and D) are not controlled by `type`.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** To find the rate given PV, FV and n, you use:\nA) r = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1  B) r = (FV − PV) ÷ n  C) r = FV ÷ PV  D) r = ln(FV ÷ PV)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Take the nth root of the growth multiple and subtract 1.\nB) Computes an average rupee gain, not a rate. C) Gives the total multiple. D) Is part of the **NPER** formula, not the rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** An investment doubles in 12 years. Using the Rule of 72, the approximate rate is:\nA) 12%  B) 6%  C) 8%  D) 9%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rate ≈ 72 ÷ 12 = **6%**.\nA) 12% doubles in 6 years. C) 8% doubles in 9 years. D) 9% doubles in 8 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which of these is a use of the present value concept?\nA) Deciding how much to invest today for a future goal  B) Calculating simple interest  C) Measuring inflation  D) Preparing a balance sheet\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV answers exactly the question \"how much do I need **today**?\"\nB) Simple interest has its own formula. C) Inflation is measured by price indices. D) Balance sheets record current values, not discounted ones.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The formula n = ln(FV ÷ PV) ÷ ln(1 + r) solves for:\nA) The rate  B) The number of periods  C) The payment  D) The present value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taking logs of FV = PV(1+r)ⁿ isolates **n**.\nA) The rate uses the nth-root formula. C) and D) have their own formulas.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A nominal rate of 12% compounded quarterly gives a periodic rate of:\nA) 12%  B) 4%  C) 3%  D) 1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Quarterly means m = 4, so the periodic rate is 12% ÷ 4 = **3%**.\nA) Is the annual nominal rate. B) 4% would be m = 3. D) 1% is the monthly rate (m = 12).\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which of the following will always be true for r > 0 and n > 1?\nA) Compound value < simple value  B) Compound value > simple value  C) They are equal  D) It depends on inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compounding earns interest on interest, so it always exceeds simple interest beyond the first period.\nA) Reverses it. C) They are equal only at n = 1. D) Inflation is irrelevant to this comparison.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The future cost of a goal is calculated as:\nA) Today's cost ÷ (1 + inflation)ⁿ  B) Today's cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ  C) Today's cost × inflation × n  D) Today's cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Goals must be inflated forward to the date they will be paid.\nA) Discounts instead of inflating. C) Uses simple growth. D) Ignoring inflation is the single most damaging planning error.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which Excel function converts a nominal rate to an effective rate?\nA) `=NOMINAL()`  B) `=EFFECT()`  C) `=RATE()`  D) `=FV()`\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — `=EFFECT(nominal_rate, npery)`.\nA) Does the reverse (effective → nominal). C) Solves for the periodic rate in a TVM problem. D) Computes future value.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In a SIP where the investment is made on the 1st of every month, the appropriate treatment is:\nA) Ordinary annuity  B) Annuity due  C) Perpetuity  D) Lump sum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Payment at the **beginning** of the period is an **annuity due**.\nA) Would apply to end-of-month investment. C) A SIP has a finite term. D) A SIP is a series, not a single payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Continuous compounding of a 12% nominal rate gives an effective rate of approximately:\nA) 12.00%  B) 12.68%  C) 12.75%  D) 13.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The limit is e^r − 1 = e^0.12 − 1 = 1.1275 − 1 = **12.75%**.\nA) Is annual compounding. B) Is monthly compounding. D) Overstates it — continuous compounding is the *ceiling*, only marginally above daily (12.747%).\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Which of these correctly describes the effect of a longer horizon on compounding?\nA) The effect is linear  B) The effect accelerates — later years add much more than early years  C) The effect diminishes over time  D) There is no effect after 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Growth is exponential, so each successive year adds more in absolute terms than the last.\nA) Linear growth describes simple interest. C) and D) contradict the exponential nature of compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A `#NUM!` error in an Excel TVM formula usually means:\nA) The rate is too high  B) All cash flows were entered with the same sign  C) The function name is misspelled  D) The file is corrupted\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — TVM functions require at least one cash flow of the **opposite sign**.\nA) High rates compute fine. C) A misspelling gives `#NAME?`. D) Corruption would produce a different failure.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** ₹2,00,000 is invested for 7 years at 8% p.a. compounded annually. What is the future value?\nA) ₹3,42,780  B) ₹3,12,000  C) ₹2,16,000  D) ₹4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FV = 2,00,000 × (1.08)⁷. (1.08)⁷ = **1.71382**. FV = 2,00,000 × 1.71382 = **₹3,42,764 ≈ ₹3,42,780**.\nB) ₹3,12,000 applies simple interest (2,00,000 × 1.56). C) ₹2,16,000 is one year's growth. D) ₹4,00,000 would need about 10.5 years at 8%.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** How much must be invested today to have ₹25,00,000 in 12 years at 10% p.a.?\nA) ₹7,96,600  B) ₹12,50,000  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = 25,00,000 ÷ (1.10)¹². (1.10)¹² = **3.13843**. PV = 25,00,000 ÷ 3.13843 = **₹7,96,575 ≈ ₹7,96,600**.\nB) ₹12,50,000 simply halves the target. C) ₹5,00,000 is far too low (it would grow to only ₹15.7 lakh). D) ₹10,00,000 would grow to ₹31.4 lakh. Excel: `=PV(10%,12,0,-2500000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** ₹5,00,000 grows to ₹12,50,000 in 10 years. What is the CAGR?\nA) 15.00%  B) 9.60%  C) 25.00%  D) 7.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = (12,50,000 ÷ 5,00,000)^(1/10) − 1 = 2.5^0.1 − 1. ln(2.5) = 0.9163; ÷ 10 = 0.09163; e^0.09163 = 1.09596. r = **9.60% p.a.**\nA) 15% would give about ₹20.2 lakh. C) 25% is the *total* gain divided by 10 years — a simple-interest style error. D) 7.20% gives only about ₹10 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** ₹15,000 invested monthly for 20 years at 12% p.a. (monthly compounding) accumulates to approximately:\nA) ₹36,00,000  B) ₹1,49,88,000  C) ₹75,00,000  D) ₹2,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.12 ÷ 12 = **0.01**; n = 240. (1.01)²⁴⁰ = **10.8926**. FV = 15,000 × [(10.8926 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 15,000 × **989.26** = **₹1,48,38,900 ≈ ₹1.49 crore**.\nA) ₹36,00,000 is just the total contributed (15,000 × 240). C) ₹75,00,000 understates the compounding badly. D) ₹2.5 crore overstates it. Excel: `=FV(12%/12,240,-15000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** What is the effective annual rate of 10% nominal compounded quarterly?\nA) 10.00%  B) 10.38%  C) 10.47%  D) 11.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR = (1 + 0.10/4)⁴ − 1 = (1.025)⁴ − 1 = 1.10381 − 1 = **10.38%**.\nA) Is the nominal rate. C) 10.47% corresponds to monthly compounding. D) 11% overstates it. Excel: `=EFFECT(10%,4)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A nominal 9% is compounded monthly. What is the effective annual rate?\nA) 9.00%  B) 9.38%  C) 9.20%  D) 9.75%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR = (1 + 0.09/12)¹² − 1 = (1.0075)¹² − 1 = 1.09381 − 1 = **9.38%**.\nA) Is the nominal rate. C) 9.20% corresponds roughly to half-yearly compounding. D) 9.75% overstates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A nominal return of 11% with inflation at 6.5% gives a real return of:\nA) 4.50%  B) 4.23%  C) 5.00%  D) 4.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real = (1.11 ÷ 1.065) − 1 = 1.042254 − 1 = **4.23%**.\nA) 4.50% is the crude subtraction (11 − 6.5), which overstates the real return. C) and D) do not follow from the formula. **The exam almost always offers the subtraction as a distractor.**\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A goal costs ₹12,00,000 today and is 8 years away. At 7% inflation, the future cost is:\nA) ₹18,72,000  B) ₹20,61,700  C) ₹16,80,000  D) ₹24,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = 12,00,000 × (1.07)⁸. (1.07)⁸ = **1.71819**. = **₹20,61,828 ≈ ₹20,61,700**.\nA) ₹18,72,000 applies simple inflation (12,00,000 × 1.56). C) ₹16,80,000 assumes only a 40% rise. D) ₹24,00,000 assumes doubling, which at 7% takes about 10.3 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** An investor needs ₹40,00,000 in 15 years and expects 10% p.a. What monthly SIP is required?\nA) ₹9,650  B) ₹22,200  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹31,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.10 ÷ 12 = **0.008333**; n = 180. (1.008333)¹⁸⁰ = **4.4539**. Annuity factor = (4.4539 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **414.47**. PMT = 40,00,000 ÷ 414.47 = **₹9,651**.\nB) ₹22,200 corresponds to a much lower assumed return. C) ₹15,000 would overshoot to about ₹62 lakh. D) ₹31,000 is close to 40,00,000 ÷ 180 (₹22,222) inflated — it ignores compounding. Excel: `=PMT(10%/12,180,0,4000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** ₹8,000 is invested at the **beginning** of every month for 10 years at 12% p.a. What is the corpus?\nA) ₹18,41,200  B) ₹18,59,600  C) ₹9,60,000  D) ₹16,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is an **annuity due**. Ordinary FV = 8,000 × [((1.01)¹²⁰ − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 8,000 × [(3.30039 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 8,000 × **230.039** = ₹18,40,312. Annuity due = × 1.01 = **₹18,58,715 ≈ ₹18,59,600**.\nA) ₹18,41,200 is the **ordinary** annuity value — it forgets the (1+r) adjustment. C) ₹9,60,000 is the total contributed. D) ₹16,00,000 understates the growth. Excel: `=FV(12%/12,120,-8000,0,1)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A pension of ₹50,000 per year is expected in perpetuity. At a 9% discount rate, its present value is:\nA) ₹4,50,000  B) ₹5,55,556  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹9,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV = PMT ÷ r = 50,000 ÷ 0.09 = **₹5,55,556**.\nA) ₹4,50,000 multiplies instead of dividing (50,000 × 9). C) ₹50,00,000 divides by 0.01. D) ₹9,00,000 has no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A payment of ₹1,00,000 next year grows 5% a year forever. At a 10% discount rate, the present value is:\nA) ₹10,00,000  B) ₹20,00,000  C) ₹6,66,667  D) Infinite\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Growing perpetuity: PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g) = 1,00,000 ÷ (0.10 − 0.05) = 1,00,000 ÷ 0.05 = **₹20,00,000**.\nA) ₹10,00,000 ignores growth (uses r alone). C) ₹6,66,667 uses r + g. D) Infinite would apply only if g ≥ r. **Growth halves the effective discount rate here, doubling the value.**\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** How long will ₹3,00,000 take to become ₹9,00,000 at 12% p.a.?\nA) 6.0 years  B) 9.69 years  C) 12.0 years  D) 8.0 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — n = ln(9,00,000 ÷ 3,00,000) ÷ ln(1.12) = ln(3) ÷ ln(1.12) = 1.0986 ÷ 0.11333 = **9.69 years**.\nA) 6 years only doubles it (Rule of 72: 72 ÷ 12 = 6). C) and D) do not follow. **Sanity check: doubling takes 6 years, so tripling must take more — but less than 12.**\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Two deposits offer 10% compounded annually and 9.8% compounded monthly. Which is better?\nA) The 10% annual, since the headline rate is higher  B) The 9.8% monthly, because its EAR is about 10.25%  C) They are identical  D) Cannot be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR of 9.8% monthly = (1 + 0.098/12)¹² − 1 = (1.0081667)¹² − 1 = **10.25%**, which beats the 10.00% EAR of the annual option.\nA) Comparing headline rates is exactly the trap. C) They differ by 0.25 percentage points. D) EAR is precisely the tool that makes them comparable.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** An investor wants ₹75,00,000 in 18 years. She has ₹10,00,000 today earning 11%. What is the shortfall at the goal date?\nA) ₹9,56,450  B) ₹10,58,000  C) Nil — she will exceed the goal  D) ₹65,43,550\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Grow the existing corpus first: 10,00,000 × (1.11)¹⁸. (1.11)¹⁸ = **6.54355**, so the corpus reaches **₹65,43,550**. Shortfall = 75,00,000 − 65,43,550 = **₹9,56,450**.\nB) ₹10,58,000 overstates the gap. C) She falls short, not over. D) ₹65,43,550 is the grown corpus itself, not the gap. *(Always compute the grown corpus first, then subtract from the target.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** ₹20,000 a month for 25 years at 11% p.a. gives a corpus of approximately:\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹3,15,27,000  C) ₹1,50,00,000  D) ₹6,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.0091667, n = 300. (1.0091667)³⁰⁰ = **15.4499**. Annuity factor = (15.4499 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = **1576.36**. FV = 20,000 × 1576.36 = **₹3,15,27,200**.\nA) ₹60,00,000 is simply the total contributed (20,000 × 300). C) ₹1.5 crore understates the growth by half. D) ₹6.5 crore overstates it. **Over 25 years, ₹60 lakh of contributions becomes about ₹3.15 crore — roughly 81% of the corpus is return.**\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Which is worth more today: ₹10,00,000 received in 5 years, or ₹6,00,000 received today, at an 8% discount rate?\nA) The ₹10,00,000 in 5 years, worth about ₹6,80,600 today  B) The ₹6,00,000 today  C) They are equal  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV of ₹10,00,000 = 10,00,000 ÷ (1.08)⁵ = 10,00,000 ÷ **1.46933** = **₹6,80,583**, which exceeds ₹6,00,000.\nB) Would be right only at a discount rate above about 10.8%. C) They differ by roughly ₹80,000. D) With PV, FV, n and r known, it is fully determinable.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** An EMI is best described as which TVM concept?\nA) The FV of an annuity  B) The PMT that makes the PV of payments equal the loan amount  C) A perpetuity  D) A lump sum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A loan is a **present value**; the EMI is the payment whose present value equals the loan amount: **PMT = PV × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]**.\nA) Uses the FV form, appropriate for savings not loans. C) Loans have a finite term. D) An EMI is a series, not a single payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** An investor's portfolio returns 14% nominal in a year with 5% inflation and pays 20% tax on gains. What is the post-tax real return?\nA) 9.00%  B) 6.29%  C) 11.20%  D) 5.90%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Two steps, in this order. **Tax first:** post-tax nominal = 14% × (1 − 0.20) = **11.2%**. **Then deflate:** real = (1.112 ÷ 1.05) − 1 = 1.05905 − 1 = **5.90%**.\nA) 9% subtracts inflation from the *pre-tax* return, ignoring tax altogether. B) 6.29% adjusts for inflation first and taxes afterwards — the wrong order. C) 11.2% is the post-tax nominal return, before inflation. **Always tax first, then deflate.**\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** ₹1,00,000 invested at 8% compounded half-yearly for 5 years gives:\nA) ₹1,46,933  B) ₹1,48,024  C) ₹1,40,000  D) ₹1,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — m = 2, so the periodic rate is 4% and n = 10 periods. FV = 1,00,000 × (1.04)¹⁰ = 1,00,000 × **1.48024** = **₹1,48,024**.\nA) ₹1,46,933 is **annual** compounding ((1.08)⁵). C) ₹1,40,000 is simple interest. D) ₹1,60,000 overstates it. **Note half-yearly beats annual by ₹1,091.**\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A client will receive ₹5,00,000 a year for 12 years, first payment one year from now. At 9%, the present value is:\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹35,79,000  C) ₹45,00,000  D) ₹30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV annuity = PMT × [(1 − (1.09)⁻¹²) ÷ 0.09]. (1.09)¹² = **2.81266**, so (1.09)⁻¹² = **0.35553**. Factor = (1 − 0.35553) ÷ 0.09 = 0.64447 ÷ 0.09 = **7.1608**. PV = 5,00,000 × 7.1608 = **₹35,80,400 ≈ ₹35,79,000**.\nA) ₹60,00,000 is the undiscounted total (5,00,000 × 12). C) and D) do not follow from the factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** If a SIP is switched from end-of-month to beginning-of-month, the corpus:\nA) Falls  B) Rises by a factor of (1 + r)  C) Is unchanged  D) Doubles\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Every payment gains one extra period of compounding, so the whole series is multiplied by **(1 + r)**.\nA) It rises, not falls. C) Timing genuinely matters. D) The uplift is one period's interest — typically well under 1% for monthly SIPs, not 100%.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A client has 25 years to retirement and can invest ₹12,000 a month at 12%. Approximately what corpus results?\nA) ₹36,00,000  B) ₹2,25,00,000  C) ₹1,00,00,000  D) ₹5,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.01, n = 300. (1.01)³⁰⁰ = **19.7885**. Factor = (19.7885 − 1) ÷ 0.01 = **1878.85**. FV = 12,000 × 1878.85 = **₹2,25,46,200**.\nA) ₹36,00,000 is the total contributed. C) ₹1 crore understates it by half. D) ₹5 crore overstates it. **Roughly 84% of the corpus is return — the case for starting early in one number.**\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A bank quotes 7.5% compounded quarterly on an FD. Inflation is 6% and the client's tax rate is 30%. The post-tax real return is approximately:\nA) +1.5%  B) −0.6%  C) +0.9%  D) −2.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR = (1 + 0.075/4)⁴ − 1 = (1.01875)⁴ − 1 = **7.71%**. Post-tax = 7.71% × 0.70 = **5.40%**. Real = (1.0540 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **−0.57% ≈ −0.6%**.\nA) +1.5% ignores tax. C) +0.9% ignores either tax or inflation. D) −2.0% overstates the erosion. **The \"safe\" FD delivers a negative real return after tax — a core planning insight.**\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** An investment of ₹6,00,000 becomes ₹6,00,000 after 10 years in nominal terms, with inflation at 6%. What happened in real terms?\nA) The investor broke even  B) The investor lost about 44% of purchasing power  C) The investor gained  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In real terms the value is 6,00,000 ÷ (1.06)¹⁰ = 6,00,000 ÷ **1.79085** = **₹3,35,040** in today's money — a loss of about **44%** of purchasing power.\nA) Nominal break-even is not real break-even. C) There is no gain at all. D) With the inflation rate given, it is fully determinable.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which pair of inputs is internally consistent for a monthly SIP over 12 years at 10% p.a.?\nA) r = 10%, n = 12  B) r = 0.10/12, n = 144  C) r = 0.10, n = 144  D) r = 0.10/12, n = 12\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Monthly rate = **0.10 ÷ 12** and monthly periods = 12 years × 12 = **144**. Both must be monthly.\nA) Uses annual rate with 12 periods — that describes 12 *years* of annual compounding, not a monthly SIP. C) Mixes an annual rate with monthly periods — the classic error. D) Mixes a monthly rate with only 12 periods (i.e. one year).\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A client needs ₹18,00,000 in 6 years and can earn 9%. She invests ₹6,00,000 today. What additional annual (year-end) investment is needed?\nA) ₹1,05,500  B) ₹1,45,700  C) ₹3,00,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Three steps. **Grow the lump sum:** 6,00,000 × (1.09)⁶ = 6,00,000 × **1.67710** = **₹10,06,260**. **Find the gap:** 18,00,000 − 10,06,260 = **₹7,93,740**. **Annuitise it:** factor = ((1.09)⁶ − 1) ÷ 0.09 = 0.6771 ÷ 0.09 = **7.5233**; PMT = 7,93,740 ÷ 7.5233 = **₹1,05,504**.\nB) ₹1,45,700 ignores the existing lump sum's growth. C) ₹3,00,000 is roughly the gap ÷ 6, ignoring returns. D) ₹50,000 falls far short. Excel: `=PMT(9%,6,0,-793740)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** The PV of an annuity **falls** when:\nA) The discount rate falls  B) The discount rate rises  C) The number of payments rises  D) The payment amount rises\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A higher discount rate shrinks every future cash flow, so the present value falls.\nA) A lower rate raises PV. C) More payments raise PV. D) Larger payments raise PV.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client compares a lump sum of ₹25,00,000 today against ₹3,00,000 a year for 12 years. At 8%, which is better?\nA) The annuity, worth about ₹22,60,000  B) The lump sum, since the annuity's PV is about ₹22,60,000  C) They are equal  D) The annuity, worth ₹36,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV of the annuity = 3,00,000 × [(1 − (1.08)⁻¹²) ÷ 0.08]. (1.08)¹² = **2.51817**, so (1.08)⁻¹² = **0.39711**. Factor = 0.60289 ÷ 0.08 = **7.5361**. PV = 3,00,000 × 7.5361 = **₹22,60,830**. The lump sum of ₹25,00,000 is worth more today.\nA) Correctly values the annuity but draws the wrong conclusion. C) They differ by about ₹2.4 lakh. D) ₹36,00,000 is the *undiscounted* total, which ignores the time value entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Doubling the investment horizon from 10 to 20 years at 12% multiplies the lump-sum corpus by approximately:\nA) 2 times  B) 3.1 times  C) 1.5 times  D) 10 times\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (1.12)¹⁰ = **3.1058**; (1.12)²⁰ = **9.6463**. Ratio = 9.6463 ÷ 3.1058 = **3.11 times**.\nA) Doubling time does *not* double the corpus — that is the linear intuition compounding defeats. C) 1.5× badly understates it. D) 10× is the total multiple over 20 years, not the incremental effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** An annuity due of ₹1,00,000 a year for 10 years at 10% has a present value of:\nA) ₹6,14,460  B) ₹6,75,900  C) ₹10,00,000  D) ₹5,58,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Ordinary PV = 1,00,000 × [(1 − (1.10)⁻¹⁰) ÷ 0.10]. (1.10)¹⁰ = **2.59374**, so (1.10)⁻¹⁰ = **0.38554**. Factor = 0.61446 ÷ 0.10 = **6.14457**. Ordinary PV = **₹6,14,457**. Annuity due = × 1.10 = **₹6,75,903**.\nA) ₹6,14,460 is the **ordinary** value, missing the (1+r) step. C) ₹10,00,000 is undiscounted. D) ₹5,58,600 over-discounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which statement about compounding frequency is TRUE?\nA) The benefit of higher frequency grows without limit  B) The benefit rises but with diminishing increments, approaching continuous compounding  C) Frequency has no effect  D) Annual compounding always gives the most\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each increase in frequency adds less than the last, converging on **e^r − 1**.\nA) The benefit is bounded by continuous compounding. C) Frequency clearly matters. D) Annual gives the *least*.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A goal is ₹30,00,000 in 10 years. The client can invest ₹15,000 a month. What return is needed (approximately)?\nA) About 5%  B) About 9.9%  C) About 15%  D) About 2%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total contributed = 15,000 × 120 = ₹18,00,000, and the target is ₹30,00,000, so a moderate return is needed. Testing 10%: factor = ((1.008333)¹²⁰ − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = (2.70704 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **204.84**; FV = 15,000 × 204.84 = **₹30,72,600** — slightly above target, so the required rate is just under 10%, about **9.9%**.\nA) 5% gives about ₹23.3 lakh. C) 15% gives about ₹41.8 lakh. D) 2% gives about ₹19.9 lakh. Excel: `=RATE(120,-15000,0,3000000)*12`.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A retiree wants ₹40,000 a month for 25 years from a corpus earning 8% p.a. What corpus is needed at retirement?\nA) ₹1,20,00,000  B) ₹51,84,000  C) ₹2,40,00,000  D) ₹80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the **PV of an ordinary annuity**. r = 0.08 ÷ 12 = **0.0066667**; n = 300. (1.0066667)³⁰⁰ = **7.34018**, so the inverse is **0.13624**. Factor = (1 − 0.13624) ÷ 0.0066667 = 0.86376 ÷ 0.0066667 = **129.56**. Corpus = 40,000 × 129.56 = **₹51,82,400 ≈ ₹51,84,000**.\nA) ₹1.2 crore is the undiscounted total (40,000 × 300). C) ₹2.4 crore doubles that. D) ₹80,00,000 overstates the requirement. **Note the corpus needed is far below the total withdrawn, because the balance keeps earning.**\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** In the standard goal calculation, the correct sequence is:\nA) Find the SIP → inflate the goal → grow existing assets  B) Inflate the goal → grow existing assets → find the gap → solve for the SIP  C) Grow existing assets → find the SIP → inflate the goal  D) Find the gap → inflate the goal → solve for the SIP\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You cannot size a SIP until you know the **gap**, and you cannot know the gap until the goal is inflated and existing assets are grown to the same date.\nA), C) and D) all attempt to compute a later step before its inputs exist.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client invests ₹3,00,000 today and ₹5,000 a month for 15 years, both earning 11% p.a. (monthly compounding). What is the total corpus?\nA) ₹15,55,230  B) ₹38,37,480  C) ₹22,82,250  D) ₹12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compute the two components separately, then add.\n**Lump sum:** r = 0.0091667, n = 180. (1.0091667)¹⁸⁰ = **5.1841**. FV = 3,00,000 × 5.1841 = **₹15,55,230**.\n**SIP:** factor = (5.1841 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 4.1841 ÷ 0.0091667 = **456.45**. FV = 5,000 × 456.45 = **₹22,82,250**.\n**Total = 15,55,230 + 22,82,250 = ₹38,37,480.**\nA) ₹15,55,230 is the lump-sum component alone. C) ₹22,82,250 is the SIP component alone. D) ₹12,00,000 is roughly the total SIP contributed (5,000 × 180 = ₹9,00,000) plus the initial ₹3,00,000, ignoring all growth. **Method: never mix a lump sum and an annuity in one formula — compute each and add.**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A client needs ₹1,00,00,000 in 20 years. She has ₹8,00,000 today and can invest monthly. Expected return 11% p.a. What monthly SIP fills the gap?\nA) ₹3,341  B) ₹8,120  C) ₹11,600  D) ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Step 1 — grow what she has:** (1.0091667)²⁴⁰ = **8.9006**; 8,00,000 × 8.9006 = **₹71,20,480**. **Step 2 — gap:** 1,00,00,000 − 71,20,480 = **₹28,79,520**. **Step 3 — annuity factor:** (8.9006 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 7.9006 ÷ 0.0091667 = **862.0**. **Step 4 — SIP:** 28,79,520 ÷ 862.0 = **₹3,341 a month**.\nB) ₹8,120 ignores part of the existing corpus's growth. C) ₹11,600 is the SIP needed if she had *nothing* saved. D) ₹15,000 overstates it further. **Key insight: the ₹8 lakh already invested does over 70% of the work — the power of a 20-year head start.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Two investors: A invests ₹10,000 a month from age 25 to 35 (10 years) then stops and lets it grow to 60. B invests ₹10,000 a month from 35 to 60 (25 years). Both earn 12% p.a. Who ends with more at 60?\nA) B, because she invested for 25 years  B) A, despite investing for only 10 years  C) They are equal  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Investor A:** SIP for 120 months: factor = ((1.01)¹²⁰ − 1) ÷ 0.01 = (3.30039 − 1) ÷ 0.01 = **230.04**; corpus at 35 = 10,000 × 230.04 = **₹23,00,400**. This then grows untouched for 25 years: × (1.01)³⁰⁰ = × **19.7885** = **₹4,55,17,000**.\n**Investor B:** SIP for 300 months: factor = (19.7885 − 1) ÷ 0.01 = **1878.85**; corpus = 10,000 × 1878.85 = **₹1,87,88,500**.\n**A ends with ~₹4.55 crore from ₹12 lakh invested; B with ~₹1.88 crore from ₹30 lakh invested.**\nA) Reverses the result. C) They differ by more than 2×. D) It is fully determinable. **This is the single most powerful illustration of compounding: time beats amount.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A ₹50,00,000 home loan at 9% for 20 years. What is the EMI?\nA) ₹44,986  B) ₹37,500  C) ₹52,000  D) ₹41,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — EMI = P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]. r = 0.09 ÷ 12 = **0.0075**; n = 240. (1.0075)²⁴⁰ = **6.00915**. EMI = 50,00,000 × [0.0075 × 6.00915] ÷ [6.00915 − 1] = 50,00,000 × 0.04506862 ÷ 5.00915 = **₹44,986**.\nB) ₹37,500 is merely the first month's interest (50,00,000 × 0.0075). C) ₹52,000 overstates it. D) ₹41,200 corresponds to a lower rate. Excel: `=PMT(9%/12,240,-5000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Using Q74's loan, what is the total amount repaid and the total interest?\nA) ₹1,07,96,640 repaid; ₹57,96,640 interest  B) ₹50,00,000 repaid; nil interest  C) ₹90,00,000 repaid; ₹40,00,000 interest  D) ₹67,47,900 repaid; ₹17,47,900 interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total repaid = EMI × n = ₹44,986 × 240 = **₹1,07,96,640**. Interest = 1,07,96,640 − 50,00,000 = **₹57,96,640**.\nB) Ignores interest entirely. C) and D) understate it. **The borrower pays more in interest than the original principal — which is why loan tenure is the most powerful lever in Chapter 4.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client can choose: (i) ₹20,00,000 today, or (ii) ₹2,50,000 a year for 15 years starting next year. At what discount rate are the two equal?\nA) About 9.1%  B) About 12.5%  C) About 5%  D) About 15%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Find the rate at which the annuity's PV equals ₹20,00,000, i.e. an annuity factor of 20,00,000 ÷ 2,50,000 = **8.0**. Test rates: at 8%, factor = (1 − (1.08)⁻¹⁵) ÷ 0.08 = (1 − 0.31524) ÷ 0.08 = **8.559** (too high). At 9%: (1 − 0.27454) ÷ 0.09 = **8.061**. At 9.2%: ≈ **7.97**. The factor hits 8.0 at roughly **9.1%**.\nB), C) and D) are all well away from the break-even. **Interpretation: if the client can earn more than about 9.1%, take the lump sum; if less, take the annuity.** Excel: `=RATE(15,2500000,-20000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A goal costs ₹15,00,000 today, is 10 years away, inflation is 6%, and the client has ₹4,00,000 earmarked earning 10%. What monthly SIP at 10% closes the gap?\nA) ₹5,950  B) ₹8,050  C) ₹13,110  D) ₹3,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The four-step template:\n**Step 1 — inflate the goal:** 15,00,000 × (1.06)¹⁰ = 15,00,000 × **1.79085** = **₹26,86,275**.\n**Step 2 — grow existing assets:** 4,00,000 × (1.10)¹⁰ = 4,00,000 × **2.59374** = **₹10,37,496**.\n**Step 3 — gap:** 26,86,275 − 10,37,496 = **₹16,48,779**.\n**Step 4 — solve for the SIP:** r = 0.008333, n = 120, (1.008333)¹²⁰ = **2.70704**; factor = (2.70704 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **204.84**. PMT = 16,48,779 ÷ 204.84 = **₹8,049**.\n\nA) ₹5,950 understates it. C) ₹13,110 is the SIP needed if the existing ₹4,00,000 were ignored entirely. D) ₹3,200 is far too low. **This four-step method is the template for every goal caselet in the exam.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** An investor earns 15% in year 1, −10% in year 2 and 20% in year 3. What is the CAGR?\nA) 8.33%  B) 7.49%  C) 24.20%  D) 6.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compound the growth factors, never average the percentages: 1.15 × 0.90 × 1.20 = **1.2420**. CAGR = (1.2420)^(1/3) − 1. ln(1.2420) = 0.21675; ÷ 3 = 0.07225; e^0.07225 = **1.07492**. CAGR = **7.49% p.a.**\nA) 8.33% is the **arithmetic mean** ((15 − 10 + 20) ÷ 3), which always **overstates** the compounded result when returns vary. C) 24.20% is the *cumulative* 3-year gain misread as annual. D) 6% understates it. **Key point: geometric mean (CAGR) < arithmetic mean whenever returns are volatile — see Chapter 16.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A client deposits ₹1,00,000 at the start of each year for 5 years at 10%. What is the value at the end of year 5?\nA) ₹6,10,510  B) ₹6,71,561  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹5,52,560\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Beginning-of-year deposits form an **annuity due**. Ordinary FV = 1,00,000 × [((1.10)⁵ − 1) ÷ 0.10] = 1,00,000 × [(1.61051 − 1) ÷ 0.10] = 1,00,000 × **6.1051** = ₹6,10,510. Annuity due = × 1.10 = **₹6,71,561**.\nA) ₹6,10,510 is the ordinary annuity value — the classic omission. C) ₹5,00,000 is the total deposited. D) ₹5,52,560 under-compounds. Excel: `=FV(10%,5,-100000,0,1)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A ₹10,00,000 investment must grow to ₹40,00,000. At 12% p.a., how long will it take?\nA) 6.0 years  B) 12.2 years  C) 18.0 years  D) 9.7 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — n = ln(4) ÷ ln(1.12) = 1.38629 ÷ 0.113329 = **12.23 years**.\nA) 6 years doubles it (Rule of 72). C) 18 years would take it to about ₹76.9 lakh. D) 9.7 years triples it. **Sanity check with the Rule of 72: doubling takes 6 years, and quadrupling is two doublings ≈ 12 years. ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client has ₹80,00,000 at retirement, wants ₹60,000 a month, and the corpus earns 7% p.a. How long will the money last?\nA) About 12 years  B) About 21.5 years  C) Indefinitely  D) About 30 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.07 ÷ 12 = **0.0058333**. **First check sustainability:** monthly interest on ₹80,00,000 = 80,00,000 × 0.0058333 = **₹46,667**, which is *less* than the ₹60,000 withdrawal — so the corpus **will** deplete.\n**Then solve for NPER:** n = −ln(1 − (PV × r ÷ PMT)) ÷ ln(1 + r) = −ln(1 − (80,00,000 × 0.0058333 ÷ 60,000)) ÷ ln(1.0058333) = −ln(0.22222) ÷ 0.0058164 = 1.50408 ÷ 0.0058164 = **258.6 months ≈ 21.5 years**.\nA) 12 years understates it. C) It would last indefinitely only if the withdrawal were at or below ₹46,667. D) 30 years overstates it. Excel: `=NPER(7%/12,60000,-8000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Continuing Q81: what monthly withdrawal could the client sustain indefinitely?\nA) ₹46,667  B) ₹60,000  C) ₹80,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **perpetuity**: withdraw only the interest earned. Monthly interest = 80,00,000 × (0.07 ÷ 12) = 80,00,000 × 0.0058333 = **₹46,667**.\nB) ₹60,000 exceeds the interest earned and depletes the corpus (as Q81 showed). C) and D) deplete it far faster. **Note this ignores inflation — a truly sustainable *real* withdrawal would be lower still, which is why the 3–4% rule exists.**\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** ₹5,00,000 is invested at 10% for 3 years compounded annually, then reinvested at 8% for 4 more years. What is the final value?\nA) ₹9,05,300  B) ₹6,65,500  C) ₹8,50,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Phase 1:** 5,00,000 × (1.10)³ = 5,00,000 × **1.331** = **₹6,65,500**. **Phase 2:** 6,65,500 × (1.08)⁴ = 6,65,500 × **1.36049** = **₹9,05,406**.\nB) ₹6,65,500 stops after phase 1. C) ₹8,50,000 understates phase 2. D) ₹10,00,000 overstates it. **Method: chain the phases — never average the two rates.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** In Q83, what single CAGR over the 7 years is equivalent?\nA) 9.00%  B) 8.86%  C) 10.00%  D) 8.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAGR = (9,05,406 ÷ 5,00,000)^(1/7) − 1 = (1.810812)^(1/7) − 1. ln(1.810812) = 0.59380; ÷ 7 = 0.084829; e^0.084829 = **1.08853**. CAGR = **8.85%**.\nA) 9% is the simple average weighted wrongly. C) and D) are the two individual phase rates. **Note the CAGR (8.85%) sits between 8% and 10%, closer to 8% because the 8% period was longer — a good sanity check.**\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A client wants ₹1,00,000 a month in today's terms at retirement in 20 years. Inflation is 6%. What monthly income will she need in the first year of retirement?\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹3,20,700  C) ₹1,60,000  D) ₹5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflate: 1,00,000 × (1.06)²⁰ = 1,00,000 × **3.20714** = **₹3,20,714**.\nA) ₹2,00,000 assumes only a doubling — at 6% doubling takes 12 years, so 20 years must give more. C) ₹1,60,000 badly understates it. D) ₹5,00,000 overstates it. **The lesson: retirement income targets must always be stated in future rupees.**\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Continuing Q85: what corpus is needed at retirement to fund ₹3,20,714 a month for 25 years, if the corpus earns 8%?\nA) ₹4,15,50,000  B) ₹9,62,00,000  C) ₹2,00,00,000  D) ₹1,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV of an ordinary annuity: r = 0.08 ÷ 12 = **0.0066667**, n = 300. From Q69 the factor is **129.56**. Corpus = 3,20,714 × 129.56 = **₹4,15,51,700**.\nB) ₹9.62 crore is the undiscounted total (3,20,714 × 300). C) and D) are far too low. **Combining Q85 and Q86 gives the complete retirement calculation: inflate the income, then discount it back as an annuity.**\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** An investor is offered 8% compounded monthly or 8.2% compounded annually. Which is better and by how much in EAR terms?\nA) 8% monthly, by about 0.10pp  B) 8.2% annual, by about 0.10pp  C) They are identical  D) 8% monthly, by about 1pp\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — EAR of 8% monthly = (1 + 0.08/12)¹² − 1 = (1.0066667)¹² − 1 = **8.30%**. The 8.2% annual option has an EAR of exactly **8.20%**. So 8% monthly wins by **0.10 percentage points**.\nB) Reverses the winner. C) They differ by 0.10pp. D) Overstates the gap tenfold. **Never compare headline rates — always convert to EAR.**\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client saves ₹20,000 a month for 5 years, then ₹40,000 a month for the next 10 years, all at 11% p.a. What is the total corpus after 15 years?\nA) ₹1,20,00,000  B) ₹1,34,05,000  C) ₹60,00,000  D) ₹85,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Split into two phases and value each.\n**Phase 1 (₹20,000 × 60 months):** (1.0091667)⁶⁰ = **1.72804**; factor = (1.72804 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = **79.42**; value at year 5 = 20,000 × 79.42 = **₹15,88,400**. This then grows untouched for 10 more years: × (1.0091667)¹²⁰ = × **2.98529** = **₹47,42,000**.\n**Phase 2 (₹40,000 × 120 months):** factor = (2.98529 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = **216.58**; value = 40,000 × 216.58 = **₹86,63,200**.\n**Total = 47,42,000 + 86,63,200 = ₹1,34,05,200.**\nA) ₹1,20,00,000 understates it. C) ₹60,00,000 is the total contributed (₹12L + ₹48L). D) ₹85,00,000 omits phase 1's carry-forward growth. **Method: value phase 1 at its end date, carry it forward, then add phase 2 — never annuitise a changing payment in one step.**\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A ₹30,00,000 loan at 10% for 15 years. If the client prepays ₹5,00,000 at the end of year 5, what happens?\nA) The EMI must rise  B) Either the EMI falls or the tenure shortens — the borrower usually chooses  C) Nothing changes  D) The interest rate falls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A prepayment reduces the outstanding principal. The lender then offers either a **lower EMI** for the same remaining tenure, or the **same EMI with a shorter tenure**.\nA) A prepayment never forces a higher EMI. C) Reducing principal always reduces total interest. D) The contractual rate is unaffected. **Keeping the EMI and shortening the tenure saves far more interest — the key Chapter 4 insight.**\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An investment grows from ₹6,00,000 to ₹7,50,000 in 18 months. What is the annualised return?\nA) 25.00%  B) 16.05%  C) 16.67%  D) 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The holding-period return is 7,50,000 ÷ 6,00,000 = **1.25** over **1.5 years**. Annualised = 1.25^(1/1.5) − 1 = 1.25^0.6667 − 1. ln(1.25) = 0.22314; × 0.6667 = 0.14876; e^0.14876 = **1.16040**. Annualised = **16.04%**.\nA) 25% is the total (un-annualised) return. C) 16.67% divides 25% by 1.5 — a simple-interest shortcut that overstates it. D) 12.5% halves the total return. **Annualising requires compounding, not division.**\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Which sequence of ₹1,00,000 invested annually for 3 years at 10% correctly shows the year-3 value (ordinary annuity)?\nA) 1,00,000×(1.10)³ + 1,00,000×(1.10)² + 1,00,000×(1.10)  B) 1,00,000×(1.10)² + 1,00,000×(1.10) + 1,00,000  C) 1,00,000×3×1.10  D) 1,00,000×(1.10)³\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In an **ordinary** annuity the last payment arrives *at* the end of year 3 and earns nothing. The year-1 payment compounds for 2 years, year-2's for 1 year, year-3's for 0. Total = 1,21,000 + 1,10,000 + 1,00,000 = **₹3,31,000**.\nA) Describes an **annuity due** (each payment gets one extra year). C) Ignores compounding. D) Compounds only one payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A client's SIP of ₹25,000 a month for 12 years returns 10% p.a. She then stops and lets it grow for 8 more years at 8%. Final value?\nA) ₹1,25,00,000  B) ₹1,28,09,000  C) ₹69,00,000  D) ₹36,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two phases.\n**Accumulation:** r = 0.008333, n = 144. (1.008333)¹⁴⁴ = **3.30692**; factor = (3.30692 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **276.83**; corpus = 25,000 × 276.83 = **₹69,20,750**.\n**Growth (no further contribution):** 69,20,750 × (1.08)⁸ = 69,20,750 × **1.85093** = **₹1,28,09,300**.\nA) ₹1,25,00,000 is close but understates it. C) ₹69,00,000 stops after the accumulation phase. D) ₹36,00,000 is the total contributed (25,000 × 144). **Note the corpus nearly doubles in 8 years with no further contribution — compounding on an accumulated base.**\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Inflation is 6% and a client's salary rises 8% a year. What is the real growth in purchasing power?\nA) 2.00%  B) 1.89%  C) 14.00%  D) 0.75%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real growth = (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.018868 − 1 = **1.89%**.\nA) 2% is the crude subtraction, which overstates it. C) 14% adds them. D) 0.75% understates it. **The same ratio formula that gives real *returns* gives real *income growth*.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A client can invest ₹10,00,000 today at 9% or receive ₹1,30,000 a year for 12 years. Which is better?\nA) The lump sum, since the annuity's PV is about ₹9,30,000  B) The annuity, since its PV is about ₹9,30,000  C) They are equal  D) The annuity, worth ₹15,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV of the annuity = 1,30,000 × [(1 − (1.09)⁻¹²) ÷ 0.09] = 1,30,000 × **7.1607** = **₹9,30,891**, which is *less* than ₹10,00,000. So investing the lump sum is better.\nB) Values it correctly but draws the wrong conclusion. C) They differ by about ₹69,000. D) ₹15,60,000 is the undiscounted total (1,30,000 × 12), which ignores the time value of money.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A ₹12,00,000 car loan at 11% for 5 years. What is the EMI and total interest?\nA) EMI ₹26,092; interest ₹3,65,520  B) EMI ₹20,000; interest ₹1,00,000  C) EMI ₹30,000; interest ₹6,00,000  D) EMI ₹22,000; interest ₹1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.11 ÷ 12 = **0.0091667**, n = 60. (1.0091667)⁶⁰ = **1.72804**. EMI = 12,00,000 × [0.0091667 × 1.72804] ÷ [1.72804 − 1] = 12,00,000 × 0.01583999 ÷ 0.72804 = **₹26,102**. Total repaid = 26,102 × 60 = ₹15,66,120; interest = **₹3,66,120**.\nB) ₹20,000 is roughly the principal ÷ 60, ignoring interest. C) Overstates both. D) Understates both. Excel: `=PMT(11%/12,60,-1200000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** An investor's ₹20,00,000 portfolio must double in real terms in 12 years. Inflation is 6%. What nominal return is required?\nA) 6.0%  B) 12.25%  C) 18.0%  D) 8.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Doubling in **real** terms in 12 years needs a real return of 2^(1/12) − 1 = **5.946%**. The required **nominal** return = (1 + real) × (1 + inflation) − 1 = 1.05946 × 1.06 − 1 = 1.12303 − 1 = **12.30%**.\nA) 6% is inflation alone, giving zero real growth. C) 18% overstates it. D) 8% gives only about 1.9% real. **The formula runs both ways: nominal = (1 + real)(1 + inflation) − 1.**\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A client invests ₹50,000 at the end of each quarter for 8 years at 10% p.a. compounded quarterly. What is the corpus?\nA) ₹16,00,000  B) ₹24,03,000  C) ₹20,00,000  D) ₹30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Quarterly rate = 0.10 ÷ 4 = **0.025**; n = 8 × 4 = **32**. (1.025)³² = **2.20376**. Factor = (2.20376 − 1) ÷ 0.025 = **48.15**. FV = 50,000 × 48.15 = **₹24,07,500 ≈ ₹24,03,000**.\nA) ₹16,00,000 is the total contributed (50,000 × 32). C) and D) do not follow. **Note the period conversion: quarterly means divide the rate by 4 and multiply the years by 4.**\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A retiree needs an income stream rising 5% a year to keep pace with inflation, starting at ₹6,00,000 next year, discounted at 9%, for 20 years. Which formula applies?\nA) PMT ÷ r  B) The growing annuity formula: [PMT₁ ÷ (r − g)] × [1 − ((1+g)/(1+r))ⁿ]  C) PMT × (1+r)  D) PMT × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **finite** stream that **grows** at a constant rate needs the growing-annuity formula. Here PV = [6,00,000 ÷ 0.04] × [1 − (1.05/1.09)²⁰] = 1,50,00,000 × [1 − 0.47615] = 1,50,00,000 × 0.52385 = **₹78,57,750**.\nA) Is a level perpetuity (infinite, no growth). C) Is the annuity-due adjustment. D) Ignores discounting and growth entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client is told \"your investment grew 100% in 6 years, that's 16.7% a year.\" This is:\nA) Correct  B) Wrong — the correct CAGR is about 12.25%  C) Wrong — the correct CAGR is about 20%  D) Correct only if compounding is annual\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Dividing the total gain by the number of years is a **simple-interest** error. The correct CAGR = 2^(1/6) − 1 = **12.25%**.\nA) 16.7% (100 ÷ 6) ignores compounding. C) 20% overstates it. D) The error exists regardless of frequency. **Sanity check with the Rule of 72: doubling in 6 years implies about 72 ÷ 6 = 12% — matching 12.25%. ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which combination of errors would most inflate a client's projected retirement corpus?\nA) Using an annuity due instead of ordinary, and inflating the goal  B) Ignoring inflation on the goal, using the arithmetic mean return instead of CAGR, and ignoring tax  C) Using CAGR and post-tax returns  D) Using monthly compounding correctly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three errors push the same way: **ignoring inflation** understates what is needed, the **arithmetic mean** overstates the return achieved, and **ignoring tax** overstates what the client keeps. Together they can overstate readiness by a wide margin.\nA) Inflating the goal is *correct* practice, and the annuity-due difference is small. C) Both are the correct, conservative choices. D) Correct method, no distortion. **The professional habit: inflate the goal, use geometric (compound) returns, and project post-tax.**\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Use a spreadsheet — this is exactly how the 2-mark caselets are set.\n\n### Case: Planning Kavita's Retirement\n\nKavita is **34 years old** and plans to retire at **60**. Her current annual household expenses are **₹9,00,000**. She expects to live until **85**.\n\nShe has already accumulated **₹22,00,000** in an EPF and equity mutual fund portfolio.\n\n**Assumptions:**\n- Inflation before *and* after retirement: **6% p.a.**\n- Return on the portfolio before retirement: **11% p.a.** (monthly compounding for SIPs)\n- Return on the corpus after retirement: **8% p.a.**\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** How many years will Kavita spend in accumulation, and how many in retirement?\nA) 26 years accumulating, 25 years retired  B) 34 years accumulating, 26 years retired  C) 26 years accumulating, 51 years retired  D) 25 years accumulating, 25 years retired\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Accumulation** = retirement age − current age = 60 − 34 = **26 years**. **Retirement** = life expectancy − retirement age = 85 − 60 = **25 years**.\nB) 34 is her current age, not a period. C) 51 is her total remaining life expectancy (85 − 34), which spans *both* phases. D) Miscounts the accumulation phase. *(Always establish these two horizons first — every later step depends on them.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What will Kavita's annual expenses be in her first year of retirement?\nA) ₹18,00,000  B) ₹40,93,000  C) ₹27,00,000  D) ₹9,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflate for **26 years** at 6%: 9,00,000 × (1.06)²⁶. (1.06)²⁶ = **4.54938**. = **₹40,94,442 ≈ ₹40,93,000**.\nA) ₹18,00,000 assumes a mere doubling — but at 6% money doubles in 12 years, so 26 years gives more than 4×. C) ₹27,00,000 assumes 3×. D) ₹9,00,000 ignores inflation entirely. **Rule of 72 check: 72 ÷ 6 = 12 years per doubling; 26 years ≈ 2.17 doublings ≈ 4.5×. ✓**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Ignoring post-retirement inflation for simplicity, what corpus does Kavita need at 60 to fund ₹40,94,442 a year for 25 years at 8%?\nA) ₹10,23,61,050  B) ₹4,37,00,000  C) ₹2,00,00,000  D) ₹40,94,442\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV of a 25-year ordinary annuity at 8%: factor = (1 − (1.08)⁻²⁵) ÷ 0.08. (1.08)²⁵ = **6.84848**, so the inverse is **0.146018**. Factor = (1 − 0.146018) ÷ 0.08 = 0.853982 ÷ 0.08 = **10.6748**. Corpus = 40,94,442 × 10.6748 = **₹4,37,07,000**.\nA) ₹10.24 crore is the **undiscounted** total (40,94,442 × 25) — it ignores that the corpus keeps earning 8% throughout retirement. C) ₹2 crore is far too low. D) Is one year's expense. **Note the corpus needed (₹4.37 cr) is well under half the total withdrawn (₹10.24 cr) — the balance does the rest.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Kavita's existing ₹22,00,000 grows at 11% for 26 years. What will it be worth at 60?\nA) ₹3,23,70,000  B) ₹1,50,00,000  C) ₹57,20,000  D) ₹5,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FV = 22,00,000 × (1.11)²⁶. (1.11)²⁶ = **14.7138**. FV = **₹3,23,70,360**.\nB) ₹1.5 crore understates it badly. C) ₹57,20,000 applies a mere 2.6× (roughly simple interest). D) ₹5 crore overstates it. **Rule of 72 check: at 11%, money doubles roughly every 6.5 years; 26 years ≈ 4 doublings ≈ 16×. ✓ The head start does most of the heavy lifting.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Given the corpus needed from QC3 and the existing portfolio's growth from QC4, what monthly SIP at 11% would close the remaining gap?\nA) About ₹6,900  B) About ₹25,000  C) No SIP is needed — the existing corpus is nearly sufficient  D) About ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Gap** = required corpus − grown existing corpus = ₹4,37,07,000 − ₹3,23,70,000 = **₹1,13,37,000**.\n**SIP:** r = 0.11 ÷ 12 = **0.0091667**, n = 26 × 12 = **312**. (1.0091667)³¹² = **16.9846**. Annuity factor = (16.9846 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 15.9846 ÷ 0.0091667 = **1743.8**. PMT = 1,13,37,000 ÷ 1743.8 = **₹6,502 a month**.\n\nSo roughly **₹6,500–6,900 a month** closes the gap — option **A)**.\nB) and D) massively overstate the requirement. C) Is tempting but wrong: the existing corpus reaches ₹3.24 crore against a ₹4.37 crore requirement, a real shortfall of over ₹1.1 crore.\n\n> ⚠️ **Important caveat the exam may probe:** this calculation **ignored post-retirement inflation**. Kavita's expenses will keep rising after 60, so the true corpus requirement is materially higher and the real SIP would be several times ₹6,500. Using the **growing annuity** formula (g = 6%, r = 8%) for the retirement phase is the technically correct approach.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "03", "title": "Cash Flow Management and Budgeting", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 3: Cash Flow Management and Budgeting — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 1 · Personal Financial Planning (37 marks).**\n> ⭐ **The ratios in section 3.9 are guaranteed exam marks.** There are ten of them, they are simple to compute, and caselets love them. Learn each formula *and* what a \"good\" value looks like.\n\n---\n\n## 3.1 Why Cash Flow Matters\n\n**Cash flow is the engine of a financial plan.** Investments, insurance premiums and loan EMIs are all funded from one place: the **surplus** between what comes in and what goes out.\n\n$$\\textbf{Surplus (Savings)} = \\textbf{Total Inflows} - \\textbf{Total Outflows}$$\n\n- **No surplus → no plan.** You cannot allocate money you never generated.\n- A client with a ₹5 lakh monthly income and ₹5 lakh of expenses is in a worse position than one earning ₹1 lakh and spending ₹70,000.\n- Cash flow problems are usually **behavioural**, not arithmetic — which is why budgeting is a planning discipline, not an accounting chore.\n\n### The two statements (don't confuse them)\n\n| | **Cash Flow Statement** | **Personal Balance Sheet** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Shows** | Inflows and outflows | Assets and liabilities |\n| **Covers** | A **period** (month/year) | A **point in time** |\n| **Key output** | **Surplus / deficit** | **Net worth** |\n| **Type** | A **flow** | A **stock** |\n\n> 🧠 The two link together: this period's **surplus** becomes an addition to next period's **assets**, raising **net worth**.\n\n---\n\n## 3.3 Cash Inflows and Outflows\n\n### Inflows\n\n| Type | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Regular / active** | Salary, professional fees, business income |\n| **Portfolio / passive** | Interest, dividends, rent |\n| **Irregular** | Bonus, incentives, gifts, maturity proceeds, capital gains |\n\n### Outflows\n\n| Type | Examples | Nature |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Fixed / committed** | EMIs, rent, insurance premiums, school fees | Contractual — hard to cut quickly |\n| **Variable / discretionary** | Groceries, fuel, dining, travel, entertainment | Controllable |\n| **Periodic / lumpy** | Annual insurance, festivals, vacations, car servicing | Predictable but infrequent — the ones people forget |\n| **Savings / investment** | SIPs, PPF, RD | Should be treated as a *committed* outflow |\n\n> ⚠️ **The most common budgeting failure** is ignoring **periodic outflows**. A budget that balances every month collapses in the month the annual insurance premium falls due. The fix: divide each annual expense by 12 and set that aside monthly.\n\n### 3.3.1 Cash management\nEnsuring enough liquidity to meet obligations **as they fall due**, without holding so much idle cash that returns are sacrificed. Two failure modes: too little (forced borrowing or asset sales) and too much (opportunity cost).\n\n### 3.3.2 Income and Expenditure Statement\nThe formal record: list all inflows, list all outflows, and derive the surplus. Best prepared **monthly** with an **annualised** view alongside so lumpy items are visible.\n\n---\n\n## 3.2 & 3.7 Preparing a Household Budget\n\nA **budget** is a *forward-looking* plan for income and spending; the income–expenditure statement is the *backward-looking* record of what actually happened.\n\n**Steps:**\n\n1. **Record** actual income and spending for 2–3 months (most people badly underestimate discretionary spending).\n2. **Categorise** into fixed, variable, periodic and savings.\n3. **Set targets** for each category.\n4. **Build in savings first** — see \"pay yourself first\" below.\n5. **Monitor** actual vs budget each month.\n6. **Revise** as circumstances change.\n\n### 🔑 \"Pay yourself first\"\n\nThe critical principle. Two orderings:\n\n| Approach | Order | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| ❌ Common | Income → Expenses → *whatever is left* is saved | Savings become the residual — usually near zero |\n| ✅ Correct | Income → **Savings (automated)** → Expenses from the rest | Savings are protected; spending adapts |\n\nAutomate it: schedule SIPs and recurring deposits for the **day after salary credit**.\n\n### The role of forecasting (3.4)\n\nBudgeting looks ahead — anticipating salary increments, a new EMI starting, school fees rising, a child's arrival, a planned car purchase. Forecasting lets the client see a shortfall **before** it becomes a crisis.\n\n### 3.5 Monitoring and provision for savings\n\nMonitoring answers: *where did the plan leak?* Variance analysis (budget vs actual, per category) locates it. Persistent overspending in one category means either the target was unrealistic or behaviour must change.\n\n---\n\n## 3.8 Contingency Planning\n\nThe **contingency (emergency) fund** protects the plan from income shocks and unplanned expenses.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Purpose** | Job loss, medical emergency, urgent repairs, income gap |\n| **Size** | **3–6 months of expenses** (go higher for irregular income, single-earner households, or many dependants) |\n| **Where** | Savings account, sweep-in FD, **liquid/overnight mutual funds** |\n| **Requirements** | **Immediate availability + certainty of value.** Return is secondary |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap:** the emergency fund is sized against **expenses**, not income — because expenses are what must be paid. And it must **never** be parked in equity: the moment you need it (recession, job loss) is exactly when equity is likely to be down.\n\n**Beyond cash:** adequate **health insurance** and **life insurance** are part of contingency planning, because they stop a single event from consuming the entire fund.\n\n---\n\n## 3.6 Personal Balance Sheet and Net Worth\n\n$$\\textbf{Net Worth} = \\textbf{Total Assets} - \\textbf{Total Liabilities}$$\n\n**Assets** (at current market value):\n- **Liquid** — cash, savings balance, liquid funds, sweep-in FDs\n- **Financial (invested)** — deposits, bonds, shares, mutual funds, PPF/EPF, insurance cash values\n- **Physical/real** — property, land, gold\n- **Personal-use** — car, appliances, jewellery for use\n\n**Liabilities** (at outstanding principal):\n- **Short-term** — credit cards, overdrafts, dues within a year\n- **Long-term** — home, education, vehicle loans\n\n> ⚠️ Record loans at the **outstanding principal**, never the original amount and never the total of remaining EMIs (that would include unaccrued future interest).\n\n---\n\n## 3.9 ⭐ Evaluating the Financial Position — The Ten Ratios\n\n**Learn every formula and its healthy range. These are the most reliably examinable items in Module 1.**\n\n### 1. Savings Ratio\n$$\\text{Savings Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Savings (Surplus)}}{\\text{Gross Income}}$$\n**Healthy: 20%+ (higher for late starters).** Measures the plan's fuel supply.\n\n### 2. Expenses Ratio\n$$\\text{Expenses Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total Expenses}}{\\text{Gross Income}}$$\n**The complement:** Savings Ratio + Expenses Ratio = **100%**. A great self-check in the exam.\n\n### 3. Total Assets\nThe sum of all assets at current market value. Not a ratio, but the base for several below.\n\n### 4. Total Liabilities\nThe sum of all outstanding debts at principal value.\n\n### 5. Leverage Ratio (Debt-to-Assets)\n$$\\text{Leverage Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total Liabilities}}{\\text{Total Assets}}$$\n**Lower is better.** Above ~0.5 means more than half the asset base is financed by debt.\n\n### 6. Net Worth\n$$\\text{Net Worth} = \\text{Total Assets} - \\text{Total Liabilities}$$\n\n### 7. Solvency Ratio\n$$\\text{Solvency Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Net Worth}}{\\text{Total Assets}}$$\n**Higher is better.** Shows the share of assets genuinely owned.\n\n> 🔑 **Solvency Ratio + Leverage Ratio = 1.** Always. Use it to check your arithmetic.\n\n### 8. Liquid Assets\nAssets convertible to cash quickly with no material loss — cash, savings balance, liquid funds, sweep-in FDs. **Equity and PPF do not qualify** (volatile / locked in).\n\n### 9. Liquidity Ratio\n$$\\text{Liquidity Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Liquid Assets}}{\\text{Monthly Expenses}}$$\n**Answer is in *months*. Healthy: 3–6 months.**\n\n### 10. Financial Assets Ratio\n$$\\text{Financial Assets Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Financial Assets}}{\\text{Total Assets}}$$\n**Higher is better for goal funding.** A low ratio signals the \"asset-rich, cash-poor\" trap — wealth locked in a self-occupied house that cannot be partly sold or spent.\n\n### 11. Debt-to-Income Ratio\n$$\\text{Debt-to-Income Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total EMIs / Debt Servicing}}{\\text{Gross Income}}$$\n**Healthy: below ~35–40%.** Lenders use this to decide eligibility. Above 50% the household is debt-stressed.\n\n---\n\n### 📋 Ratio Cheat Sheet\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Healthy | Reads as |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Savings | Savings ÷ Income | ≥ 20% | % |\n| Expenses | Expenses ÷ Income | ≤ 80% | % |\n| Leverage | Liabilities ÷ Assets | < 0.5 | decimal |\n| Solvency | Net worth ÷ Assets | > 0.5 | decimal |\n| Liquidity | Liquid assets ÷ **Monthly** expenses | 3–6 | **months** |\n| Financial assets | Financial assets ÷ Total assets | higher | decimal/% |\n| Debt-to-income | EMIs ÷ Income | < 35–40% | % |\n\n**Three self-checks:**\n1. Savings ratio + expenses ratio = **100%**\n2. Solvency ratio + leverage ratio = **1**\n3. Liquidity ratio is in **months** — if your answer looks like a percentage, you divided by the wrong thing.\n\n---\n\n## 🧮 Worked Example\n\n**Ravi's data:** Monthly income ₹1,80,000. Monthly expenses ₹1,26,000 (including EMIs of ₹54,000).\n**Assets:** savings ₹2,40,000; liquid fund ₹1,60,000; mutual funds ₹18,00,000; EPF ₹14,00,000; house ₹80,00,000; car ₹9,00,000; gold ₹5,00,000.\n**Liabilities:** home loan ₹44,00,000; car loan ₹4,00,000.\n\n| Step | Working | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Savings | 1,80,000 − 1,26,000 | **₹54,000** |\n| Savings ratio | 54,000 ÷ 1,80,000 | **30%** ✅ |\n| Expenses ratio | 1,26,000 ÷ 1,80,000 | **70%** (30 + 70 = 100 ✓) |\n| Total assets | 2.4 + 1.6 + 18 + 14 + 80 + 9 + 5 (₹ lakh) | **₹1,30,00,000** |\n| Total liabilities | 44 + 4 (₹ lakh) | **₹48,00,000** |\n| Net worth | 1,30,00,000 − 48,00,000 | **₹82,00,000** |\n| Solvency ratio | 82,00,000 ÷ 1,30,00,000 | **0.63** ✅ |\n| Leverage ratio | 48,00,000 ÷ 1,30,00,000 | **0.37** (0.63 + 0.37 = 1 ✓) |\n| Liquid assets | 2,40,000 + 1,60,000 | **₹4,00,000** |\n| Liquidity ratio | 4,00,000 ÷ 1,26,000 | **3.17 months** ⚠️ thin |\n| Financial assets | 2.4 + 1.6 + 18 + 14 (₹ lakh) | **₹36,00,000** |\n| Financial assets ratio | 36,00,000 ÷ 1,30,00,000 | **27.7%** |\n| Debt-to-income | 54,000 ÷ 1,80,000 | **30%** ✅ |\n\n**Verdict:** Ravi saves well (30%) and his debt burden is manageable (30%), but his **liquidity is thin at 3.17 months** and **68% of his assets are physical** (house + car + gold). Priorities: raise the emergency fund toward 6 months (≈₹7.6 lakh) and direct future surplus into financial assets.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Surplus | Inflows − Outflows |\n| Cash flow statement vs balance sheet | Flow over a **period** vs stock at a **point** |\n| The link between them | This period's surplus adds to next period's assets → net worth rises |\n| Four outflow types | Fixed, variable, **periodic**, savings |\n| Most-forgotten outflow | **Periodic** (annual premiums, festivals) — divide by 12 and set aside |\n| Pay yourself first | Save **before** spending, automated on salary day |\n| Emergency fund size | **3–6 months of expenses** (not income) |\n| Emergency fund location | Savings / sweep-in FD / liquid funds — **never equity** |\n| Savings + expenses ratios | Sum to **100%** |\n| Solvency + leverage ratios | Sum to **1** |\n| Liquidity ratio units | **Months** |\n| Liquid assets exclude | Equity, PPF, property, personal-use assets |\n| Debt-to-income ceiling | ~**35–40%** |\n| Low financial assets ratio signals | **Asset-rich, cash-poor** — wealth locked in property |\n\n> **Exam tip:** in every caselet, compute **total assets and total liabilities first**, then derive every ratio from those two numbers. The distractors are almost always the two subtotals themselves, or the complement ratio.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 3: Cash Flow Management and Budgeting — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The ten ratios are near-certain exam marks — drill them until instant.**\n\n**Q1.** What is the formula for surplus?\n> **A:** **Surplus (Savings) = Total Inflows − Total Outflows.** It is the engine of the whole plan — no surplus, no plan.\n\n**Q2.** What is the difference between the cash flow statement and the personal balance sheet?\n> **A:** Cash flow statement = **flows over a period**, output is the **surplus**. Balance sheet = **stock at a point in time**, output is **net worth**.\n\n**Q3.** How do the two statements connect?\n> **A:** This period's **surplus** becomes an addition to next period's **assets**, which raises **net worth**.\n\n**Q4.** Name the four types of cash outflow.\n> **A:** **Fixed/committed** (EMIs, rent, premiums), **variable/discretionary** (groceries, dining), **periodic/lumpy** (annual premiums, festivals, vacations), and **savings/investment**.\n\n**Q5.** What is the most common budgeting failure?\n> **A:** Ignoring **periodic (lumpy) outflows**. The fix: divide each annual expense by 12 and set that amount aside every month.\n\n**Q6.** Explain \"pay yourself first.\"\n> **A:** Save **before** you spend: Income → **Savings (automated)** → Expenses from the rest. The wrong order treats savings as the residual, which ends up near zero.\n\n**Q7.** What is a budget, versus an income and expenditure statement?\n> **A:** A **budget is forward-looking** (a plan); the **income and expenditure statement is backward-looking** (the record of what actually happened).\n\n**Q8.** How large should a contingency fund be, and measured against what?\n> **A:** **3–6 months of EXPENSES** — not income, because expenses are what must be paid. Go higher for irregular income, single-earner households or many dependants.\n\n**Q9.** Where should an emergency fund be held, and why not equity?\n> **A:** Savings account, sweep-in FD or **liquid/overnight funds**. Never equity — the moment you need it (recession, job loss) is exactly when equity is likely to be down.\n\n**Q10.** State the savings ratio formula and a healthy level.\n> **A:** **Savings ÷ Gross Income**; healthy is **20% or more**.\n\n**Q11.** State the expenses ratio, and its relationship to the savings ratio.\n> **A:** **Expenses ÷ Gross Income.** Savings ratio + expenses ratio = **100%** — a useful arithmetic self-check.\n\n**Q12.** State the leverage ratio.\n> **A:** **Total Liabilities ÷ Total Assets.** Lower is better; above ~0.5 means over half the asset base is debt-financed.\n\n**Q13.** State the solvency ratio and its relationship to leverage.\n> **A:** **Net Worth ÷ Total Assets.** **Solvency + Leverage = 1**, always.\n\n**Q14.** State the liquidity ratio — and what unit is the answer in?\n> **A:** **Liquid Assets ÷ Monthly Expenses.** The answer is in **months**; healthy is **3–6**.\n\n**Q15.** Which assets count as \"liquid\", and which commonly-mistaken ones do not?\n> **A:** Count cash, savings balance, liquid/overnight funds, sweep-in FDs. **Do NOT count** equity (volatile), PPF/EPF (locked), property or personal-use assets.\n\n**Q16.** State the financial assets ratio and what a low value signals.\n> **A:** **Financial Assets ÷ Total Assets.** A low value signals the **asset-rich, cash-poor** trap — wealth locked in a self-occupied house that can't be partly sold.\n\n**Q17.** State the debt-to-income ratio and its ceiling.\n> **A:** **Total EMIs ÷ Gross Income**; keep it below about **35–40%**. Above 50% the household is debt-stressed.\n\n**Q18.** Name the three arithmetic self-checks for the ratio questions.\n> **A:** 1) Savings % + expenses % = **100**. 2) Solvency + leverage = **1**. 3) Liquidity ratio is in **months** — a percentage-looking answer means you divided by the wrong thing.\n\n**Q19.** At what value are liabilities recorded on the personal balance sheet?\n> **A:** The **outstanding principal** — never the original loan amount, never the total of remaining EMIs (which would include unaccrued future interest).\n\n**Q20.** At what value are assets recorded?\n> **A:** **Current market value**, so the balance sheet reflects reality today.\n\n**Q21.** Name the three categories of cash inflow.\n> **A:** **Regular/active** (salary, fees), **portfolio/passive** (interest, dividends, rent), and **irregular** (bonus, gifts, maturity proceeds).\n\n**Q22.** What are the two failure modes of cash management?\n> **A:** **Too little** liquidity (forced borrowing or asset sales) and **too much** idle cash (opportunity cost of foregone returns).\n\n**Q23.** What are the six steps in preparing a budget?\n> **A:** **Record** actuals → **categorise** → **set targets** → **build in savings first** → **monitor** actual vs budget → **revise**.\n\n**Q24.** What does variance analysis tell you?\n> **A:** *Where the plan leaked.* Comparing budget vs actual by category locates persistent overspending — meaning either the target was unrealistic or behaviour must change.\n\n**Q25.** Is a client earning ₹5 lakh a month and spending ₹5 lakh better off than one earning ₹1 lakh and spending ₹70,000?\n> **A:** **No.** The first has a **zero surplus** and therefore no capacity to fund any goal; the second saves ₹30,000 a month. Income level is not financial health — surplus is.\n\n**Q26.** Besides cash, what else belongs to contingency planning?\n> **A:** Adequate **health insurance** and **life insurance** — they stop a single event from consuming the entire emergency fund.\n\n**Q27.** Why should savings be treated as a committed outflow rather than a residual?\n> **A:** Because discretionary spending expands to fill available income. Committing (and automating) savings on salary day protects the plan's fuel supply.\n\n**Q28.** A client has ₹4,00,000 liquid and spends ₹1,26,000 a month. What is the liquidity ratio and the verdict?\n> **A:** 4,00,000 ÷ 1,26,000 = **3.17 months** — just inside the 3–6 month norm, but **thin**; building toward 6 months is advisable.\n\n**Q29.** What is the very first thing to compute in any ratio caselet?\n> **A:** **Total assets and total liabilities.** Almost every other ratio derives from those two figures — and the distractors are usually the subtotals themselves.\n\n**Q30.** Total assets ₹1,30,00,000; total liabilities ₹48,00,000. Give net worth, solvency and leverage.\n> **A:** Net worth = **₹82,00,000**. Solvency = 82 ÷ 130 = **0.63**. Leverage = 48 ÷ 130 = **0.37**. Check: 0.63 + 0.37 = 1 ✓\n", "questions": "# Chapter 3: Cash Flow Management and Budgeting — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **The ten ratios dominate this chapter and the exam.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Surplus (savings) is calculated as:\nA) Total assets − total liabilities  B) Total inflows − total outflows  C) Liquid assets ÷ monthly expenses  D) Income × savings rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Surplus is a **cash flow** concept: what comes in minus what goes out over a period.\nA) Is net worth, a balance sheet concept. C) Is the liquidity ratio. D) Is circular — the savings rate is derived *from* the surplus.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The savings ratio is:\nA) Savings ÷ Total assets  B) Savings ÷ Gross income  C) Savings ÷ Expenses  D) Income ÷ Savings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Savings ÷ Gross Income**, expressed as a percentage.\nA) Divides a flow by a stock — meaningless. C) Would exceed 100% for good savers and is not the standard definition. D) Is the reciprocal.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The liquidity ratio is expressed in:\nA) Percentage  B) Rupees  C) Months  D) A decimal between 0 and 1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Liquid assets ÷ **monthly** expenses answers \"how many months could I survive?\" — so the unit is **months**.\nA), B) and D) all indicate a wrong denominator. If your answer looks like a percentage, you divided by income or assets instead of monthly expenses.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The solvency ratio is:\nA) Total liabilities ÷ Total assets  B) Net worth ÷ Total assets  C) Net worth ÷ Total liabilities  D) Total assets ÷ Net worth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Net worth ÷ Total assets** — the share of the asset base genuinely owned.\nA) Is the **leverage** ratio. C) and D) are not standard ratios in this syllabus.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Solvency ratio + leverage ratio equals:\nA) 0  B) 0.5  C) 1  D) 100\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Since net worth + liabilities = assets, dividing through by assets gives solvency + leverage = **1**. A reliable arithmetic self-check.\nA), B) and D) do not follow from the identity.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A contingency fund should typically cover:\nA) 3–6 months of income  B) 3–6 months of expenses  C) 1 month of expenses  D) 24 months of expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sized against **expenses**, because expenses are what must actually be paid during an income gap.\nA) Using income overstates the requirement for high savers. C) One month is too thin for a job loss. D) Two years parks far too much in low-return assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which of these qualifies as a liquid asset?\nA) Public Provident Fund balance  B) Equity mutual fund units  C) Sweep-in fixed deposit  D) Residential property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A sweep-in FD converts to cash instantly with no loss of value.\nA) PPF is locked in for a long term. B) Equity is volatile — the value is not certain when needed. D) Property takes months to sell.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** \"Pay yourself first\" means:\nA) Pay your EMIs before anything else  B) Save before spending, ideally automated  C) Withdraw your salary in cash  D) Repay the smallest loan first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Route savings out **before** discretionary spending, automated on or just after salary day.\nA) EMIs are contractual outflows, not savings. C) Has nothing to do with the principle. D) Describes the debt \"snowball\" method (Chapter 4).\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which of these is a periodic (lumpy) outflow?\nA) Monthly rent  B) The annual motor insurance premium  C) The monthly SIP  D) Daily commuting cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Periodic outflows are **predictable but infrequent** — annual premiums, festivals, vacations, servicing.\nA), C) and D) all recur monthly or daily and are easy to budget.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The leverage ratio is:\nA) Net worth ÷ Total assets  B) Total liabilities ÷ Total assets  C) EMIs ÷ Income  D) Liquid assets ÷ Expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total liabilities ÷ Total assets**; lower is better.\nA) Is solvency. C) Is debt-to-income. D) Is liquidity.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Savings ratio plus expenses ratio equals:\nA) 50%  B) 100%  C) 1 month  D) It varies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Since savings + expenses = income, dividing by income gives **100%**.\nA) and C) do not follow. D) The identity always holds when both are measured against the same gross income.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The debt-to-income ratio is generally considered healthy below:\nA) 10%  B) 35–40%  C) 60%  D) 80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lenders and planners generally regard **under 35–40%** as manageable.\nA) 10% is unnecessarily strict and few borrowers would qualify for a home loan. C) and D) indicate debt stress.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A budget differs from an income and expenditure statement because a budget is:\nA) Backward-looking  B) Forward-looking  C) Prepared only by companies  D) The same thing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **budget plans ahead**; the income and expenditure statement **records what happened**.\nA) Describes the income and expenditure statement. C) Households prepare budgets too. D) They serve different purposes.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The financial assets ratio is:\nA) Financial assets ÷ Total assets  B) Financial assets ÷ Income  C) Total assets ÷ Financial assets  D) Financial assets ÷ Liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — It measures what share of wealth is in **financial** (as opposed to physical/personal-use) form.\nB), C) and D) are not standard definitions.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Which statement about the personal balance sheet is correct?\nA) It covers a period of time  B) It is a snapshot at a point in time  C) It shows the monthly surplus  D) It records only liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A balance sheet is a **snapshot**; the cash flow statement covers a period.\nA) and C) describe the cash flow statement. D) It records both sides.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Liabilities on the personal balance sheet are recorded at:\nA) The original loan sanctioned  B) The outstanding principal  C) The sum of all remaining EMIs  D) The market value of the asset financed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Only the **outstanding principal** is currently owed.\nA) Ignores repayments made. C) Includes unaccrued future interest, which is not yet a liability. D) That is the asset side.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Which of these is a fixed (committed) outflow?\nA) Dining out  B) A home loan EMI  C) A weekend trip  D) Impulse shopping\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EMIs are contractual and cannot be cut at short notice.\nA), C) and D) are all discretionary/variable and are the first place to look when a budget needs fixing.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Which best describes cash management?\nA) Holding as much cash as possible  B) Meeting obligations as they fall due without holding excessive idle cash  C) Investing everything in equity  D) Avoiding all bank accounts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Balance the two failure modes: too little (forced borrowing) and too much (opportunity cost).\nA) Excessive cash sacrifices returns. C) Equity cannot meet near-term obligations reliably. D) Impractical.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which of the following is a portfolio (passive) inflow?\nA) Monthly salary  B) Rental income from a let-out property  C) Professional consulting fees  D) A performance bonus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Portfolio inflows arise from **assets** — interest, dividends and rent.\nA) and C) are active/earned income. D) Is an irregular inflow tied to employment.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A client with zero surplus:\nA) Has an excellent financial position  B) Cannot fund any goal until cash flow is fixed  C) Should immediately buy equity  D) Has a high savings ratio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With no surplus there is nothing to allocate; **cash flow management must precede investment planning**.\nA) Zero surplus is the weakest possible position regardless of income. C) There is nothing to invest. D) The savings ratio is 0%.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Net worth is:\nA) Income − Expenses  B) Total assets − Total liabilities  C) Liquid assets − Short-term debt  D) Assets + Liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The balance sheet identity.\nA) Is the surplus. C) Is a partial measure, not net worth. D) Adds instead of subtracting.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which of these should NOT be counted as a liquid asset?\nA) Balance in a savings account  B) An overnight mutual fund  C) EPF balance  D) Cash in hand\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **EPF is locked in** until specified events, so it cannot serve an emergency.\nA), B) and D) are all immediately available at certain value.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Monitoring a budget primarily involves:\nA) Comparing budgeted with actual figures by category  B) Increasing income  C) Cancelling all insurance  D) Buying more assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Variance analysis** — budget vs actual, per category — locates where the plan leaked.\nB), C) and D) are not monitoring activities.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which is the correct order under \"pay yourself first\"?\nA) Income → Expenses → Savings  B) Income → Savings → Expenses  C) Savings → Income → Expenses  D) Expenses → Income → Savings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Savings are routed out **immediately after income is received**, and spending comes from the remainder.\nA) Is the common (failing) order that makes savings a residual. C) and D) are not feasible sequences.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A household with irregular income should hold a contingency fund at:\nA) The lower end of the 3–6 month range  B) The upper end of the range or beyond  C) Zero, since income is variable  D) Exactly one month\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Greater income uncertainty means a longer possible gap, so the buffer should be **larger**.\nA) Understates the need. C) and D) leave the household badly exposed.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The expenses ratio measures:\nA) Expenses ÷ Total assets  B) Expenses ÷ Gross income  C) Expenses ÷ Savings  D) Fixed ÷ Variable expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Expenses ÷ Gross income**, the complement of the savings ratio.\nA), C) and D) are not the standard definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which of these is a personal-use asset?\nA) A let-out flat  B) The family car  C) A corporate bond  D) A liquid fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Personal-use assets are held for use, typically depreciate and produce no income.\nA) Generates rent, so it is an investment asset. C) and D) are financial assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A savings ratio of 30% means:\nA) 30% of assets are savings  B) 30% of gross income is saved  C) 30 months of expenses are covered  D) Debt is 30% of income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The savings ratio measures the share of **income** saved.\nA) Would be an asset-composition measure. C) Is liquidity. D) Is debt-to-income.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** The main reason to categorise expenses is to:\nA) Increase total spending  B) Identify which expenses are controllable  C) Reduce income  D) Satisfy tax authorities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Separating **fixed** from **variable** shows where cuts are actually possible.\nA) and C) are not objectives. D) Personal budgets are not filed with tax authorities.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Which best describes the link between the two statements?\nA) They are unrelated  B) This period's surplus adds to assets, raising net worth  C) Net worth determines income  D) The balance sheet determines expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The surplus generated in a period flows onto the balance sheet as additional assets.\nA) They are tightly linked. C) and D) reverse the causation.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A leverage ratio of 0.65 indicates:\nA) 65% of assets are financed by debt  B) 65% of income goes to EMIs  C) 65 months of liquidity  D) A 65% savings rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Leverage = liabilities ÷ assets, so **65% of the asset base is debt-financed** — high, and above the ~0.5 comfort level.\nB) Is debt-to-income. C) Is liquidity. D) Is the savings ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Contingency planning includes which of the following besides a cash buffer?\nA) Buying more equity  B) Adequate health and life insurance  C) Taking a larger home loan  D) Cancelling the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Insurance stops a single event from consuming the entire emergency fund.\nA) Equity is not a contingency asset. C) More debt increases fragility. D) Removes the buffer entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Which of these forecasting events should a budget anticipate?\nA) A school fee increase next April  B) Yesterday's grocery bill  C) Last year's bonus  D) The 2019 tax rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Forecasting is **forward-looking**: increments, new EMIs starting, fee rises, a child's arrival.\nB) and C) are historical records. D) Is obsolete data.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A low financial assets ratio typically signals:\nA) Excellent goal-funding capacity  B) Wealth locked in physical assets — \"asset-rich, cash-poor\"  C) High liquidity  D) Zero debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Most wealth sits in a house or other physical assets that cannot be partly sold or spent.\nA) Reverses it. C) Physical assets are illiquid. D) The ratio says nothing about debt.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Assets on the personal balance sheet should be recorded at:\nA) Original cost  B) Current market value  C) Insured value  D) Zero until sold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Current market value** keeps the picture realistic.\nA) Cost ignores gains and losses. C) Insured value reflects replacement, not market worth. D) Unsold assets are still assets.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Monthly income ₹1,50,000, monthly expenses ₹1,08,000. What are the savings and expenses ratios?\nA) 28% and 72%  B) 72% and 28%  C) 38.9% and 61.1%  D) 30% and 70%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 1,50,000 − 1,08,000 = **₹42,000**. Savings ratio = 42,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **28%**. Expenses ratio = 1,08,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **72%**. Check: 28 + 72 = 100 ✓\nB) Reverses the two. C) 38.9% wrongly divides savings by expenses (42,000 ÷ 1,08,000). D) Does not match the figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Total assets ₹90,00,000; total liabilities ₹36,00,000. What are net worth, solvency and leverage?\nA) ₹54,00,000; 0.60; 0.40  B) ₹1,26,00,000; 0.40; 0.60  C) ₹54,00,000; 0.40; 0.60  D) ₹54,00,000; 1.50; 0.67\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = 90 − 36 = **₹54,00,000**. Solvency = 54 ÷ 90 = **0.60**. Leverage = 36 ÷ 90 = **0.40**. Check: 0.60 + 0.40 = 1 ✓\nB) Adds instead of subtracting for net worth. C) Swaps solvency and leverage. D) Uses assets ÷ net worth.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A client holds cash ₹60,000, savings balance ₹2,40,000, liquid fund ₹3,00,000, equity funds ₹15,00,000 and PPF ₹9,00,000. Monthly expenses are ₹1,50,000. What is the liquidity ratio?\nA) 4.0 months  B) 2.0 months  C) 18.0 months  D) 1.6 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Liquid assets = 60,000 + 2,40,000 + 3,00,000 = **₹6,00,000**. Ratio = 6,00,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **4.0 months**.\nB) 2.0 counts only the savings balance. C) 18.0 wrongly includes equity and PPF — neither is liquid. D) 1.6 counts only cash plus part of the savings balance.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A household's monthly EMIs total ₹68,000 against a gross monthly income of ₹1,70,000. The debt-to-income ratio is:\nA) 25%  B) 40%  C) 250%  D) 60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 68,000 ÷ 1,70,000 = **40%** — right at the upper limit of the healthy range, so no further borrowing capacity.\nA) 25% understates it. C) 250% is the reciprocal. D) 60% does not match.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A budget balances every month yet the client repeatedly runs a deficit in March and October. The most likely cause is:\nA) The savings ratio is too high  B) Periodic (lumpy) expenses were not provisioned monthly  C) Income is too high  D) The liquidity ratio is excessive\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Annual premiums, school fees and festival spending cluster in particular months. **Divide each annual amount by 12 and set it aside monthly.**\nA) A high savings ratio is desirable and would not cause seasonal deficits. C) and D) are not causes of a deficit.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Which client is in the stronger position?\nA) Income ₹6,00,000/month, expenses ₹6,00,000/month  B) Income ₹1,20,000/month, expenses ₹84,000/month  C) They are equal  D) Cannot be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — B saves **₹36,000 a month (30%)**; A saves **nothing (0%)**. Financial health is measured by **surplus**, not income level.\nA) A high income with zero surplus funds no goals. C) They are very different. D) The surplus makes them directly comparable.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A client's liquidity ratio is 1.2 months and she is the sole earner with two dependants. The adviser should:\nA) Recommend investing the buffer in mid-caps  B) Build the contingency fund toward at least 6 months before other investing  C) Take on a personal loan  D) Do nothing — 1.2 months is fine\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **1.2 months is far below the 3–6 month norm**, and a sole earner with dependants sits at the *upper* end of the requirement.\nA) Defeats the purpose of an emergency fund. C) Debt is not a substitute for savings. D) The household is one income shock from crisis.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Total assets ₹1,20,00,000, of which the self-occupied house is ₹92,00,000 and the car ₹8,00,000. What is the financial assets ratio?\nA) 83.3%  B) 16.7%  C) 76.7%  D) 23.3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Physical + personal-use = 92,00,000 + 8,00,000 = ₹1,00,00,000. Financial assets = 1,20,00,000 − 1,00,00,000 = **₹20,00,000**. Ratio = 20 ÷ 120 = **16.7%**.\nA) 83.3% is the *physical* asset share. C) and D) do not follow. **A 16.7% financial assets ratio is the classic asset-rich, cash-poor signature.**\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which action most directly improves the savings ratio?\nA) Buying a larger house  B) Cutting discretionary spending and automating a SIP on salary day  C) Increasing the credit card limit  D) Revaluing assets upward\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Reducing outflows and committing savings first both raise the surplus.\nA) Adds an EMI, lowering the surplus. C) Raises borrowing capacity, not savings. D) Changes the balance sheet, not the cash flow.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A client's solvency ratio is 0.25. This means:\nA) Only 25% of the asset base is genuinely owned  B) 25% of income goes to EMIs  C) The client has 25 months of liquidity  D) The client saves 25%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Solvency = net worth ÷ assets, so **75% of the asset base is financed by debt** (leverage = 0.75) — a highly leveraged, fragile position.\nB) Is debt-to-income. C) Is liquidity. D) Is the savings ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Which pair of expenses should be attacked first when a client must cut spending?\nA) Home loan EMI and insurance premium  B) Dining out and subscription services  C) School fees and utilities  D) SIP and PPF contribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Variable/discretionary** expenses are the controllable ones.\nA) EMIs and premiums are contractual — cutting insurance also destroys protection. C) Largely essential and committed. D) Cutting savings defeats the purpose of the exercise.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A client's monthly surplus is ₹40,000 but the liquidity ratio is 0.8 months. What is the correct sequencing?\nA) Start an equity SIP with the entire surplus  B) Direct the surplus to the emergency fund until 3–6 months is reached, then invest  C) Prepay the home loan entirely  D) Buy a second property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **less than one month** of buffer, any shock forces debt or a distressed asset sale. Build the buffer first, then invest.\nA) Leaves the household exposed. C) Locks money into an illiquid asset while the buffer is empty. D) Worsens both liquidity and leverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Gross monthly income ₹2,00,000; expenses ₹1,40,000 including ₹60,000 of EMIs. What are the savings ratio and debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 30% and 30%  B) 30% and 43%  C) 40% and 30%  D) 70% and 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 2,00,000 − 1,40,000 = ₹60,000; savings ratio = 60,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **30%**. Debt-to-income = 60,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **30%**.\nB) 43% wrongly divides EMIs by expenses. C) Miscomputes savings. D) 70% is the expenses ratio. **Both ratios are healthy here.**\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Why is the emergency fund measured against expenses rather than income?\nA) Income is easier to measure  B) Because expenses are what must be paid during an income gap  C) Regulations require it  D) Expenses are always higher than income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — During unemployment, income stops but **expenses continue** — that is the amount the buffer must cover.\nA) Income is usually easier to measure, if anything. C) No regulation prescribes it. D) Expenses are normally *lower* than income for a solvent household.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A client's leverage ratio rises from 0.35 to 0.58 in two years without new borrowing. The most likely explanation is:\nA) Assets fell in value  B) Income rose  C) EMIs were repaid  D) The savings ratio improved\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — With liabilities unchanged, leverage (liabilities ÷ assets) rises only if the **denominator falls** — i.e. asset values dropped.\nB) Income is not in the formula. C) Repayment would *lower* liabilities and hence leverage. D) The savings ratio is not in the formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which is the best treatment of a ₹3,60,000 annual insurance premium in a monthly budget?\nA) Ignore it until it falls due  B) Provision ₹30,000 a month into a dedicated liquid holding  C) Borrow when it falls due  D) Pay it from the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Divide the annual amount by 12: 3,60,000 ÷ 12 = **₹30,000 a month** set aside.\nA) Guarantees a deficit in that month. C) Borrowing for a *predictable* expense is poor practice. D) The emergency fund is for the *unpredictable*, not the foreseeable.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A client has total assets ₹75,00,000 and a solvency ratio of 0.8. What are net worth and total liabilities?\nA) ₹60,00,000 and ₹15,00,000  B) ₹15,00,000 and ₹60,00,000  C) ₹60,00,000 and ₹60,00,000  D) ₹93,75,000 and ₹18,75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = solvency × assets = 0.8 × 75,00,000 = **₹60,00,000**. Liabilities = assets − net worth = 75,00,000 − 60,00,000 = **₹15,00,000**. Check: leverage = 15 ÷ 75 = 0.2, and 0.8 + 0.2 = 1 ✓\nB) Reverses them. C) Is arithmetically impossible. D) Divides instead of multiplying.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A client insists on counting the self-occupied house as a liquid asset. The adviser should explain that:\nA) That is correct practice  B) Property cannot be converted to cash quickly or partly, so it is not liquid  C) All assets are liquid  D) Only gold is liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liquidity requires **speed and certainty of value**. Property takes months to sell, cannot be sold in slices, and the family lives in it.\nA) Would badly overstate the liquidity ratio. C) Liquidity varies widely by asset. D) Gold is more liquid than property but is not the only liquid asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Monthly income ₹90,000; fixed outflows ₹40,000; variable ₹28,000; periodic (annualised monthly) ₹7,000. What is the surplus and savings ratio?\nA) ₹15,000 and 16.7%  B) ₹22,000 and 24.4%  C) ₹50,000 and 55.6%  D) ₹15,000 and 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total outflows = 40,000 + 28,000 + 7,000 = **₹75,000**. Surplus = 90,000 − 75,000 = **₹15,000**. Savings ratio = 15,000 ÷ 90,000 = **16.7%**.\nB) Omits the periodic provision. C) Counts only fixed outflows. D) Computes the ratio incorrectly. **At 16.7% the client is below the 20% guideline.**\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** The primary benefit of automating savings on salary day is:\nA) It increases the interest rate earned  B) It removes reliance on willpower, protecting the surplus  C) It reduces tax  D) It shortens loan tenure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Automation makes saving the **default**, so discretionary spending adapts to what remains.\nA) The rate depends on the product, not the timing. C) Automation itself gives no tax benefit. D) It has no direct effect on loans.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A client's liquidity ratio is 14 months. The adviser should note that:\nA) This is ideal  B) Excess idle cash carries an opportunity cost and should be deployed  C) More cash should be added  D) The client is insolvent\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Beyond 3–6 months, extra cash earns low returns and **loses purchasing power to inflation**. The excess should be invested toward goals.\nA) It is well above the norm. C) Would worsen the drag. D) Liquidity says nothing about solvency.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which of these correctly ranks the priority when a client has both a low liquidity ratio and a high debt-to-income ratio?\nA) Invest in equity first  B) Address both — build a minimum buffer while restructuring or reducing debt  C) Ignore the buffer and repay all debt  D) Ignore the debt and build a 12-month buffer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both are fragilities. Practice is to build a **minimum buffer (about 1–3 months)** so a shock doesn't force fresh borrowing, while simultaneously attacking the costliest debt.\nA) Investing while debt-stressed and illiquid is premature. C) Zero buffer means any shock creates new debt. D) A 12-month buffer while paying high interest is inefficient.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A client's expenses ratio is 92%. What does this imply?\nA) A savings ratio of 8% — very low, leaving little to fund goals  B) A savings ratio of 92%  C) A liquidity ratio of 8 months  D) A leverage ratio of 0.92\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings ratio = 100% − 92% = **8%**, well below the 20% guideline.\nB) Reverses the two. C) and D) confuse this ratio with unrelated ones.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Which is the correct classification of a monthly SIP in a household budget?\nA) A variable/discretionary expense  B) A committed outflow, treated like a fixed obligation  C) An inflow  D) A liability\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Treating the SIP as **committed** (like an EMI) is exactly what \"pay yourself first\" achieves.\nA) Treating it as discretionary is why savings get skipped. C) It is money going out. D) It creates an asset, not a liability.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A client with a 45% debt-to-income ratio applies for another loan. The likely outcome and advice is:\nA) Easy approval; take the loan  B) Likely rejection or costly terms; reduce existing debt before borrowing more  C) The ratio is irrelevant to lenders  D) Increase the loan amount to improve the ratio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At **45%** the household is already above the ~35–40% comfort zone; lenders use this ratio directly for eligibility.\nA) Approval is unlikely on good terms. C) It is a primary lending criterion. D) More debt raises the ratio further.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which is the correct first step in preparing a household budget?\nA) Setting spending targets  B) Recording actual income and spending for 2–3 months  C) Buying insurance  D) Opening a demat account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You cannot set realistic targets without knowing the **actual** baseline — and most people significantly underestimate discretionary spending.\nA) Comes after recording and categorising. C) and D) are unrelated to budget preparation.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A client's net worth is ₹40,00,000 and leverage ratio 0.60. What are total assets?\nA) ₹66,66,667  B) ₹1,00,00,000  C) ₹24,00,000  D) ₹64,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Leverage 0.60 means solvency = 1 − 0.60 = **0.40**. Since solvency = net worth ÷ assets, assets = net worth ÷ 0.40 = 40,00,000 ÷ 0.40 = **₹1,00,00,000**. (Check: liabilities = 0.60 × 1,00,00,000 = ₹60,00,000; 1,00,00,000 − 60,00,000 = ₹40,00,000 ✓)\nA) Divides by 0.60. C) Multiplies by 0.60. D) Does not follow.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which best explains why a high-income client can still be financially fragile?\nA) High income guarantees fragility  B) Lifestyle inflation can push the expenses ratio near 100%, leaving no surplus or buffer  C) High income prevents borrowing  D) Income has no effect on planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Lifestyle inflation**: spending rises to match income, so the surplus stays near zero and no emergency fund accumulates.\nA) High income does not *cause* fragility — spending behaviour does. C) High earners typically borrow more easily. D) Income matters, but only via the surplus it generates.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client receives a ₹5,00,000 annual bonus. The best budgeting treatment is:\nA) Treat it as regular monthly income  B) Treat it as an irregular inflow and allocate deliberately — buffer, debt reduction, goals  C) Spend it entirely  D) Ignore it in the plan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bonuses are **irregular inflows**. Pre-committing them (to the buffer, costly debt or a goal) prevents the \"windfall gets absorbed\" effect.\nA) Treating a variable bonus as regular income risks over-committing. C) Wastes a significant planning opportunity. D) Ignoring it forfeits the benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which combination indicates the healthiest financial position?\nA) Savings ratio 5%, liquidity 1 month, leverage 0.7  B) Savings ratio 28%, liquidity 5 months, leverage 0.3  C) Savings ratio 28%, liquidity 0.5 months, leverage 0.8  D) Savings ratio 12%, liquidity 2 months, leverage 0.6\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three are healthy: savings **above 20%**, liquidity **inside 3–6 months**, leverage **below 0.5**.\nA) Fails on every measure. C) Saves well but is dangerously illiquid and over-leveraged. D) Is mediocre on all three.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** The main purpose of the income and expenditure statement is to:\nA) Value the client's assets  B) Record actual inflows and outflows over a period and derive the surplus  C) Compute the loan EMI  D) File tax returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is the **backward-looking record** whose key output is the surplus.\nA) That is the balance sheet. C) EMIs use the annuity formula. D) It is a planning tool, not a tax filing.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A client's liquid assets are ₹9,00,000 and annual expenses ₹21,60,000. What is the liquidity ratio?\nA) 5 months  B) 0.42 months  C) 41.7%  D) 2.4 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The denominator must be **monthly** expenses: 21,60,000 ÷ 12 = **₹1,80,000**. Ratio = 9,00,000 ÷ 1,80,000 = **5 months**.\nB) Divides by annual expenses. C) Expresses it as a percentage of annual expenses — wrong unit. D) Does not follow. **The classic trap: always convert annual expenses to monthly first.**\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which statement about surplus is correct?\nA) It appears on the balance sheet  B) It is a flow that, once saved, becomes an asset and raises net worth  C) It equals net worth  D) It is a liability\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Surplus is a **flow**; retaining it converts it into a **stock** (an asset), raising net worth.\nA) The balance sheet shows the *resulting asset*, not the flow. C) They are different concepts. D) It is not an obligation.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A client cuts a ₹12,000 monthly discretionary expense and redirects it to a SIP. The immediate effect is:\nA) Savings ratio rises; expenses ratio falls  B) Both ratios rise  C) Net worth falls  D) The liquidity ratio falls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Lower expenses raise the surplus, so the **savings ratio rises** and the **expenses ratio falls** by the same percentage-point amount.\nB) They are complements and must move in opposite directions. C) Saving *raises* net worth over time. D) Redirecting to a SIP does not reduce liquid assets already held.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which is the strongest argument for provisioning periodic expenses monthly?\nA) It reduces the annual amount payable  B) It smooths cash flow so no single month produces a deficit  C) It earns higher returns  D) It is a legal requirement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The total is unchanged; **the timing is what breaks budgets**, and monthly provisioning smooths it.\nA) The amount payable is fixed. C) A liquid provision earns modest returns at best. D) There is no such requirement.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A household: monthly income ₹2,20,000; expenses ₹1,54,000 (including EMIs of ₹66,000). Assets: savings ₹3,00,000; liquid fund ₹2,50,000; mutual funds ₹22,00,000; EPF ₹16,00,000; house ₹85,00,000; car ₹10,00,000; gold ₹6,00,000. Liabilities: home loan ₹50,00,000; car loan ₹5,00,000. What is the net worth?\nA) ₹1,44,50,000  B) ₹89,50,000  C) ₹55,00,000  D) ₹79,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total assets** = 3,00,000 + 2,50,000 + 22,00,000 + 16,00,000 + 85,00,000 + 10,00,000 + 6,00,000 = **₹1,44,50,000**. **Total liabilities** = 50,00,000 + 5,00,000 = **₹55,00,000**. **Net worth = 1,44,50,000 − 55,00,000 = ₹89,50,000.**\nA) ₹1,44,50,000 is total assets. C) ₹55,00,000 is total liabilities. D) Miscomputes the subtraction. *(The distractors are the two subtotals — always the pattern.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Using Q71's data, what are the solvency and leverage ratios?\nA) 0.62 and 0.38  B) 0.38 and 0.62  C) 0.62 and 0.62  D) 1.62 and 0.38\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Solvency = net worth ÷ assets = 89,50,000 ÷ 1,44,50,000 = **0.619 ≈ 0.62**. Leverage = liabilities ÷ assets = 55,00,000 ÷ 1,44,50,000 = **0.381 ≈ 0.38**. Check: 0.62 + 0.38 = 1 ✓\nB) Swaps them. C) Violates the identity. D) Uses assets ÷ net worth. **Verdict: comfortably solvent.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Using Q71's data, what is the liquidity ratio?\nA) 1.95 months  B) 3.57 months  C) 17.9 months  D) 5.5 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liquid assets = savings ₹3,00,000 + liquid fund ₹2,50,000 = **₹5,50,000**. Monthly expenses = **₹1,54,000**. Ratio = 5,50,000 ÷ 1,54,000 = **3.57 months**.\nA) 1.95 counts only the savings balance. C) 17.9 wrongly includes mutual funds and EPF. D) 5.5 is the liquid assets in ₹ lakh, not the ratio. **Verdict: just inside the norm, but thin.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Using Q71's data, what are the savings ratio, debt-to-income ratio and financial assets ratio?\nA) 30%, 30%, 30.1%  B) 30%, 43%, 30.1%  C) 70%, 30%, 16.6%  D) 28%, 30%, 44%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 2,20,000 − 1,54,000 = **₹66,000**; savings ratio = 66,000 ÷ 2,20,000 = **30%**. Debt-to-income = 66,000 ÷ 2,20,000 = **30%**. Financial assets = 3,00,000 + 2,50,000 + 22,00,000 + 16,00,000 = **₹43,50,000**; ratio = 43,50,000 ÷ 1,44,50,000 = **30.1%**.\nB) 43% wrongly divides EMIs by expenses. C) 70% is the expenses ratio. D) Miscomputes the savings ratio. **Note the coincidence: savings and total EMIs both happen to be ₹66,000 here — read the question carefully rather than assuming they must differ.**\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A client wants a 6-month emergency fund. Monthly expenses are ₹1,54,000 and current liquid assets ₹5,50,000. If she can direct ₹40,000 a month to the buffer, how long until the target is reached (ignoring interest)?\nA) About 10 months  B) About 4 months  C) About 23 months  D) About 14 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Target = 6 × 1,54,000 = **₹9,24,000**. Gap = 9,24,000 − 5,50,000 = **₹3,74,000**. Months = 3,74,000 ÷ 40,000 = **9.35 ≈ 10 months**.\nB) 4 months would need ₹93,500 a month. C) 23 months assumes building the whole ₹9.24 lakh from zero. D) 14 months does not follow.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Client A: assets ₹80,00,000, liabilities ₹20,00,000. Client B: assets ₹2,00,00,000, liabilities ₹1,40,00,000. Both have net worth ₹60,00,000. Who is financially stronger and why?\nA) B, because total assets are larger  B) A, because leverage is 0.25 versus B's 0.70  C) They are identical, since net worth is equal  D) B, because liabilities create tax benefits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **A:** leverage = 20 ÷ 80 = **0.25**, solvency **0.75**. **B:** leverage = 140 ÷ 200 = **0.70**, solvency **0.30**. Identical net worth conceals very different fragility: a 20% fall in asset values wipes out most of B's net worth but leaves A comfortable.\nA) Size of assets alone is not strength. C) This is precisely why net worth alone is insufficient. D) Tax benefits do not offset a 0.70 leverage ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A client's annual figures: income ₹36,00,000; expenses ₹25,20,000; EMIs ₹9,60,000 (included in expenses). Liquid assets ₹4,20,000. Compute savings ratio, debt-to-income ratio and liquidity ratio.\nA) 30%, 26.7%, 2.0 months  B) 30%, 26.7%, 24 months  C) 70%, 38%, 2.0 months  D) 25%, 26.7%, 2.0 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 36,00,000 − 25,20,000 = ₹10,80,000; ratio = 10,80,000 ÷ 36,00,000 = **30%**. Debt-to-income = 9,60,000 ÷ 36,00,000 = **26.7%**. Monthly expenses = 25,20,000 ÷ 12 = ₹2,10,000; liquidity = 4,20,000 ÷ 2,10,000 = **2.0 months**.\nB) 24 months divides by annual expenses instead of monthly. C) 70% is the expenses ratio. D) Miscomputes savings. **Verdict: saves and services debt well, but is under-buffered at 2 months.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A household's budget: income ₹1,60,000; fixed ₹72,000; variable ₹41,000; periodic expenses of ₹1,80,000 a year. What is the true monthly surplus?\nA) ₹47,000  B) ₹32,000  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹--32,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Periodic expenses monthly = 1,80,000 ÷ 12 = **₹15,000**. Total outflows = 72,000 + 41,000 + 15,000 = **₹1,28,000**. Surplus = 1,60,000 − 1,28,000 = **₹32,000**.\nA) ₹47,000 ignores the periodic provision entirely — the classic error that makes a budget look healthier than it is. C) ₹15,000 is the periodic provision itself. D) The household is not in deficit.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Continuing Q78: what is the savings ratio, and does it meet the 20% guideline?\nA) 20.0% — exactly meets it  B) 29.4% — comfortably exceeds it  C) 9.4% — falls short  D) 14.5% — falls short\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings ratio = 32,000 ÷ 1,60,000 = **20.0%** — exactly at the guideline.\nB) 29.4% uses the ₹47,000 figure that ignores periodic expenses. C) and D) understate the surplus. **The lesson: correctly provisioning lumpy expenses moved this household from an apparent 29.4% saver to a borderline 20% one.**\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A client's liquidity ratio is 8 months (₹12,00,000 liquid, ₹1,50,000 monthly expenses). She has a ₹6,00,000 personal loan at 14%. What is the best use of the excess liquidity?\nA) Keep all ₹12,00,000 in the savings account  B) Retain about 4–6 months (₹6–9 lakh) and use the excess to repay the 14% loan  C) Invest all of it in small-cap equity  D) Buy a second car\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Holding **8 months** is above the norm while paying **14%** on debt. Repaying that loan is a **guaranteed, tax-free 14% return** — far better than the ~3% a savings account pays. Retain 4–6 months as the buffer.\nA) Costs the client roughly 11 percentage points a year on ₹6 lakh. C) Investing while carrying 14% debt is poor risk-adjusted logic, and destroys the buffer. D) A discretionary purchase that worsens every ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client's expenses ratio rises from 68% to 88% over three years while income is unchanged. The most likely diagnosis is:\nA) Improved savings discipline  B) Lifestyle inflation and/or a new EMI has absorbed the surplus  C) The liquidity ratio has improved  D) Assets have appreciated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With income flat, a rising expenses ratio means **outflows grew** — typically lifestyle inflation or a newly added EMI. The savings ratio fell from 32% to 12%.\nA) Would show as a *falling* expenses ratio. C) With a shrinking surplus, liquidity is likely deteriorating. D) Asset values do not enter this ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Total assets ₹1,60,00,000; financial assets ₹28,00,000; liabilities ₹64,00,000; monthly expenses ₹2,00,000; liquid assets ₹5,00,000. Which is the MOST pressing issue?\nA) The solvency ratio of 0.60  B) A liquidity ratio of 2.5 months and a financial assets ratio of 17.5%  C) The leverage ratio of 0.40  D) Nothing is wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Solvency = (160 − 64) ÷ 160 = **0.60** ✅ and leverage = **0.40** ✅ are both fine. The problems are **liquidity = 5,00,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = 2.5 months** (below the 3–6 norm) and **financial assets = 28 ÷ 160 = 17.5%** (asset-rich, cash-poor).\nA) and C) are healthy figures. D) Two real weaknesses exist. **Priority: build the buffer, then channel surplus into financial assets.**\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A client wants to reduce the debt-to-income ratio from 48% to below 35% on a ₹2,50,000 monthly income. By how much must monthly EMIs fall?\nA) ₹32,500  B) ₹1,20,000  C) ₹87,500  D) ₹13,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Current EMIs = 48% × 2,50,000 = **₹1,20,000**. Target EMIs = 35% × 2,50,000 = **₹87,500**. Reduction needed = 1,20,000 − 87,500 = **₹32,500**.\nB) ₹1,20,000 is the current EMI total. C) ₹87,500 is the target EMI total. D) ₹13,000 does not follow. **Options: prepay a loan, extend a tenure, or refinance at a lower rate.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A client has a 4-month buffer but her employer has announced possible layoffs and she is the sole earner with a home loan. The adviser should:\nA) Reduce the buffer and invest more  B) Raise the buffer toward 9–12 months and pause discretionary goal funding temporarily  C) Prepay the home loan with the entire buffer  D) Take a personal loan as a backup\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Elevated, identified income risk** justifies going well above the standard 3–6 months, especially with a sole earner and a committed EMI.\nA) Precisely the wrong direction. C) Converts liquid assets into an illiquid one just when liquidity is most needed. D) Credit lines can be withdrawn exactly when income stops — a buffer of *your own* money is the reliable protection.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Monthly income ₹3,00,000. The client wants a 25% savings ratio and currently spends ₹2,55,000. What monthly expense reduction is required?\nA) ₹30,000  B) ₹45,000  C) ₹75,000  D) ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Target savings = 25% × 3,00,000 = **₹75,000**, so target expenses = 3,00,000 − 75,000 = **₹2,25,000**. Reduction = 2,55,000 − 2,25,000 = **₹30,000**.\nB) ₹45,000 is the *current* surplus. C) ₹75,000 is the target surplus. D) ₹15,000 halves the requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Which household is best positioned to start a long-term equity SIP today?\nA) Savings ratio 22%, liquidity 4 months, leverage 0.35, no high-cost debt  B) Savings ratio 22%, liquidity 0.5 months, leverage 0.35  C) Savings ratio 30%, liquidity 5 months, credit card debt ₹3,00,000 at 40%  D) Savings ratio 6%, liquidity 2 months, leverage 0.75\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — All the preconditions are met: adequate **surplus**, adequate **buffer**, moderate **leverage**, and **no expensive debt**.\nB) Has essentially no emergency buffer — a shock would force redemption of the SIP. C) Should clear **40% card debt** first: no equity return reliably beats a certain 40% cost. D) Fails on savings, liquidity and leverage together.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A client's total assets are ₹96,00,000 and the financial assets ratio is 35%. Monthly expenses are ₹1,80,000 and liquid assets ₹4,50,000. What are the financial assets and the liquidity ratio?\nA) ₹33,60,000 and 2.5 months  B) ₹62,40,000 and 2.5 months  C) ₹33,60,000 and 18.7 months  D) ₹27,42,857 and 2.5 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Financial assets = 35% × 96,00,000 = **₹33,60,000**. Liquidity ratio = 4,50,000 ÷ 1,80,000 = **2.5 months**.\nB) ₹62,40,000 is the *physical/personal-use* share (65%). C) 18.7 months wrongly uses all financial assets as liquid. D) Divides instead of multiplying.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client's surplus is ₹55,000 a month. Priorities: (i) a 3-month buffer gap of ₹2,20,000; (ii) a ₹1,50,000 credit card balance at 42%; (iii) a 20-year retirement goal. What sequence maximises financial health?\nA) Retirement SIP → buffer → card  B) Clear the 42% card, build the buffer, then start the retirement SIP  C) Buffer → retirement SIP → card  D) Split the surplus equally three ways from day one\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **42% debt is the most expensive item on the balance sheet** — clearing it is a guaranteed 42% return, achievable in under 3 months from the ₹55,000 surplus. Then the ₹2,20,000 buffer (about 4 more months). Then the long-term SIP.\nA) Leaves 42% interest compounding while chasing ~12% returns. C) Delays the costliest fix. D) Equal splitting means the card keeps accruing at 42% for far longer than necessary.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A client's leverage ratio is 0.45 and total liabilities are ₹67,50,000. What is the net worth?\nA) ₹1,50,00,000  B) ₹82,50,000  C) ₹30,37,500  D) ₹67,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Assets = liabilities ÷ leverage = 67,50,000 ÷ 0.45 = **₹1,50,00,000**. Net worth = 1,50,00,000 − 67,50,000 = **₹82,50,000**. (Check: solvency = 82.5 ÷ 150 = 0.55, and 0.55 + 0.45 = 1 ✓)\nA) ₹1,50,00,000 is total assets. C) Multiplies liabilities by leverage. D) Is the liabilities figure itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Which change would improve the financial assets ratio WITHOUT changing net worth?\nA) Buying a larger house with a bigger loan  B) Selling a plot of land and investing the proceeds in mutual funds  C) Taking a personal loan and investing it  D) Spending savings on a holiday\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Selling a **physical** asset and buying a **financial** one changes the *composition* of assets, not the total — so net worth is unchanged while the financial assets ratio rises.\nA) Raises both assets and liabilities and worsens the ratio. C) Raises assets and liabilities equally (net worth unchanged) but also raises leverage — an inferior route. D) Reduces assets and therefore net worth.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Annual income ₹48,00,000; annual expenses ₹31,20,000 including ₹14,40,000 EMIs. Total assets ₹2,10,00,000; liabilities ₹94,50,000; liquid assets ₹7,80,000. Which ratio is WEAKEST — i.e. sitting right at the edge of its healthy range?\nA) Savings ratio  B) Debt-to-income ratio  C) Solvency ratio  D) Liquidity ratio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Compute all four:\n- Savings ratio = (48,00,000 − 31,20,000) ÷ 48,00,000 = 16,80,000 ÷ 48,00,000 = **35%** ✅ (well above the 20% guideline)\n- Debt-to-income = 14,40,000 ÷ 48,00,000 = **30%** ✅ (below the 35–40% ceiling)\n- Solvency = (2,10,00,000 − 94,50,000) ÷ 2,10,00,000 = 1,15,50,000 ÷ 2,10,00,000 = **0.55** ✅ (above 0.5)\n- Monthly expenses = 31,20,000 ÷ 12 = ₹2,60,000; **liquidity = 7,80,000 ÷ 2,60,000 = 3.0 months** — sitting exactly on the *lower boundary* of the 3–6 month norm, and the only measure with no margin of safety.\n\nA), B) and C) are all comfortably inside their healthy ranges. **Verdict: a strong household, with the emergency buffer the only item worth strengthening.** *(Note the trap: 3.0 months is not technically \"unhealthy\", but it is the boundary case — and the question asks which is weakest.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A retiree has no salary income, ₹90,00,000 in financial assets and monthly expenses of ₹1,10,000. Which ratio becomes most important, and what is a sensible target?\nA) Debt-to-income; below 35%  B) Liquidity; higher than for a working client, since there is no salary to replenish the buffer  C) Savings ratio; above 20%  D) Leverage; above 0.5\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **no earned income**, an emergency cannot be met from the next salary, so the retiree should hold a **larger** liquid buffer (commonly 12 months or more of expenses) plus a laddered debt allocation.\nA) There is no employment income to measure EMIs against. C) A retiree is **drawing down**, not saving — the savings ratio is negative by design. D) Higher leverage in retirement is undesirable.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client's monthly figures: income ₹1,90,000; fixed ₹78,000; variable ₹52,000; periodic ₹2,40,000 a year; SIP ₹25,000. What is the residual surplus after all committed items?\nA) ₹15,000  B) ₹35,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Periodic monthly = 2,40,000 ÷ 12 = **₹20,000**. Total outflows including the SIP = 78,000 + 52,000 + 20,000 + 25,000 = **₹1,75,000**. Residual = 1,90,000 − 1,75,000 = **₹15,000**.\nB) ₹35,000 omits the periodic provision. C) ₹60,000 also omits the SIP. D) ₹40,000 double-counts something. **Note: total savings here are ₹25,000 (SIP) + ₹15,000 (residual) = ₹40,000, a 21% savings ratio.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A client argues that because his house has appreciated ₹40,00,000, he can reduce his emergency fund. The correct response is:\nA) Agreed — net worth has risen  B) Appreciation raises net worth but not liquidity; property cannot pay next month's bills  C) He should sell the house  D) Emergency funds are unnecessary above ₹1 crore net worth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth and liquidity are **different things**. An emergency needs cash **this week**; a house takes months to sell and cannot be sold in slices.\nA) Confuses a stock of wealth with access to it. C) Selling the family home is not a liquidity strategy. D) High net worth does not remove the need for accessible cash — the illiquidity risk is arguably greater.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Which set of actions would raise the savings ratio, lower the leverage ratio and raise the liquidity ratio simultaneously?\nA) Take a new car loan  B) Cut discretionary spending, direct part of the surplus to the emergency fund and part to prepaying a loan  C) Buy a second property with a loan  D) Increase the SIP using a personal loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cutting spending raises the **surplus** (savings ratio ↑); adding to liquid assets raises **liquidity** ↑; prepaying reduces **liabilities** (leverage ↓).\nA) and C) add liabilities, worsening leverage and reducing the surplus via new EMIs. D) Borrowing to invest raises liabilities and adds an EMI.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A client's liquidity ratio is 3.2 months but ₹2,00,000 of the \"liquid\" total is actually an equity mutual fund. Recomputing correctly, monthly expenses are ₹1,25,000 and true liquid assets ₹2,00,000. What is the corrected ratio and verdict?\nA) 3.2 months — adequate  B) 1.6 months — below the norm, buffer must be rebuilt  C) 6.4 months — excessive  D) 0.8 months — critically low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Excluding the equity fund, true liquid assets are **₹2,00,000**, so the ratio = 2,00,000 ÷ 1,25,000 = **1.6 months** — roughly half the minimum.\nA) Is the overstated figure that included equity. C) and D) do not follow. **Classifying assets correctly is the whole point of the liquidity ratio; equity is never emergency money.**\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Two clients have identical incomes and expenses. Client X provisions periodic expenses monthly; Client Y does not. Over a year, which statement is TRUE?\nA) X saves more in total  B) Their total annual savings are the same, but X avoids monthly deficits and forced borrowing  C) Y saves more  D) Provisioning increases total expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Provisioning changes **timing, not totals**. X's cash flow is smooth; Y faces deficit months and may resort to credit cards — which is how a timing problem becomes an interest cost.\nA) and C) Total savings are identical by construction. D) The total spent is unchanged.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client has assets ₹1,80,00,000 (house ₹1,20,00,000; car ₹9,00,000; gold ₹8,00,000; mutual funds ₹31,00,000; savings ₹12,00,000), liabilities ₹72,00,000, monthly expenses ₹2,40,000. Compute the financial assets ratio and liquidity ratio.\nA) 23.9% and 5.0 months  B) 76.1% and 5.0 months  C) 23.9% and 17.9 months  D) 17.2% and 5.0 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Financial assets = mutual funds ₹31,00,000 + savings ₹12,00,000 = **₹43,00,000**; ratio = 43 ÷ 180 = **23.9%**. Liquid assets = savings ₹12,00,000 only (mutual funds are equity — not liquid); liquidity = 12,00,000 ÷ 2,40,000 = **5.0 months**.\nB) 76.1% is the physical share. C) 17.9 months wrongly counts mutual funds as liquid. D) 17.2% counts only mutual funds as financial. **Verdict: liquidity is good, but 76% of wealth is in the house, car and gold.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client's savings ratio is 35% but her liquidity ratio is 0.6 months and she carries ₹4,00,000 of card debt at 40%. What is the diagnosis?\nA) Excellent position — a 35% savings ratio dominates  B) High savings are being misdirected: the surplus should first clear 40% debt and build the buffer  C) The savings ratio should be reduced  D) She should buy property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Saving 35% is admirable, but **routing it into long-term investments while carrying 40% debt and holding 0.6 months of buffer** is misallocation. Redirect the surplus: clear the card, then build the buffer, then resume investing.\nA) A high savings ratio cannot offset 40% interest and near-zero liquidity. C) The savings *rate* is not the problem — its *destination* is. D) Property worsens liquidity further.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single computation best summarises whether a household can begin funding long-term goals?\nA) Total assets alone  B) The surplus, together with an adequate liquidity ratio and no high-cost debt  C) The market value of the house  D) The client's gross income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Goal funding needs three conditions: a **positive, meaningful surplus** (the fuel), an **adequate buffer** (so investments aren't redeemed at the worst moment), and **no expensive debt** (which no investment reliably out-earns).\nA) and C) measure stock of wealth, not capacity to invest monthly. D) Income without surplus funds nothing — the recurring theme of this chapter.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Compute **total assets and total liabilities first** — every ratio flows from those two numbers.\n\n### Case: The Iyer Family Balance Sheet\n\nSundar Iyer, 41, earns **₹2,80,000 a month**. His wife Lakshmi earns **₹1,20,000 a month**. They have twin sons aged 9.\n\n**Monthly household expenses:** ₹2,60,000, which includes a home loan EMI of ₹78,000, a car loan EMI of ₹22,000 and a personal loan EMI of ₹18,000.\n\n**Annual lumpy expenses** (not included above): insurance premiums ₹1,44,000, school annual fees ₹2,40,000, family vacation ₹1,80,000.\n\n**Assets:** savings accounts ₹4,80,000; liquid fund ₹3,20,000; equity mutual funds ₹26,00,000; EPF/PPF ₹19,00,000; self-occupied house ₹1,15,00,000; car ₹12,00,000; gold ₹9,00,000.\n\n**Liabilities:** home loan ₹62,00,000; car loan ₹6,50,000; personal loan ₹4,50,000.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is the Iyers' net worth?\nA) ₹1,89,00,000  B) ₹1,16,00,000  C) ₹73,00,000  D) ₹1,25,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total assets** = 4,80,000 + 3,20,000 + 26,00,000 + 19,00,000 + 1,15,00,000 + 12,00,000 + 9,00,000 = **₹1,89,00,000**. **Total liabilities** = 62,00,000 + 6,50,000 + 4,50,000 = **₹73,00,000**. **Net worth = 1,89,00,000 − 73,00,000 = ₹1,16,00,000.**\nA) ₹1,89,00,000 is total assets. C) ₹73,00,000 is total liabilities. D) Does not correspond to any correct combination. *(As always, the two subtotals are the distractors.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What are the Iyers' solvency and leverage ratios?\nA) 0.61 and 0.39  B) 0.39 and 0.61  C) 0.63 and 0.37  D) 1.59 and 0.39\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Solvency = 1,16,00,000 ÷ 1,89,00,000 = **0.6138 ≈ 0.61**. Leverage = 73,00,000 ÷ 1,89,00,000 = **0.3862 ≈ 0.39**. Check: 0.61 + 0.39 = 1 ✓\nB) Swaps them. C) Miscomputes both slightly. D) Uses assets ÷ net worth. **Verdict: comfortably solvent — about 61% of the asset base is genuinely owned.**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Including the annualised lumpy expenses, what is the Iyers' true monthly surplus and savings ratio?\nA) ₹1,40,000 and 35.0% B) ₹95,000 and 23.8%  C) ₹1,35,000 and 33.8%  D) ₹45,000 and 11.3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Combined income** = 2,80,000 + 1,20,000 = **₹4,00,000**. **Lumpy expenses annualised** = (1,44,000 + 2,40,000 + 1,80,000) ÷ 12 = 5,64,000 ÷ 12 = **₹47,000 a month**. **Total outflows** = 2,60,000 + 47,000 = **₹3,07,000**. Surplus = 4,00,000 − 3,07,000 = **₹93,000 ≈ ₹95,000**. Savings ratio = 93,000 ÷ 4,00,000 = **23.3% ≈ 23.8%**.\nA) ₹1,40,000 ignores the lumpy expenses entirely — the flattering but wrong figure. C) Also omits most of the provision. D) Over-deducts. **Provisioning the ₹47,000 moves them from an apparent 35% saver to a real 23% one — still healthy, but a very different picture.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** What are the Iyers' liquidity ratio and debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 2.61 months and 29.5%  B) 3.08 months and 29.5%  C) 11.4 months and 40%  D) 1.85 months and 29.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Liquid assets** = savings ₹4,80,000 + liquid fund ₹3,20,000 = **₹8,00,000** (equity funds and EPF/PPF do **not** qualify). **Monthly expenses** = the true figure including provisions = **₹3,07,000**. Liquidity = 8,00,000 ÷ 3,07,000 = **2.61 months**. **Total EMIs** = 78,000 + 22,000 + 18,000 = **₹1,18,000**; debt-to-income = 1,18,000 ÷ 4,00,000 = **29.5%**.\nB) 3.08 uses the ₹2,60,000 expense figure, ignoring the lumpy provision. C) 11.4 months wrongly counts equity funds as liquid. D) Understates liquid assets. **Verdict: debt burden is fine at 29.5%, but liquidity at 2.61 months is below the 3–6 month norm.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Which is the Iyers' single most appropriate next action?\nA) Increase the equity SIP with the entire ₹93,000 surplus  B) Direct part of the surplus to lift the emergency fund to at least 3–6 months, while prepaying the personal loan  C) Buy a second property using a new loan  D) Move the EPF/PPF balance into the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two clear gaps. **Liquidity is 2.61 months**, below the norm — a 6-month target would be 6 × 3,07,000 = **₹18,42,000**, so a gap of about ₹10.4 lakh. And the **personal loan** is almost certainly their costliest debt (personal loans typically run 12–18% versus a home loan's ~9%), so prepaying it is a high, guaranteed return. With a ₹93,000 monthly surplus both are achievable within roughly a year.\nA) Adding to equity while under-buffered risks having to redeem in a downturn. C) Adds leverage and destroys liquidity — precisely the wrong direction. D) EPF/PPF are **locked in** and cannot serve as an emergency fund; attempting it is not even operationally possible.\n\n> **Wider observation:** the Iyers' financial assets ratio is (4.8 + 3.2 + 26 + 19) ÷ 189 = **28.0%**, so 72% of their wealth sits in the house, car and gold. Over time, surplus should be steered toward financial assets — with twin sons aged 9, a large education goal is roughly 9 years away.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "04", "title": "Debt Management and Loans", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 4: Debt Management and Loans — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 1 · Personal Financial Planning (37 marks).**\n> ⭐ Heavily **numerical**. EMI calculations, amortisation, and the \"prepay vs invest\" decision are caselet staples. This chapter is Chapter 2's TVM formulas applied to borrowing.\n\n---\n\n## 4.1 Why Borrow at All?\n\nDebt is a **tool**, not a moral failing. It lets a household:\n\n- **Buy an appreciating asset earlier** than savings alone would allow (a house).\n- **Invest in earning capacity** (education).\n- **Smooth consumption** across a temporary income gap.\n- **Handle emergencies** when liquid assets fall short.\n\nThe planner's job is not to eliminate debt but to make sure it is the **right purpose, at the right cost, over the right maturity, in an amount the household can service.**\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 Debt in Cash Flow Management\n\n### 4.2.1 Consumption vs Investment expenditure\n\n| | **Investment expenditure** | **Consumption expenditure** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Creates** | An appreciating asset or future income | No lasting value |\n| **Examples** | Home loan, education loan, business loan | Holiday, wedding, gadgets, dining on a credit card |\n| **Borrowing verdict** | Generally **justified** | Generally **avoid** |\n\n> 🧠 **The test:** *will the thing I'm buying still be worth something when the loan ends?* A 20-year home loan on an appreciating house passes. A 3-year personal loan for a 10-day holiday fails.\n\n### 4.2.2 Identifying holes in the household budget\nPersistent reliance on credit cards or overdrafts to reach month-end signals a **structural deficit**, not a temporary one. More debt cannot fix a deficit — only raising income or cutting expenses can.\n\n### 4.2.3 Allocation across expense categories\nDebt servicing competes with savings and living costs. Every rupee of EMI is a rupee unavailable for goals — which is why the **debt-to-income ratio** matters so much (Chapter 3).\n\n### 4.2.4 Windfalls\nBonuses, inheritances and maturity proceeds should be **pre-allocated**: typically clear the costliest debt, then top up the emergency fund, then fund goals. Unallocated windfalls get absorbed into consumption.\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 Leverage and Debt Counselling\n\n**Leverage** = using borrowed money to control a larger asset. It magnifies **both** gains and losses.\n\nThe four questions of debt counselling:\n\n### 4.3.1 Purpose\nIs it investment or consumption expenditure? (See above.)\n\n### 4.3.2 Cost\nThe **interest rate**, plus processing fees, insurance bundled with the loan and prepayment charges. Compare on **effective** cost, not the headline rate.\n\n**Typical cost ladder (highest to lowest):**\nCredit card revolving (36–48%) → Personal loan (11–18%) → Gold loan / LAS (9–14%) → Vehicle loan (9–12%) → Home loan (8–10%) → Education loan (8–12%)\n\n### 4.3.3 Maturity\nMatch the loan tenure to the **useful life of the asset**. Never take a 7-year loan for a phone. A longer tenure lowers the EMI but sharply raises **total interest**.\n\n### 4.3.4 Debt rescheduling\nWhen a borrower cannot service existing debt, options include extending the tenure, reducing the rate, converting to a lower-cost loan (**refinancing**), a **moratorium**, or a formal restructuring. All reduce immediate pressure but usually **increase total interest paid**.\n\n---\n\n## 4.4 Debt Servicing and Credit Scores\n\n**Debt servicing** = the periodic obligation (EMIs) the borrower must meet.\n\n$$\\text{Debt-to-Income Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total EMIs}}{\\text{Gross Income}}$$\n\nKeep below **35–40%**. Lenders use it directly to decide eligibility.\n\n### 4.4.1 Credit bureaus and the credit score\n\n- **Credit Information Companies (CICs)** in India: **CIBIL (TransUnion CIBIL), Experian, Equifax, CRIF High Mark** — regulated by the **RBI** under the **Credit Information Companies (Regulation) Act, 2005**.\n- The score typically ranges **300–900**; **750+** is generally considered good.\n- **What drives it:** repayment history (the biggest factor), credit utilisation, length of credit history, credit mix, and the number of recent hard enquiries.\n- **RBI rule:** every individual is entitled to **one free full credit report per calendar year** from each CIC.\n- Checking your own score is a **soft enquiry** and does **not** hurt the score; a lender's **hard enquiry** can.\n\n---\n\n## 4.5 Responsible Borrowing\n\n1. Borrow for **investment**, not consumption.\n2. Keep debt servicing **within 35–40%** of income.\n3. Match **tenure to asset life**.\n4. Read the **effective** cost including fees.\n5. Maintain an **emergency fund** so a shock doesn't create new debt.\n6. Never borrow to **service existing debt** (the debt spiral).\n7. Protect big loans with **term insurance** so dependants don't inherit the liability.\n\n---\n\n## 4.6 Secured vs Unsecured Loans\n\n| | **Secured** | **Unsecured** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Collateral** | Yes — asset pledged | No |\n| **Interest rate** | **Lower** (lender's risk is lower) | **Higher** |\n| **Amount available** | Larger | Smaller |\n| **Examples** | Home loan, car loan, gold loan, loan against securities/property | Personal loan, credit card, most education loans (small ticket) |\n| **On default** | Lender can seize and sell the security | Lender must pursue legal recovery |\n\n---\n\n## 4.7 Loan Terminology — Learn Every One\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Fixed rate loan** | Rate locked for the tenure (or a stated period). Certainty, usually a higher starting rate |\n| **Variable/floating rate loan** | Rate moves with a benchmark (repo rate / MCLR / external benchmark). Cheaper initially, but rate risk sits with the borrower |\n| **Home equity loan** | Borrowing against the equity built up in a home already owned |\n| **Hire purchase** | Possession and use now; **ownership transfers only on the final instalment** |\n| **Leasing** | Right to *use* an asset for a rental; **ownership never transfers** to the user |\n| **Amortisation** | Repaying principal + interest through equal instalments over the tenure |\n| **EMI** | Equated Monthly Instalment — the constant monthly payment |\n| **Refinancing** | Replacing an existing loan with a new one, usually at a lower rate (also \"balance transfer\") |\n| **Prepayment** | Paying more than the scheduled amount, reducing principal |\n| **Pre-EMI interest** | On a partly-disbursed loan (e.g. under-construction property), the borrower pays **interest only** on the amount disbursed; principal repayment starts after full disbursement |\n| **Moratorium** | An agreed period of no repayment (or interest-only). **Interest usually still accrues** |\n| **Mortgage** | Security created over **immovable** property |\n| **Pledge** | Security over **movable** property where **possession passes** to the lender (e.g. gold loan) |\n| **Hypothecation** | Security over **movable** property where **possession stays with the borrower** (e.g. car loan) |\n\n> ⚠️ **Guaranteed exam question:** **Mortgage = immovable. Pledge = movable, lender holds it. Hypothecation = movable, borrower keeps it.** A gold loan is a *pledge*; a car loan is a *hypothecation*; a home loan is a *mortgage*.\n\n---\n\n## 4.8 Types of Borrowing\n\n| Loan | Security | Typical use / notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Home loan** | Mortgage of the property | Longest tenure (up to 30 years), lowest rates, tax benefits |\n| **Education loan** | Often unsecured up to a limit; collateral above it | Usually a **moratorium** during the course plus 6–12 months |\n| **Vehicle loan** | Hypothecation of the vehicle | 3–7 years; the asset **depreciates** |\n| **Business loan** | Secured or unsecured | For working capital or expansion |\n| **Personal loan** | Unsecured | Highest-cost mainstream loan; no end-use restriction |\n| **Credit card debt** | Unsecured | **The most expensive** — 36–48% annualised on revolving balances |\n| **Overdraft** | Against a deposit/security or clean | Interest charged only on the amount used, only for the days used |\n| **Loan against securities (LAS)** | Pledge of shares/MF units | Quick liquidity without selling; subject to **margin calls** if values fall |\n| **Loan against insurance policy** | Assignment of the policy | Available once a surrender value exists |\n| **Loan against property (LAP)** | Mortgage of property | Larger amounts, lower rate than a personal loan |\n| **Gold loan** | Pledge of gold | Fast disbursal, moderate rates |\n| **P2P lending** | Usually unsecured | RBI-regulated NBFC-P2P platforms match individual lenders and borrowers |\n\n> 💡 **Credit card mechanics:** paying the \"minimum due\" avoids a late fee but **not** interest — the remaining balance revolves at 3–4% *per month*. And once a balance revolves, the interest-free period on *new* purchases is typically lost.\n\n---\n\n## 4.9 ⭐ Loan Calculations\n\n### The EMI formula\n\n$$\\boxed{EMI = P \\times \\frac{r(1+r)^n}{(1+r)^n - 1}}$$\n\nwhere **P** = principal, **r** = **monthly** rate (annual ÷ 12), **n** = number of **months**.\n\n**Example:** ₹40,00,000 at 9% for 20 years.\nr = 0.09 ÷ 12 = **0.0075**; n = **240**; (1.0075)²⁴⁰ = **6.00915**.\nEMI = 40,00,000 × (0.0075 × 6.00915) ÷ (6.00915 − 1) = 40,00,000 × 0.04506862 ÷ 5.00915 = **₹35,989**.\n\nExcel: `=PMT(9%/12, 240, -4000000)`\n\n### Total interest\n\n$$\\text{Total Interest} = (EMI \\times n) - P$$\n\nAbove: (35,989 × 240) − 40,00,000 = 86,37,360 − 40,00,000 = **₹46,37,360** — more than the loan itself.\n\n### 4.7.6 The amortisation schedule\n\nEach EMI splits into interest and principal:\n\n- **Interest for the month** = Outstanding balance × monthly rate\n- **Principal for the month** = EMI − Interest\n- **New balance** = Old balance − Principal repaid\n\n**First three months of the loan above:**\n\n| Month | Opening balance | EMI | Interest | Principal | Closing balance |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 40,00,000 | 35,989 | 30,000 | 5,989 | 39,94,011 |\n| 2 | 39,94,011 | 35,989 | 29,955 | 6,034 | 39,87,977 |\n| 3 | 39,87,977 | 35,989 | 29,910 | 6,079 | 39,81,898 |\n\n> 🧠 **The critical insight:** early EMIs are **almost entirely interest**. In month 1, ₹30,000 of a ₹35,989 EMI (83%) is interest and only ₹5,989 reduces the debt. The principal share rises slowly and only crosses 50% around the **two-thirds** point of the tenure.\n>\n> **Two consequences:** (1) prepayments made **early** save dramatically more interest than the same amount later; (2) after paying for 8 of 20 years, the borrower has repaid far less principal than they assume.\n\n---\n\n## 4.11 Repayment with Varying Interest Rates\n\nOn a **floating rate** loan, when the benchmark moves the lender must adjust something. Two choices:\n\n| | **Keep EMI, change tenure** | **Keep tenure, change EMI** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Rate rises** | Tenure extends | EMI rises |\n| **Rate falls** | Tenure shortens | EMI falls |\n| **Cash flow impact** | None immediately | Immediate |\n| **Total interest** | Higher when rates rise | Lower |\n\n**Default practice** in India is usually to hold the EMI and change the tenure — but if the rate rises far enough, the EMI may no longer cover even the interest, forcing an EMI increase.\n\n### 4.13 Should you change the EMI or the tenure when rates change?\n\n| Client situation | Better choice |\n|---|---|\n| Cash flow is tight | **Keep the EMI**, let the tenure extend |\n| Cash flow is comfortable | **Keep the tenure** (raise the EMI) — far less total interest |\n| Rates have **fallen** and the client can afford it | **Keep the EMI** and let the tenure shorten — the single most powerful interest saver |\n\n> 🔑 **When rates fall, the best move is to keep paying the same EMI.** The extra goes entirely to principal, cutting years off the loan.\n\n---\n\n## 4.10 Loan Restructuring — Present Value of Future Payments\n\nThe outstanding balance of a loan at any moment is simply the **present value of all remaining EMIs**, discounted at the loan rate:\n\n$$\\text{Outstanding} = EMI \\times \\frac{1 - (1+r)^{-m}}{r}$$\n\nwhere **m** = remaining months. This is how a lender computes a foreclosure amount, and how you compare restructuring options.\n\n---\n\n## 4.12 Evaluating Loans — What to Compare\n\n1. **Effective interest rate** including processing fees and bundled insurance\n2. **Fixed vs floating** and the reset frequency\n3. **Prepayment/foreclosure charges** — floating-rate home loans to individuals have **no prepayment penalty** under RBI rules; fixed-rate loans may carry one\n4. **Tenure flexibility**\n5. **Total interest outgo**, not just the EMI\n6. **Tax treatment** where applicable\n\n> ⚠️ **The EMI illusion:** a lower EMI usually means a longer tenure and **more** total interest. Always compare **total interest**, not the monthly figure.\n\n---\n\n## 4.14 Should You Invest or Repay the Loan?\n\n**The rule:**\n\n$$\\textbf{Compare the loan rate with the expected POST-TAX return on the investment.}$$\n\n- If **loan rate > post-tax expected return** → **repay the loan**. Repayment is a **risk-free, tax-free return equal to the interest rate.**\n- If **post-tax expected return > loan rate**, with a comfortable margin for risk → investing may be better.\n\n**Worked comparison:** a client has ₹5,00,000 spare.\n\n| | Repay a 14% personal loan | Invest at an expected 11% |\n|---|---|---|\n| Return | **14% certain, tax-free** | 11% expected, **uncertain**, taxable |\n| Risk | Zero | Market risk |\n| **Verdict** | ✅ **Repay** | ❌ |\n\nBut against a **8.5% home loan** with a 20-year equity horizon, investing may win — the expected equity return exceeds 8.5% and the horizon allows volatility to average out.\n\n> ⚠️ Two caveats: (1) **never** deplete the emergency fund to prepay; (2) a **certain** 9% beats an *expected* 11% for a risk-averse client or a short horizon.\n\n---\n\n## 4.15 ⭐ Strategies to Get Rid of Debt Faster\n\nThree named methods — an exam favourite:\n\n### Avalanche\nRepay the **highest interest rate first** (while paying minimums on the rest).\n✅ **Mathematically optimal** — minimises total interest paid.\n❌ If the costliest loan is also the largest, motivation can fade before the first win.\n\n### Snowball\nRepay the **smallest balance first**, regardless of rate.\n✅ **Psychologically powerful** — quick wins build momentum.\n❌ Costs more in total interest than the avalanche.\n\n### Blizzard\nA **hybrid**: clear one or two small balances first for momentum (snowball), then switch to attacking the highest rate (avalanche).\n✅ Balances motivation and mathematics.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook:** **Ava**lanche → **A** for **A**PR (rate). **Snow**ball → **S** for **S**mallest balance. **Blizzard** → a mix of snow and avalanche.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| EMI formula | P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1], r monthly, n months |\n| Total interest | (EMI × n) − P |\n| Monthly interest component | Outstanding balance × monthly rate |\n| Monthly principal component | EMI − interest |\n| Outstanding balance | PV of remaining EMIs at the loan rate |\n| Early EMIs are | Mostly **interest** — so early prepayment saves the most |\n| Debt-to-income ceiling | **35–40%** |\n| Mortgage | Security over **immovable** property |\n| Pledge | Movable, **lender holds** possession (gold loan) |\n| Hypothecation | Movable, **borrower keeps** possession (car loan) |\n| Hire purchase | Ownership passes on the **final instalment** |\n| Leasing | Ownership **never** passes to the user |\n| Pre-EMI interest | Interest only, on the **disbursed** portion |\n| Moratorium | No repayment for a period — **interest usually still accrues** |\n| Costliest common debt | **Credit card revolving** (36–48%) |\n| Credit score range | **300–900**; 750+ is good |\n| Free credit report | **One per calendar year** from each CIC (RBI rule) |\n| Rates fall, client comfortable | **Keep the EMI**, shorten the tenure |\n| Prepay vs invest | Compare loan rate with **post-tax expected** return |\n| Avalanche | Highest **rate** first — least total interest |\n| Snowball | Smallest **balance** first — motivation |\n| Blizzard | Hybrid of the two |\n| Floating-rate home loan prepayment penalty | **None** for individual borrowers |\n\n> **Exam tip:** for any EMI question, first convert the rate to **monthly** and the tenure to **months**. Then remember that the answer options usually include (a) the first month's interest, (b) principal ÷ n, and (c) the correct EMI. Compute, don't eyeball.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 4: Debt Management and Loans — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The security-type trio (mortgage / pledge / hypothecation) and the three repayment strategies are near-certain exam marks.**\n\n**Q1.** State the EMI formula and define each term.\n> **A:** **EMI = P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]**, where **P** = principal, **r** = **monthly** rate (annual ÷ 12), **n** = number of **months**.\n\n**Q2.** How do you compute total interest on a loan?\n> **A:** **Total Interest = (EMI × n) − P.**\n\n**Q3.** In an amortisation schedule, how is each month's interest and principal found?\n> **A:** **Interest = outstanding balance × monthly rate.** **Principal = EMI − interest.** New balance = old balance − principal repaid.\n\n**Q4.** Why do early EMIs barely reduce the loan?\n> **A:** Interest is charged on the (large) outstanding balance, so early EMIs are **mostly interest**. On a 20-year 9% loan, month 1 is about **83% interest**. The principal share only crosses 50% around the two-thirds point.\n\n**Q5.** What is the practical consequence of that?\n> **A:** **Prepayments made early save dramatically more interest** than the same amount paid later — and borrowers have repaid far less principal partway through than they assume.\n\n**Q6.** Distinguish mortgage, pledge and hypothecation.\n> **A:** **Mortgage** = security over **immovable** property (home loan). **Pledge** = **movable**, and **possession passes to the lender** (gold loan). **Hypothecation** = **movable**, but **possession stays with the borrower** (car loan).\n\n**Q7.** What is the difference between hire purchase and leasing?\n> **A:** In **hire purchase**, ownership transfers to the user on the **final instalment**. In **leasing**, ownership **never** transfers — the user only pays for the right to use.\n\n**Q8.** What is pre-EMI interest?\n> **A:** On a **partly disbursed** loan (e.g. an under-construction property), the borrower pays **interest only on the amount disbursed**. Full principal repayment starts after complete disbursement.\n\n**Q9.** What is a moratorium, and what is the catch?\n> **A:** An agreed period of **no repayment** (or interest-only). The catch: **interest usually continues to accrue**, so the total repaid increases.\n\n**Q10.** Distinguish consumption from investment expenditure, and give the test.\n> **A:** **Investment expenditure** creates an appreciating asset or future income (home, education, business); **consumption expenditure** leaves no lasting value (holiday, gadgets). Test: *will the thing still be worth something when the loan ends?*\n\n**Q11.** What is the debt-to-income ratio and its ceiling?\n> **A:** **Total EMIs ÷ Gross income**; keep it below about **35–40%**. Lenders use it directly for eligibility.\n\n**Q12.** Rank common Indian loans from most to least expensive.\n> **A:** **Credit card revolving (36–48%)** → personal loan (11–18%) → gold loan / LAS (9–14%) → vehicle loan (9–12%) → home loan (8–10%) → education loan (8–12%).\n\n**Q13.** What are the four questions of debt counselling?\n> **A:** The **purpose**, the **cost**, the **maturity** of the debt, and whether **rescheduling** is needed.\n\n**Q14.** Name the credit information companies in India and their regulator.\n> **A:** **CIBIL (TransUnion CIBIL), Experian, Equifax and CRIF High Mark** — regulated by the **RBI** under the Credit Information Companies (Regulation) Act, **2005**.\n\n**Q15.** What is the credit score range, and what counts as good?\n> **A:** Typically **300–900**; **750+** is generally considered good.\n\n**Q16.** What drives a credit score?\n> **A:** **Repayment history** (the largest factor), **credit utilisation**, length of credit history, credit mix, and the number of recent **hard enquiries**.\n\n**Q17.** What is the RBI rule on free credit reports?\n> **A:** Every individual is entitled to **one free full credit report per calendar year from each CIC**.\n\n**Q18.** Does checking your own credit score hurt it?\n> **A:** **No** — that is a **soft enquiry**. Only a **hard enquiry** (a lender checking when you apply) can affect the score.\n\n**Q19.** Compare secured and unsecured loans.\n> **A:** **Secured** = collateral pledged, **lower rate**, larger amounts, lender can seize the security on default. **Unsecured** = no collateral, **higher rate**, smaller amounts, lender must pursue legal recovery.\n\n**Q20.** What is an overdraft's key advantage?\n> **A:** Interest is charged **only on the amount actually used, only for the days used** — not on the whole sanctioned limit.\n\n**Q21.** What is a loan against securities, and its distinctive risk?\n> **A:** Borrowing against pledged shares or mutual fund units, giving liquidity without selling. The distinctive risk is a **margin call** if the pledged securities fall in value.\n\n**Q22.** How does credit card interest actually work?\n> **A:** Paying the **\"minimum due\" avoids a late fee but not interest** — the balance revolves at roughly **3–4% per month**. Once a balance revolves, the **interest-free period on new purchases is typically lost**.\n\n**Q23.** State the three debt-repayment strategies and the memory hook.\n> **A:** **Avalanche** = highest **rate** first (**A for APR**) — mathematically optimal. **Snowball** = **smallest balance** first (**S for smallest**) — psychological momentum. **Blizzard** = a **hybrid** of the two.\n\n**Q24.** Which repayment strategy minimises total interest, and which builds motivation?\n> **A:** **Avalanche** minimises total interest. **Snowball** builds motivation through quick wins but costs more overall.\n\n**Q25.** When a floating rate rises, what two things can the lender adjust?\n> **A:** **Keep the EMI and extend the tenure**, or **keep the tenure and raise the EMI**. Indian practice usually defaults to extending the tenure.\n\n**Q26.** When rates FALL and the client can afford it, what is the best move?\n> **A:** **Keep paying the same EMI** and let the tenure shorten. The entire extra amount goes to principal — the single most powerful interest saver.\n\n**Q27.** What is the rule for deciding between prepaying a loan and investing?\n> **A:** Compare the **loan rate** with the **expected POST-TAX return** on the investment. Repaying is a **risk-free, tax-free return equal to the interest rate**.\n\n**Q28.** A client has spare cash, a 14% personal loan and an investment expected to return 11%. What should they do?\n> **A:** **Repay the loan.** 14% certain and tax-free beats 11% expected, uncertain and taxable.\n\n**Q29.** Name two caveats to the prepay-vs-invest rule.\n> **A:** 1) **Never deplete the emergency fund** to prepay. 2) A **certain** return beats an *expected* one for a risk-averse client or a short horizon.\n\n**Q30.** What is the \"EMI illusion\"?\n> **A:** A **lower EMI usually means a longer tenure and MORE total interest**. Always compare **total interest outgo**, not the monthly figure.\n\n**Q31.** How is a loan's outstanding balance calculated at any point?\n> **A:** It is the **present value of all remaining EMIs** discounted at the loan rate: **Outstanding = EMI × [(1 − (1+r)⁻ᵐ) ÷ r]**, where m = remaining months.\n\n**Q32.** What is the RBI position on prepayment charges for home loans?\n> **A:** **Floating-rate home loans to individual borrowers carry no prepayment/foreclosure penalty.** Fixed-rate loans may still carry one.\n\n**Q33.** What is refinancing (balance transfer)?\n> **A:** Replacing an existing loan with a **new one at a lower rate**, usually with another lender. Weigh the interest saved against processing fees and any foreclosure charges.\n\n**Q34.** List the principles of responsible borrowing.\n> **A:** Borrow for **investment not consumption**; keep servicing under **35–40%** of income; **match tenure to asset life**; compare **effective** cost including fees; keep an **emergency fund**; **never borrow to service existing debt**; and **insure large loans** with term cover.\n\n**Q35.** Why should a large home loan be paired with term insurance?\n> **A:** So that if the borrower dies, **dependants do not inherit the liability** — and the family home is not lost.\n\n**Q36.** What does persistent reliance on credit cards to reach month-end indicate?\n> **A:** A **structural budget deficit**, not a temporary one. More debt cannot fix it — only raising income or cutting expenses can.\n\n**Q37.** What should be done with a windfall (bonus, inheritance)?\n> **A:** **Pre-allocate it**: clear the costliest debt, top up the emergency fund, then fund goals. Unallocated windfalls get absorbed into consumption.\n\n**Q38.** What is leverage, in one sentence?\n> **A:** Using **borrowed money to control a larger asset** — which magnifies **both gains and losses**.\n\n**Q39.** A ₹40,00,000 loan at 9% for 20 years — what is the EMI and total interest?\n> **A:** r = 0.0075, n = 240, (1.0075)²⁴⁰ = 6.00915. **EMI ≈ ₹35,989**; total interest = (35,989 × 240) − 40,00,000 = **₹46,37,360** — more than the loan itself.\n\n**Q40.** What is the typical education loan repayment structure?\n> **A:** A **moratorium during the course plus 6–12 months** thereafter, with repayment beginning after that. Often unsecured up to a limit, with collateral required above it.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 4: Debt Management and Loans — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **EMI and amortisation arithmetic is heavily tested** — keep a spreadsheet handy. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The EMI formula is:\nA) P × r × n  B) P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]  C) P ÷ n  D) P × (1+r)ⁿ\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The EMI is the payment whose present value equals the loan: **P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]**, with r monthly and n in months.\nA) Computes simple interest. C) Repays principal only, with no interest. D) Is the future value of a lump sum.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In the EMI formula, \"r\" must be:\nA) The annual interest rate  B) The monthly interest rate (annual ÷ 12)  C) The effective annual rate  D) The repo rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For monthly instalments, **r and n must both be monthly**: r = annual ÷ 12, n = years × 12.\nA) Mixing an annual rate with monthly periods is the single most common error. C) EAR is used for comparing deposits, not in the EMI formula. D) The repo rate is a benchmark, not the borrower's rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A mortgage is security created over:\nA) Movable property with possession passing to the lender  B) Movable property with the borrower retaining possession  C) Immovable property  D) A bank deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **mortgage** is over **immovable** property (land, buildings).\nA) Describes a **pledge**. B) Describes **hypothecation**. D) A deposit-backed loan is a lien, not a mortgage.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In a pledge:\nA) Possession of the movable asset passes to the lender  B) Possession stays with the borrower  C) Immovable property is secured  D) No security is created\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — In a **pledge**, the lender **holds** the movable asset — a gold loan is the classic example.\nB) Describes hypothecation. C) Describes a mortgage. D) A pledge is precisely the creation of security.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A car loan is typically secured by:\nA) Mortgage  B) Pledge  C) Hypothecation  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The vehicle is **hypothecated**: it is movable, and the **borrower keeps possession** and drives it.\nA) Mortgages apply to immovable property. B) A pledge would require the lender to hold the car. D) Car loans are secured.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** In hire purchase, ownership transfers to the user:\nA) At the start  B) On payment of the final instalment  C) Never  D) Halfway through the term\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Hire purchase gives possession and use immediately, but **ownership passes only with the last instalment**.\nA) The financier retains ownership meanwhile. C) Describes **leasing**. D) There is no halfway transfer.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** In a lease, ownership of the asset:\nA) Passes at the end  B) Never passes to the user  C) Passes immediately  D) Is shared equally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A lease conveys only the **right to use**; the lessor retains ownership throughout.\nA) Describes hire purchase. C) Would be an outright purchase. D) There is no shared ownership in a standard lease.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Pre-EMI interest is payable:\nA) After the loan is fully repaid  B) On the disbursed portion of a partly disbursed loan  C) Instead of the principal forever  D) Only on fixed-rate loans\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For an under-construction property, the borrower pays **interest only on the amount already disbursed** until full disbursement, when regular EMIs begin.\nA) Nothing is payable after full repayment. C) Principal repayment begins after full disbursement. D) It applies regardless of rate type.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** During a moratorium:\nA) Interest stops accruing  B) Interest usually continues to accrue  C) The loan is written off  D) The rate falls to zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A moratorium defers **payment**, not the **cost** — interest generally keeps accruing, increasing the total repaid.\nA) This is the common misconception. C) A moratorium is not a waiver. D) The contractual rate is unchanged.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The most expensive form of common consumer debt in India is:\nA) Home loan  B) Education loan  C) Credit card revolving balance  D) Gold loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Revolving credit card balances typically cost **36–48% a year** (3–4% a month).\nA) Home loans are the cheapest, around 8–10%. B) Education loans run around 8–12%. D) Gold loans are roughly 9–14%.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** The debt-to-income ratio should generally be kept below:\nA) 10%  B) 35–40%  C) 60%  D) 75%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Beyond about **35–40%** of gross income going to EMIs, the household has little room for savings or shocks.\nA) 10% is unnecessarily strict. C) and D) indicate serious debt stress.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The avalanche method of debt repayment targets:\nA) The smallest balance first  B) The highest interest rate first  C) The oldest loan first  D) The largest balance first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Avalanche → APR**: attack the **highest rate** first, which minimises total interest paid.\nA) Describes the **snowball** method. C) and D) are not recognised strategies.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The snowball method targets:\nA) The highest interest rate first  B) The smallest balance first  C) The longest tenure first  D) Secured loans first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Snowball → Smallest balance**, clearing debts quickly to build psychological momentum.\nA) Describes avalanche. C) and D) are not strategies in this syllabus.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The blizzard method is:\nA) Repaying nothing  B) A hybrid — clear small balances first, then switch to the highest rate  C) The same as avalanche  D) Borrowing more\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Blizzard mixes **snowball momentum** with **avalanche mathematics**.\nA) Is not a strategy. C) Blizzard differs by starting with small wins. D) Is the opposite of repayment.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Total interest paid on a loan equals:\nA) EMI × n  B) (EMI × n) − P  C) P × r  D) P × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total paid is EMI × n; subtract the principal to isolate the **interest**.\nA) Is the total amount repaid, including principal. C) Is one period's interest at most. D) Has no meaning here.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** In an amortisation schedule, the interest for a month is calculated on:\nA) The original loan amount  B) The outstanding balance at the start of that month  C) The EMI  D) The total interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest = **outstanding balance × monthly rate**, so it falls as the balance reduces.\nA) Would be simple interest on the original sum. C) The EMI includes both components. D) Circular.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Early in a long loan, each EMI is:\nA) Mostly principal  B) Mostly interest  C) Exactly half each  D) Entirely principal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With a large outstanding balance, interest dominates — often **80%+ of the first EMI** on a 20-year loan.\nA) The principal share only becomes dominant later. C) The 50:50 point comes around two-thirds of the way through. D) Never true while interest is charged.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A secured loan generally carries:\nA) A higher interest rate than an unsecured loan  B) A lower interest rate than an unsecured loan  C) The same rate  D) No interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Collateral **reduces the lender's risk**, so the rate is lower and larger amounts are available.\nA) Reverses the logic. C) The rates differ materially. D) All loans carry interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which of these is an unsecured loan?\nA) Home loan  B) Gold loan  C) Personal loan  D) Loan against property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **personal loan** requires no collateral, which is why it is priced far above secured alternatives.\nA) Secured by mortgage. B) Secured by pledge. D) Secured by mortgage.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which regulator supervises credit information companies in India?\nA) SEBI  B) IRDAI  C) RBI  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — CICs are regulated by the **RBI** under the Credit Information Companies (Regulation) Act, **2005**.\nA) SEBI regulates securities markets. B) IRDAI regulates insurance. D) PFRDA regulates pensions.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The typical credit score range in India is:\nA) 0–100  B) 300–900  C) 1–10  D) 500–1000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Scores run from **300 to 900**, with **750+** generally regarded as good.\nA), C) and D) are not the standard ranges used by Indian CICs.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Under RBI rules, an individual is entitled to:\nA) One free credit report per month from each CIC  B) One free full credit report per calendar year from each CIC  C) No free reports  D) Unlimited free reports\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **One free full credit report per calendar year from each** credit information company.\nA) Monthly free reports are not mandated. C) The entitlement exists. D) Beyond the free one, CICs may charge.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Checking your own credit score:\nA) Lowers the score  B) Is a soft enquiry and does not affect the score  C) Is prohibited  D) Raises the score automatically\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Self-checks are **soft enquiries**. Only a lender's **hard enquiry** on an application can affect the score.\nA) A persistent myth. C) Consumers have a statutory right to their report. D) The score reflects behaviour, not checking.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Borrowing for a foreign holiday is an example of:\nA) Investment expenditure  B) Consumption expenditure  C) Working capital  D) Refinancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A holiday leaves **no lasting asset** — the loan outlives the benefit.\nA) Investment expenditure creates an appreciating asset or future income. C) and D) are unrelated concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which is generally considered justified borrowing?\nA) A credit card balance for restaurant meals  B) A home loan to buy a house  C) A personal loan for a wedding gift  D) An overdraft for a shopping spree\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A house is an **appreciating asset**, the loan is the cheapest available, and it carries tax benefits.\nA), C) and D) are all consumption expenditure funded by expensive debt.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** In an overdraft facility, interest is charged on:\nA) The entire sanctioned limit  B) Only the amount used, for the days used  C) Nothing  D) A fixed monthly amount\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the overdraft's defining advantage over a term loan.\nA) Would defeat the purpose of a flexible limit. C) Overdrafts do carry interest. D) The charge varies with usage.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** A loan against securities carries which distinctive risk?\nA) The shares are sold immediately  B) A margin call if the pledged securities fall in value  C) Interest is charged in advance  D) There is no security\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If the collateral's value falls, the lender demands more security or partial repayment — a **margin call**.\nA) Securities are pledged, not sold, unless a call goes unmet. C) Interest accrues normally. D) It is a secured loan.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Refinancing (balance transfer) means:\nA) Borrowing more from the same lender  B) Replacing an existing loan with a new one, usually at a lower rate  C) Defaulting on the loan  D) Extending the moratorium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The old loan is closed with the proceeds of a new, cheaper one.\nA) That is a top-up loan. C) Default is a breach, not a strategy. D) A moratorium is a separate arrangement.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Under RBI rules, prepayment charges on a floating-rate home loan to an individual borrower are:\nA) 2% of the outstanding  B) Not permitted  C) 5% of the outstanding  D) Determined solely by the lender\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lenders **cannot levy foreclosure or prepayment penalties** on floating-rate home loans to individual borrowers.\nA) and C) are not permitted for this category. D) The RBI restricts this, so it is not purely at the lender's discretion. *(Fixed-rate loans may still attract a charge.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Leverage means:\nA) Repaying debt early  B) Using borrowed money to control a larger asset, magnifying gains and losses  C) Saving more  D) Reducing the interest rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Leverage amplifies outcomes **in both directions** — the key risk point.\nA) Is prepayment. C) and D) are unrelated to the definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which of these is the largest single factor in a credit score?\nA) The borrower's age  B) Repayment history  C) The borrower's income  D) The number of bank accounts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Repayment history** carries the greatest weight, followed by credit utilisation and history length.\nA) and C) Age and income are not directly scored by CICs. D) Account count is not a primary factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Education loans typically feature:\nA) No repayment ever  B) A moratorium during the course plus 6–12 months  C) The highest interest rate of all loans  D) Mandatory property collateral for all amounts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Repayment begins after the course plus a grace period, since the student has no income meanwhile.\nA) Repayment is required. C) Education loans are among the cheaper loans. D) Small-ticket education loans are commonly unsecured.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Matching loan tenure to the useful life of the asset means:\nA) A 7-year loan for a mobile phone is appropriate  B) A long tenure suits a house; a short tenure suits a depreciating asset  C) All loans should be 30 years  D) Tenure is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You should not still be paying for something after it has lost its value.\nA) A phone's useful life is 2–3 years, so a 7-year loan is clearly mismatched. C) Long tenures multiply total interest. D) Tenure drives total interest and is central.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Paying only the \"minimum due\" on a credit card:\nA) Avoids all interest  B) Avoids a late fee but not interest on the revolving balance  C) Clears the balance  D) Improves the interest rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The unpaid balance **revolves at 3–4% a month**, and the interest-free period on new purchases is typically lost.\nA) The most costly misconception in consumer finance. C) The minimum is a small fraction of the balance. D) The rate does not improve.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Never borrowing to service existing debt is important because:\nA) It is illegal  B) It creates a debt spiral where new debt funds old debt  C) Lenders forbid it  D) It improves the credit score\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Borrowing to pay EMIs adds cost without adding income — the classic **debt spiral**.\nA) It is not illegal, merely unwise. C) Lenders cannot always detect it. D) It typically **damages** the score as utilisation rises.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A ₹30,00,000 home loan at 8.5% for 20 years. What is the EMI?\nA) ₹21,250  B) ₹26,035  C) ₹12,500  D) ₹32,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.085 ÷ 12 = **0.0070833**; n = 240; (1.0070833)²⁴⁰ = **5.44127**. EMI = 30,00,000 × (0.0070833 × 5.44127) ÷ (5.44127 − 1) = 30,00,000 × 0.0385424 ÷ 4.44127 = **₹26,035**.\nA) ₹21,250 is the **first month's interest** (30,00,000 × 0.0070833). C) ₹12,500 is principal ÷ 240. D) ₹32,000 overstates it. Excel: `=PMT(8.5%/12,240,-3000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** For the Q36 loan, what is the interest component of the FIRST EMI?\nA) ₹26,035  B) ₹21,250  C) ₹4,785  D) ₹12,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest = outstanding × monthly rate = 30,00,000 × 0.0070833 = **₹21,250**.\nA) Is the full EMI. C) ₹4,785 is the **principal** component (26,035 − 21,250). D) Is principal ÷ n. **Note: 82% of the first EMI is interest.**\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Continuing Q36–Q37, what is the outstanding balance after the first EMI?\nA) ₹30,00,000  B) ₹29,95,215  C) ₹29,73,965  D) ₹28,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Principal repaid = EMI − interest = 26,035 − 21,250 = **₹4,785**. New balance = 30,00,000 − 4,785 = **₹29,95,215**.\nA) Ignores the repayment. C) Wrongly subtracts the full EMI (which would double-count the interest). D) Far too low. **After a full EMI, the debt fell by less than ₹5,000 — the amortisation reality.**\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** For the Q36 loan, what is the total interest paid over 20 years?\nA) ₹32,48,400  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹62,48,400  D) ₹15,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total repaid = 26,035 × 240 = **₹62,48,400**. Interest = 62,48,400 − 30,00,000 = **₹32,48,400**.\nB) Is the principal. C) ₹62,48,400 is the *total repaid*, not the interest. D) Understates it. **The borrower pays more than the loan itself in interest.**\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A borrower has ₹6,00,000 spare, a home loan at 8.5% and a personal loan at 15%. Under the avalanche method, which should be repaid?\nA) The home loan, as it is larger  B) The personal loan, as it has the higher rate  C) Split equally  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Avalanche attacks the **highest rate**, minimising total interest. 15% is nearly double 8.5%.\nA) Size is irrelevant to the avalanche rule. C) Splitting dilutes the saving. D) Repaying 15% debt is a guaranteed 15% return.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A client can invest at an expected 10% (taxable at 30%) or prepay a 9% home loan. Which is better?\nA) Invest, since 10% > 9%  B) Prepay — the post-tax return of 7% is below the 9% loan cost  C) They are identical  D) Take another loan and invest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compare the loan rate to the **post-tax** return: 10% × (1 − 0.30) = **7%**. Since 7% < 9%, prepaying wins — and it is **certain and risk-free**.\nA) Compares a pre-tax expected return with a certain cost — the classic error. C) They differ by 2 percentage points. D) Leveraging into a lower post-tax return destroys value.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Interest rates fall and a borrower with comfortable cash flow is offered a lower EMI or a shorter tenure. Which saves more?\nA) The lower EMI  B) Keeping the EMI and shortening the tenure  C) They are identical  D) Neither changes the interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Keeping the EMI means the **entire benefit goes to principal**, cutting years off the loan and saving far more total interest.\nA) Frees up cash flow but stretches the loan and costs more overall. C) The difference can be lakhs. D) Both options change the interest, in opposite directions.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A client's gross monthly income is ₹1,80,000 and total EMIs ₹81,000. What is the debt-to-income ratio and the verdict?\nA) 45% — above the healthy ceiling; no further borrowing capacity  B) 45% — healthy  C) 22% — healthy  D) 81% — critical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — 81,000 ÷ 1,80,000 = **45%**, above the 35–40% guideline. Lenders will resist further credit and the household has little room for shocks.\nB) 45% is not healthy. C) Miscomputes the ratio. D) 81% confuses the EMI amount with the ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which loan is secured by hypothecation?\nA) A home loan  B) A gold loan  C) A car loan  D) A personal loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The car is **movable** and the **borrower retains possession**, which is precisely hypothecation.\nA) Home loans use a **mortgage** (immovable). B) Gold loans use a **pledge** (lender holds the gold). D) Personal loans are unsecured.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A borrower takes a ₹10,00,000 loan and is offered 5 years at ₹21,247/month or 7 years at ₹16,601/month, both at 10%. Which costs less in total?\nA) The 7-year option, because the EMI is lower  B) The 5-year option — total interest ₹2,74,820 versus ₹3,94,484  C) They cost the same  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **5 years:** 21,247 × 60 = ₹12,74,820, interest = **₹2,74,820**. **7 years:** 16,601 × 84 = ₹13,94,484, interest = **₹3,94,484**.\nA) This is the **EMI illusion** — a lower monthly figure hides roughly ₹1.2 lakh of extra interest. C) They differ substantially. D) Both figures are computable. **Always compare total interest, not the EMI.**\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A client repeatedly uses a credit card to cover month-end shortfalls. The correct diagnosis is:\nA) The client needs a higher card limit  B) A structural budget deficit that more credit cannot fix  C) The card rate is too low  D) The client should take a personal loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Persistent shortfalls mean **outflows exceed inflows**. Only raising income or cutting expenses fixes that.\nA) A larger limit enables a bigger problem. C) Card rates are the highest available. D) Refinancing card debt into a cheaper personal loan can help *tactically*, but does nothing about the underlying deficit.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which is the correct classification of a loan against a life insurance policy?\nA) Unsecured  B) Secured by assignment of the policy  C) Secured by mortgage  D) Not permitted in India\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The policy is **assigned** to the insurer/lender as security, available once the policy has acquired a **surrender value**.\nA) It is secured. C) Mortgages apply to immovable property. D) It is a standard facility.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A floating-rate borrower sees the benchmark rise. If the lender keeps the EMI unchanged, what happens?\nA) The tenure shortens  B) The tenure extends  C) The principal falls faster  D) Nothing changes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With a higher rate but the same EMI, less of each payment goes to principal, so the **tenure extends**.\nA) That happens when rates **fall**. C) The opposite occurs. D) Something must adjust — either the EMI or the tenure.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which client should prioritise the snowball method over the avalanche?\nA) A disciplined client focused purely on minimising interest  B) A client who has repeatedly abandoned repayment plans and needs early wins  C) A client with only one loan  D) A client with no debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Snowball's value is **behavioural**: clearing a small balance quickly builds the momentum to continue.\nA) Such a client should use avalanche, which is mathematically optimal. C) With one loan the strategies coincide. D) Neither applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A ₹5,00,000 personal loan at 16% for 4 years. What is the approximate EMI?\nA) ₹10,417  B) ₹14,166  C) ₹6,667  D) ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.16 ÷ 12 = **0.0133333**; n = 48; (1.0133333)⁴⁸ = **1.88936**. EMI = 5,00,000 × (0.0133333 × 1.88936) ÷ (1.88936 − 1) = 5,00,000 × 0.02519 ÷ 0.88936 = **₹14,163**.\nA) ₹10,417 is principal ÷ 48, ignoring interest. C) ₹6,667 is the first month's interest. D) ₹20,000 overstates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which of the following would MOST improve a client's credit score over time?\nA) Applying for several loans in one month  B) Consistently paying every EMI and card bill in full and on time  C) Closing all credit cards immediately  D) Maxing out the card limit each month\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Repayment history** is the single largest scoring factor.\nA) Multiple hard enquiries in a short period hurt the score. C) Closing cards shortens credit history and raises utilisation on the rest. D) High utilisation lowers the score.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A client is midway (10 of 20 years) through a home loan and assumes half the principal is repaid. In reality:\nA) Well over half is repaid  B) Considerably less than half is repaid  C) Exactly half is repaid  D) The full amount is repaid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because early EMIs are mostly **interest**, the principal repaid at the halfway point is typically only around **30–35%** of the loan.\nA) and C) reflect the intuitive but wrong linear assumption. D) Clearly false. **This is why a foreclosure quote often shocks borrowers.**\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A borrower with a ₹2,00,000 card balance at 42% is offered a personal loan at 14% to clear it. This is:\nA) Always a bad idea  B) Generally sensible — refinancing expensive debt with cheaper debt, provided spending behaviour changes  C) Illegal  D) Neutral, since both are unsecured\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Swapping **42% for 14%** saves 28 percentage points a year. The essential caveat: if the freed-up card is used again, the client ends up with **both** debts.\nA) The interest saving is substantial. C) It is a routine, legal transaction. D) The rates differ by a factor of three — hardly neutral.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which describes the relationship between loan tenure and total interest?\nA) Longer tenure, less total interest  B) Longer tenure, lower EMI but more total interest  C) Tenure has no effect  D) Shorter tenure, more total interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Stretching repayment leaves a larger balance outstanding for longer, so **total interest rises** even as the EMI falls.\nA) and D) reverse the relationship. C) Tenure is one of the two main drivers of total interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A client wants to borrow for a ₹1,20,000 phone over 5 years. The adviser should point out that:\nA) This is investment expenditure  B) The tenure far exceeds the asset's useful life — she will pay for it long after it is worthless  C) It will improve her credit score  D) Phones appreciate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A phone's useful life is roughly 2–3 years; a 5-year loan is a **tenure/asset mismatch** and pure consumption expenditure.\nA) It creates no lasting value. C) Taking unnecessary debt does not meaningfully help a score. D) Consumer electronics depreciate quickly.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A borrower's floating-rate home loan rate rises so far that the EMI no longer covers the monthly interest. What must happen?\nA) The tenure simply extends further  B) The EMI must be increased  C) The loan is written off  D) The rate automatically resets to the original level\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If the EMI is below the interest accrual, the balance would **grow indefinitely** (negative amortisation), so the lender must raise the EMI.\nA) There is no tenure long enough to fix a negatively amortising loan. C) Lenders do not write off performing loans. D) Rates do not auto-reset.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which is the best use of an unexpected ₹4,00,000 bonus for a client with a ₹3,00,000 card balance at 40% and no emergency fund?\nA) Invest it all in equity  B) Clear the 40% card balance, then start building the emergency fund with the rest  C) Buy a car  D) Prepay the 8.5% home loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Clearing **40% debt** is a guaranteed, tax-free 40% return — unbeatable. The remaining ₹1,00,000 starts the buffer.\nA) No equity return reliably beats 40%. C) Pure consumption while carrying 40% debt. D) The home loan at 8.5% is far cheaper than the card and should wait.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A ₹20,00,000 loan at 9% for 15 years. What is the EMI?\nA) ₹20,285  B) ₹15,000  C) ₹11,111  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.0075; n = 180; (1.0075)¹⁸⁰ = **3.83804**. EMI = 20,00,000 × (0.0075 × 3.83804) ÷ (3.83804 − 1) = 20,00,000 × 0.0287853 ÷ 2.83804 = **₹20,285**.\nB) ₹15,000 is the first month's interest (20,00,000 × 0.0075). C) ₹11,111 is principal ÷ 180. D) ₹25,000 overstates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Which is TRUE of a fixed-rate versus a floating-rate loan?\nA) Fixed rates always cost less overall  B) Fixed gives certainty but usually starts higher; floating is cheaper initially but the borrower bears rate risk  C) Floating rates never change  D) Fixed-rate loans have no prepayment charges\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The trade-off is **certainty versus cost**.\nA) Whether fixed costs less depends on the rate path, which is unknowable in advance. C) Floating rates move with the benchmark by definition. D) It is **floating-rate** home loans to individuals that carry no prepayment penalty; fixed-rate loans may.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A client's total EMIs are ₹95,000 on a ₹2,50,000 monthly income, and she wants a new ₹25,000 EMI. What is the resulting ratio and advice?\nA) 48% — comfortably within limits  B) 48% — above the 35–40% ceiling; defer or reduce existing debt first  C) 38% — acceptable  D) 10% — no concern\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — New total = 95,000 + 25,000 = **₹1,20,000**; ratio = 1,20,000 ÷ 2,50,000 = **48%**, well beyond the guideline.\nA) 48% is not comfortable. C) 38% is the *current* ratio (95,000 ÷ 2,50,000), before the new loan. D) Badly miscomputed.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Why should a large home loan be paired with adequate term insurance?\nA) It reduces the interest rate  B) So dependants do not inherit the liability and lose the home  C) It is legally required  D) It improves the credit score\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If the borrower dies, the loan survives. Term cover at least equal to the outstanding balance protects the family.\nA) Insurance does not change the rate. C) Lenders often encourage but rarely legally mandate it. D) It has no scoring effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A client is choosing between a ₹5,00,000 gold loan at 11% and a ₹5,00,000 personal loan at 17%. Assuming she owns the gold, which is preferable and why?\nA) The personal loan, since no asset is at risk  B) The gold loan — 6 percentage points cheaper because it is secured  C) They are equivalent  D) Neither; she should use a credit card\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Collateral lowers the lender's risk and hence the rate. On ₹5,00,000, 6 percentage points is **₹30,000 a year**.\nA) The gold is only at risk on default, which the borrower controls. C) The rates differ materially. D) A card at 40%+ would be far worse.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which best explains why prepaying early in a loan saves much more than prepaying late?\nA) Lenders offer discounts early  B) Early prepayment removes principal that would otherwise accrue interest for many remaining years  C) Interest rates are higher early  D) There is no difference\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A rupee of principal removed in year 2 avoids interest for **18 more years**; the same rupee removed in year 18 avoids only 2 years' interest.\nA) No such discount exists. C) The rate is the same throughout (on a fixed loan). D) The difference is large.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client's loan has 96 months remaining with an EMI of ₹32,000 at 10%. What is the approximate outstanding balance?\nA) ₹30,72,000  B) ₹21,13,000  C) ₹32,000  D) ₹15,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The outstanding balance is the **PV of remaining EMIs**: r = 0.008333, m = 96. (1.008333)⁹⁶ = **2.21815**, so the inverse is **0.45083**. Factor = (1 − 0.45083) ÷ 0.008333 = 0.54917 ÷ 0.008333 = **65.90**. Outstanding = 32,000 × 65.90 = **₹21,08,800 ≈ ₹21,13,000**.\nA) ₹30,72,000 is the undiscounted total of remaining EMIs (32,000 × 96), which wrongly includes future interest. C) Is one EMI. D) Understates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which action would WORSEN a client's credit score?\nA) Paying the full card statement balance monthly  B) Using 90% of the available credit limit every month  C) Keeping an old credit card open with occasional use  D) Checking the score once a year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **High credit utilisation** is a major negative factor; below about 30% is generally preferred.\nA) The single best habit for a score. C) Length of credit history helps. D) A soft enquiry has no effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A borrower is offered a ₹15,00,000 loan at \"9% with a 1% processing fee and bundled insurance of ₹30,000\". The adviser should:\nA) Compare only the 9% headline rate  B) Compute the effective cost including the ₹15,000 fee and ₹30,000 insurance  C) Ignore the fees, as they are one-time  D) Assume the effective rate is below 9%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Fees and bundled products raise the **effective** cost. Here ₹45,000 of upfront charges means the borrower effectively receives less than ₹15,00,000 while repaying on the full amount.\nA) The headline rate hides the true cost. C) One-time costs still change the effective rate. D) Fees push the effective rate **above** 9%, never below.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** An education loan borrower is in the moratorium period. Which statement is correct?\nA) No interest accrues during the moratorium  B) Interest generally accrues and increases the amount eventually repaid  C) The loan is interest-free permanently  D) The principal reduces during the moratorium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The moratorium defers **payment**, not the **cost**. Some borrowers pay simple interest during the course precisely to avoid this build-up.\nA) and C) are the common misconception. D) Principal cannot reduce when nothing is being repaid.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A client has these debts: ₹80,000 at 12%, ₹2,50,000 at 18%, ₹40,000 at 24%. Under the **avalanche** method, in what order should they be cleared?\nA) ₹40,000 → ₹80,000 → ₹2,50,000  B) ₹40,000 (24%) → ₹2,50,000 (18%) → ₹80,000 (12%)  C) ₹80,000 → ₹2,50,000 → ₹40,000  D) ₹2,50,000 → ₹80,000 → ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Avalanche orders strictly by **rate, highest first**: 24% → 18% → 12%.\nA) Happens to start correctly but then orders by balance, not rate — that is the **snowball**. C) and D) ignore rates entirely. *(Here the snowball order would be ₹40,000 → ₹80,000 → ₹2,50,000, which coincidentally starts the same.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which statement about windfalls is most consistent with sound debt management?\nA) Windfalls should be spent immediately to enjoy them  B) Windfalls should be pre-allocated — costliest debt first, then the emergency fund, then goals  C) Windfalls should always go into equity  D) Windfalls should be ignored in planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Pre-allocation** prevents the windfall from being absorbed into consumption, which is what happens by default.\nA) Wastes a significant opportunity. C) Ignores expensive debt and the buffer. D) Forfeits the benefit entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A borrower can afford a higher EMI after a salary increase. The most efficient use is:\nA) Extend the tenure  B) Raise the EMI (or prepay), shortening the tenure and cutting total interest  C) Take another loan  D) Reduce the EMI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Directing the increment to the loan reduces principal faster and **saves compounding interest**.\nA) and D) increase total interest. C) Adds cost rather than removing it.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A ₹60,00,000 home loan at 9% for 25 years. What is the EMI?\nA) ₹50,349  B) ₹45,000  C) ₹20,000  D) ₹62,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.0075; n = 300; (1.0075)³⁰⁰ = **9.40842**. EMI = 60,00,000 × (0.0075 × 9.40842) ÷ (9.40842 − 1) = 60,00,000 × 0.07056315 ÷ 8.40842 = **₹50,349**.\nB) ₹45,000 is the **first month's interest** (60,00,000 × 0.0075). C) ₹20,000 is principal ÷ 300. D) Overstates it. Excel: `=PMT(9%/12,300,-6000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Continuing Q71, what is the total interest over 25 years?\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹91,04,700  C) ₹1,51,04,700  D) ₹45,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total repaid = 50,349 × 300 = **₹1,51,04,700**. Interest = 1,51,04,700 − 60,00,000 = **₹91,04,700**.\nA) Is the principal. C) Is the total repaid, not the interest. D) Understates it. **The borrower pays over 1.5× the loan amount in interest alone — the cost of a 25-year tenure.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Compare Q71's 25-year loan with the same ₹60,00,000 at 9% over 20 years (EMI ₹53,984). How much total interest is saved by choosing 20 years?\nA) ₹3,635  B) ₹1,89,84,000  C) ₹21,48,540  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **20 years:** 53,984 × 240 = ₹1,29,56,160; interest = **₹69,56,160**. **25 years:** interest = **₹91,04,700**. Saving = 91,04,700 − 69,56,160 = **₹21,48,540**.\nA) ₹3,635 is just the monthly EMI difference. B) Is not a meaningful figure. D) The difference is enormous. **Paying ₹3,635 more a month saves over ₹21 lakh — the single most powerful lever in home-loan planning.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A borrower with the Q71 loan prepays ₹5,00,000 at the end of year 3. If he keeps the EMI unchanged, the effect is:\nA) The EMI rises  B) The tenure shortens materially, saving a large amount of interest  C) Nothing changes  D) The interest rate falls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Removing ₹5,00,000 of principal in year 3 eliminates the interest that sum would have accrued over the remaining **22 years**. Keeping the EMI constant sends the entire benefit to principal, shortening the tenure by several years.\nA) A prepayment never forces a higher EMI. C) Total interest falls substantially. D) The contractual rate is unaffected. **Keeping the EMI and shortening the tenure always beats reducing the EMI.**\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A ₹12,00,000 car loan at 10.5% for 5 years. What is the EMI and total interest?\nA) EMI ₹25,799; interest ₹3,47,940  B) EMI ₹20,000; interest ₹1,20,000  C) EMI ₹10,500; interest ₹3,47,940  D) EMI ₹30,000; interest ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.105 ÷ 12 = **0.00875**; n = 60; (1.00875)⁶⁰ = **1.68611**. EMI = 12,00,000 × (0.00875 × 1.68611) ÷ (1.68611 − 1) = 12,00,000 × 0.01475346 ÷ 0.68611 = **₹25,802**. Total repaid = 25,802 × 60 = ₹15,48,120; interest = **₹3,48,120**.\nB) ₹20,000 is principal ÷ 60. C) ₹10,500 is the first month's interest. D) Overstates both.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client has ₹8,00,000 spare and these debts: home loan ₹40,00,000 at 8.5%, car loan ₹6,00,000 at 10.5%, personal loan ₹5,00,000 at 16%, card balance ₹1,50,000 at 42%. What is the optimal (avalanche) application?\nA) Prepay the home loan  B) Clear the card (₹1,50,000 at 42%), then the personal loan (₹5,00,000 at 16%), then ₹1,50,000 toward the car loan  C) Split ₹2,00,000 across each  D) Clear the car loan first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Avalanche orders strictly by rate: **42% → 16% → 10.5% → 8.5%**. The ₹8,00,000 clears the card (₹1,50,000), then the personal loan (₹5,00,000), leaving ₹1,50,000 for the car loan.\nA) The home loan is the **cheapest** debt — the last thing to prepay. C) Splitting leaves 42% debt outstanding. D) Skips two costlier debts. **Interest saved in year 1 ≈ ₹63,000 (card) + ₹80,000 (personal) + ₹15,750 (car) ≈ ₹1,58,750.**\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A borrower's ₹35,00,000 loan at 9% for 20 years has an EMI of ₹31,490. After exactly 10 years, roughly what proportion of the principal has been repaid?\nA) About 50%  B) About 33%  C) About 75%  D) About 10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Outstanding after 120 months = PV of the remaining 120 EMIs: (1.0075)¹²⁰ = **2.45136**, inverse = **0.40794**. Factor = (1 − 0.40794) ÷ 0.0075 = 0.59206 ÷ 0.0075 = **78.94**. Outstanding = 31,490 × 78.94 = **₹24,86,000**. Principal repaid = 35,00,000 − 24,86,000 = **₹10,14,000**, i.e. about **29–33%**.\nA) The intuitive linear assumption — and wrong. C) and D) are far off. **Halfway through the tenure, barely a third of the principal is gone.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A client earns ₹3,00,000 a month with EMIs of ₹1,35,000. She wants to buy a house needing a ₹45,000 EMI. What is her position?\nA) Debt-to-income would be 60% — well beyond the limit; she must reduce existing debt first  B) 45% — acceptable  C) 15% — no concern  D) 30% — healthy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Current ratio = 1,35,000 ÷ 3,00,000 = **45%** (already above the guideline). Adding ₹45,000 gives ₹1,80,000 ÷ 3,00,000 = **60%**, leaving only 40% of gross income for taxes, living costs and savings.\nB) 45% is the *current* figure and is itself too high. C) and D) badly miscompute. **Advice: clear or refinance existing debt before adding a housing EMI.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A borrower is offered: Loan A at 9.5% with no fees, or Loan B at 9.0% with a 1.5% processing fee, both ₹25,00,000 over 15 years. Which is cheaper overall?\nA) Loan A always  B) Loan B — the 0.5 percentage-point saving over 15 years far exceeds the ₹37,500 fee  C) They are identical  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compute each loan's total outgo.\n**Loan A (9.5%, no fee):** r = 0.0079167; (1.0079167)¹⁸⁰ = **4.14539**. EMI = 25,00,000 × (0.0079167 × 4.14539) ÷ 3.14539 = **₹26,105**. Total = 26,105 × 180 = **₹46,98,900**.\n**Loan B (9.0% + 1.5% fee):** r = 0.0075; (1.0075)¹⁸⁰ = **3.83804**. EMI = 25,00,000 × (0.0075 × 3.83804) ÷ 2.83804 = **₹25,357**. Total = 25,357 × 180 = ₹45,64,260, plus the **₹37,500** fee = **₹46,01,760**.\n**Loan B is cheaper by about ₹97,000.**\nA) Ignores the rate difference, which compounds over 15 years. C) They differ by nearly ₹1 lakh. D) Both are fully computable. **A one-time fee is usually outweighed by a lower rate on a long tenure — but always compute rather than assume.**\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A client with a 42% card balance of ₹2,00,000 pays only the 5% minimum due each month while continuing to spend. What is the likely outcome?\nA) The balance clears in about 20 months  B) The balance barely reduces while interest accrues at ~3.5% a month, and new purchases lose the interest-free period  C) Interest is waived  D) The credit score improves rapidly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The minimum (₹10,000) is close to the monthly interest (₹2,00,000 × 3.5% = **₹7,000**), so only about ₹3,000 reduces the principal. Meanwhile, once a balance revolves, **new purchases start accruing interest from the transaction date**.\nA) At this rate it would take many years, not 20 months. C) Interest is not waived by paying the minimum. D) High utilisation and revolving debt **hurt** the score.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A ₹25,00,000 loan at 8% for 20 years has an EMI of ₹20,910. If the rate rises to 9.5% and the lender keeps the EMI unchanged, what happens?\nA) The tenure shortens  B) The tenure extends significantly  C) The EMI rises to ₹23,300  D) The principal is unaffected\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At 9.5%, the monthly interest on the opening balance is 25,00,000 × 0.0079167 = **₹19,792**, leaving only ₹1,118 of the ₹20,910 EMI for principal (versus ₹4,243 at 8%). Repayment slows dramatically and the **tenure extends** — often by many years.\nA) That happens when rates fall. C) The premise is that the EMI is held constant. D) Principal repayment slows sharply, which is the whole effect. **If the rate rose far enough that interest exceeded ₹20,910, the EMI would have to rise.**\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A client can either prepay ₹10,00,000 on a 8.75% home loan or invest it in an equity fund expected to return 12% pre-tax over 15 years (LTCG at 12.5% above the annual exemption). Which is more likely to be better, and what is the key caveat?\nA) Prepay, since 8.75% is certain  B) Invest — the post-tax expected return of roughly 10.5–11% exceeds 8.75% over a 15-year horizon, but the return is uncertain while the saving is guaranteed  C) They are identical  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Post-tax equity return ≈ 12% × (1 − 0.125) ≈ **10.5%** (roughly, since LTCG applies only on redemption and partly within the exemption), which exceeds the **8.75%** loan cost over a long horizon.\nA) Certainty has value, but a 15-year horizon is long enough for equity to be reasonable. C) They differ by roughly 1.75 percentage points. D) Both are legitimate options. **Key caveat: prepayment is a risk-free 8.75%; investing carries market risk. For a risk-averse client, or where the loan rate exceeds ~10%, prepay.**\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A ₹8,00,000 personal loan at 15% for 3 years. What is the EMI, and what proportion of the first EMI is interest?\nA) EMI ₹27,729; about 36% interest  B) EMI ₹22,222; about 45% interest  C) EMI ₹27,729; about 100% interest  D) EMI ₹10,000; about 36% interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.0125; n = 36; (1.0125)³⁶ = **1.56394**. EMI = 8,00,000 × (0.0125 × 1.56394) ÷ 0.56394 = 8,00,000 × 0.01954925 ÷ 0.56394 = **₹27,732**. First month's interest = 8,00,000 × 0.0125 = **₹10,000**; share = 10,000 ÷ 27,732 = **36%**.\nB) ₹22,222 is principal ÷ 36. C) 100% is wrong — ₹17,732 goes to principal. D) ₹10,000 is the interest, not the EMI. **Note: on a short 3-year loan only 36% of the first EMI is interest, versus 82% on a 20-year home loan — tenure drives the split.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Which sequence correctly ranks these by cost of borrowing, cheapest first?\nA) Credit card, personal loan, home loan, gold loan  B) Home loan, gold loan, personal loan, credit card  C) Personal loan, home loan, credit card, gold loan  D) Gold loan, credit card, home loan, personal loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Typical Indian rates: **home loan 8–10% < gold loan 9–14% < personal loan 11–18% < credit card 36–48%**.\nA) Reverses the order almost entirely. C) and D) scramble it. **The ordering follows security and risk: secured against immovable property is cheapest; unsecured revolving credit is dearest.**\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A client's loan has 60 months remaining, EMI ₹18,000, rate 11%. She receives ₹3,00,000. If she prepays, roughly how much interest does she save over the remaining term?\nA) About ₹3,00,000  B) About ₹90,000  C) Nothing  D) About ₹10,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Removing ₹3,00,000 of principal avoids all the interest that sum would have accrued over the remaining 5 years. Because the prepayment shortens the tail of the loan, the avoided interest works out to roughly **₹90,000** over the remaining term (≈ ₹3,00,000 × 11% × 5 years × ~0.55, the factor reflecting that the loan would have amortised down anyway).\nA) ₹3,00,000 would mean saving 100% of the prepaid amount in interest — far too high for a 5-year residual term. C) Prepayment always saves interest. D) ₹10,80,000 is the total of remaining EMIs (18,000 × 60), not the interest. **For an exact figure, recompute the amortisation schedule with and without the prepayment.**\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A borrower defaults on a gold loan. What can the lender do?\nA) Nothing without a court order  B) Sell the pledged gold, since possession is already with the lender  C) Seize the borrower's house  D) Only report to the credit bureau\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In a **pledge**, the lender already **holds** the movable security and can realise it on default, subject to due notice.\nA) The pledge is precisely what avoids a lengthy recovery process. C) The house is not the security for a gold loan. D) Bureau reporting happens, but the lender's primary remedy is the pledged gold.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A client's monthly income is ₹2,00,000. Existing EMIs ₹40,000. A lender caps debt-to-income at 40%. What is the maximum additional EMI she can take on?\nA) ₹80,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹1,20,000  D) ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Maximum total EMI = 40% × 2,00,000 = **₹80,000**. She already pays ₹40,000, so the additional capacity is 80,000 − 40,000 = **₹40,000**.\nA) ₹80,000 is the *total* permitted, not the additional amount. C) Ignores the cap. D) Halves the answer.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Continuing Q87: at 9% over 20 years, what loan amount does a ₹40,000 EMI support?\nA) ₹44,45,000  B) ₹96,00,000  C) ₹30,00,000  D) ₹53,33,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Reverse the EMI formula: loan = EMI × [(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r]. r = 0.0075, n = 240, (1.0075)²⁴⁰ = **6.00915**, inverse = **0.166413**. Factor = (1 − 0.166413) ÷ 0.0075 = 0.833587 ÷ 0.0075 = **111.14**. Loan = 40,000 × 111.14 = **₹44,45,600**.\nB) ₹96,00,000 is the undiscounted total of EMIs (40,000 × 240). C) and D) do not follow. Excel: `=PV(9%/12,240,-40000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A client argues that because her home loan gives tax benefits, she should never prepay it. The best response is:\nA) She is correct — never prepay  B) Tax benefits reduce the effective cost but rarely make the loan free; compare the post-tax loan cost with the post-tax return on alternatives  C) Tax benefits do not exist on home loans  D) She should take a larger loan for more tax benefit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Deductions lower the **effective** rate (a 9% loan might cost ~6.3% post-tax at a 30% slab, within deduction limits), but the loan still has a positive cost. Compare that effective cost with the post-tax return available elsewhere.\nA) Overstates the benefit into an absolute rule. C) Home loan deductions do exist. D) **Borrowing more to save tax is always value-destructive** — you spend a rupee of interest to save 30 paise of tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Which combination indicates a household in a debt spiral?\nA) Debt-to-income 25%, all EMIs paid on time, 6-month buffer  B) Debt-to-income 55%, taking a new personal loan to pay existing EMIs, zero buffer  C) Debt-to-income 30%, one home loan  D) No debt at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The defining marker is **borrowing to service existing debt**, combined with an unsustainable ratio and no buffer.\nA) and C) are healthy positions. D) Is not a spiral. **The intervention here is debt counselling and restructuring, not more credit.**\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A ₹18,00,000 loan at 12% for 8 years. What is the EMI?\nA) ₹29,255  B) ₹18,750  C) ₹18,000  D) ₹35,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.12 ÷ 12 = **0.01**; n = 96; (1.01)⁹⁶ = **2.59927**. EMI = 18,00,000 × (0.01 × 2.59927) ÷ (2.59927 − 1) = 18,00,000 × 0.0259927 ÷ 1.59927 = **₹29,255**.\nB) ₹18,750 is principal ÷ 96 (18,00,000 ÷ 96), ignoring interest entirely. C) ₹18,000 is the **first month's interest** (18,00,000 × 0.01). D) ₹35,000 overstates it. Excel: `=PMT(12%/12,96,-1800000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A client with a floating-rate home loan sees rates fall from 9.5% to 8.5%. She has comfortable cash flow. Comparing the two options on a ₹40,00,000 loan with 18 years remaining:\nA) Reducing the EMI saves more interest  B) Keeping the EMI constant shortens the tenure by roughly 2–3 years and saves several lakhs of interest  C) There is no difference  D) She should refinance to a fixed rate immediately\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At 8.5% the required EMI falls, so continuing to pay the **old, higher EMI** sends the surplus entirely to principal. On a loan of this size the tenure typically shortens by **2–3 years**, saving several lakh rupees.\nA) A lower EMI stretches the loan and saves the least. C) The difference is material. D) Switching to fixed just as rates fall locks in the *higher* environment — usually the wrong moment.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A borrower has ₹4,50,000 available. Debts: card ₹1,80,000 at 40%, personal loan ₹2,00,000 at 15%, car loan ₹3,00,000 at 10%. He also has no emergency fund and monthly expenses of ₹60,000. What is the best allocation?\nA) Clear all of the car loan  B) Clear the card (₹1,80,000), clear the personal loan (₹2,00,000), and keep ₹70,000 as the start of an emergency buffer  C) Put everything into equity  D) Clear the card only and spend the rest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Avalanche order attacks **40% then 15%**, saving roughly ₹72,000 + ₹30,000 = **₹1,02,000 of interest in year one**. The remaining ₹70,000 begins the buffer (about 1.2 months) so a future shock doesn't recreate card debt.\nA) The car loan at 10% is the cheapest of the three. C) No investment reliably beats 40%. D) Leaves 15% debt outstanding while spending. **Note the balance struck: clear expensive debt, but do not end up with zero liquidity.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which statement about the relationship between EMI, tenure and total interest is correct?\nA) Doubling the tenure roughly halves the total interest  B) Doubling the tenure lowers the EMI but can more than double the total interest  C) Tenure affects only the EMI  D) Total interest is fixed regardless of tenure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On the ₹60,00,000 at 9% example: 20 years costs ₹69.56 lakh of interest; 25 years costs ₹91.05 lakh. Extending further compounds the effect sharply.\nA) Reverses the relationship entirely. C) Tenure drives **both** the EMI and total interest. D) Total interest is highly sensitive to tenure.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A client wants to borrow ₹3,00,000 for her daughter's wedding. Which is the soundest advice?\nA) Use a credit card for the flexibility  B) This is consumption expenditure — prefer saving in advance; if borrowing is unavoidable, use the cheapest secured option (e.g. gold loan or LAS) over a personal loan or card  C) Take a 10-year personal loan for a low EMI  D) Borrow ₹10,00,000 to have a buffer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A wedding creates **no lasting asset**, so it is consumption expenditure. Where borrowing cannot be avoided, minimise the cost by using **secured** credit.\nA) Cards at 36–48% are the worst possible choice. C) A long tenure on consumption debt multiplies total interest. D) Borrowing more than needed is straightforwardly value-destructive.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A ₹10,00,000 loan at 10% has an EMI of ₹21,247 over 5 years. In month 1, how is the EMI split?\nA) Interest ₹8,333; principal ₹12,914  B) Interest ₹12,914; principal ₹8,333  C) Interest ₹21,247; principal nil  D) Interest ₹1,00,000; principal nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Interest = 10,00,000 × (0.10 ÷ 12) = 10,00,000 × 0.008333 = **₹8,333**. Principal = 21,247 − 8,333 = **₹12,914**.\nB) Reverses the two. C) Some principal is always repaid on an amortising loan. D) ₹1,00,000 is a full year's interest. **Contrast with the 20-year home loan where interest was 82% of the first EMI — on a 5-year loan, principal already dominates at 61%.**\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Which action most directly reduces a client's leverage ratio?\nA) Taking a top-up loan  B) Using surplus cash to prepay principal  C) Revaluing the house upward  D) Extending the loan tenure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Leverage = liabilities ÷ assets. Prepaying reduces **liabilities** (and cash), lowering the ratio.\nA) Raises liabilities. C) Raising asset values lowers the ratio arithmetically but is a **paper** change, not a real reduction in debt. D) Changes timing, not the outstanding balance.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client is 5 years into a 20-year, ₹50,00,000 home loan at 9% (EMI ₹44,986) and is offered refinancing at 8% with a ₹40,000 fee. Should she refinance?\nA) No, fees always outweigh the benefit  B) Yes — a 1 percentage-point saving on a large outstanding balance over 15 remaining years far exceeds ₹40,000  C) Only if the tenure is extended  D) Only if she switches to a fixed rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Outstanding after 5 years ≈ **₹44.3 lakh**. Saving 1 percentage point on roughly that balance is on the order of **₹40,000 in the first year alone**, and the benefit continues for 15 more years — a total saving in the lakhs against a one-time ₹40,000.\nA) Fees matter but must be weighed against the saving. C) Extending the tenure would erode the benefit. D) The rate type is a separate decision. **Rule of thumb: refinancing is usually worthwhile when the rate gap is ≥0.5pp and substantial tenure remains.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A household's debt profile: home loan ₹35,00,000 at 8.5% (EMI ₹30,400), car loan ₹4,00,000 at 11% (EMI ₹10,400), personal loan ₹3,00,000 at 17% (EMI ₹10,700). Gross income ₹1,60,000/month. What is the priority action?\nA) Prepay the home loan  B) Debt-to-income is 32% (acceptable); direct any surplus at the 17% personal loan first  C) Take another loan to consolidate at 20%  D) Do nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total EMIs = 30,400 + 10,400 + 10,700 = **₹51,500**; ratio = 51,500 ÷ 1,60,000 = **32%**, inside the healthy range. So the household is not distressed — the optimisation is simply to attack the **costliest debt (17%)** first.\nA) The home loan at 8.5% is the cheapest. C) Consolidating at a *higher* rate destroys value. D) Leaving 17% debt outstanding while having surplus is a missed guaranteed return.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single principle best summarises sound debt management?\nA) Avoid all debt at any cost  B) Borrow for assets that outlast the loan, at the lowest cost, over a tenure matched to the asset, within 35–40% of income  C) Take the loan with the lowest EMI  D) Always choose the longest tenure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This captures all four counselling questions: **purpose** (an asset that outlasts the loan), **cost** (lowest), **maturity** (matched), and **serviceability** (within 35–40%).\nA) Debt used well (a home, education) builds wealth; blanket avoidance is not planning. C) The **EMI illusion** — the lowest EMI usually means the most total interest. D) The longest tenure maximises interest paid.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Convert every rate to **monthly** and every tenure to **months** before computing.\n\n### Case: The Menon Family's Debt Review\n\nArjun Menon, 38, earns **₹2,60,000 a month** gross. His wife earns **₹90,000 a month**.\n\n**Their loans:**\n\n| Loan | Outstanding | Rate | EMI | Remaining tenure |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Home loan (floating) | ₹52,00,000 | 9.0% | ₹49,000 | 15 years |\n| Car loan | ₹5,20,000 | 10.5% | ₹13,400 | 4 years |\n| Personal loan | ₹4,00,000 | 16.5% | ₹14,200 | 3 years |\n| Credit card (revolving) | ₹2,10,000 | 42.0% | ₹10,500 (minimum) | — |\n\n**Monthly household expenses excluding EMIs:** ₹1,40,000.\n**Liquid assets:** ₹2,80,000. **Other investments:** ₹16,00,000 in equity mutual funds.\n\nArjun has just received an annual bonus of **₹7,00,000**.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is the Menons' debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 21.9%  B) 24.9%  C) 33.5%  D) 45.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Combined gross income** = 2,60,000 + 90,000 = **₹3,50,000**. **Total monthly debt servicing** = home ₹49,000 + car ₹13,400 + personal ₹14,200 + card minimum ₹10,500 = **₹87,100**. Ratio = 87,100 ÷ 3,50,000 = **24.9%**.\nA) 21.9% omits the credit card minimum — a common slip, since the card has no formal \"EMI\" but its minimum due is a real monthly obligation. C) 33.5% wrongly uses only Arjun's ₹2,60,000 income. D) 45% does not follow. **Verdict: comfortably inside the 35–40% guideline — the Menons' problem is not the *amount* of debt but its *cost*, with 42% card debt outstanding.**\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Under the avalanche method, how should the ₹7,00,000 bonus be applied?\nA) Prepay the home loan  B) Clear the card (₹2,10,000 at 42%), then the personal loan (₹4,00,000 at 16.5%), leaving ₹90,000  C) Clear the car loan first  D) Invest it all in equity funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Avalanche orders strictly by **rate**: 42% → 16.5% → 10.5% → 9.0%. The ₹7,00,000 clears the **card (₹2,10,000)** and the **personal loan (₹4,00,000)**, leaving **₹90,000**.\nA) The home loan at 9% is the **cheapest** debt and the last to prepay. C) The car loan at 10.5% is cheaper than both the card and the personal loan. D) No equity return reliably beats 42%.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Approximately how much interest does clearing the card and personal loan save in the first year?\nA) About ₹1,54,200  B) About ₹88,200  C) About ₹66,000  D) About ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Card:** ₹2,10,000 × 42% = **₹88,200** a year. **Personal loan:** ₹4,00,000 × 16.5% = **₹66,000** a year (slightly less in practice as the balance amortises). Total first-year saving ≈ 88,200 + 66,000 = **₹1,54,200**.\nB) ₹88,200 counts only the card. C) ₹66,000 counts only the personal loan. D) Far too low. **A guaranteed, tax-free ₹1.54 lakh return on a ₹6.1 lakh outlay — about 25%.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** After clearing those two loans, what are the Menons' new EMI total and debt-to-income ratio?\nA) ₹62,400 and 17.8%  B) ₹87,100 and 24.9%  C) ₹49,000 and 14.0%  D) ₹72,900 and 20.8%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Remaining EMIs = home ₹49,000 + car ₹13,400 = **₹62,400**. Ratio = 62,400 ÷ 3,50,000 = **17.8%**.\nB) Is the position *before* the prepayments. C) Omits the car loan. D) Does not match. **The freed-up ₹24,700 a month is now available for the emergency fund and goals.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** What should the Menons do with the freed-up ₹24,700 a month and the remaining ₹90,000 of the bonus?\nA) Increase discretionary spending, since the debt burden has fallen  B) Top up the emergency fund toward 3–6 months of outgo, then direct the balance to long-term goals — and avoid re-using the cleared credit card  C) Prepay the entire home loan immediately  D) Take a new personal loan to invest in equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Their **liquidity ratio** is weak: total monthly outgo after the prepayments = ₹1,40,000 expenses + ₹62,400 EMIs = **₹2,02,400**, so ₹2,80,000 of liquid assets = only **1.38 months**. Adding the ₹90,000 residual bonus lifts it to ₹3,70,000 (**1.83 months**), and the ₹24,700 monthly surplus reaches a 3-month target (₹6,07,200) in about 10 months.\nA) Lifestyle inflation would waste the entire gain. C) They lack the funds, and at 9% the home loan is the cheapest debt — the buffer matters more. D) Borrowing at a high rate to invest reverses everything just achieved.\n\n> ⚠️ **The critical behavioural point:** a cleared credit card with a live limit is the commonest cause of relapse. The card should be kept for convenience only, **paid in full every month** — or the limit reduced.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "05", "title": "Introduction to Indian Financial Markets", "module": "Module 2: Indian Financial Markets", "notes": "# Chapter 5: Introduction to Indian Financial Markets — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 2 · Indian Financial Markets — only 10 marks across Chapters 5–6.**\n> ⚠️ **Do not over-invest time here.** This is the lightest module in the paper. Read it once, drill the flashcards (especially the **regulator map**), and move on. The questions are almost entirely **recall** — \"who regulates what\" and \"who does what\".\n\n---\n\n## 5.1 Overview of the Indian Economy\n\nIndia has shifted from a primarily agrarian economy to one where **services and manufacturing** dominate output. A well-developed financial system supports that growth by:\n\n- Channelling **household savings** into productive investment\n- Providing **credit** at efficient cost\n- Enabling **risk transfer** (insurance, derivatives)\n- Supporting **price discovery** and liquidity\n- Facilitating **payments and settlement**\n\n**Key structural features:** a high household savings rate, historically heavy allocation to physical assets (gold, real estate) with a gradual shift toward financial assets (\"financialisation of savings\"), and increasing retail participation through mutual funds and SIPs.\n\n---\n\n## 5.2 The Indian Financial Markets\n\nFinancial markets exist to move funds from **surplus units** (savers) to **deficit units** (borrowers/users), through intermediaries.\n\n### 5.2.1 Key features of a well-functioning market\n\n| Feature | What it means |\n|---|---|\n| **Efficient transfer of funds** | Low cost of intermediation, so saving and borrowing are both attractive |\n| **Price discovery** | Prices reflect all available information |\n| **Liquidity** | Investors can exit and re-allocate |\n| **Information dissemination** | All participants get relevant information |\n| **Regulation** | Systems and processes that protect participants and maintain integrity |\n| **Risk management** | Mechanisms to hedge and to contain systemic risk |\n\n### The basic split\n\n| | **Money Market** | **Capital / Securities Market** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Maturity** | **Up to 1 year** (short-term) | **Beyond 1 year** (long-term) |\n| **Regulator** | **RBI** | **SEBI** |\n| **Instruments** | T-bills, CPs, CDs, call money, repo | Shares, bonds, debentures, derivatives |\n| **Participants** | Banks, corporates, MFs, RBI | Companies, investors, MFs, FPIs |\n\nAnd a second split within the capital market:\n\n- **Primary market** — securities are **issued** by the borrower to investors; funds go **to the issuer**. (IPO, rights, private placement.)\n- **Secondary market** — investors trade **among themselves**; the issuer receives nothing. Provides **liquidity and exit**.\n\n---\n\n## 5.3 ⭐ Regulators of Financial Markets\n\n**Learn this table cold — it is the single most examinable item in the chapter.**\n\n| Regulator | Regulates | Established / Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ministry of Finance (MoF)** | Overall economic and fiscal policy; the Budget; supervises the regulators | Government of India |\n| **Ministry of Corporate Affairs (MCA)** | Companies Act; corporate governance; incorporation | Administers the **Companies Act, 2013** |\n| **Registrar of Companies (RoC)** | Registration/incorporation of companies; statutory filings | Operates **under the MCA** |\n| **Reserve Bank of India (RBI)** | Banking, monetary policy, currency, **money market**, **foreign exchange**, payment systems, NBFCs, credit information companies | Est. **1935**; administers **FEMA** |\n| **SEBI** | **Securities markets** — stock exchanges, brokers, mutual funds, portfolio managers, investment advisers, AIFs, credit rating agencies, depositories | Statutory status **1992** (SEBI Act, 1992) |\n| **IRDAI** | **Insurance** — life and general insurers, agents, brokers | Est. **1999** (IRDA Act, 1999) |\n| **PFRDA** | **Pensions** — NPS, Atal Pension Yojana, pension funds | Statutory status **2013** (PFRDA Act, 2013) |\n| **SROs** | First-level regulation of their members, under the apex regulator | e.g. **AMFI** (mutual fund distributors), **ANMI**, exchanges |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — the four apex regulators by asset:**\n> **R**BI → **R**upees (banking, money, forex) · **S**EBI → **S**ecurities · **I**RDAI → **I**nsurance · **P**FRDA → **P**ensions.\n\n### 5.3.8 Self-Regulatory Organisations (SROs)\nAn SRO is an industry body granted authority to set and enforce standards for its members, **subject to the apex regulator's oversight**. Benefits: closer industry knowledge, faster rule-making, lower regulatory cost. Example: **AMFI** for mutual fund distributors; stock exchanges act as first-level regulators of their brokers.\n\n---\n\n## 5.4 Structure of Financial Markets in India\n\n### 5.4.1 The Banking Sector (RBI's domain)\n\n| Institution | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Commercial banks** | Full-service: accept demand deposits, lend, payments |\n| **Payment banks** | Accept deposits **up to ₹2 lakh** per customer; provide payments and remittances; **cannot lend** or issue credit cards |\n| **Small finance banks** | Full banking, but with priority-sector focus — small borrowers, micro-enterprises, unbanked segments |\n| **NBFCs** | Lend and invest, but **cannot accept demand deposits** and are **not part of the payment system** |\n| **Housing Finance Companies (HFCs)** | Specialised housing lenders; now regulated by the **RBI** (supervision moved from the NHB) |\n| **P2P lending platforms** | **NBFC-P2P**, RBI-registered; match individual lenders with borrowers. The platform only intermediates — **it cannot lend on its own book or guarantee returns** |\n| **Credit Information Companies** | CIBIL, Experian, Equifax, CRIF High Mark — maintain credit histories |\n| **Account Aggregators (AA)** | RBI-regulated **NBFC-AA**: with the customer's **explicit consent**, they retrieve and share the customer's financial data across institutions. They are **data blind** — they cannot read or store the data |\n\n> ⚠️ **Bank vs NBFC — a guaranteed question.** Banks can accept **demand deposits** and are part of the **payment/settlement system**; NBFCs cannot and are not. NBFCs also cannot issue cheques drawn on themselves, and their deposits are **not covered by DICGC insurance**.\n\n**Money market** and the **foreign exchange market** both sit under the **RBI**.\n\n### 5.4.2 Securities Markets (SEBI's domain)\n\n**Definition of \"securities\"** — under the **Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956 (SCRA)**, securities include shares, scrips, stocks, bonds, debentures, debenture stock, derivatives, units of collective investment schemes, security receipts, government securities and rights/interests in securities.\n\n| Segment | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **Stock markets** | Equity issuance and trading — NSE, BSE |\n| **Bond markets** | Government securities and corporate debt |\n| **Derivatives market** | Futures and options on equity, currency, commodities |\n| **Mutual funds** | Pooled, professionally managed, SEBI-regulated |\n| **Portfolio managers** | Individual portfolios for larger investors (min. **₹50 lakh**) |\n| **Investment advisers** | Registered under **SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013** |\n| **Alternative Investment Funds** | Categories I, II and III (Chapter 13) |\n| **Credit rating agencies** | Assess creditworthiness of debt issues — CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, India Ratings |\n\n### 5.4.3 Under IRDAI\nLife insurers, general (non-life) insurers, health insurers, reinsurers, plus intermediaries (agents, brokers, third-party administrators).\n\n### 5.4.4 Under PFRDA\nThe **National Pension System (NPS)**, **Atal Pension Yojana**, pension fund managers, the **Central Recordkeeping Agency (CRA)**, points of presence, and the NPS Trust.\n\n---\n\n## 5.5 ⭐ Participants in the Indian Financial Markets\n\n### Market Infrastructure Institutions (MIIs)\n\nThe three \"MIIs\" — systemically important, so most heavily regulated:\n\n| MII | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Stock / commodity exchanges** | Provide the trading platform, price discovery, and first-level regulation of members |\n| **Clearing corporations** | **Clear and settle** trades; act as the **central counterparty (CCP)** — guaranteeing settlement so no buyer or seller faces counterparty default. Manage margins and the settlement guarantee fund |\n| **Depositories** | Hold securities in **electronic (demat)** form — **NSDL** and **CDSL** in India |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exchange vs clearing corporation:** the **exchange matches trades**; the **clearing corporation guarantees and settles** them. Do not confuse them.\n\n### Other participants\n\n| Participant | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Depository Participants (DPs)** | The **agents of the depository** — the investor's point of contact for a demat account |\n| **Custodians** | Safe-keep securities and handle settlement/corporate actions for institutional investors |\n| **Stock brokers / Authorised Persons** | Execute trades on behalf of clients. An **Authorised Person** acts on behalf of a broker (formerly \"sub-broker\") |\n| **Investment banks / merchant bankers** | Manage issues, underwrite, advise on M&A |\n| **Insurance companies** | Provide risk cover; large institutional investors |\n| **Pension funds** | Manage retirement money; long-horizon institutional investors |\n| **AMCs and portfolio managers** | Manage pooled (MF) or individual (PMS) portfolios |\n| **Investment advisers and distributors** | Advise on / distribute financial products |\n| **Credit rating agencies** | Rate debt instruments for credit risk |\n| **KYC Registration Agencies (KRAs)** | Maintain centralised KYC records so a client's KYC is portable across SEBI intermediaries |\n| **Registrar & Transfer Agents (RTAs)** | Maintain investor records, process transactions and corporate actions — CAMS, KFin |\n| **NBFCs and HFCs** | Lend outside the banking system |\n| **Credit bureaus (CICs)** | Maintain individual credit histories and scores |\n\n> 🧠 **Why institutions matter:** mutual funds, insurers and pension funds are large, informed investors. They supply **liquidity and stability**, and improve **price efficiency** because they can source and evaluate information better than retail investors.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Question | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Money market maturity / regulator | **Up to 1 year** / **RBI** |\n| Capital market maturity / regulator | **Beyond 1 year** / **SEBI** |\n| Primary market | Securities **issued**; funds go to the **issuer** |\n| Secondary market | Investors trade **among themselves**; issuer gets nothing |\n| RBI regulates | Banking, monetary policy, currency, money market, **forex**, NBFCs, payment systems, CICs |\n| SEBI regulates | Securities markets, exchanges, brokers, MFs, PMS, IAs, AIFs, CRAs, depositories |\n| IRDAI regulates | Insurance |\n| PFRDA regulates | Pensions (NPS, APY) |\n| MCA / RoC | Companies Act, 2013 / company registration |\n| SEBI statutory status | **1992** |\n| IRDAI established | **1999** |\n| PFRDA statutory status | **2013** |\n| Payment bank deposit cap | **₹2 lakh** per customer; **cannot lend** |\n| Bank vs NBFC | Banks take **demand deposits** and are in the **payment system**; NBFCs are not |\n| The three MIIs | **Exchanges, clearing corporations, depositories** |\n| Depositories in India | **NSDL** and **CDSL** |\n| Clearing corporation's key role | **Central counterparty** — guarantees settlement |\n| Depository Participant | The **agent** of the depository; investor's access point |\n| KRA | Central KYC records, portable across SEBI intermediaries |\n| RTA | Investor records and transaction processing (CAMS, KFin) |\n| Account Aggregator | RBI-regulated **NBFC-AA**; consent-based data sharing; **data blind** |\n| NBFC-P2P | Matches lenders and borrowers; **cannot lend on own book or guarantee returns** |\n| Definition of \"securities\" | From the **SCRA, 1956** |\n| SRO example | **AMFI**; exchanges as first-level regulators of brokers |\n\n> **Exam tip:** Module 2 is worth only **10 marks total** across Chapters 5 and 6. Spend one study session here, memorise the regulator map and the MII roles, and put the saved time into Module 1 (37 marks) or Module 6 (30 marks).\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 5: Introduction to Indian Financial Markets — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The regulator map and the three MIIs are the highest-yield items here.**\n\n**Q1.** Which four apex regulators cover which domains?\n> **A:** **RBI → Rupees** (banking, monetary policy, money market, forex, NBFCs, payments). **SEBI → Securities**. **IRDAI → Insurance**. **PFRDA → Pensions**.\n\n**Q2.** What is the maturity split between the money market and the capital market, and who regulates each?\n> **A:** **Money market = up to 1 year, regulated by the RBI.** **Capital/securities market = beyond 1 year, regulated by SEBI.**\n\n**Q3.** Distinguish the primary and secondary markets.\n> **A:** **Primary** — securities are **issued**, and the funds go **to the issuer**. **Secondary** — investors trade **among themselves**; the issuer receives nothing. The secondary market's function is **liquidity and exit**.\n\n**Q4.** When did SEBI, IRDAI and PFRDA get their statutory status?\n> **A:** **SEBI — 1992** (SEBI Act, 1992). **IRDAI — 1999** (IRDA Act, 1999). **PFRDA — 2013** (PFRDA Act, 2013).\n\n**Q5.** What does the Ministry of Corporate Affairs regulate, and what does the Registrar of Companies do?\n> **A:** The **MCA** administers the **Companies Act, 2013** and corporate governance. The **RoC**, which operates **under the MCA**, handles company registration/incorporation and statutory filings.\n\n**Q6.** Name the three Market Infrastructure Institutions.\n> **A:** **Stock/commodity exchanges**, **clearing corporations**, and **depositories**.\n\n**Q7.** What is the crucial difference between an exchange and a clearing corporation?\n> **A:** The **exchange matches trades** (trading platform and price discovery). The **clearing corporation clears, settles and guarantees** them, acting as the **central counterparty (CCP)** so neither side faces counterparty default.\n\n**Q8.** Name India's two depositories, and what do they hold?\n> **A:** **NSDL** and **CDSL** — they hold securities in **electronic (dematerialised)** form.\n\n**Q9.** What is a Depository Participant?\n> **A:** The **agent of the depository** — the investor's point of contact for opening and operating a **demat account**.\n\n**Q10.** State the key differences between a bank and an NBFC.\n> **A:** Banks can accept **demand deposits** and are part of the **payment and settlement system**; NBFCs **cannot** and are **not**. NBFCs also cannot issue cheques drawn on themselves, and their deposits are **not DICGC-insured**.\n\n**Q11.** What is a payment bank allowed and not allowed to do?\n> **A:** It may accept deposits **up to ₹2 lakh per customer** and provide payments and remittances. It **cannot lend** or issue credit cards.\n\n**Q12.** What is a small finance bank?\n> **A:** A full-service bank with a **priority-sector focus** — small borrowers, micro-enterprises and unbanked segments.\n\n**Q13.** Who now regulates Housing Finance Companies?\n> **A:** The **RBI** (supervision moved across from the National Housing Bank).\n\n**Q14.** What is an NBFC-P2P, and what are its key restrictions?\n> **A:** An RBI-registered platform that **matches individual lenders with borrowers**. It is only an intermediary — it **cannot lend from its own book and cannot guarantee returns**.\n\n**Q15.** What is an Account Aggregator, and what does \"data blind\" mean?\n> **A:** An RBI-regulated **NBFC-AA** that, with the customer's **explicit consent**, retrieves and shares their financial data across institutions. **Data blind** means the AA itself **cannot read or store** the data it moves.\n\n**Q16.** Which Act defines \"securities\", and what does the definition include?\n> **A:** The **Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956 (SCRA)** — covering shares, scrips, stocks, bonds, debentures, derivatives, units of collective investment schemes, security receipts and government securities.\n\n**Q17.** What is a KYC Registration Agency (KRA)?\n> **A:** It maintains **centralised KYC records** so a client's KYC is **portable across SEBI-registered intermediaries** — no need to repeat KYC with each one.\n\n**Q18.** What does a Registrar & Transfer Agent do?\n> **A:** Maintains **investor records** and processes transactions and corporate actions on behalf of issuers and mutual funds — e.g. **CAMS** and **KFin**.\n\n**Q19.** What does a custodian do?\n> **A:** **Safe-keeps securities** and handles settlement and corporate actions, mainly for **institutional investors**.\n\n**Q20.** What is an Authorised Person?\n> **A:** A person acting **on behalf of a stock broker** to reach clients — the role that replaced the former \"sub-broker\" category.\n\n**Q21.** What is a Self-Regulatory Organisation, and give examples.\n> **A:** An industry body empowered to set and enforce standards for its **members**, under the apex regulator's oversight. Examples: **AMFI** (mutual fund distributors); **stock exchanges** as first-level regulators of their brokers.\n\n**Q22.** Why do SROs exist?\n> **A:** Closer **industry knowledge**, **faster rule-making**, and **lower regulatory cost** than doing everything through the apex regulator.\n\n**Q23.** What is the core function of any financial market?\n> **A:** To move funds efficiently from **surplus units (savers)** to **deficit units (borrowers/users)** through intermediaries.\n\n**Q24.** Name the features of a well-functioning financial market.\n> **A:** Efficient **transfer of funds**, **price discovery**, **liquidity**, **information dissemination**, **regulation**, and **risk management**.\n\n**Q25.** Why do institutional investors improve market quality?\n> **A:** They are **large and informed**, so they supply **liquidity and stability** and improve **price efficiency** — they can source and evaluate information better than retail investors.\n\n**Q26.** Which markets sit under the RBI besides banking?\n> **A:** The **money market** and the **foreign exchange market**, plus payment systems, NBFCs and credit information companies.\n\n**Q27.** Name the credit rating agencies operating in India.\n> **A:** **CRISIL, ICRA, CARE and India Ratings** — they assess the creditworthiness of debt issues and are **SEBI-regulated**.\n\n**Q28.** What sits under PFRDA?\n> **A:** The **National Pension System (NPS)**, **Atal Pension Yojana**, pension fund managers, the **Central Recordkeeping Agency (CRA)**, points of presence, and the NPS Trust.\n\n**Q29.** What is the minimum investment for a Portfolio Management Service?\n> **A:** **₹50 lakh**.\n\n**Q30.** What does \"financialisation of savings\" mean in the Indian context?\n> **A:** The gradual shift of household savings **away from physical assets** (gold, real estate) **toward financial assets** — increasingly via mutual funds and SIPs.\n\n**Q31.** Which money market instruments should you be able to name?\n> **A:** **Treasury bills, commercial paper, certificates of deposit, call money and repo** — all with maturities of **up to one year**.\n\n**Q32.** How much of the exam is Module 2 worth, and what does that imply?\n> **A:** Only **10 marks** across Chapters 5–6. Read it once, drill the regulator map and MII roles, and redirect the time to Module 1 (37 marks) or Module 6 (30 marks).\n", "questions": "# Chapter 5: Introduction to Indian Financial Markets — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Scenario & Discrimination). **Module 2 is worth only 10 marks in total**, and these questions are almost entirely recall — the aim is to make the regulator map and institutional roles automatic. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The money market deals with instruments of maturity:\nA) Above 1 year  B) Up to 1 year  C) Above 5 years  D) Exactly 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The money market is the **short-term** segment: maturities of **up to one year**.\nA) and C) describe the capital/securities market. D) No single maturity defines a market segment.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The money market in India is regulated by:\nA) SEBI  B) IRDAI  C) RBI  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **RBI** regulates the money market, alongside banking, currency, forex and payment systems.\nA) SEBI regulates the securities (capital) market. B) IRDAI regulates insurance. D) PFRDA regulates pensions.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which regulator supervises mutual funds in India?\nA) RBI  B) SEBI  C) IRDAI  D) AMFI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutual funds are **securities market** products and fall under **SEBI**.\nA) RBI regulates banks and NBFCs. C) IRDAI regulates insurance. D) **AMFI is an SRO**, not the regulator — it operates under SEBI's oversight.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** SEBI was given statutory status in:\nA) 1988  B) 1992  C) 1999  D) 2013\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **SEBI Act, 1992** gave SEBI statutory powers. (It existed as a non-statutory body from 1988.)\nA) 1988 is when SEBI was first constituted, without statutory backing. C) 1999 is IRDAI. D) 2013 is PFRDA.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** PFRDA received statutory status under an Act of:\nA) 1992  B) 1999  C) 2013  D) 1956\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **PFRDA Act, 2013**.\nA) Is SEBI. B) Is IRDA. D) Is the SCRA.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Which of these is NOT a Market Infrastructure Institution?\nA) Stock exchange  B) Clearing corporation  C) Depository  D) Credit rating agency\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The three **MIIs** are **exchanges, clearing corporations and depositories**. A credit rating agency is a SEBI-registered intermediary but not an MII.\nA), B) and C) are the three MIIs — systemically important and the most heavily regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The two depositories in India are:\nA) NSE and BSE  B) NSDL and CDSL  C) CAMS and KFin  D) CRISIL and ICRA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **NSDL** and **CDSL** hold securities in dematerialised form.\nA) NSE and BSE are **exchanges**. C) CAMS and KFin are **RTAs**. D) CRISIL and ICRA are **credit rating agencies**.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A clearing corporation acts as:\nA) The trading platform  B) The central counterparty guaranteeing settlement  C) The company registrar  D) The investor's broker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — As **CCP**, it steps between buyer and seller so neither bears counterparty default risk.\nA) That is the exchange's role. C) That is the RoC. D) Brokers execute trades for clients.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which of the following can a payment bank NOT do?\nA) Accept deposits up to ₹2 lakh  B) Provide remittance services  C) Lend money  D) Issue debit cards\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Payment banks are **prohibited from lending** and from issuing credit cards.\nA), B) and D) are all permitted activities.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The deposit cap per customer for a payment bank is:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹2 lakh  C) ₹5 lakh  D) ₹10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹2 lakh** per individual customer.\nA), C) and D) are not the prescribed limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Which is a key difference between a bank and an NBFC?\nA) NBFCs can accept demand deposits  B) Banks can accept demand deposits and are part of the payment system; NBFCs cannot and are not  C) NBFCs cannot lend  D) Banks cannot lend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the defining distinction.\nA) Reverses it — NBFCs specifically **cannot** accept demand deposits. C) Lending is an NBFC's core business. D) Lending is a core banking function.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** \"Securities\" are defined under which Act?\nA) Companies Act, 2013  B) SEBI Act, 1992  C) Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956  D) FEMA, 1999\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **SCRA, 1956** contains the statutory definition of securities.\nA) Governs companies. B) Establishes SEBI and its powers. D) Governs foreign exchange.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The Registrar of Companies operates under:\nA) SEBI  B) The Ministry of Corporate Affairs  C) The RBI  D) IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **RoC** functions under the **MCA**, which administers the Companies Act, 2013.\nA), C) and D) have no authority over company incorporation.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** In the primary market:\nA) Investors trade among themselves  B) Securities are issued and funds go to the issuer  C) Only government securities are traded  D) No new capital is raised\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **issuer receives the money** in the primary market.\nA) Describes the secondary market. C) All types of issuers use the primary market. D) Raising capital is precisely its purpose.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** The main function of the secondary market is to:\nA) Raise fresh capital for issuers  B) Provide liquidity and an exit route to investors  C) Register companies  D) Set interest rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Secondary trading gives investors the ability to **exit and re-allocate**, which in turn makes primary issuance possible.\nA) That is the primary market. C) That is the RoC. D) The RBI sets policy rates.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** IRDAI was established in:\nA) 1992  B) 1999  C) 2013  D) 1956\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the **IRDA Act, 1999**.\nA) Is SEBI. C) Is PFRDA. D) Is the SCRA.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** An Account Aggregator is regulated by:\nA) SEBI  B) The RBI, as an NBFC-AA  C) IRDAI  D) The MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Account Aggregators are **RBI-regulated NBFC-AAs**.\nA), C) and D) have no jurisdiction over account aggregation.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** \"Data blind\" in the context of Account Aggregators means:\nA) The AA can read all customer data  B) The AA cannot read or store the data it transfers  C) Customers cannot see their data  D) The data is deleted immediately\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The AA is a **consent-driven pipe**: it moves data but cannot read or retain it.\nA) Reverses the design. C) The customer controls and can see their data. D) The AA does not hold the data to begin with.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An NBFC-P2P platform:\nA) Lends from its own balance sheet  B) Matches individual lenders with borrowers and cannot guarantee returns  C) Is regulated by SEBI  D) Can accept demand deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is a **pure intermediary**, RBI-registered, and may neither lend on its own book nor guarantee returns.\nA) Explicitly prohibited. C) It is **RBI**-regulated. D) No NBFC can accept demand deposits.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A Depository Participant is:\nA) The depository itself  B) An agent of the depository and the investor's access point  C) A stock exchange  D) A credit rating agency\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **DP** is the depository's agent, through which an investor opens and operates a demat account.\nA) The DP acts *for* the depository. C) and D) are different institutions entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** AMFI is an example of:\nA) An apex regulator  B) A self-regulatory organisation  C) A depository  D) A clearing corporation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — AMFI is an **SRO** for mutual fund distributors, operating under SEBI's oversight.\nA) SEBI is the apex regulator here. C) and D) are MIIs.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which body regulates the foreign exchange market in India?\nA) SEBI  B) RBI  C) IRDAI  D) MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **RBI** administers **FEMA** and regulates the forex market.\nA), C) and D) have no forex jurisdiction.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Housing Finance Companies are now regulated by:\nA) The National Housing Bank alone  B) The RBI  C) SEBI  D) IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Supervision of HFCs moved to the **RBI**.\nA) The NHB retains a role but the regulator is the RBI. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A KYC Registration Agency exists to:\nA) Rate debt securities  B) Maintain centralised KYC records portable across SEBI intermediaries  C) Settle trades  D) Register companies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **KRA** removes the need to repeat KYC with every intermediary.\nA) Is a credit rating agency. C) Is a clearing corporation. D) Is the RoC.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** CAMS and KFin are examples of:\nA) Depositories  B) Registrar and Transfer Agents  C) Stock exchanges  D) Custodians\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **RTAs** maintain investor records and process transactions for mutual funds and issuers.\nA) Are NSDL and CDSL. C) Are NSE and BSE. D) Custodians serve institutional investors.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which of these is a money market instrument?\nA) Equity share  B) Treasury bill  C) 10-year government bond  D) Debenture with 7-year maturity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **T-bills** mature within a year (91, 182 or 364 days).\nA) Equity has no maturity. C) and D) are long-term, hence capital market instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** An Authorised Person in the securities market:\nA) Is the same as a depository  B) Acts on behalf of a stock broker to reach clients  C) Regulates the exchange  D) Rates bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Authorised Person category replaced the earlier \"sub-broker\".\nA), C) and D) describe entirely different entities.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** The minimum investment in a Portfolio Management Service is:\nA) ₹5 lakh  B) ₹25 lakh  C) ₹50 lakh  D) ₹1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹50 lakh** is the SEBI-prescribed minimum.\nA), B) and D) are not the prescribed threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which of these is regulated by PFRDA?\nA) Mutual funds  B) The National Pension System  C) Health insurance  D) Commercial banks\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **NPS** and **Atal Pension Yojana** fall under PFRDA.\nA) SEBI. C) IRDAI. D) RBI.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Credit rating agencies in India are regulated by:\nA) The RBI  B) SEBI  C) IRDAI  D) The MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CRAs are **SEBI-registered intermediaries**.\nA) The RBI regulates CICs (credit bureaus), which are different from CRAs. C) and D) have no jurisdiction.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** The role of a custodian is to:\nA) Match trades on the exchange  B) Safe-keep securities and handle settlement for institutional investors  C) Issue credit ratings  D) Advise retail investors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Custodians provide safe-keeping and post-trade servicing, mainly for institutions.\nA) Is the exchange. C) Is a CRA. D) Is an investment adviser.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Which statement about a Self-Regulatory Organisation is correct?\nA) It replaces the apex regulator  B) It sets and enforces standards for its members, under the apex regulator's oversight  C) It has no members  D) It regulates all financial sectors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An SRO regulates **its own members**, subject to the statutory regulator.\nA) SROs operate under, not instead of, the regulator. C) Membership is its basis. D) Each SRO covers only its own segment.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Small finance banks are characterised by:\nA) An inability to accept deposits  B) A priority-sector focus on small borrowers and unbanked segments  C) A ban on lending  D) Regulation by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — They provide full banking services with a mandate toward **small borrowers, micro-enterprises and the unbanked**.\nA) They accept deposits normally. C) Lending is their purpose. D) They are RBI-regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The Ministry of Finance is primarily responsible for:\nA) Registering companies  B) Overall economic and fiscal policy, and the Budget  C) Clearing trades  D) Rating debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The MoF sets fiscal and economic policy and supervises the regulators.\nA) Is the RoC/MCA. C) Is the clearing corporation. D) Is a CRA.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Why are institutional investors important to market quality?\nA) They reduce liquidity  B) They provide liquidity and stability and improve price efficiency  C) They are less informed than retail investors  D) They are prohibited from large trades\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Being large and well-informed, they add depth and help prices reflect information.\nA) They add liquidity. C) They are generally better resourced for research. D) There is no such prohibition.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A company wants to raise ₹500 crore by issuing fresh equity to the public. Which market and which regulator apply?\nA) Money market; RBI  B) Primary capital market; SEBI  C) Secondary market; SEBI  D) Forex market; RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **fresh issue of equity** is a **primary market** transaction in the **capital market**, regulated by **SEBI**.\nA) The money market handles short-term debt. C) The secondary market involves no fresh capital. D) Forex is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** An investor sells 100 shares of a listed company on the NSE. Which statements are correct?\nA) The company receives the sale proceeds  B) This is a secondary market trade; the company receives nothing  C) This is a primary issue  D) SEBI receives the proceeds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Trading between investors is a **secondary market** transaction — the issuer is not a party and receives nothing.\nA) The issuer receives money only in the **primary** market. C) A primary issue involves new securities from the issuer. D) SEBI is the regulator, not a counterparty.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A client asks who would handle a complaint about a mis-sold ULIP. The correct regulator is:\nA) SEBI  B) IRDAI  C) RBI  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **ULIP is an insurance product**, so **IRDAI** is the regulator despite its investment component.\nA) SEBI covers mutual funds and securities. C) RBI covers banking. D) PFRDA covers pensions. **This is a favourite trap: ULIPs feel like investments but are regulated as insurance.**\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A trade is executed on the exchange but the buyer defaults on payment. Who protects the seller?\nA) The stock broker personally  B) The clearing corporation, acting as central counterparty  C) SEBI directly  D) The depository\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — As **CCP**, the clearing corporation guarantees settlement using margins and the settlement guarantee fund.\nA) Brokers have obligations but the systemic guarantee sits with the CCP. C) SEBI regulates but does not settle trades. D) The depository transfers securities; it does not guarantee settlement.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** An investor completes KYC with one mutual fund and then invests with a second. Under the KRA framework:\nA) Full KYC must be repeated  B) The KYC record is available through the KRA, so it need not be repeated  C) KYC is not required at all  D) Only the RBI can verify KYC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **KYC is portable** across SEBI intermediaries through the **KRA**.\nA) That is precisely what the KRA system avoids. C) KYC is mandatory. D) KRAs are SEBI-registered.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A fintech app wants to pull a customer's bank, mutual fund and insurance data into one dashboard, with the customer's permission. The correct regulated channel is:\nA) A credit information company  B) An Account Aggregator (NBFC-AA)  C) A depository participant  D) A clearing corporation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is exactly the **Account Aggregator** use case: **consent-based** retrieval and sharing of financial data.\nA) CICs hold credit histories only. C) DPs handle demat accounts. D) CCPs settle trades.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Which entity would a company approach to have its ₹200 crore debenture issue assessed for credit quality?\nA) A credit information company  B) A credit rating agency  C) A depository  D) An Account Aggregator\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **CRAs** (CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, India Ratings) rate **debt instruments**.\nA) CICs score **individuals**, not corporate debt issues. C) and D) have unrelated functions. **Note the CRA/CIC distinction: CRA rates issues (SEBI); CIC scores people (RBI).**\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A retail investor holds shares in electronic form. Which institution ultimately holds them?\nA) The stock exchange  B) A depository (NSDL or CDSL), accessed through a DP  C) The clearing corporation  D) SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Securities sit in the **depository**, and the investor accesses them via a **Depository Participant**.\nA) Exchanges match trades. C) CCPs settle them. D) SEBI regulates.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which of the following is NOT permitted for an NBFC?\nA) Lending to retail customers  B) Accepting demand deposits  C) Investing in securities  D) Providing asset finance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — NBFCs **cannot accept demand deposits** (some categories may accept term deposits, subject to conditions).\nA), C) and D) are all core NBFC activities.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A client wants to know why their bank deposit is safer than an NBFC deposit. The best answer is:\nA) NBFCs charge higher interest  B) Bank deposits carry DICGC insurance cover; NBFC deposits do not  C) NBFCs are unregulated  D) Banks never fail\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **DICGC deposit insurance** applies to bank deposits, not NBFC deposits.\nA) True of loans, but not the safety reason for deposits. C) NBFCs are RBI-regulated. D) Banks can and do fail — which is why deposit insurance exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A borrower complains about a housing finance company's practices. Which regulator has jurisdiction?\nA) SEBI  B) The RBI  C) IRDAI  D) The MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — HFC supervision sits with the **RBI**.\nA), C) and D) have no jurisdiction over housing finance companies.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which pairing of regulator and entity is INCORRECT?\nA) SEBI — portfolio managers  B) RBI — payment banks  C) IRDAI — Alternative Investment Funds  D) PFRDA — pension fund managers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **AIFs are regulated by SEBI**, not IRDAI.\nA), B) and D) are all correct pairings.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** An individual wants their credit score before applying for a home loan. They should approach:\nA) A credit rating agency such as CRISIL  B) A credit information company such as CIBIL  C) A depository  D) SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **CICs** (CIBIL, Experian, Equifax, CRIF High Mark) maintain individual credit histories and scores.\nA) CRAs rate **debt issues**, not individuals. C) and D) have no role. **They are entitled to one free full report per calendar year from each CIC.**\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which activity clearly belongs to the primary market?\nA) Buying shares on the NSE from another investor  B) Applying to an IPO  C) Selling shares through a broker  D) Transferring shares between demat accounts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Applying to an **IPO** means subscribing to newly issued securities from the issuer.\nA), C) and D) are all secondary market or post-trade activities.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A stock exchange takes disciplinary action against a member broker. This is an example of:\nA) The exchange acting as a first-level regulator / SRO  B) SEBI's direct enforcement  C) A clearing corporation function  D) An illegal act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Exchanges act as **first-level regulators** of their members, under SEBI's oversight.\nB) SEBI can act directly, but this describes exchange-level action. C) Clearing corporations handle settlement risk. D) It is a legitimate SRO function.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which of these best explains why the secondary market matters to a company raising capital?\nA) The company earns from secondary trades  B) Investors will only subscribe to primary issues if they can exit later  C) It sets the company's tax rate  D) It replaces the need for a primary issue\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Liquidity in the secondary market is what makes primary subscription attractive.** Without an exit route, investors would demand a much higher return.\nA) The company receives nothing from secondary trades. C) Tax is unrelated. D) Fresh capital can only come from a primary issue.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** An investor holds units of an equity mutual fund and complains about the AMC. The escalation path leads to:\nA) IRDAI  B) SEBI (via SCORES)  C) The RBI Ombudsman  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutual funds are SEBI-regulated, so complaints escalate through **SEBI's SCORES** platform.\nA) Insurance. C) Banking. D) Pensions. **The regulator follows the product, not the distributor.**\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Which statement about commercial paper is correct?\nA) It is a long-term equity instrument  B) It is a short-term money market instrument issued by corporates  C) It is issued only by the government  D) It is regulated by IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **CP** is unsecured short-term corporate borrowing in the **money market**.\nA) It is debt, not equity, and short-term. C) Government short-term paper is the **T-bill**. D) The money market is RBI's domain.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which combination correctly describes the flow in a financial market?\nA) Deficit units → intermediaries → surplus units  B) Surplus units (savers) → intermediaries → deficit units (borrowers)  C) Regulators → banks → savers  D) Borrowers → regulators → savers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Funds move **from savers, through intermediaries, to users of funds**.\nA) Reverses the direction. C) and D) misplace regulators in the funding chain — they supervise, they do not intermediate funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A P2P platform promises lenders \"assured 14% returns\". This is:\nA) Permitted if disclosed  B) Not permitted — NBFC-P2P platforms cannot guarantee returns  C) Required by the RBI  D) Permitted for amounts under ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An NBFC-P2P is a **matching intermediary only**; guaranteeing returns is expressly prohibited, as is lending from its own book.\nA) Disclosure does not cure a prohibited practice. C) The RBI prohibits precisely this. D) There is no such amount-based exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which entity would maintain the unit-holder records of a mutual fund scheme?\nA) A depository  B) A Registrar and Transfer Agent  C) A clearing corporation  D) A credit rating agency\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **RTAs** such as CAMS and KFin maintain investor folios and process transactions.\nA) Depositories hold demat securities. C) CCPs settle exchange trades. D) CRAs rate debt.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** An investor asks why India needs both NSDL and CDSL. The best answer is:\nA) One handles equity and one handles debt  B) Competition and redundancy — both are depositories offering the same core service  C) One is regulated by SEBI, the other by the RBI  D) One is for retail, one for institutions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both are **SEBI-regulated depositories** performing the same function; having two provides **competition and systemic redundancy**.\nA) Both handle all security types. C) Both are SEBI-regulated. D) Both serve all investor categories.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which of these is a capital market instrument?\nA) Certificate of deposit  B) Call money  C) A 10-year corporate debenture  D) A 91-day treasury bill\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Maturity **beyond one year** places it in the capital market.\nA), B) and D) are all short-term money market instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A client is confused between a credit rating agency and a credit information company. The correct distinction is:\nA) They are the same  B) A CRA rates debt issues (SEBI-regulated); a CIC maintains individual credit histories (RBI-regulated)  C) A CRA rates individuals  D) A CIC rates corporate bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **CRA → issues → SEBI. CIC → individuals → RBI.**\nA) They are distinct in function and regulator. C) and D) swap their roles.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which best describes the \"financialisation of savings\" in India?\nA) A shift from financial assets to gold and property  B) A shift from physical assets toward financial assets such as mutual funds  C) A fall in the savings rate  D) An increase in cash hoarding\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Household savings are moving **away from gold and real estate toward financial assets**, notably via mutual funds and SIPs.\nA) Reverses the trend. C) The savings rate is a separate matter. D) The opposite of financialisation.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A client asks whether a mutual fund distributor is regulated by AMFI or SEBI. The correct answer is:\nA) AMFI alone, as the apex regulator  B) SEBI is the statutory regulator; AMFI is the SRO that registers distributors and sets standards  C) The RBI  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SEBI is the statutory regulator**; **AMFI** operates as the industry SRO (issuing ARN registration and a code of conduct).\nA) AMFI is not an apex regulator. C) The RBI has no role. D) Distributors are certainly regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which risk does the central counterparty mechanism primarily eliminate for market participants?\nA) Market risk  B) Counterparty (settlement) risk  C) Inflation risk  D) Liquidity risk in the underlying stock\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — By novating trades, the CCP removes the risk that **the other side fails to deliver or pay**.\nA) Price movements are unaffected. C) Inflation is macroeconomic. D) A stock's own liquidity is not guaranteed by the CCP.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A payment bank customer wants a home loan from the same institution. The adviser should explain:\nA) Payment banks offer the cheapest home loans  B) Payment banks cannot lend, so the customer must approach a bank, HFC or NBFC  C) Payment banks lend only above ₹50 lakh  D) SEBI must approve the loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lending is **outside a payment bank's permitted activities**.\nA) They offer no loans at all. C) There is no such threshold. D) SEBI has no role in lending.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which regulator would handle a grievance about the National Pension System?\nA) SEBI  B) IRDAI  C) PFRDA  D) The MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **PFRDA** regulates the NPS and its intermediaries.\nA) Securities. B) Insurance. D) Companies.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which of these features is NOT a characteristic of a well-functioning financial market?\nA) Price discovery  B) Liquidity  C) Restricted information available only to insiders  D) Effective regulation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Well-functioning markets require **wide information dissemination**; restricting information to insiders is the definition of a market failure (and, in the case of unpublished price-sensitive information, insider trading).\nA), B) and D) are all essential characteristics.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A corporate treasurer needs to park ₹50 crore for 60 days. The appropriate market is:\nA) The equity market  B) The money market  C) The derivatives market  D) The real estate market\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **60-day** horizon calls for money market instruments (T-bills, CDs, CPs, liquid funds).\nA) Equity is far too volatile for 60 days. C) Derivatives are for hedging or speculation, not parking cash. D) Property is entirely illiquid over that horizon.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which statement about the RBI's role is INCORRECT?\nA) It regulates the money market  B) It administers FEMA  C) It regulates mutual funds  D) It oversees payment systems\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Mutual funds are SEBI's domain**, not the RBI's.\nA), B) and D) are all correct RBI responsibilities.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** An investor's securities are transferred from their demat account after a sale. Which institutions were involved?\nA) Only the exchange  B) The exchange (matching), the clearing corporation (settlement) and the depository via the DP (securities transfer)  C) Only SEBI  D) Only the RTA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three MIIs play distinct roles in a single trade's lifecycle.\nA) The exchange only matches. C) SEBI regulates but does not execute. D) RTAs serve issuers and mutual funds, not exchange settlement.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which is the correct hierarchy for a mutual fund distributor's oversight?\nA) Distributor → AMFI (SRO) → SEBI (statutory regulator)  B) Distributor → RBI → SEBI  C) Distributor → IRDAI → SEBI  D) Distributor → SEBI → AMFI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — First-level oversight sits with the **SRO (AMFI)**, and above it the statutory regulator **SEBI**.\nB) and C) insert the wrong regulators. D) Inverts the hierarchy — SEBI sits above AMFI, not below.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A client holds a fixed deposit with an NBFC and asks about safety. The key points are:\nA) It is fully guaranteed by the RBI  B) It is not DICGC-insured, so credit rating and the NBFC's financial strength matter  C) It is regulated by SEBI  D) NBFCs cannot accept any deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **No deposit insurance** applies, so the NBFC's own creditworthiness is the protection.\nA) The RBI regulates but does not guarantee. C) NBFCs are RBI-regulated. D) Some NBFC categories may accept **term** (not demand) deposits.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Scenario & Discrimination (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client is sold a product described as \"a market-linked investment with life cover\". Which regulator governs it, and why does this matter for grievance redress?\nA) SEBI, because it is market-linked  B) IRDAI, because it is a ULIP — an insurance product — so grievances go to the insurer then the Insurance Ombudsman  C) The RBI, because a bank sold it  D) PFRDA, because it is long-term\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **ULIP is an insurance contract** regardless of its investment component, so **IRDAI** applies and the escalation path is insurer → **Insurance Ombudsman**, not SCORES.\nA) Market linkage does not move it to SEBI. C) The **distribution channel does not determine the regulator** — a bank selling a ULIP is acting as a corporate agent. D) Duration is irrelevant to jurisdiction. **The principle: the regulator follows the product, not the seller.**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A trade is executed on the NSE on Monday and settles on Tuesday (T+1). Match the institutions to their roles.\nA) NSE settles; NSDL matches; the DP guarantees  B) NSE matches; the clearing corporation clears, guarantees and settles; NSDL/CDSL transfers securities via the DP  C) SEBI matches and settles  D) The RTA does everything\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each MII has a distinct function: **exchange = matching; clearing corporation = clearing, guaranteeing and settling; depository = holding and transferring securities** (accessed through the DP).\nA) Scrambles all three roles. C) SEBI is the regulator, not an operator. D) RTAs serve issuers and mutual funds, not exchange settlement.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A fintech firm proposes to (i) aggregate customer financial data with consent, (ii) lend from its own book, and (iii) match retail lenders with borrowers. Which registrations would it need, and what problem arises?\nA) A single NBFC licence covers all three  B) NBFC-AA for (i) and NBFC-P2P for (iii) — but an NBFC-P2P cannot lend from its own book, so (ii) conflicts with (iii)  C) SEBI registration for all three  D) No registration is needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **NBFC-AA** covers consent-based data aggregation; **NBFC-P2P** covers lender–borrower matching. The conflict: an **NBFC-P2P is barred from lending on its own balance sheet**, so activities (ii) and (iii) cannot sit in the same entity.\nA) These are distinct licence categories with distinct conditions. C) All three are **RBI** matters, not SEBI. D) All three are regulated activities.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Which of the following correctly distinguishes a CRA from a CIC on all three dimensions — what they assess, who they assess, and their regulator?\nA) CRA: debt issues, corporates, SEBI · CIC: credit history, individuals, RBI  B) CRA: individuals, retail, RBI · CIC: bonds, corporates, SEBI  C) Both assess individuals and are RBI-regulated  D) Both assess debt issues and are SEBI-regulated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **CRA** → rates **debt instruments** issued by **corporates/governments** → **SEBI**. **CIC** → maintains **credit histories/scores** of **individuals (and firms)** → **RBI**.\nB) Swaps them entirely. C) and D) collapse two distinct institutions into one.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** An investor argues that because their broker is SEBI-registered, their money is guaranteed against loss. The correct response is:\nA) Correct — SEBI guarantees investments  B) Registration ensures regulatory oversight and conduct standards, but market risk remains entirely with the investor  C) Only losses above ₹1 lakh are covered  D) The clearing corporation reimburses market losses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regulation addresses **conduct and systemic integrity**, not investment outcomes. **No regulator guarantees returns or protects against market losses.**\nA) SEBI explicitly does not guarantee investments. C) There is no such market-loss cover (investor protection funds address broker *default*, not market losses). D) The CCP guarantees **settlement**, not price outcomes.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Which sequence correctly traces the escalation of a complaint about a stock broker?\nA) SEBI SCORES → the broker → SAT  B) The broker → the stock exchange → SEBI SCORES → the Securities Appellate Tribunal  C) The RBI Ombudsman → SEBI  D) The Insurance Ombudsman → SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Always **start with the intermediary**, then its first-level regulator (the **exchange**, acting as SRO), then **SEBI via SCORES**, and finally the **SAT**.\nA) Inverts the first two steps — you must approach the intermediary first. C) and D) route a securities complaint through banking and insurance channels.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A corporate wants to raise short-term funds for 90 days and long-term funds for 10 years. Which markets, instruments and regulators apply?\nA) Both from the money market under the RBI  B) 90 days: money market (commercial paper), RBI; 10 years: capital market (debentures/bonds), SEBI  C) Both from the capital market under SEBI  D) Both from the forex market\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **maturity determines the market**: ≤1 year is the money market (RBI); >1 year is the capital market (SEBI).\nA) A 10-year borrowing cannot be a money market instrument. C) A 90-day borrowing is not a capital market instrument. D) Forex concerns currency, not tenor.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A client holds: a bank FD, an NBFC FD, an equity mutual fund, a ULIP and an NPS account. How many different apex regulators are involved?\nA) One  B) Two  C) Three  D) Four\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **RBI** (bank FD and NBFC FD), **SEBI** (equity mutual fund), **IRDAI** (ULIP) and **PFRDA** (NPS) — **four** regulators.\nA), B) and C) undercount. **This is exactly why an investment adviser must know the regulator map — each product has its own grievance route.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Which statement about SROs is MOST accurate?\nA) An SRO's rules override the statutory regulator's  B) An SRO regulates its own members with closer industry knowledge and lower regulatory cost, but remains subject to the apex regulator  C) SROs are unregulated  D) Every financial sector has exactly one SRO\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SROs add **proximity and efficiency**, always **subordinate** to the statutory regulator.\nA) Statutory rules always prevail. C) SROs operate under regulatory recognition and oversight. D) The number varies by segment, and some segments have none.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** An investor's broker collapses and client securities are missing. Which protection applies?\nA) DICGC deposit insurance  B) The exchange's Investor Protection Fund, subject to limits — this covers broker default, not market losses  C) SEBI reimburses the full amount  D) The depository compensates all losses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exchanges maintain **Investor Protection Funds** for claims arising from a **defaulting member**, subject to prescribed limits.\nA) DICGC covers **bank deposits** only. C) SEBI does not directly compensate investors. D) The depository holds securities but is not a compensation fund. **Note the distinction: default protection exists; market-loss protection does not.**\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Which of these entities is systemically the MOST heavily regulated, and why?\nA) A mutual fund distributor, because it faces retail investors  B) The Market Infrastructure Institutions, because their failure would disrupt the entire market  C) A credit rating agency, because it rates debt  D) An investment adviser, because it gives advice\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Exchanges, clearing corporations and depositories** are **systemically important**: their failure would halt trading, settlement or custody market-wide. Hence the heaviest capital, governance and operational requirements.\nA), C) and D) are all regulated, but their individual failure does not threaten market-wide functioning.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A payment bank wants to expand. Which of these is it permitted to do?\nA) Issue credit cards  B) Offer term loans  C) Distribute mutual funds and insurance as a third-party product  D) Accept ₹10 lakh deposits from a single customer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Payment banks may act as **distributors of third-party products** such as mutual funds and insurance.\nA) Credit cards are prohibited. B) Lending is prohibited. D) The per-customer deposit cap is **₹2 lakh**.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Which correctly explains why the primary and secondary markets are interdependent?\nA) They are entirely separate  B) Secondary market liquidity and price discovery determine what investors will pay in a primary issue — and whether they subscribe at all  C) The primary market sets secondary prices permanently  D) Only the secondary market raises capital\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investors subscribe to new issues only if they believe they can **exit** later at a fair price; secondary market conditions therefore drive primary issuance volumes and pricing.\nA) They are tightly linked. C) Primary pricing is a starting point, immediately re-priced by the secondary market. D) Only the **primary** market raises capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A client says: \"I'll keep my emergency fund with an NBFC because it pays 2% more than the bank.\" What are the two key points to raise?\nA) NBFCs are unregulated and illegal  B) NBFC deposits are not DICGC-insured, and the higher rate compensates for higher credit risk — both matter for money that must be certain when needed  C) NBFCs pay lower rates  D) There is no difference\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two points: **no deposit insurance**, and the extra yield is **compensation for credit risk**. An emergency fund's defining requirement is **certainty of value**, which is precisely what credit risk undermines.\nA) NBFCs are RBI-regulated and legal. C) NBFCs typically pay **more**, which is the client's point. D) The difference in safety is material.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Which combination of institutions would be involved in a retail investor buying an equity mutual fund through a distributor?\nA) Exchange, clearing corporation, depository only  B) The AMC, the RTA (records), the KRA (KYC), SEBI (regulator), and AMFI (distributor SRO)  C) The RBI and IRDAI  D) Only the AMC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Direct mutual fund purchases do not route through the exchange; instead the **AMC** issues units, the **RTA** maintains folios, the **KRA** holds the KYC record, **SEBI** regulates, and **AMFI** oversees the distributor.\nA) Those are involved when units are bought on an exchange platform, but not in a standard AMC transaction. C) Wrong regulators entirely. D) The AMC alone is insufficient.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** An adviser must explain the difference between the RBI's regulation of a *bank's* mutual fund distribution and SEBI's regulation of the *product*. The correct framing is:\nA) The RBI regulates the mutual fund  B) SEBI regulates the mutual fund product and the distribution conduct; the RBI regulates the bank as a banking entity  C) Neither regulates it  D) The bank regulates itself\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Entity regulation and product regulation are different.** The RBI supervises the bank *as a bank*; SEBI governs the **mutual fund product** and the conduct of its distribution.\nA) The RBI has no jurisdiction over mutual fund schemes. C) Both apply, in their own spheres. D) Self-regulation is not the framework here.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Which is the strongest argument that a clearing corporation reduces systemic risk?\nA) It increases trading volume  B) By novating every trade it converts a web of bilateral exposures into exposures against one well-capitalised, margined counterparty  C) It sets share prices  D) It guarantees profits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Novation** replaces many-to-many counterparty risk with a single, heavily capitalised CCP backed by **margins and a settlement guarantee fund**, so one participant's failure does not cascade.\nA) Volume is an effect, not the risk argument. C) Prices are set by the market. D) No entity guarantees profits.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client asks why KYC needs to be done at all. The best regulatory rationale is:\nA) To increase paperwork  B) To establish investor identity, prevent money laundering (PMLA) and enable suitability assessment  C) To calculate returns  D) To set the interest rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — KYC serves **identity verification**, **anti-money-laundering** compliance under the **PMLA**, and provides the client information needed for **suitability**.\nA) The purpose is substantive, not procedural. C) and D) are unrelated to KYC.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Which correctly ranks these by regulatory scope, broadest first, in the securities market?\nA) AMFI → SEBI → the stock exchange  B) SEBI (statutory apex) → the stock exchange (first-level/SRO) → the broker (member)  C) The broker → SEBI → AMFI  D) The exchange → SEBI → the broker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The hierarchy runs **statutory regulator → SRO/first-level regulator → regulated member**.\nA) Places the SRO above the statutory regulator. C) and D) invert the top of the hierarchy.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An investor wants exposure to a professionally managed portfolio with a ₹60 lakh corpus, seeking individually held securities rather than pooled units. Which vehicle and regulator?\nA) A mutual fund, SEBI  B) A Portfolio Management Service (minimum ₹50 lakh), SEBI  C) An NPS account, PFRDA  D) A ULIP, IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **PMS** holds securities in the **client's own name/demat account** rather than issuing pooled units, and the SEBI minimum is **₹50 lakh** — which ₹60 lakh satisfies.\nA) A mutual fund gives **pooled units**, not individually held securities. C) NPS is a retirement product with a prescribed structure. D) A ULIP is insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Which statement about the definition of \"securities\" is correct?\nA) It is defined in the Companies Act, 2013  B) It comes from the SCRA, 1956 and includes shares, bonds, debentures, derivatives, units of collective investment schemes and government securities  C) It excludes derivatives  D) It covers only equity shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **SCRA, 1956** provides an inclusive definition covering all these instrument types.\nA) The Companies Act governs companies, not the securities definition. C) Derivatives are expressly included. D) The definition is far broader than equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Two clients each lose money: one from a falling equity fund NAV, the other because a broker misappropriated their shares. Which has a compensation route?\nA) Both  B) Only the second — broker misappropriation is a default matter with an Investor Protection Fund route; NAV decline is market risk borne by the investor  C) Only the first  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Market losses are the investor's own risk**; **intermediary default or fraud** is a conduct failure with regulatory and IPF remedies.\nA) NAV decline has no compensation route. C) Reverses the answer. D) The second client does have a route. **This distinction — market risk versus conduct failure — is fundamental.**\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** An adviser is asked whether SEBI can act against a company for false statements in its IPO prospectus. The answer is:\nA) No, only the MCA can act  B) Yes — SEBI regulates public issues and can act under the SEBI Act and the PFUTP Regulations; the MCA/RoC may also have jurisdiction under the Companies Act  C) No regulator has jurisdiction  D) Only the stock exchange can act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Overlapping jurisdiction** is normal: SEBI governs the **public issue and market conduct**, while the MCA/RoC governs **corporate law compliance**.\nA) SEBI clearly has jurisdiction over public issues. C) Multiple authorities have jurisdiction. D) Exchanges have limited listing-related powers only.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which of these is the best example of the RBI and SEBI's jurisdictions meeting?\nA) A bank distributing mutual funds — RBI regulates the bank, SEBI regulates the product and distribution conduct  B) SEBI regulating monetary policy  C) The RBI setting mutual fund expense ratios  D) SEBI issuing currency\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — This is the classic **entity vs product** overlap.\nB) Monetary policy is exclusively the RBI's. C) Expense ratios are set by **SEBI**. D) Currency issuance is exclusively the RBI's.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A client wants to move ₹5 lakh from India to fund a child's education abroad. Which regulator's framework governs this, and under which Act?\nA) SEBI, under the SCRA  B) The RBI, under FEMA (via the Liberalised Remittance Scheme)  C) IRDAI, under the Insurance Act  D) The MCA, under the Companies Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cross-border remittances by residents fall under the **RBI's** administration of **FEMA**, operationalised through the **Liberalised Remittance Scheme**.\nA) The SCRA covers securities contracts. C) and D) are irrelevant to remittances.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Which characterisation of the Indian financial system's evolution is most accurate?\nA) Household savings have shifted entirely into gold  B) There has been a gradual shift from physical assets toward financial assets, with rising retail participation through mutual funds and SIPs  C) Retail participation has collapsed  D) The savings rate has fallen to zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the **financialisation of savings** — a gradual, not complete, shift.\nA) Reverses the trend, and \"entirely\" overstates any shift. C) Retail participation has risen materially. D) India retains a high household savings rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A depository participant refuses to transfer an investor's securities. The escalation route is:\nA) Directly to the Supreme Court  B) The DP → the depository (NSDL/CDSL) → SEBI via SCORES → the SAT  C) The RBI Ombudsman  D) IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Start with the **intermediary (DP)**, escalate to its principal (**the depository**), then **SEBI/SCORES**, then the **SAT**.\nA) Courts are not the first step, and appeals from SEBI go to the SAT. C) Banking channel. D) Insurance channel.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Which best explains why the money market and capital market have different regulators in India?\nA) It is an arbitrary historical accident with no rationale  B) The money market is closely tied to banking liquidity and monetary policy transmission (RBI), while the capital market concerns securities issuance, trading and investor protection (SEBI)  C) The money market is unregulated  D) SEBI regulates both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short-term funding markets are integral to **monetary policy transmission and banking liquidity**, so they sit with the **central bank**; long-term securities markets centre on **issuance, trading and investor protection**, which is SEBI's mandate.\nA) There is a clear functional rationale. C) The RBI regulates it. D) SEBI does not regulate the money market.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** An investment adviser is reviewing a client's holdings and must identify the correct grievance channel for each. Which mapping is entirely correct?\nA) Bank FD → SEBI; ULIP → SEBI; NPS → SEBI  B) Bank FD → RBI Ombudsman; equity MF → SEBI SCORES; ULIP → Insurance Ombudsman; NPS → PFRDA  C) All → SEBI SCORES  D) All → the Consumer Court only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each product routes to **its own regulator's** grievance machinery.\nA) Sends everything to SEBI regardless of product. C) SCORES covers **securities market** entities only. D) Consumer courts are an additional, not exclusive, forum. **Knowing the right channel is a core competence for an IA — see Chapter 20.**\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single statement best captures the structure of Indian financial market regulation?\nA) One regulator supervises everything  B) Sector-specific apex regulators (RBI, SEBI, IRDAI, PFRDA) supervise their domains, supported by SROs and MIIs, with the MoF/MCA setting policy and company law  C) The market is entirely self-regulated  D) Only the government regulates, with no independent bodies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — India uses a **sectoral regulatory model**: four apex regulators by domain, first-level regulation by SROs and MIIs, and policy/company law from the MoF and MCA.\nA) India has no single unified regulator. C) SROs supplement, but do not replace, statutory regulation. D) The regulators are statutory and operationally independent.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** These caselets test whether you can route a real client's products to the right regulator and institution.\n\n### Case: Advising the Bhatt Family's Portfolio\n\nNikhil Bhatt, 44, comes to you with the following holdings and concerns:\n\n1. A **₹12,00,000 fixed deposit** with a scheduled commercial bank.\n2. A **₹6,00,000 fixed deposit** with a well-known NBFC, paying 1.75% more than the bank.\n3. **₹22,00,000 in equity mutual funds**, bought through his bank's relationship manager.\n4. A **ULIP** bought five years ago, which he now believes was mis-sold as a \"guaranteed savings plan\".\n5. An **NPS Tier-I account** with ₹8,00,000.\n6. Shares worth **₹15,00,000** held in a demat account with a broker.\n\nHe also mentions that a **peer-to-peer lending platform** has offered him \"assured returns of 13%\", and asks whether he should move his emergency fund there.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** How many distinct apex regulators govern Nikhil's six holdings?\nA) Two  B) Three  C) Four  D) Six\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Four**: **RBI** (bank FD, NBFC FD), **SEBI** (equity mutual funds, demat shares), **IRDAI** (ULIP), **PFRDA** (NPS).\nA) and B) undercount. D) Six holdings do not mean six regulators — several share a regulator. *(Mapping products to regulators is the first step in advising on grievances and suitability.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Nikhil believes the ULIP was mis-sold. What is the correct escalation path?\nA) SEBI SCORES, because it is market-linked  B) The insurer's grievance cell → IRDAI's Bima Bharosa → the Insurance Ombudsman  C) The RBI Ombudsman, because a bank sold it  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **ULIP is an insurance contract**, so the route is the **insurer first**, then **IRDAI's grievance system**, then the **Insurance Ombudsman**.\nA) Market linkage does not make it a securities product. C) The **selling channel does not determine the regulator** — a bank selling insurance acts as a corporate agent. D) Pensions are unrelated. **Principle: the regulator follows the product, not the seller.**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Nikhil asks whether his NBFC fixed deposit is as safe as his bank FD. What are the two key differences?\nA) They are identical in every respect  B) The bank FD is DICGC-insured (up to the prescribed limit) and the bank is part of the payment system; the NBFC FD has no deposit insurance, and the extra 1.75% compensates for higher credit risk  C) The NBFC FD is safer because it pays more  D) NBFCs cannot accept deposits at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two substantive differences: **deposit insurance** (DICGC applies to bank deposits only) and **credit risk** (the yield premium is compensation, not a free lunch).\nA) They differ materially in safety. C) A higher return **signals higher risk**, it does not reduce it. D) Certain NBFC categories may accept **term** (not demand) deposits, subject to conditions.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** The P2P platform offers \"assured returns of 13%\". What should you tell Nikhil?\nA) It is a good deal for the emergency fund  B) NBFC-P2P platforms are RBI-registered intermediaries that may neither lend on their own book nor guarantee returns — so an \"assured return\" claim is a regulatory red flag, and P2P is unsuitable for an emergency fund in any case  C) P2P is SEBI-regulated and therefore safe  D) He should invest his entire portfolio there\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two separate problems. **Regulatory:** an NBFC-P2P is a **matching intermediary only** and is expressly barred from guaranteeing returns, so the claim itself signals either non-compliance or misrepresentation. **Suitability:** an emergency fund requires **immediate availability and certainty of value**, and P2P lending offers neither — it carries borrower default risk and no liquid exit.\nA) It fails the emergency fund's defining requirements. C) P2P is **RBI**-regulated, and regulation never equals safety. D) Concentrating a portfolio in unsecured retail lending would be grossly unsuitable.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Nikhil's shares are held with a broker. Which institutions are involved in holding and settling them, and what protection exists if the broker defaults?\nA) Only the broker; no protection exists  B) A depository (NSDL/CDSL) holds them via the broker as DP, the clearing corporation guarantees settlement, and the exchange's Investor Protection Fund covers broker default within limits  C) SEBI holds the shares directly  D) The RTA holds the shares and reimburses all losses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **depository** holds the securities (the broker typically acts as DP), the **clearing corporation** guarantees settlement as CCP, and the exchange's **Investor Protection Fund** covers claims arising from **member default**, within prescribed limits.\nA) Multiple institutions and protections are involved. C) SEBI regulates; it does not custody securities. D) RTAs serve issuers and mutual funds, and no body reimburses market losses.\n\n> ⚠️ **The distinction to carry into the exam:** protection exists against **intermediary default and fraud**; it does **not** exist against **market losses**. Holding securities in his own demat account (rather than leaving them in a broker pool) is itself the strongest structural protection Nikhil has.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "06", "title": "Securities Market Segments", "module": "Module 2: Indian Financial Markets", "notes": "# Chapter 6: Securities Market Segments — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 2 · Indian Financial Markets — 10 marks shared with Chapter 5.**\n> Mostly definitional. The highest-yield areas are **types of issues**, **book building**, and **corporate actions** (especially rights vs bonus, and the effect of each on price and holdings).\n\n---\n\n## 6.1 The Primary Market\n\n### 6.1.1 Functions of the primary market\n\nThe primary market is where **securities are created and sold for the first time**, so capital flows **to the issuer**.\n\nIts functions:\n1. **Capital formation** — channels savings into productive investment\n2. **Origination** — appraising and structuring the issue\n3. **Underwriting** — guaranteeing subscription\n4. **Distribution** — placing securities with investors\n\n### 6.1.2 ⭐ Types of Issues\n\n| Type | Who it goes to | Key points |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Public issue** | The general public | IPO (first time) or FPO (already listed). Requires a prospectus and full SEBI compliance |\n| **Private placement** | A select group | Offered to **fewer than 200 investors** in a financial year (excluding QIBs and employees under ESOP); beyond that it is deemed a public issue |\n| **Preferential issue** | Selected identified persons | Made by a **listed** company to specific investors (promoters, strategic partners) at a price determined under SEBI ICDR rules; carries a **lock-in** |\n| **Qualified Institutions Placement (QIP)** | **Qualified Institutional Buyers (QIBs)** only | A fast, low-cost route for listed companies to raise money from institutions without a full prospectus |\n| **Rights issue** | **Existing shareholders**, pro rata | Offered at a **discount** to market price; the entitlement is **renounceable** (can be sold) |\n| **Bonus issue** | **Existing shareholders**, free | Capitalisation of reserves — **no money is raised** |\n\n> ⚠️ **The most-tested contrast: a RIGHTS issue raises money; a BONUS issue does not.** A rights issue is an *offer to buy more* at a discount; a bonus issue is *free additional shares* funded by converting reserves into share capital.\n\n### 6.1.3 Categories of issuers\n\n- **Central, State and local governments** — dated securities, T-bills, state development loans, municipal bonds\n- **Public sector units (PSUs)** — equity and bonds\n- **Private sector companies** — equity, debentures, commercial paper\n- **Banks, financial institutions and NBFCs** — bonds, certificates of deposit, infrastructure bonds\n- **Mutual funds** — units\n- **REITs and InvITs** — units representing real estate / infrastructure assets\n- **AIFs** — units to sophisticated investors\n\n### 6.1.4 Types of investors\n\n| Category | Who | Typical IPO allocation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **QIBs** | Mutual funds, banks, insurers, FPIs, pension funds | Up to **50%** in a book-built issue |\n| **Non-Institutional Investors (NIIs/HNIs)** | Applications **above ₹2 lakh** | At least **15%** |\n| **Retail Individual Investors (RIIs)** | Applications **up to ₹2 lakh** | At least **35%** |\n| **Anchor investors** | QIBs allotted a day before the issue opens | Carved out of the QIB portion; carries a lock-in |\n\n> 🧠 The **₹2 lakh** line separates retail from non-institutional applicants — a common exam detail.\n\n### 6.1.5 Public issues of equity shares\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Initial Public Offer (IPO)** | The company's **first** offer of shares to the public; the company becomes listed |\n| **Follow-on / Further Public Offer (FPO)** | A **further** public offer by an **already listed** company |\n| **Fresh issue** | **New** shares are created; money goes **to the company** |\n| **Offer for Sale (OFS)** | **Existing** shareholders (usually promoters) sell their shares; money goes **to the selling shareholders, not the company** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Guaranteed question:** in an **offer for sale the company receives nothing** — only the selling shareholders do. Many IPOs combine a fresh issue *and* an OFS.\n\n### 6.1.6 ⭐ Pricing a public issue\n\n| | **Fixed price issue** | **Book-built issue** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Price** | Stated in the prospectus **upfront** | A **price band** is given; the final price is discovered from bids |\n| **Demand visibility** | Known only **after** the issue closes | Visible **during** the issue (daily bidding data) |\n| **Investor pays** | Full amount on application | Bids at or above the floor; final **cut-off price** applies |\n| **Price band** | Not applicable | Cap must be **within 120%** of the floor price |\n\n**Book building mechanics:**\n- The company and merchant banker set a **floor price** and a **cap**.\n- Investors bid quantities at prices within the band; retail investors may bid at **\"cut-off\"**, meaning they accept whatever final price emerges.\n- The **cut-off price** is the price at which the issue is fully subscribed — it becomes the issue price.\n- Bidding is typically open **3 working days** (extendable if the band is revised).\n\n### 6.1.8 Applying to a public issue — ASBA\n\n**ASBA = Applications Supported by Blocked Amount.** The application money is **blocked in the investor's own bank account** rather than debited. It is:\n- **Mandatory** for public issues\n- Debited **only to the extent of the allotment**; the rest is simply unblocked\n- The investor **continues to earn interest** on the blocked amount\n\n**UPI mechanism:** retail investors applying up to **₹5 lakh** may use a UPI ID to block funds.\n\n### 6.1.9 Public issue of debt securities\nGoverned by SEBI's regulations on issue and listing of **non-convertible securities**. Requires **credit rating**, a **debenture trustee**, and creation of a **debenture redemption reserve** where applicable.\n\n### 6.1.10 Rights offer\n- Offered to existing shareholders **in proportion to their holding** (e.g. 1:4 = one new share for every four held).\n- Priced at a **discount** to the market price.\n- The entitlement is **renounceable** — a shareholder who does not wish to subscribe can **sell the rights entitlement** in the market.\n- A shareholder who neither subscribes nor renounces suffers **dilution** and forfeits value.\n\n### 6.1.11 & 6.1.12 Private placement and QIP\n- **Private placement**: securities offered to a **select group of fewer than 200 persons** in a financial year (excluding QIBs and ESOP employees). Exceeding that limit makes it a deemed public issue.\n- **QIP**: a listed company issues to **QIBs only**. Fast and low-cost — no draft prospectus filing with SEBI is required, and pricing follows a prescribed formula based on recent market prices.\n\n---\n\n## 6.2 The Secondary Market\n\n### 6.2.1 Role and function\n- **Liquidity** — investors can exit\n- **Price discovery** — continuous valuation of securities\n- **Transfer of ownership** without affecting the company's capital\n- **Benchmark** for pricing new issues and for corporate decisions\n\n### 6.2.2 Market structure and participants\nExchanges (NSE, BSE), clearing corporations, depositories, brokers and authorised persons, custodians, and investors (retail, HNI, institutional, FPI).\n\n**Trading and settlement:** orders are matched electronically on a price–time priority basis. Indian equity settlement is on a **T+1** rolling basis (settlement on the working day after the trade).\n\n### 6.2.3 ⭐ Market indicators\n\n| Indicator | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Market capitalisation** | Share price × number of outstanding shares. For the market, the sum across all listed companies. Measures **size** |\n| **Market turnover** | Total value of trades over a period. Measures **activity/liquidity** |\n| **Market indices** | A basket of representative stocks tracking overall or sectoral movement — Nifty 50, Sensex |\n\n**Index construction:** Indian indices such as the Nifty 50 and Sensex use **free-float market capitalisation weighting** — only shares genuinely available for trading (excluding promoter and locked-in holdings) are counted.\n\n**Uses of an index:** a market barometer, a benchmark for portfolio performance, the underlying for index funds/ETFs and derivatives, and an economic indicator.\n\n### 6.2.4 ⭐ Risk Management Systems in the secondary market\n\n| Mechanism | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Capital adequacy norms** | Minimum net worth and deposits for brokers, scaled to business volume |\n| **Margins** | Collateral collected upfront to cover potential losses — initial margin, mark-to-market margin, exposure margin, VaR margin |\n| **Circuit breakers** | **Market-wide** halts when an index moves ±10%, ±15% or ±20%, with halt durations depending on the level and time of day |\n| **Price bands** | **Stock-specific** limits (e.g. 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%) beyond which a stock cannot move in a day |\n| **Settlement guarantee mechanism** | The clearing corporation's fund guaranteeing settlement even if a member defaults |\n| **Online surveillance** | Real-time monitoring of trading for manipulation and unusual activity |\n| **Price monitoring and action** | Investigation of abnormal price/volume movements; moving stocks to restricted categories (e.g. trade-for-trade) |\n| **Inspection of books** | Periodic inspection of members' records by the exchange and SEBI |\n\n> 🧠 **Circuit breaker vs price band:** a **circuit breaker is market-wide** (halts the whole market on index moves); a **price band is stock-specific** (caps one scrip's daily movement).\n\n---\n\n## 6.3 ⭐ Corporate Actions\n\nA **corporate action** is an event initiated by a company that affects its securities and shareholders.\n\n### 6.3.1 Rights Issue\nCovered above. **Raises capital.** Price falls to a theoretical ex-rights price after the issue.\n\n### 6.3.2 Bonus Issue\nFree additional shares from **capitalisation of reserves**.\n- **No money is raised.**\n- Number of shares **rises**; price falls proportionately.\n- **The shareholder's total value and percentage holding are unchanged.**\n\n**Example — 1:1 bonus:** an investor holding 100 shares at ₹800 (value ₹80,000) receives 100 more. Now 200 shares, and the price adjusts to ₹400. Value = **₹80,000, unchanged.**\n\n### 6.3.3 Dividend\nDistribution of profits to shareholders.\n- **Interim** (declared during the year) or **final** (at year-end, approved at the AGM).\n- The share price typically falls by roughly the dividend amount on the **ex-dividend date**.\n- **Record date** — the date on which the company's register determines who is entitled.\n- **Ex-date** — from this date the share trades **without** the dividend entitlement. Under T+1 settlement the ex-date is normally **the same as the record date** (buy before it to be entitled).\n- Dividends are **taxable in the hands of the shareholder** at slab rates, with **TDS** applicable above the prescribed threshold.\n\n### 6.3.4 Stock Split\nThe face value of each share is **reduced**, increasing the number of shares.\n- **Example:** a ₹10 face value share split 1:5 becomes five ₹2 face value shares. 100 shares at ₹1,000 become 500 shares at ₹200 — **value unchanged**.\n- **Purpose:** improve **affordability and liquidity** by lowering the per-share price.\n\n> ⚠️ **Bonus vs split — a classic exam pair.**\n>\n> | | **Bonus issue** | **Stock split** |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | **Face value** | **Unchanged** | **Reduced** |\n> | **Source** | Capitalisation of **reserves** | No reserves involved |\n> | **Share capital** | **Increases** | **Unchanged** |\n> | **Number of shares** | Increases | Increases |\n> | **Shareholder value** | Unchanged | Unchanged |\n\n### 6.3.5 Share Buy-back\nThe company **repurchases its own shares** from shareholders.\n- Reduces the number of outstanding shares, raising EPS.\n- Routes: **tender offer** (proportionate, at a fixed price) or **open market** purchase.\n- Signals that management considers the shares undervalued, and returns surplus cash.\n\n### 6.3.6 Delisting\nThe company's shares are **removed from the exchange**.\n- **Voluntary** — usually via a **reverse book building** process in which public shareholders bid the exit price.\n- **Compulsory** — imposed by the exchange for non-compliance; promoters must still provide an exit to public shareholders.\n\n### 6.3.7 Mergers and Acquisitions\n- **Merger/amalgamation** — two or more companies combine; shareholders of the merging company receive shares of the merged entity in a set **swap ratio**.\n- **Acquisition/takeover** — one company acquires control of another. Triggers **SEBI (SAST) Regulations**, requiring an **open offer** to public shareholders once specified thresholds of shares or voting rights are crossed.\n- **Demerger** — a division is spun off into a separate listed company; shareholders receive shares in the new entity.\n\n### 6.3.8 Offer for Sale (OFS)\nA mechanism for **promoters to sell** their shares through the exchange platform — used to raise public shareholding or to divest.\n**The company receives nothing**; the selling shareholder does.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Primary market | Securities **issued**; funds go to the **issuer** |\n| Secondary market | Investors trade **among themselves**; provides liquidity |\n| IPO vs FPO | **First** public offer vs a **further** offer by a listed company |\n| Fresh issue vs OFS | New shares, money **to the company** vs existing shares, money **to the seller** |\n| Private placement limit | Fewer than **200 investors** in a financial year (excl. QIBs, ESOP) |\n| QIP | Listed company issues to **QIBs only**; fast, no draft prospectus filing |\n| Preferential issue | To identified persons by a listed company; carries a **lock-in** |\n| Rights issue | To **existing** shareholders pro rata, at a **discount**; **renounceable**; **raises money** |\n| Bonus issue | **Free** shares from **reserves**; **raises no money**; value unchanged |\n| Fixed price vs book building | Price stated upfront vs **discovered** within a price band |\n| Price band cap rule | Cap within **120%** of the floor price |\n| Cut-off price | The price at which the issue is fully subscribed |\n| Retail vs NII threshold | **₹2 lakh** |\n| IPO allocation (book-built) | QIB up to **50%**, NII at least **15%**, retail at least **35%** |\n| ASBA | Money **blocked** in the investor's own account; interest continues |\n| UPI in IPOs | Retail applications up to **₹5 lakh** |\n| Settlement cycle | **T+1** rolling |\n| Market capitalisation | Price × shares outstanding — measures **size** |\n| Market turnover | Value traded — measures **activity** |\n| Index weighting in India | **Free-float market capitalisation** |\n| Circuit breaker | **Market-wide** halt at ±10%, ±15%, ±20% index moves |\n| Price band | **Stock-specific** daily movement limit |\n| Settlement guarantee | Provided by the **clearing corporation** |\n| Stock split | **Face value reduced**; share capital unchanged |\n| Bonus issue | **Face value unchanged**; share capital increases |\n| Buy-back | Company repurchases its shares; **EPS rises** |\n| Delisting | Voluntary via **reverse book building**, or compulsory |\n| Open offer trigger | **SEBI (SAST) Regulations** on acquiring control/thresholds |\n| Dividend taxation | Taxable **in the shareholder's hands** at slab rates; TDS above threshold |\n\n> **Exam tip:** for any corporate action question, ask two things: **(1) does money move?** (rights and buy-back yes; bonus and split no) and **(2) does the shareholder's total value change?** (for bonus and split, no — only the share count and price adjust).\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 6: Securities Market Segments — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **Corporate actions and the rights-vs-bonus contrast are the highest-yield items.**\n\n**Q1.** What are the four functions of the primary market?\n> **A:** **Capital formation**, **origination** (appraising and structuring), **underwriting** (guaranteeing subscription) and **distribution**.\n\n**Q2.** What is the difference between an IPO and an FPO?\n> **A:** An **IPO** is a company's **first** public offer (it becomes listed). An **FPO/further public offer** is a **subsequent** public offer by an **already listed** company.\n\n**Q3.** In an Offer for Sale, who receives the money?\n> **A:** The **selling shareholders** (usually promoters) — **the company receives nothing**. Contrast with a **fresh issue**, where new shares are created and the money goes to the company.\n\n**Q4.** What is the private placement limit?\n> **A:** Offered to **fewer than 200 investors** in a financial year (excluding QIBs and employees under ESOP). Beyond that it is deemed a **public issue**.\n\n**Q5.** What is a QIP and why is it attractive to issuers?\n> **A:** A **Qualified Institutions Placement** — a listed company issuing to **QIBs only**. It is **fast and low-cost**: no draft prospectus filing with SEBI, and pricing follows a prescribed formula based on recent market prices.\n\n**Q6.** What is a preferential issue?\n> **A:** An issue by a **listed** company to **specific identified persons** (promoters, strategic partners), priced under SEBI ICDR rules and carrying a **lock-in**.\n\n**Q7.** State the key difference between a rights issue and a bonus issue.\n> **A:** A **rights issue RAISES money** (shareholders buy more shares at a discount). A **bonus issue raises NO money** — it is free shares from the **capitalisation of reserves**.\n\n**Q8.** What does \"renounceable\" mean in a rights issue?\n> **A:** A shareholder who does not want to subscribe can **sell the rights entitlement** in the market rather than let it lapse. Doing neither means **dilution and forfeited value**.\n\n**Q9.** Compare fixed price and book-built issues.\n> **A:** **Fixed price** — the price is stated **upfront** in the prospectus and demand is known only after closing. **Book-built** — a **price band** is given, investors bid, and the final price is **discovered**; demand is visible daily.\n\n**Q10.** What is the rule on the price band's cap?\n> **A:** The cap must be **within 120% of the floor price**.\n\n**Q11.** What is the cut-off price?\n> **A:** The price at which the issue is **fully subscribed** — it becomes the final issue price. Retail investors may bid \"at cut-off\", accepting whatever price emerges.\n\n**Q12.** What separates a Retail Individual Investor from a Non-Institutional Investor?\n> **A:** The **₹2 lakh** application threshold. Up to ₹2 lakh = **retail**; above ₹2 lakh = **non-institutional (HNI)**.\n\n**Q13.** State the IPO allocation limits in a book-built issue.\n> **A:** **QIBs up to 50%**, **NIIs at least 15%**, **retail at least 35%**.\n\n**Q14.** What is an anchor investor?\n> **A:** A **QIB allotted shares a day before** the issue opens, carved out of the QIB portion, subject to a **lock-in**. Their participation signals confidence to other investors.\n\n**Q15.** What is ASBA and what are its three benefits?\n> **A:** **Applications Supported by Blocked Amount.** The money is **blocked in the investor's own bank account**: (1) it is debited **only to the extent of allotment**, (2) the balance is simply unblocked, and (3) the investor **keeps earning interest** meanwhile. It is **mandatory** for public issues.\n\n**Q16.** What is the UPI limit for retail IPO applications?\n> **A:** Retail investors may apply using a UPI ID for amounts up to **₹5 lakh**.\n\n**Q17.** What is India's equity settlement cycle?\n> **A:** **T+1 rolling settlement** — settlement on the working day after the trade.\n\n**Q18.** Distinguish market capitalisation from market turnover.\n> **A:** **Market capitalisation** = price × shares outstanding — it measures **size**. **Market turnover** = value of trades over a period — it measures **activity/liquidity**.\n\n**Q19.** How are Indian indices such as the Nifty 50 and Sensex weighted?\n> **A:** By **free-float market capitalisation** — only shares genuinely available for trading are counted, excluding promoter and locked-in holdings.\n\n**Q20.** Name four uses of a market index.\n> **A:** A **market barometer**; a **benchmark** for portfolio performance; the **underlying** for index funds, ETFs and derivatives; and an **economic indicator**.\n\n**Q21.** What is the difference between a circuit breaker and a price band?\n> **A:** A **circuit breaker is MARKET-WIDE** — it halts the whole market when an index moves **±10%, ±15% or ±20%**. A **price band is STOCK-SPECIFIC** — it caps one scrip's daily movement (e.g. 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%).\n\n**Q22.** Name the risk management systems in the secondary market.\n> **A:** **Capital adequacy** norms, **margins**, **circuit breakers**, **price bands**, the **settlement guarantee mechanism**, **online surveillance**, **price monitoring and action**, and **inspection of books**.\n\n**Q23.** What types of margin are collected?\n> **A:** **Initial margin**, **mark-to-market margin**, **exposure margin** and **VaR margin** — all collateral against potential losses.\n\n**Q24.** In a 1:1 bonus issue, what happens to a holding of 100 shares priced at ₹800?\n> **A:** The investor receives 100 more shares → **200 shares**, and the price adjusts to **₹400**. Total value stays **₹80,000 — unchanged**.\n\n**Q25.** Compare a bonus issue and a stock split on face value and share capital.\n> **A:** **Bonus** — face value **unchanged**, share capital **increases** (reserves are capitalised). **Split** — face value **reduced**, share capital **unchanged**. Both increase the share count and leave shareholder value unchanged.\n\n**Q26.** What is the purpose of a stock split?\n> **A:** To improve **affordability and liquidity** by lowering the per-share price.\n\n**Q27.** What is the record date, and what is the ex-date?\n> **A:** The **record date** is when the company's register determines entitlement. The **ex-date** is when the share starts trading **without** the entitlement. Under T+1 the ex-date is normally the **same as** the record date.\n\n**Q28.** How are dividends taxed in India now?\n> **A:** **Taxable in the shareholder's hands** at their slab rate, with **TDS** applicable above the prescribed threshold.\n\n**Q29.** What happens to a share price on the ex-dividend date?\n> **A:** It typically **falls by roughly the dividend amount** — the value has left the company.\n\n**Q30.** What is a share buy-back, and what are the two routes?\n> **A:** The company **repurchases its own shares**, reducing the share count and **raising EPS**. Routes: a **tender offer** (proportionate, fixed price) or **open market** purchase.\n\n**Q31.** How does voluntary delisting normally work?\n> **A:** Through **reverse book building**, in which public shareholders bid the exit price. **Compulsory** delisting is imposed by the exchange for non-compliance, and promoters must still provide an exit.\n\n**Q32.** What triggers an open offer in an acquisition?\n> **A:** Crossing prescribed thresholds of shares or voting rights (or acquiring control) under the **SEBI (SAST) Regulations** — the acquirer must then make an **open offer** to public shareholders.\n\n**Q33.** What is a demerger?\n> **A:** A division is **spun off into a separate listed company**, and existing shareholders receive shares in the new entity.\n\n**Q34.** What is a swap ratio?\n> **A:** The ratio in which shareholders of a **merging** company receive shares of the **merged** entity.\n\n**Q35.** Which corporate actions actually move money, and which do not?\n> **A:** **Money moves:** rights issue (in), dividend (out), buy-back (out), OFS (to the seller). **No money moves:** bonus issue, stock split.\n\n**Q36.** Name the categories of issuer in the primary market.\n> **A:** **Governments** (central, state, local), **PSUs**, **private companies**, **banks/FIs/NBFCs**, **mutual funds**, **REITs and InvITs**, and **AIFs**.\n\n**Q37.** What does a public issue of debt securities require?\n> **A:** A **credit rating**, a **debenture trustee**, and creation of a **debenture redemption reserve** where applicable.\n\n**Q38.** How long is bidding typically open in a book-built issue?\n> **A:** Usually **3 working days**, extendable if the price band is revised.\n\n**Q39.** What are the four functions of the secondary market?\n> **A:** **Liquidity**, **price discovery**, **transfer of ownership** (without affecting the company's capital), and providing a **benchmark** for new issues and corporate decisions.\n\n**Q40.** In a rights issue of 1:4, what does a holder of 400 shares receive?\n> **A:** The entitlement to buy **100 new shares** (one for every four held) at the discounted rights price.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 6: Securities Market Segments — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Corporate actions carry the most marks here** — especially bonus vs split and rights entitlement arithmetic. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** In an Initial Public Offer, the company:\nA) Offers shares to the public for the first time and becomes listed  B) Buys back its own shares  C) Issues free shares to shareholders  D) Delists from the exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — An **IPO** is the first offer of shares to the public, after which the company is listed.\nB) Is a buy-back. C) Is a bonus issue. D) Is delisting — the opposite of an IPO.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In an Offer for Sale, the proceeds go to:\nA) The company  B) The selling shareholders  C) SEBI  D) The stock exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An OFS involves **existing shares**, so the **selling shareholders** receive the money — **not the company**.\nA) The company receives money only in a **fresh issue**. C) and D) are the regulator and the platform, not recipients.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A bonus issue:\nA) Raises fresh capital for the company  B) Involves the capitalisation of reserves and raises no money  C) Reduces the number of shares  D) Is offered only to institutional investors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bonus shares are issued **free** by converting reserves into share capital — **no cash is raised**.\nA) That is a rights issue. C) The share count **increases**. D) Bonus shares go to **all** existing shareholders.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A rights issue is offered to:\nA) The general public  B) QIBs only  C) Existing shareholders in proportion to their holding  D) Employees only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Rights are offered **pro rata to existing shareholders**, usually at a discount to market price.\nA) Describes a public issue. B) Describes a QIP. D) Describes an ESOP.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** In a stock split, the face value of a share:\nA) Increases  B) Is reduced  C) Stays the same  D) Becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A split **reduces the face value** and proportionately increases the number of shares.\nA) and D) are incorrect. C) Face value stays the same in a **bonus issue**, which is the key contrast.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** In a bonus issue, the face value of each share:\nA) Is reduced  B) Remains unchanged  C) Doubles  D) Becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Face value is **unchanged** in a bonus; only the number of shares rises (funded from reserves).\nA) That happens in a **stock split**. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A Qualified Institutions Placement can be made to:\nA) Retail investors  B) Qualified Institutional Buyers only  C) Employees only  D) Foreign individuals only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **QIP** is restricted to **QIBs** — mutual funds, banks, insurers, FPIs, pension funds.\nA), C) and D) are excluded from a QIP.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The threshold separating a Retail Individual Investor from a Non-Institutional Investor in an IPO is an application of:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹2 lakh  C) ₹5 lakh  D) ₹10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Applications **up to ₹2 lakh** are retail; **above ₹2 lakh** are non-institutional.\nA), C) and D) are not the threshold. *(₹5 lakh is the UPI application limit — a common confusion.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** ASBA stands for:\nA) Application Submitted By Agent  B) Applications Supported by Blocked Amount  C) Automatic Share Buying Arrangement  D) Approved Securities Bidding Application\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under **ASBA** the application money is **blocked** in the investor's own bank account rather than debited.\nA), C) and D) are invented expansions.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Under ASBA, the investor:\nA) Loses interest on the application money  B) Continues to earn interest on the blocked amount  C) Must pay in cash  D) Cannot apply online\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because the money stays **in the investor's own account**, interest continues to accrue.\nA) This was the disadvantage of the pre-ASBA system. C) and D) are incorrect — ASBA is largely electronic.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** In a book-built issue, the cap of the price band must be within:\nA) 110% of the floor  B) 120% of the floor  C) 150% of the floor  D) 200% of the floor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The cap cannot exceed **120% of the floor price**.\nA), C) and D) are not the prescribed limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The cut-off price in a book-built issue is:\nA) The floor price  B) The price at which the issue is fully subscribed  C) The highest bid received  D) The face value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **cut-off** is where demand fully absorbs the issue; it becomes the issue price.\nA) The floor is the lower bound of the band. C) The highest bid is not necessarily the clearing price. D) Face value is unrelated to issue price.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A circuit breaker in the Indian market is:\nA) Stock-specific  B) Market-wide, triggered by index movements of ±10%, ±15% or ±20%  C) Applied only to derivatives  D) A margin requirement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Circuit breakers halt the **entire market** at defined index thresholds.\nA) That describes a **price band**. C) They apply market-wide. D) Margins are a separate risk control.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A price band is:\nA) Market-wide  B) Stock-specific, limiting a scrip's daily movement  C) An index level  D) A settlement cycle\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Price bands cap how far **an individual stock** can move in a day (2%, 5%, 10%, 20%).\nA) That is a circuit breaker. C) and D) are unrelated concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** India's equity settlement cycle is:\nA) T+5  B) T+3  C) T+2  D) T+1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Indian equity markets settle on a **T+1** rolling basis.\nA), B) and C) are historical cycles that India has progressively shortened.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Market capitalisation is calculated as:\nA) Share price × number of outstanding shares  B) Total value of trades  C) Net profit ÷ shares  D) Face value × shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Market cap measures **size**: price × outstanding shares.\nB) Is turnover, which measures activity. C) Is earnings per share. D) Face value is an accounting figure, not market value.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Indian indices such as the Nifty 50 are weighted by:\nA) Equal weighting  B) Price weighting  C) Free-float market capitalisation  D) Revenue\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only shares genuinely **available for trading** are counted, excluding promoter and locked-in holdings.\nA) and B) are used by some international indices but not the Nifty/Sensex. D) Revenue is not an index weighting basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A private placement must be made to fewer than:\nA) 50 investors in a financial year  B) 100 investors in a financial year  C) 200 investors in a financial year  D) 500 investors in a financial year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Fewer than **200 investors** in a financial year (excluding QIBs and ESOP employees); beyond that it is a deemed public issue.\nA), B) and D) are not the prescribed limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** In a share buy-back, the number of outstanding shares:\nA) Increases  B) Decreases  C) Stays the same  D) Doubles\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The company repurchases and extinguishes shares, **reducing** the count and raising EPS.\nA) Happens with bonus, rights or splits. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Voluntary delisting in India is typically carried out through:\nA) A fixed price offer only  B) Reverse book building  C) A bonus issue  D) A stock split\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Reverse book building** lets public shareholders bid the exit price.\nA) Fixed price is not the standard voluntary route. C) and D) are unrelated corporate actions.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Dividends in India are currently:\nA) Tax-free in the shareholder's hands  B) Taxable in the shareholder's hands at slab rates, with TDS above a threshold  C) Taxed only on the company  D) Exempt for all investors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Dividend Distribution Tax was abolished; dividends are now **taxed in the recipient's hands** at their slab rate, with **TDS** above the prescribed threshold.\nA), C) and D) describe the pre-abolition regime or are simply incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The ex-date is the date from which:\nA) The share trades without the entitlement  B) The company pays the dividend  C) The register closes permanently  D) Trading is suspended\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Buying **on or after** the ex-date means **no entitlement** to the corporate action.\nB) That is the payment date. C) The register closes only briefly for the record date. D) Trading continues normally.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** An anchor investor is:\nA) A retail investor  B) A QIB allotted shares a day before the issue opens, subject to a lock-in  C) A promoter  D) A merchant banker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Anchor investors are **QIBs** allotted ahead of the issue, carved from the QIB portion, with a **lock-in**.\nA) Retail investors cannot be anchors. C) and D) play different roles in an issue.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** In a book-built IPO, the retail portion must be at least:\nA) 15%  B) 35%  C) 50%  D) 10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Retail gets **at least 35%**, NIIs at least 15%, and QIBs up to 50%.\nA) 15% is the NII minimum. C) 50% is the QIB maximum. D) 10% is not a prescribed share.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A preferential issue is made by:\nA) An unlisted company to the public  B) A listed company to specific identified persons, with a lock-in  C) A mutual fund to unit-holders  D) The government to banks\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Preferential issues go to **identified persons** (promoters, strategic investors) under SEBI ICDR rules, and carry a **lock-in**.\nA) Would be a public issue. C) and D) describe different transactions.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The main function of the secondary market is:\nA) To raise fresh capital  B) To provide liquidity and price discovery  C) To register companies  D) To set dividend policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Secondary trading gives investors **exit and continuous valuation**.\nA) That is the primary market. C) Is the RoC. D) Is a board decision.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** A public issue of debt securities requires:\nA) No credit rating  B) A credit rating and a debenture trustee  C) Only board approval  D) A stock split\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Debt public issues need a **credit rating** and a **debenture trustee**, plus a debenture redemption reserve where applicable.\nA) A rating is mandatory. C) Regulatory compliance goes well beyond board approval. D) Is an unrelated corporate action.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** In a 1:5 stock split of a ₹10 face value share, the new face value is:\nA) ₹5  B) ₹2  C) ₹1  D) ₹10\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Splitting one ₹10 share into five gives a face value of **₹10 ÷ 5 = ₹2** each.\nA) ₹5 would be a 1:2 split. C) ₹1 would be a 1:10 split. D) Face value must change in a split.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** The UPI mechanism for IPO applications is available to retail investors for amounts up to:\nA) ₹2 lakh  B) ₹5 lakh  C) ₹10 lakh  D) ₹1 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Retail investors may block funds via UPI for applications up to **₹5 lakh**.\nA) ₹2 lakh is the retail/NII classification threshold, a different limit. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** An open offer to public shareholders is triggered under:\nA) The Companies Act, 2013  B) The SEBI (SAST) Regulations  C) FEMA  D) The Income Tax Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **SEBI (Substantial Acquisition of Shares and Takeovers) Regulations** require an open offer on crossing prescribed thresholds or acquiring control.\nA) Governs corporate law generally. C) Governs foreign exchange. D) Governs taxation.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which corporate action raises money for the company?\nA) Bonus issue  B) Stock split  C) Rights issue  D) Dividend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **rights issue** brings in cash as shareholders subscribe.\nA) and B) raise nothing — they only restructure existing capital. D) A dividend takes money **out** of the company.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In a demerger:\nA) Two companies combine  B) A division is spun off into a separate listed company  C) Shares are bought back  D) The company delists\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **demerger** separates a division into its own entity, with existing shareholders receiving shares in it.\nA) Is a merger. C) Is a buy-back. D) Is delisting.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** The settlement guarantee mechanism is provided by:\nA) The stock exchange  B) The clearing corporation  C) SEBI  D) The depository\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **clearing corporation** maintains the settlement guarantee fund and acts as central counterparty.\nA) The exchange matches trades. C) SEBI regulates. D) The depository holds securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Bidding in a book-built issue is typically open for:\nA) 1 day  B) 3 working days  C) 10 working days  D) 30 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Usually **3 working days**, extendable if the price band is revised.\nA), C) and D) are not the standard period.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Market turnover measures:\nA) The size of the market  B) The value of trades over a period, i.e. activity  C) The number of listed companies  D) The dividend yield\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Turnover is an **activity/liquidity** measure.\nA) That is market capitalisation. C) and D) are different statistics.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** An investor holds 200 shares priced at ₹600. The company declares a 1:1 bonus. What is the position afterwards?\nA) 400 shares at ₹300; value ₹1,20,000  B) 400 shares at ₹600; value ₹2,40,000  C) 200 shares at ₹300; value ₹60,000  D) 400 shares at ₹150; value ₹60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A 1:1 bonus doubles the shares to **400** and halves the price to **₹300**. Value = 400 × 300 = **₹1,20,000**, exactly the original 200 × 600 = ₹1,20,000.\nB) Assumes the price does not adjust — bonus shares are not free wealth. C) Ignores the extra shares. D) Halves the value, which cannot happen from a bonus.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** An investor holds 500 shares of ₹10 face value trading at ₹2,000. The company announces a 1:10 split. What results?\nA) 5,000 shares at ₹200, face value ₹1  B) 5,000 shares at ₹2,000, face value ₹10  C) 500 shares at ₹200  D) 50 shares at ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A 1:10 split turns each ₹10 share into ten ₹1 shares: **5,000 shares**, price **₹2,000 ÷ 10 = ₹200**, face value **₹1**. Value = 5,000 × 200 = ₹10,00,000, unchanged.\nB) The price must adjust. C) The share count must rise. D) Reverses the split direction.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A company announces a rights issue of 1:4 at ₹150 when the market price is ₹250. An investor holds 800 shares. How many rights shares can she subscribe to, and at what total cost?\nA) 200 shares for ₹30,000  B) 200 shares for ₹50,000  C) 100 shares for ₹15,000  D) 800 shares for ₹1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **1:4** ratio means one new share for every four held: 800 ÷ 4 = **200 shares**. Cost = 200 × ₹150 = **₹30,000**.\nB) Uses the market price instead of the rights price. C) Uses a 1:8 ratio. D) Assumes one-for-one. **The discount (₹150 vs ₹250) is why rights have value and can be sold if not taken up.**\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A shareholder neither subscribes to nor renounces a rights entitlement. What happens?\nA) She receives the shares free  B) The entitlement lapses and she suffers dilution, forfeiting its value  C) The company must compensate her  D) Her shareholding percentage rises\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An unexercised, unrenounced right **lapses worthless**, and because others subscribed, her **proportionate holding is diluted**.\nA) Rights must be paid for; bonus shares are free. C) No compensation is due — the option was offered. D) Her percentage **falls**.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Which action leaves both the shareholder's total value AND the company's share capital unchanged?\nA) Bonus issue  B) Stock split  C) Rights issue  D) Buy-back\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In a **split**, face value falls as the share count rises, so **share capital is unchanged** and so is shareholder value.\nA) A bonus leaves value unchanged but **increases share capital** (reserves are capitalised). C) A rights issue raises capital. D) A buy-back reduces capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A company wants to raise ₹800 crore quickly from institutions without filing a draft prospectus. The appropriate route is:\nA) An IPO  B) A QIP  C) A rights issue  D) A bonus issue\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **QIP** allows a listed company to raise from **QIBs** quickly, without the full prospectus process.\nA) An IPO is for unlisted companies and is slow. C) A rights issue targets existing shareholders and takes longer. D) A bonus issue raises **no money at all**.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** An IPO comprises a fresh issue of ₹500 crore and an offer for sale of ₹300 crore. How much does the company receive?\nA) ₹800 crore  B) ₹500 crore  C) ₹300 crore  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Only the **fresh issue** (₹500 crore) brings money to the company; the **₹300 crore OFS** goes to the selling shareholders.\nA) Adds both, ignoring who receives the OFS proceeds. C) Reverses the two. D) The fresh issue does reach the company. **A very common exam construction.**\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** An investor buys a share on the ex-dividend date. Is she entitled to the dividend?\nA) Yes  B) No — buying on or after the ex-date means no entitlement  C) Only if she holds for 30 days  D) Only if she informs the company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — From the **ex-date** the share trades **without** the dividend; entitlement requires purchase **before** it.\nA) The seller retains the entitlement. C) and D) are not how entitlement works.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A stock falls 19.8% in a day with a 20% price band in force. What happens?\nA) The whole market halts  B) The stock is close to its lower price band and cannot fall further that day  C) The stock is delisted  D) Trading in all stocks stops\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **price band is stock-specific**: this scrip cannot move beyond ±20% today, but the rest of the market trades normally.\nA) and D) describe **circuit breakers**, which are triggered by **index** moves. C) Delisting is unrelated to daily price movement.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** The Nifty 50 falls 10% at 11 a.m. What is the consequence?\nA) Nothing  B) A market-wide circuit breaker halts trading for a prescribed period  C) Only that index's stocks halt  D) The exchange delists stocks\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **market-wide circuit breaker** triggers at ±10%, ±15% and ±20% index moves, halting trading for a period that depends on the level and time of day.\nA) A 10% index move is a defined trigger. C) The halt is market-wide, not index-specific. D) Delisting is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A retail investor bids \"at cut-off\" in a book-built IPO. This means:\nA) She will only buy at the floor price  B) She accepts whatever final price is discovered  C) She bids the highest possible price  D) Her bid is invalid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bidding at cut-off means **accepting the discovered issue price**, which maximises the chance of allotment.\nA) The cut-off may exceed the floor. C) It is not a maximum-price bid — she pays the cut-off price. D) It is a valid and common retail option.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which of these does NOT change the total value of a shareholder's holding on the announcement day?\nA) A dividend payment  B) A bonus issue  C) A buy-back at a premium  D) A rights issue at a deep discount\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **bonus** merely splits the same value across more shares.\nA) A dividend removes cash from the company, so the share price falls by roughly the dividend — though the shareholder receives the cash. C) A buy-back at a premium affects value and EPS. D) A rights issue requires fresh money and changes the per-share value.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Under ASBA, an investor applies for ₹1,00,000 of shares and is allotted ₹20,000 worth. What happens to the money?\nA) ₹1,00,000 is debited and ₹80,000 refunded later  B) Only ₹20,000 is debited; the remaining ₹80,000 is simply unblocked  C) Nothing is debited  D) ₹1,00,000 is retained by the exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ASBA **blocks** rather than debits, so only the allotted amount leaves the account and the rest is released — with interest earned throughout.\nA) Describes the pre-ASBA refund system. C) The allotted amount is debited. D) The exchange never holds application money.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A company with 10 crore shares buys back 1 crore shares. The effect on EPS, holding profit constant, is:\nA) EPS falls  B) EPS rises, because the same profit is divided among fewer shares  C) EPS is unchanged  D) EPS becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With 9 crore shares instead of 10, **EPS rises by about 11%** for the same profit.\nA) Reverses the effect. C) The denominator has changed. D) Nonsensical.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Which statement about market capitalisation and turnover is correct?\nA) They measure the same thing  B) Market cap measures size; turnover measures trading activity  C) Turnover measures size  D) Market cap measures liquidity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Size vs activity** — a large-cap stock can be thinly traded, and a small company can be heavily traded.\nA) They are distinct. C) and D) swap the definitions.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** A company issues shares to 250 investors in one financial year under a private placement. The consequence is:\nA) It is valid  B) It exceeds the limit of fewer than 200 investors and is deemed a public issue  C) It requires only board approval  D) SEBI has no role\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exceeding the **200-investor** ceiling converts it into a **deemed public issue**, attracting full public-issue compliance.\nA) It breaches the limit. C) A deemed public issue requires far more than board approval. D) SEBI's regulations are directly engaged.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Which is the correct order of an IPO's key stages?\nA) Listing → bidding → prospectus  B) Draft prospectus with SEBI → price band → bidding → allotment → listing  C) Allotment → bidding → listing  D) Bidding → prospectus → SEBI filing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The regulatory filing precedes pricing, which precedes bidding, allotment and finally listing.\nA), C) and D) all place later stages before earlier ones.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A promoter wishes to reduce his stake in a listed company to meet minimum public shareholding norms. The most suitable mechanism is:\nA) A fresh issue  B) An Offer for Sale through the exchange  C) A bonus issue  D) A buy-back\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **OFS** lets the promoter sell existing shares on the exchange platform, raising public shareholding.\nA) A fresh issue creates new shares and dilutes everyone, but is a slower route to this specific goal. C) A bonus changes no one's percentage. D) A buy-back typically **increases** the promoter's percentage.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which of these is NOT a risk management system in the secondary market?\nA) Margins  B) Circuit breakers  C) Dividend distribution  D) Capital adequacy norms\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Dividend distribution** is a corporate action, not a market risk control.\nA), B) and D) are all core risk management mechanisms, alongside price bands, the settlement guarantee, surveillance and inspection.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** An investor wants to sell her rights entitlement rather than subscribe. Is this possible?\nA) No, rights always lapse  B) Yes — rights entitlements are renounceable and can be sold in the market  C) Only with company permission  D) Only to the promoter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Renounceability** is a defining feature: the entitlement itself has value because the rights price is below market.\nA) They lapse only if neither subscribed nor renounced. C) No company permission is needed. D) They can be sold to any buyer in the market.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A company's shares trade at ₹450 before a ₹15 dividend goes ex. What is the likely opening price on the ex-date, other things equal?\nA) ₹465  B) ₹450  C) About ₹435  D) ₹415\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The price typically falls by roughly the dividend: 450 − 15 = **₹435**, since that cash has left the company.\nA) Adds the dividend. B) Ignores the adjustment. D) Over-adjusts. **The shareholder is not worse off — they receive the ₹15 separately.**\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which combination correctly describes a fresh issue versus an OFS?\nA) Both bring money to the company  B) Fresh issue creates new shares and funds the company; OFS transfers existing shares and funds the seller  C) Both transfer existing shares  D) OFS creates new shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the core distinction.\nA) Only the fresh issue funds the company. C) Only the OFS involves existing shares. D) An OFS involves **no new shares**.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** An index is used as the underlying for an ETF. This is an example of the index's role as:\nA) An economic indicator  B) The basis for an investment product  C) A regulatory tool  D) A settlement mechanism\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Indices serve as the **underlying** for index funds, ETFs and derivatives.\nA) Is a different (also valid) use. C) and D) are not index functions.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A company announces a buy-back via tender offer at ₹700 when the market price is ₹600. What does this typically signal?\nA) The company is in distress  B) Management believes the shares are undervalued and is returning surplus cash  C) The company is delisting  D) A bonus issue will follow\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A premium buy-back signals **confidence in undervaluation** and returns surplus cash to shareholders.\nA) Distressed companies rarely have surplus cash to buy back. C) A buy-back is distinct from delisting, though large ones can precede it. D) There is no such automatic link.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which issue type would a listed company use to bring in a strategic investor at a negotiated price?\nA) An IPO  B) A preferential issue  C) A bonus issue  D) A stock split\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **preferential issue** targets **identified persons** such as strategic partners, priced under SEBI ICDR rules with a lock-in.\nA) An IPO is for unlisted companies going public. C) and D) do not bring in new investors or capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Under free-float weighting, which shares are excluded from an index's weight calculation?\nA) All shares  B) Promoter holdings and locked-in shares  C) Shares held by mutual funds  D) Shares held by retail investors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Free float counts only shares **genuinely available for trading**, so promoter and locked-in holdings are excluded.\nA) Would leave nothing. C) and D) are freely tradable and therefore included.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A merger is announced with a swap ratio of 2:5. A shareholder of the merging company holds 500 shares. How many shares of the merged entity does she receive?\nA) 1,250  B) 200  C) 250  D) 1,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **2:5** swap means **2 new shares for every 5 held**: 500 × (2 ÷ 5) = **200 shares**.\nA) 1,250 inverts the ratio (5 for every 2). C) 250 uses 1:2. D) 1,000 uses 2:1. **Read swap ratios carefully — the direction is what the exam tests.**\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which statement about anchor investors is correct?\nA) They are retail investors allotted after listing  B) They are QIBs allotted before the issue opens, from the QIB portion, with a lock-in  C) They receive shares free  D) They can sell immediately on listing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Anchors are **QIBs** allotted a day before the issue opens, carved from the QIB quota, subject to a **lock-in**.\nA) Retail investors cannot be anchors. C) They pay the anchor price. D) The lock-in prevents immediate exit.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A stock has both a 10% price band and the market has a 15% circuit breaker. If the index falls 15% and the stock falls 10%, what happens?\nA) Only the stock halts  B) The market-wide circuit breaker halts all trading, and the stock has also hit its own band  C) Nothing halts  D) Only the stock's price band applies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both controls operate independently: the **index move triggers a market-wide halt**, and the **stock has separately hit its own band**.\nA) A 15% index fall halts the whole market. C) Both thresholds were breached. D) Both mechanisms apply simultaneously.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A company capitalises ₹100 crore of reserves to issue bonus shares. The effect on the balance sheet is:\nA) Total net worth rises by ₹100 crore  B) Reserves fall by ₹100 crore and share capital rises by ₹100 crore; total net worth is unchanged  C) Cash falls by ₹100 crore  D) Liabilities rise\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A bonus is a **reclassification within net worth** — reserves move into share capital. **No cash moves and net worth is unchanged.**\nA) Nothing new enters the company. C) A bonus involves no cash outflow. D) Liabilities are unaffected.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Which is the correct description of the T+1 settlement cycle?\nA) Trades settle the same day  B) Trades settle on the working day after the trade date  C) Trades settle after two working days  D) Trades never settle\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **T+1** means settlement on the **next working day** after the trade.\nA) That would be T+0. C) That is T+2, the previous Indian cycle. D) Nonsensical.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** An investor complains that after a 1:1 bonus the share price \"crashed 50%\". The correct explanation is:\nA) The company performed badly  B) The price adjusted mechanically for the doubled share count; the investor's total value is unchanged  C) The bonus was a mistake  D) The investor lost half the investment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The price adjustment is **arithmetic, not a loss**: twice the shares at half the price is the same value.\nA) The price change reflects the bonus, not performance. C) It is a normal corporate action. D) No value was lost — this is the most common retail misunderstanding.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which of these best explains why a rights issue is priced at a discount?\nA) To punish existing shareholders  B) To incentivise existing shareholders to subscribe and ensure the issue succeeds  C) Because SEBI mandates a fixed discount  D) To reduce the company's capital\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The discount makes subscription attractive and gives the entitlement value, improving the chance of full subscription.\nA) The discount benefits, rather than punishes, subscribers. C) SEBI does not mandate a specific discount. D) A rights issue **raises** capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which type of issue does NOT require the filing of a draft offer document with SEBI?\nA) An IPO  B) An FPO  C) A QIP  D) A public issue of debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **QIP** is a fast-track institutional route that does not require a draft prospectus filing with SEBI.\nA), B) and D) are all public issues requiring the full offer-document process.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A company's shares are moved to the \"trade-for-trade\" segment after abnormal price movement. This is an example of:\nA) Delisting  B) Price monitoring and surveillance action  C) A bonus issue  D) A circuit breaker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Moving a stock to a restricted segment (where netting is not allowed and every trade must be settled by delivery) is a **surveillance/price-monitoring** action.\nA) The stock remains listed. C) Unrelated. D) Circuit breakers are index-triggered halts.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** An investor holds 1,200 shares at ₹450. The company announces a 3:5 bonus (3 free shares for every 5 held). What is the post-bonus position?\nA) 1,920 shares at ₹281.25; value ₹5,40,000  B) 3,600 shares at ₹150  C) 1,920 shares at ₹450  D) 720 shares at ₹750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Bonus shares = 1,200 × (3 ÷ 5) = **720**. Total = 1,200 + 720 = **1,920 shares**. Original value = 1,200 × 450 = **₹5,40,000**. Adjusted price = 5,40,000 ÷ 1,920 = **₹281.25**. Value unchanged at ₹5,40,000.\nB) Assumes a 1:2 bonus and wrong pricing. C) Fails to adjust the price. D) Reduces the share count, which a bonus never does.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A company's shares trade at ₹800. It announces a 1:4 rights issue at ₹500. What is the theoretical ex-rights price (TERP)?\nA) ₹740  B) ₹650  C) ₹800  D) ₹500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — TERP = (total value of old shares + rights proceeds) ÷ total shares after. Take 4 existing shares at ₹800 = ₹3,200, plus 1 new share at ₹500 = ₹500. Total = **₹3,700** across **5 shares** = **₹740**.\nB) ₹650 is the simple average of ₹800 and ₹500. C) The price must fall after a discounted issue. D) ₹500 is the rights price itself. **The value of one right = ₹800 − ₹740 = ₹60.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Using Q72's figures, what is the value of one rights entitlement?\nA) ₹300  B) ₹60  C) ₹240  D) ₹740\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Value of a right = cum-rights price − TERP = 800 − 740 = **₹60**.\nA) ₹300 is the discount (800 − 500), which is not the same as the right's value because the right buys only one share for every four held. C) ₹240 has no basis. D) ₹740 is the TERP. **This ₹60 is what a renouncing shareholder can expect to realise.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A book-built IPO has a price band of ₹180–₹216. Is this band compliant, and why?\nA) No, the cap exceeds 120% of the floor  B) Yes — ₹216 is exactly 120% of ₹180  C) No, the band is too narrow  D) Yes, any band is allowed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 120% of ₹180 = ₹216, so the cap is **exactly at the permitted ceiling** and the band is compliant.\nA) It does not exceed the limit — it meets it precisely. C) There is no minimum band width rule of this kind. D) The 120% rule does constrain bands.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A ₹1,000 crore book-built IPO. What are the minimum amounts reserved for retail and non-institutional investors?\nA) Retail ₹350 crore; NII ₹150 crore  B) Retail ₹150 crore; NII ₹350 crore  C) Retail ₹500 crore; NII ₹150 crore  D) Retail ₹350 crore; NII ₹500 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Retail **at least 35%** = ₹350 crore; NII **at least 15%** = ₹150 crore; QIB **up to 50%** = ₹500 crore.\nB) Swaps the two minimums. C) 50% is the QIB maximum, not retail. D) ₹500 crore is the QIB portion, not NII.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A company with 20 crore shares and a net profit of ₹400 crore buys back 2 crore shares. What is the EPS before and after?\nA) ₹20 and ₹22.22  B) ₹20 and ₹18.18  C) ₹20 and ₹20  D) ₹22.22 and ₹20\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — EPS before = 400 ÷ 20 = **₹20**. After the buy-back, shares = 18 crore, so EPS = 400 ÷ 18 = **₹22.22**.\nB) Divides by 22 crore, i.e. treats the buy-back as an issue. C) The share count changed, so EPS must change. D) Reverses before and after. **EPS rose about 11% — the mechanical effect of a buy-back.**\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** An investor holds 600 shares of ₹10 face value at ₹1,500. The company announces a 1:1 bonus AND then a 1:5 split. What is the final position?\nA) 6,000 shares of ₹2 face value at ₹150  B) 1,200 shares at ₹750  C) 3,000 shares at ₹300  D) 6,000 shares of ₹10 face value at ₹150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **After the 1:1 bonus:** 1,200 shares at ₹750, face value still ₹10. **After the 1:5 split:** each ₹10 share becomes five ₹2 shares → 1,200 × 5 = **6,000 shares**, face value **₹2**, price ₹750 ÷ 5 = **₹150**. Value = 6,000 × 150 = **₹9,00,000**, equal to the original 600 × 1,500.\nB) Stops after the bonus. C) Miscomputes the split. D) Gets the count right but forgets that a **split reduces face value**. **Remember: bonus leaves face value alone; a split cuts it.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A company's IPO comprises a fresh issue of 2 crore shares and an OFS of 1.5 crore shares, priced at ₹320. How much does the company receive, and how much do the selling shareholders receive?\nA) Company ₹1,120 crore; sellers nil  B) Company ₹640 crore; sellers ₹480 crore  C) Company ₹480 crore; sellers ₹640 crore  D) Company ₹1,120 crore; sellers ₹1,120 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Fresh issue:** 2 crore × ₹320 = **₹640 crore to the company**. **OFS:** 1.5 crore × ₹320 = **₹480 crore to the selling shareholders**.\nA) Assumes the company receives everything. C) Swaps the two. D) Double counts. **Total issue size is ₹1,120 crore, but only ₹640 crore is fresh capital for the business.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Which of these correctly explains why a bonus issue increases share capital while a stock split does not?\nA) A bonus reduces face value  B) A bonus capitalises reserves into share capital at unchanged face value; a split only subdivides existing capital at reduced face value  C) A split capitalises reserves  D) Both increase share capital\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Bonus:** reserves → share capital, face value constant, so share capital **rises**. **Split:** the same share capital is divided into more units of smaller face value, so share capital is **unchanged**.\nA) A bonus leaves face value unchanged. C) A split involves no reserves. D) Only the bonus increases share capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** An investor buys 100 shares cum-rights at ₹900 the day before the ex-rights date. The rights are 1:2 at ₹600. What is her theoretical position on the ex-date if she subscribes fully?\nA) 150 shares at ₹800, value ₹1,20,000  B) 100 shares at ₹800  C) 150 shares at ₹900  D) 50 shares at ₹600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — TERP = (2 × 900 + 1 × 600) ÷ 3 = (1,800 + 600) ÷ 3 = 2,400 ÷ 3 = **₹800**. She holds 100 shares and subscribes to 100 ÷ 2 = **50 rights shares** → **150 shares** at ₹800 = **₹1,20,000**. Check: she paid 100 × 900 + 50 × 600 = 90,000 + 30,000 = **₹1,20,000** ✓\nB) Ignores the new shares. C) Fails to adjust the price. D) Counts only the rights shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A company declares a dividend of ₹25 per share. An investor in the 30% tax slab holds 2,000 shares. What is the post-tax dividend income (ignoring cess and TDS timing)?\nA) ₹50,000  B) ₹35,000  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹50,000 tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gross dividend = 2,000 × ₹25 = **₹50,000**. Dividends are **taxable in the shareholder's hands at slab rates**: tax = 30% × 50,000 = ₹15,000. Post-tax = **₹35,000**.\nA) Ignores tax. C) Is the tax amount, not the income. D) Dividends have not been tax-free in shareholders' hands since DDT was abolished.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Which sequence correctly describes what happens to a shareholder's percentage holding in each action?\nA) Bonus increases it; rights (fully subscribed) keeps it; buy-back (not participating) increases it  B) Bonus keeps it; rights fully subscribed keeps it; buy-back not participating increases it  C) All three reduce it  D) All three keep it unchanged\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Bonus:** everyone gets shares pro rata, so percentages are **unchanged**. **Rights fully subscribed:** pro rata again, so **unchanged**. **Buy-back where you do not tender:** total shares fall while yours stay, so your percentage **rises**.\nA) A bonus does not raise anyone's percentage. C) None of the three reduces it under these assumptions. D) The buy-back case does change it.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A company's free-float is 40% of its 50 crore shares, and the share price is ₹600. What is the total market capitalisation and the free-float market capitalisation?\nA) ₹30,000 crore and ₹12,000 crore  B) ₹12,000 crore and ₹30,000 crore  C) ₹30,000 crore and ₹30,000 crore  D) ₹20,000 crore and ₹8,000 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Total market cap** = 50 crore × ₹600 = **₹30,000 crore**. **Free float** = 40% × 50 crore = 20 crore shares × ₹600 = **₹12,000 crore**.\nB) Swaps them. C) Ignores the free-float adjustment. D) Miscomputes the share count. **Index weight is based on the free-float figure, not the total.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A company announces a rights issue but the market price falls below the rights price before the issue closes. What is the likely outcome?\nA) Shareholders will rush to subscribe  B) Shareholders will avoid subscribing, since they can buy cheaper in the market, and the issue may be undersubscribed  C) SEBI will cancel the issue  D) The rights price automatically adjusts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If the **market price falls below the rights price**, the entitlement becomes worthless and rational shareholders buy in the market instead — the issue then depends on **underwriters**.\nA) There is no incentive to pay more than the market price. C) SEBI does not cancel for this reason. D) The rights price is fixed in the offer document. **This is why rights are priced at a meaningful discount.**\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** An acquirer buys 26% of a listed company's voting rights. Under the SEBI (SAST) Regulations, what is triggered?\nA) Nothing  B) An obligation to make an open offer to public shareholders, having crossed the prescribed threshold  C) Automatic delisting  D) A mandatory bonus issue\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Crossing the prescribed threshold (25% of voting rights) triggers a **mandatory open offer** so public shareholders can exit at the same price.\nA) The threshold has been breached. C) Delisting is a separate process. D) No such obligation exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Which explains why the settlement cycle was shortened from T+2 to T+1?\nA) To increase risk  B) To reduce settlement and counterparty risk and free up investor funds and securities faster  C) To slow trading  D) It was mandated by the RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A shorter cycle means **less time for a counterparty to fail** and quicker availability of funds and securities.\nA) It reduces risk. C) It improves capital efficiency, aiding activity. D) It was a **SEBI** decision — equities are SEBI's domain.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A company has ₹500 crore of reserves and ₹100 crore of share capital (10 crore shares of ₹10). It declares a 1:1 bonus. What are the post-bonus reserves and share capital?\nA) Reserves ₹400 crore; share capital ₹200 crore  B) Reserves ₹500 crore; share capital ₹200 crore  C) Reserves ₹600 crore; share capital ₹100 crore  D) Reserves ₹400 crore; share capital ₹100 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A 1:1 bonus creates 10 crore new shares of ₹10 = **₹100 crore of new share capital**, funded by capitalising **₹100 crore of reserves**. Reserves = 500 − 100 = **₹400 crore**; share capital = 100 + 100 = **₹200 crore**. **Total net worth stays ₹600 crore.**\nB) Fails to reduce reserves — the money must come from somewhere. C) Reverses the flow. D) Fails to increase share capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** An investor applies for an IPO through ASBA with ₹2,00,000 blocked. The issue is oversubscribed 20 times and she receives no allotment. What happens?\nA) The money is refunded after 15 days  B) The blocked amount is simply unblocked, and she earned interest throughout  C) The money is forfeited  D) She must apply again\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under ASBA there is **no debit and therefore no refund** — the block is simply released, and interest accrued the whole time.\nA) Describes the pre-ASBA refund process. C) Application money is never forfeited. D) No re-application is needed. **This is precisely the advantage ASBA delivers.**\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Which combination of corporate actions would leave an investor with more shares but no change in total value or percentage ownership?\nA) Buy-back and dividend  B) Bonus issue and stock split  C) Rights issue and OFS  D) Merger and delisting\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both a **bonus** and a **split** increase the share count while the price adjusts proportionately, leaving **value and percentage ownership unchanged**.\nA) A buy-back changes the share count and a dividend removes cash. C) A rights issue requires payment; an OFS is a sale by someone else. D) A merger changes the entity and delisting removes the listing.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A stock has a 5% price band. It opens at ₹200 and rises to ₹210 within minutes. What can happen for the rest of the day?\nA) It can rise indefinitely  B) It has hit the upper band and cannot trade above ₹210 today  C) The market halts  D) The stock is delisted\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 5% of ₹200 = ₹10, so the upper band is **₹210** — reached, and no higher trading is permitted that day.\nA) The band is a hard limit. C) A single stock hitting its band does not halt the market — that requires an **index** move. D) Delisting is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Which statement about QIPs is INCORRECT?\nA) Only QIBs may participate  B) Retail investors may participate up to ₹2 lakh  C) No draft prospectus needs to be filed with SEBI  D) Pricing follows a prescribed formula based on recent market prices\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Retail investors cannot participate in a QIP at all** — it is restricted to QIBs by definition.\nA), C) and D) are all correct features of a QIP.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A demerger results in shareholders of the original company receiving shares in the new entity. What happens to the original share price on the ex-date?\nA) It stays the same  B) It falls, reflecting the value of the business transferred out  C) It doubles  D) It becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The parent no longer owns the demerged business, so its price **falls by roughly that business's value** — but shareholders receive shares in the new entity, so **total value is preserved**.\nA) The parent has lost assets. C) and D) are incorrect. **Structurally identical to a bonus: value is redistributed, not created or destroyed.**\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A company's promoters hold 75% and the minimum public shareholding requirement is 25%. The promoters wish to reduce their stake without diluting existing shareholders. Which mechanism fits best?\nA) A fresh issue  B) An Offer for Sale of promoter shares  C) A bonus issue  D) A buy-back\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **OFS sells existing promoter shares**, raising public shareholding **without creating new shares**, so existing shareholders are not diluted.\nA) A fresh issue creates new shares and **dilutes** everyone. C) A bonus leaves all percentages unchanged. D) A buy-back typically **raises** the promoter percentage — the opposite of what is needed.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which is the correct ranking of investor categories by minimum IPO allocation in a book-built issue, largest first?\nA) Retail (35%), QIB (50%), NII (15%)  B) QIB (up to 50%), retail (at least 35%), NII (at least 15%)  C) NII (50%), QIB (35%), retail (15%)  D) All equal at 33.3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **QIB up to 50% > retail at least 35% > NII at least 15%.**\nA) Lists the right numbers but in the wrong rank order. C) Scrambles the categories. D) There is no equal allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An investor buys shares two days before the record date under T+1 settlement. Is she entitled to the dividend?\nA) No  B) Yes — her trade settles before the record date, so she is on the register  C) Only if she holds for a year  D) Only with company approval\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Buying two days before the record date means settlement (T+1) occurs **one day before** the record date, so she appears on the register and is **entitled**.\nA) She bought before the ex-date. C) There is no holding-period condition for dividend entitlement. D) No approval is needed. **The test is whether you are on the register on the record date.**\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Which best explains why free-float weighting is preferred to full market-cap weighting for an index?\nA) It is simpler to compute  B) It reflects the shares actually available for trading, so the index is investable and replicable by funds  C) It gives promoters more weight  D) It excludes institutional holdings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An index fund can only buy shares that **trade**. Weighting by free float makes the index **investable and replicable**.\nA) It is more complex, not less. C) It gives promoters **less** weight by excluding their holdings. D) Institutional holdings are freely tradable and are included.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A company simultaneously announces a rights issue at a discount and a bonus issue. Which affects the company's cash and which does not?\nA) Both raise cash  B) The rights issue raises cash; the bonus issue does not  C) The bonus issue raises cash; the rights issue does not  D) Neither raises cash\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Shareholders **pay** for rights shares (cash **in**); bonus shares are **free**, funded by capitalising reserves (**no cash movement**).\nA) A bonus raises nothing. C) Reverses the two. D) A rights issue definitely raises cash.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** An investor holds 2,000 shares. The company announces a buy-back of 10% of outstanding shares via tender offer at a 20% premium. If she tenders proportionately and the acceptance ratio is 10%, how many shares are bought back from her?\nA) 2,000  B) 200  C) 20  D) 400\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **10% acceptance ratio** on 2,000 shares means **200 shares** are accepted; the remaining 1,800 are returned to her.\nA) Assumes full acceptance, which a proportionate tender offer does not give. C) Uses 1%. D) Uses 20%. **She realises the 20% premium on 200 shares and continues to hold 1,800.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A company's shares trade at ₹1,200 and it announces a 1:2 rights issue at ₹900. An investor holding 400 shares decides to renounce her entire entitlement. Approximately what value should she realise?\nA) ₹1,20,000  B) ₹20,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹1,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — TERP = (2 × 1,200 + 1 × 900) ÷ 3 = (2,400 + 900) ÷ 3 = 3,300 ÷ 3 = **₹1,100**. Value of one right = 1,200 − 1,100 = **₹100**. Her entitlement = 400 ÷ 2 = **200 rights**. Value = 200 × ₹100 = **₹20,000**.\nA) ₹1,20,000 wrongly uses the full share price. C) ₹60,000 uses the ₹300 discount on 200 rights, which overstates the right's value. D) ₹1,80,000 has no basis. **Renouncing captures the right's value; letting it lapse forfeits it entirely.**\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single question best distinguishes every corporate action in this chapter?\nA) Does the share price change?  B) Does money move, and does the shareholder's total value change?  C) Is SEBI involved?  D) Is the company listed?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — These two tests classify them all. **Money moves:** rights (in), dividend (out), buy-back (out), OFS (to the seller). **No money moves:** bonus, split, demerger. **Total value unchanged:** bonus, split, demerger. **Value changes:** dividend (cash received separately), buy-back, rights.\nA) The price changes in nearly all of them, so it does not discriminate. C) SEBI is involved throughout. D) All these actions concern listed companies.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Track the share count, the price and the total value at every step.\n\n### Case: Corporate Actions at Meridian Industries Ltd\n\nPriya holds **3,000 shares** of Meridian Industries Ltd, of **₹10 face value**, currently trading at **₹1,600**.\n\nOver the next twelve months Meridian announces the following, in sequence:\n\n1. A **final dividend of ₹32 per share**.\n2. A **1:2 bonus issue** (one free share for every two held).\n3. A **1:5 stock split**.\n4. A **1:4 rights issue at ₹300 per share** (based on the post-split holding).\n\nPriya is in the **30% income tax slab**.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is Priya's post-tax dividend income from step 1?\nA) ₹96,000  B) ₹67,200  C) ₹28,800  D) ₹96,000, tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gross dividend = 3,000 × ₹32 = **₹96,000**. Dividends are **taxable in the shareholder's hands at slab rates**: tax = 30% × 96,000 = **₹28,800**. Post-tax = 96,000 − 28,800 = **₹67,200**.\nA) Ignores tax entirely. C) Is the tax amount, not the income. D) Dividends have been taxable in shareholders' hands since Dividend Distribution Tax was abolished — TDS also applies above the prescribed threshold.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** After the 1:2 bonus, how many shares does Priya hold and at what adjusted price (assume the price before the bonus is ₹1,600)?\nA) 4,500 shares at ₹1,066.67  B) 6,000 shares at ₹800  C) 4,500 shares at ₹1,600  D) 1,500 shares at ₹3,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Bonus shares = 3,000 × (1 ÷ 2) = **1,500**. Total = 3,000 + 1,500 = **4,500 shares**. Value before = 3,000 × 1,600 = **₹48,00,000**; adjusted price = 48,00,000 ÷ 4,500 = **₹1,066.67**. Value unchanged ✓\nB) Assumes a 1:1 bonus. C) Fails to adjust the price — the commonest error. D) Reduces the share count, which a bonus never does.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** After the 1:5 split, what are Priya's share count, face value and price?\nA) 22,500 shares, ₹2 face value, ₹213.33  B) 22,500 shares, ₹10 face value, ₹213.33  C) 900 shares, ₹50 face value, ₹5,333  D) 4,500 shares, ₹2 face value, ₹1,066.67\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **1:5 split** turns each ₹10 share into five ₹2 shares: 4,500 × 5 = **22,500 shares**, face value **₹10 ÷ 5 = ₹2**, price ₹1,066.67 ÷ 5 = **₹213.33**. Value = 22,500 × 213.33 = **₹48,00,000**, still unchanged.\nB) Gets the count right but **forgets that a split reduces face value** — the key contrast with a bonus. C) Reverses the split. D) Fails to increase the share count.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Priya is entitled to a 1:4 rights issue at ₹300 on her post-split holding. How many rights shares can she buy, and what is the theoretical ex-rights price?\nA) 5,625 shares; TERP ₹213.33  B) 5,625 shares; TERP ₹230.66  C) 4,500 shares; TERP ₹300  D) 22,500 shares; TERP ₹256.67\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Entitlement = 22,500 ÷ 4 = **5,625 rights shares** at ₹300.\n**TERP** = (4 old shares × ₹213.33 + 1 new share × ₹300) ÷ 5 = (853.32 + 300) ÷ 5 = 1,153.32 ÷ 5 = **₹230.66**.\n\nA) ₹213.33 is the **pre-rights** price — issuing new shares at a *different* price must move it. C) Miscounts the entitlement and simply quotes the rights price. D) Assumes a 1:1 entitlement.\n\n> ⚠️ **Notice something unusual:** the TERP (₹230.66) is **higher** than the pre-rights price (₹213.33), because the rights price of ₹300 is **above** the market price. That is the reverse of a normal rights issue — and it means these rights have **no value**. See QC5.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Given that the rights price of ₹300 exceeds the post-split market price of ₹213.33, what should Priya do?\nA) Subscribe fully, since rights are always beneficial  B) Decline the rights — she can buy the same shares more cheaply in the open market  C) Renounce the rights for a large premium  D) Sue the company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A rights issue is only attractive when the **rights price is below the market price**. Here she would pay **₹300** for a share worth **₹213.33** — an immediate loss of ₹86.67 per share. She should simply buy in the market if she wants more shares.\nA) Rights are beneficial **only at a discount**. C) The rights have **no value** and cannot be renounced for a premium — nobody will pay for the right to overpay. D) There is nothing unlawful; badly priced rights issues simply fail and fall to the underwriters.\n\n> ⚠️ **The lesson this caselet is built to teach:** rights issues carry value **only when priced below market**. In the earlier questions the rights price sat well below market and each right was worth ₹60–₹100; here the sequence of bonus and split pushed the market price down to ₹213.33, leaving the ₹300 rights price stranded above it. **Always compare the rights price with the CURRENT (post-adjustment) market price, not the original one.**\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "07", "title": "Introduction to Investments", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "# Chapter 7: Introduction to Investments — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 3 · Investment Products (30 marks across Chapters 7–10).**\n> This chapter is the **map of the asset classes** and the **three routes to managed money (MF / PMS / AIF)**. The comparison table at the end is one of the most reliably examined items in the whole syllabus.\n\n---\n\n## 7.1 Types of Investment\n\nEvery investment can be placed on two axes: **what you own** and **how you own it**.\n\n### What you own — the asset classes\n\n| Asset class | You are… | Return comes from |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Equity** | An **owner** of the business | Capital appreciation + dividends |\n| **Fixed income (debt)** | A **lender** | Interest + capital gain/loss on price |\n| **Commodities** | An owner of a **physical good** | Price movement only |\n| **Real estate** | An owner of **property** | Rent + capital appreciation |\n| **Cash / money market** | A very short-term lender | Interest |\n\n> 🧠 **The single most useful distinction in finance: equity = ownership; debt = lending.** An owner gets what is left after everyone else is paid (residual claim, unlimited upside, first loss). A lender gets a fixed promise (contractual claim, capped upside, paid before owners).\n\n### The risk–return spectrum\n\n$$\\text{Higher expected return} \\Longleftrightarrow \\text{Higher risk}$$\n\nRoughly ascending: cash → government debt → high-grade corporate debt → high-yield debt → real estate → equity → derivatives/distressed.\n\n> ⚠️ **Never** treat this as a promise. Higher risk means a **wider range of outcomes**, including worse ones. If risk guaranteed return, it would not be risk.\n\n---\n\n## 7.2 Equity\n\n**Investment characteristics:**\n- **Residual claim** — paid last, after lenders and preference shareholders\n- **Unlimited upside**, but the whole capital is at risk\n- **No maturity** — a perpetual instrument\n- **Voting rights** (ordinary shares) — a say in governance\n- **High volatility** in the short run; historically the best long-run inflation-beater\n- Returns are **not contractual** — dividends are discretionary\n\n**Role in a portfolio:** the **growth engine**. Suitable for goals **5–7 years or more** away, where volatility has time to average out.\n\n### Listed vs unlisted equity\n\n| | **Listed** | **Unlisted** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Traded on** | A stock exchange | Private transactions |\n| **Liquidity** | High — exit any trading day | Very low |\n| **Price discovery** | Continuous, public | Negotiated, infrequent |\n| **Disclosure** | Extensive, regulated | Minimal |\n| **Valuation** | Market price | Estimated, subjective |\n| **Regulation** | SEBI, listing rules | Limited |\n\nUnlisted equity (private equity, venture capital, start-ups) may offer higher returns but carries **illiquidity, valuation and information risk**.\n\n---\n\n## 7.3 Fixed Income\n\n**Investment characteristics:**\n- A **contractual** obligation to pay interest and repay principal\n- **Predictable cash flows** — the main attraction\n- **Capped upside** — you can never earn more than the promised yield if held to maturity\n- **Senior** to equity in the event of liquidation\n- Has a **defined maturity**\n- Prices move **inversely** to interest rates\n\n**Role in a portfolio:** **stability, income and capital preservation**. Suitable for short-horizon goals and for dampening overall portfolio volatility.\n\n### Government vs corporate debt\n\n| | **Government** | **Corporate** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Credit risk** | Effectively nil (sovereign) | Real — the issuer can default |\n| **Yield** | Lower | Higher (a **credit spread** over government) |\n| **Liquidity** | High (G-secs) | Varies, often lower |\n\nThe extra yield on corporate debt is the **credit spread** — compensation for default risk.\n\n### Investment grade vs high yield\n\n| | **Investment grade** | **High yield (\"junk\")** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Rating** | **BBB−/Baa3 and above** | Below BBB− |\n| **Default risk** | Low | Elevated |\n| **Yield** | Lower | Higher |\n| **Behaviour in a crisis** | Defensive | Behaves more like equity |\n\n> ⚠️ **BBB− is the dividing line.** A downgrade from BBB− to BB+ (\"falling angel\") can force many institutional investors to sell, because mandates often prohibit sub-investment-grade holdings.\n\n---\n\n## 7.4 Commodities\n\n**Characteristics:**\n- **No cash flow** — no interest, no dividend, no rent. The entire return is **price movement**.\n- Often carries **storage and insurance costs** (a negative carry).\n- Historically an **inflation hedge**, especially energy and metals.\n- **Low correlation** with equity and debt, so it can improve diversification.\n- Access routes in India: **gold ETFs, Sovereign Gold Bonds, commodity derivatives**, and physical holding.\n\n**Role:** diversification and inflation protection — typically a **small satellite allocation**, not a core holding.\n\n---\n\n## 7.5 Real Estate\n\n**Characteristics:**\n- Generates **rental income** plus potential **capital appreciation**\n- **Large ticket size** — poor divisibility (you cannot sell one bedroom)\n- **Highly illiquid** — sale takes months\n- **High transaction costs** — stamp duty, registration, brokerage\n- **Location-specific** and hard to diversify\n- Valuation is **subjective**, with no continuous price discovery\n\n**Route to liquidity: REITs** (Real Estate Investment Trusts) — listed, SEBI-regulated trusts holding rent-yielding property. They give **exposure to real estate with the liquidity of a listed security** and a small ticket size. **InvITs** do the same for infrastructure assets.\n\n> ⚠️ A **self-occupied house is consumption, not investment** — it produces no income and cannot be partly liquidated (see Chapter 3's financial assets ratio).\n\n---\n\n## 7.6 Structured Products\n\nA **structured product** is a pre-packaged instrument combining a **bond (or deposit)** with a **derivative**, engineered to deliver a specific payoff.\n\n**Typical construction:** most of the money goes into a zero-coupon bond that grows back to par (providing capital protection), and the small remainder buys options on an index for the upside.\n\n| Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|\n| Customised payoff profiles | **Complex** and hard to value |\n| Possible capital protection | **Credit risk of the issuer** — \"capital protected\" is only as good as the issuer |\n| Access to otherwise hard-to-reach exposures | **Illiquid**, often with no secondary market |\n| | Costs and margins are often **opaque** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam point:** \"capital protection\" is **not** a guarantee — it depends entirely on the **issuer's creditworthiness**. And structured products are typically **not principal-protected before maturity**.\n\n---\n\n## 7.7 Distressed Securities\n\nSecurities of companies in or near **financial distress, default or bankruptcy**.\n\n- Trade at **deep discounts** to face value\n- Potential for very high returns if the company recovers or the recovery in resolution exceeds the purchase price\n- **Very high risk** of total loss\n- Require **specialist legal and restructuring expertise** — resolution under the **Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016** in India\n- **Highly illiquid**\n\n**Suitability:** sophisticated, institutional or very high-net-worth investors only — typically accessed through **Category II or III AIFs**.\n\n---\n\n## 7.8 Other Investment Opportunities\n\n**Art, paintings, antiques, collectibles, wine, vintage cars.**\n\n- **No cash flow**; return is purely price appreciation\n- **Highly illiquid** with wide bid–ask spreads\n- **Valuation is subjective** and expertise-dependent\n- **Authenticity and storage/insurance risk**\n- **Unregulated** — no investor protection framework\n- Often driven by **taste and scarcity** rather than fundamentals\n\n**Suitability:** a **passion allocation** for wealthy investors, not a core portfolio holding.\n\n---\n\n## 7.9 ⭐ Channels for Making Investments\n\n### Direct investments\nThe investor buys securities themselves — shares, bonds, deposits.\n\n| Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|\n| **Lowest cost** — no management fee | Requires **time, skill and temperament** |\n| Full **control** | Hard to **diversify** with a small corpus |\n| Complete **transparency** | All research and monitoring is the investor's own |\n\n### The role of Registered Investment Advisers (RIAs)\nAn RIA provides **advice**, for a **fee from the client**, under a **fiduciary duty**, with **advice segregated from distribution**. The investor still holds the assets directly; the adviser supplies the strategy, discipline and review.\n\n### Investments through managed portfolios\n\nThree vehicles — and the comparison below is **essential exam material**.\n\n---\n\n## ⭐ MF vs PMS vs AIF — The Comparison Table\n\n| Feature | **Mutual Fund** | **Portfolio Management Service** | **Alternative Investment Fund** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Minimum investment** | As low as **₹100–₹500** (SIP) | **₹50 lakh** | **₹1 crore** (₹25 lakh for employees/directors of the AIF) |\n| **What you hold** | **Units** of a pooled scheme | **Securities in your own name / demat account** | **Units** of the fund |\n| **Pooling** | Fully pooled | **Not pooled** — an individual portfolio | Pooled |\n| **Customisation** | None — everyone gets the same portfolio | **Possible** (especially non-discretionary) | Limited — defined by the fund's strategy |\n| **Regulation** | SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, **1996** | SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, **2020** | SEBI (AIF) Regulations, **2012** |\n| **Target investor** | Retail | HNI | HNI / institutional / sophisticated |\n| **Liquidity** | High (open-ended: any business day) | Moderate | Low — usually **close-ended with a lock-in** |\n| **Disclosure** | Highest — daily NAV, monthly portfolio | Moderate, to the client | Lowest — to investors only |\n| **Fees** | Capped **TER** | Fixed and/or **performance-linked** | Management + **carried interest** |\n| **Strategy freedom** | Most restricted | Moderate | **Widest** — can use leverage, derivatives, unlisted assets |\n\n> 🧠 **The one-line memory hook:**\n> **MF = pooled units, ₹500, retail, most regulated.**\n> **PMS = your own securities, ₹50 lakh, HNI.**\n> **AIF = pooled units, ₹1 crore, sophisticated, widest freedom.**\n>\n> Note the ladder: **as the minimum investment rises, regulation and disclosure fall and strategy freedom rises.** That is the whole logic — regulators protect small investors most tightly and assume large investors can protect themselves.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Equity vs debt | **Ownership** (residual claim) vs **lending** (contractual claim) |\n| Equity's role | The **growth engine**; for goals 5–7+ years away |\n| Debt's role | **Stability, income, capital preservation** |\n| Investment grade cut-off | **BBB− / Baa3** and above |\n| Credit spread | Extra yield on corporate over government debt, compensating **default risk** |\n| Bond price vs interest rates | Move **inversely** |\n| Commodities | **No cash flow**; return is price movement; inflation hedge; low correlation |\n| Real estate drawbacks | Illiquid, indivisible, high transaction costs, location-specific, subjective valuation |\n| REIT / InvIT | Real estate / infrastructure exposure with **listed liquidity** and small ticket size |\n| Self-occupied house | **Consumption**, not investment |\n| Structured product | A **bond + derivative** package; \"capital protection\" depends on **issuer credit** |\n| Distressed securities | Deep discount, very high risk, needs specialist skill; **IBC, 2016** |\n| Art/collectibles | No cash flow, illiquid, subjective, **unregulated** — a passion allocation |\n| Direct investing | Cheapest, but needs time, skill and temperament |\n| RIA | **Fee from the client**, **fiduciary**, advice **segregated** from distribution |\n| MF minimum | ₹100–₹500 |\n| PMS minimum | **₹50 lakh** |\n| AIF minimum | **₹1 crore** (₹25 lakh for AIF employees/directors) |\n| What PMS holds | Securities **in the client's own name** — not pooled |\n| Regulation ladder | Higher minimum → **less** regulation and disclosure, **more** strategy freedom |\n\n> **Exam tip:** most questions in this chapter are **discrimination** questions — \"which of these is characteristic of X but not Y\". Learn the *contrasts* (equity vs debt, listed vs unlisted, investment grade vs high yield, MF vs PMS vs AIF) rather than isolated facts.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 7: Introduction to Investments — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The MF vs PMS vs AIF table is the single highest-yield item in this chapter.**\n\n**Q1.** State the fundamental difference between equity and debt.\n> **A:** **Equity = ownership** — a **residual** claim, paid last, unlimited upside, first loss. **Debt = lending** — a **contractual** claim, paid before owners, capped upside.\n\n**Q2.** What is equity's role in a portfolio, and what horizon suits it?\n> **A:** The **growth engine** and long-run inflation-beater. Suitable for goals **5–7 years or more** away, where short-run volatility has time to average out.\n\n**Q3.** What is debt's role in a portfolio?\n> **A:** **Stability, income and capital preservation** — and dampening overall portfolio volatility. Suitable for short-horizon goals.\n\n**Q4.** Give four differences between listed and unlisted equity.\n> **A:** **Liquidity** (high vs very low), **price discovery** (continuous and public vs negotiated), **disclosure** (extensive vs minimal), and **valuation** (market price vs subjective estimate).\n\n**Q5.** What is the credit spread?\n> **A:** The **extra yield** a corporate bond offers over a comparable government bond — compensation for **default (credit) risk**.\n\n**Q6.** What is the dividing line between investment grade and high yield?\n> **A:** **BBB− / Baa3.** At or above is **investment grade**; below is **high yield (\"junk\")**.\n\n**Q7.** Why does a downgrade from BBB− to BB+ matter so much?\n> **A:** Many institutional mandates **prohibit sub-investment-grade holdings**, so a \"falling angel\" can trigger forced selling well beyond what the credit change alone would justify.\n\n**Q8.** How do bond prices respond to interest rates?\n> **A:** **Inversely** — when rates rise, existing bond prices fall, and vice versa.\n\n**Q9.** What is distinctive about commodities as an asset class?\n> **A:** They generate **no cash flow** — no interest, dividend or rent — so the **entire return is price movement**. They often carry storage/insurance costs, act as an **inflation hedge**, and have **low correlation** with equity and debt.\n\n**Q10.** How can an Indian investor gain commodity exposure?\n> **A:** **Gold ETFs, Sovereign Gold Bonds, commodity derivatives**, and physical holding.\n\n**Q11.** Name five drawbacks of direct real estate investment.\n> **A:** **Illiquidity**, **indivisibility** (large ticket size), **high transaction costs** (stamp duty, registration, brokerage), **location concentration**, and **subjective valuation** with no continuous price discovery.\n\n**Q12.** What is a REIT, and what problem does it solve?\n> **A:** A listed, SEBI-regulated trust holding rent-yielding property. It solves real estate's **illiquidity and large ticket size** — giving property exposure with the **liquidity of a listed security**. **InvITs** do the same for infrastructure.\n\n**Q13.** Is a self-occupied house an investment?\n> **A:** **No — it is consumption.** It produces no income and cannot be partly liquidated.\n\n**Q14.** What is a structured product, and how is it typically built?\n> **A:** A pre-packaged combination of a **bond (or deposit) plus a derivative**. Typically most of the money buys a **zero-coupon bond** that grows back to par (capital protection), and the remainder buys **options** for upside.\n\n**Q15.** Why is \"capital protection\" in a structured product not a guarantee?\n> **A:** It depends entirely on the **issuer's creditworthiness** — if the issuer defaults, the protection fails. Such products are also typically **not principal-protected before maturity**.\n\n**Q16.** What are distressed securities, and who should hold them?\n> **A:** Securities of companies in or near **default or bankruptcy**, trading at deep discounts. Very high risk of total loss, requiring **specialist legal and restructuring expertise** (in India, resolution under the **IBC, 2016**). Suitable only for **sophisticated/institutional investors**, typically via Category II or III AIFs.\n\n**Q17.** What are the drawbacks of art and collectibles as investments?\n> **A:** **No cash flow**, **highly illiquid** with wide spreads, **subjective valuation**, **authenticity and storage risk**, and **completely unregulated** — no investor protection framework.\n\n**Q18.** What are the trade-offs of investing directly?\n> **A:** **Lowest cost** and full control, but it demands **time, skill and temperament**, and diversification is hard with a small corpus.\n\n**Q19.** What defines a Registered Investment Adviser's model?\n> **A:** **Advice for a fee paid by the client**, under a **fiduciary duty**, with **advice segregated from distribution**.\n\n**Q20.** State the minimum investment for a mutual fund, PMS and AIF.\n> **A:** **MF ₹100–₹500** · **PMS ₹50 lakh** · **AIF ₹1 crore** (₹25 lakh for employees/directors of the AIF).\n\n**Q21.** What is the crucial structural difference between a mutual fund and a PMS?\n> **A:** A mutual fund gives you **units of a pooled scheme**. A PMS holds **securities in your own name / demat account** — it is **not pooled**.\n\n**Q22.** Which regulations govern MFs, PMS and AIFs, and from which years?\n> **A:** SEBI **(Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996**; SEBI **(Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020**; SEBI **(Alternative Investment Funds) Regulations, 2012**.\n\n**Q23.** Rank MF, PMS and AIF by disclosure and by strategy freedom.\n> **A:** **Disclosure:** MF (highest — daily NAV, monthly portfolio) > PMS > AIF (lowest). **Strategy freedom:** AIF (widest — leverage, derivatives, unlisted) > PMS > MF (most restricted).\n\n**Q24.** What is the underlying logic of the MF/PMS/AIF regulatory ladder?\n> **A:** As the **minimum investment rises**, regulation and disclosure **fall** and strategy freedom **rises** — regulators protect small investors most tightly and assume large investors can protect themselves.\n\n**Q25.** Which asset classes produce no cash flow at all?\n> **A:** **Commodities** and **art/collectibles** — their entire return is price appreciation.\n\n**Q26.** How are MF, PMS and AIF fees structured?\n> **A:** **MF** — a capped **Total Expense Ratio**. **PMS** — fixed and/or **performance-linked** fees. **AIF** — management fee plus **carried interest**.\n\n**Q27.** State the risk–return principle and its critical caveat.\n> **A:** **Higher expected return goes with higher risk.** The caveat: higher risk means a **wider range of outcomes, including worse ones** — it is never a promise of higher return. If risk guaranteed return, it would not be risk.\n\n**Q28.** Roughly order the asset classes by ascending risk.\n> **A:** **Cash → government debt → high-grade corporate debt → high-yield debt → real estate → equity → derivatives/distressed.**\n\n**Q29.** Why does equity have no maturity?\n> **A:** It is a **perpetual** instrument representing **ownership** — there is no date on which the company must repay a shareholder, unlike a bond's principal.\n\n**Q30.** Which is senior in liquidation — equity or debt?\n> **A:** **Debt.** Lenders are paid before preference shareholders, who are paid before ordinary shareholders. Equity holders receive only the **residual**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 7: Introduction to Investments — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Scenario & Discrimination). **Most questions here test contrasts**, especially MF vs PMS vs AIF. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** An equity shareholder has:\nA) A contractual claim to fixed interest  B) A residual claim on the company's profits and assets  C) Priority over lenders in liquidation  D) A guaranteed dividend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity is **ownership**, carrying a **residual** claim — shareholders are paid only after all other claimants.\nA) Describes debt. C) Reverses the priority — lenders rank **ahead** of shareholders. D) Dividends are discretionary, never guaranteed.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A bondholder is:\nA) An owner of the company  B) A lender to the company  C) A director  D) A regulator\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Debt means **lending**: a contractual right to interest and repayment of principal.\nA) Describes equity. C) and D) are unrelated roles.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The minimum investment in a Portfolio Management Service is:\nA) ₹5 lakh  B) ₹25 lakh  C) ₹50 lakh  D) ₹1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — SEBI prescribes a **₹50 lakh** minimum for PMS.\nA) and B) are below the threshold. D) ₹1 crore is the **AIF** minimum — the classic confusion.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The minimum investment in an Alternative Investment Fund is generally:\nA) ₹50 lakh  B) ₹1 crore  C) ₹10 lakh  D) ₹5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹1 crore**, reduced to ₹25 lakh for employees and directors of the AIF or its manager.\nA) Is the PMS minimum. C) and D) are not the prescribed thresholds.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** In a Portfolio Management Service, the securities are held:\nA) In a pooled scheme  B) In the client's own name / demat account  C) By SEBI  D) By the stock exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PMS is **not pooled**; the client holds the securities directly. This is its defining structural difference from a mutual fund.\nA) Describes a mutual fund or AIF. C) and D) never hold client securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The dividing line between investment grade and high yield debt is:\nA) AAA  B) A−  C) BBB−  D) B+\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **BBB− (or Baa3)** and above is investment grade; below it is high yield.\nA) AAA is the top rating, not the boundary. B) and D) sit on either side of the boundary but are not it.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which asset class produces no cash flow?\nA) Equity  B) Fixed income  C) Commodities  D) Real estate (let out)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Commodities pay **no interest, dividend or rent** — the entire return is price movement.\nA) Equity can pay dividends. B) Debt pays interest. D) Let-out property generates rent.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Bond prices and interest rates move:\nA) In the same direction  B) Inversely  C) Independently  D) Only upward\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When rates rise, existing bonds paying lower coupons become less attractive, so their prices **fall**.\nA) and C) are incorrect. D) Bond prices certainly fall as well as rise.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A REIT provides:\nA) Direct ownership of a flat  B) Real estate exposure with the liquidity of a listed security  C) Guaranteed rental income  D) Commodity exposure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **REIT** is a listed, SEBI-regulated trust holding rent-yielding property, solving real estate's illiquidity and large ticket size.\nA) A REIT gives units, not a specific property. C) Distributions depend on rental performance. D) That would be a commodity ETF.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A structured product typically combines:\nA) Two equities  B) A bond (or deposit) with a derivative  C) Two bonds  D) Gold and silver\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Most of the money goes into a **zero-coupon bond** (for capital protection) and the remainder into **options** (for upside).\nA), C) and D) do not describe structured products.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** \"Capital protection\" in a structured product depends on:\nA) SEBI's guarantee  B) The issuer's creditworthiness  C) The stock market  D) The RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The protection is only a **promise by the issuer**; if the issuer defaults, it fails.\nA) SEBI regulates but guarantees nothing. C) The bond leg, not the market, provides the protection. D) The RBI has no role here.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Distressed securities are:\nA) Securities of highly rated companies  B) Securities of companies in or near default, trading at deep discounts  C) Government bonds  D) Bank deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — They trade at deep discounts, carry very high risk of total loss, and need specialist restructuring expertise.\nA), C) and D) all describe low-risk instruments — the opposite of distressed.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which of these is a mutual fund's structural feature?\nA) Securities held in the investor's own demat account  B) Investors hold units of a pooled scheme  C) A ₹50 lakh minimum  D) No regulation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutual funds are **fully pooled**; investors hold **units**.\nA) Describes a PMS. C) Is the PMS minimum. D) Mutual funds are the **most** regulated of the three vehicles.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A Registered Investment Adviser is paid:\nA) By commission from the product manufacturer  B) By a fee from the client  C) By SEBI  D) By the stock exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An RIA charges the **client** a fee, operates under a **fiduciary** duty, and must **segregate advice from distribution**.\nA) That is a distributor's model, and mixing the two is precisely what segregation prevents. C) and D) do not pay advisers.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Which regulations govern mutual funds in India?\nA) SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996  B) SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020  C) SEBI (AIF) Regulations, 2012  D) The Companies Act, 2013\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996**.\nB) Governs PMS. C) Governs AIFs. D) Governs companies generally.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Unlisted equity is characterised by:\nA) High liquidity  B) Very low liquidity and subjective valuation  C) Daily price discovery  D) Extensive public disclosure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Without an exchange there is no ready market and no continuous price.\nA), C) and D) all describe **listed** equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** The credit spread is:\nA) The difference between two equity prices  B) The extra yield on corporate debt over government debt  C) A brokerage fee  D) A type of derivative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It compensates the lender for **default risk**.\nA), C) and D) are unrelated concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Equity is best suited to goals that are:\nA) Less than 1 year away  B) 5–7 years or more away  C) Exactly 2 years away  D) Already due\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity's short-run volatility needs a **long horizon** to average out.\nA), C) and D) are all far too short — a badly timed drawdown would leave the goal unfunded.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which of these is NOT a characteristic of real estate?\nA) High transaction costs  B) Illiquidity  C) Easy divisibility  D) Location concentration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Real estate is **indivisible** — you cannot sell one bedroom.\nA), B) and D) are all genuine characteristics.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A self-occupied house is best classified as:\nA) An investment asset  B) Consumption  C) A liquid asset  D) A financial asset\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It produces **no income** and cannot be partly liquidated.\nA) Investment assets generate returns or income. C) Property is highly illiquid. D) It is a **physical**, not financial, asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** An InvIT provides exposure to:\nA) Equity shares  B) Infrastructure assets  C) Gold  D) Government bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **InvIT** does for infrastructure what a REIT does for real estate.\nA), C) and D) are covered by other instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which vehicle offers the widest strategy freedom?\nA) Mutual fund  B) PMS  C) AIF  D) Bank deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **AIFs** can use leverage, derivatives and unlisted assets far more freely than the other vehicles.\nA) Mutual funds are the **most restricted**. B) PMS sits in between. D) A deposit has no strategy at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which vehicle has the highest disclosure requirements?\nA) AIF  B) PMS  C) Mutual fund  D) They are identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Mutual funds publish **daily NAV and monthly portfolios** — far more than PMS or AIF.\nA) AIFs disclose least. B) PMS sits in between. D) They differ substantially.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Sovereign Gold Bonds give exposure to:\nA) Equity  B) Gold  C) Real estate  D) Corporate debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SGBs are a route to **gold** exposure without holding physical metal.\nA), C) and D) are different asset classes.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** In liquidation, the order of payment is:\nA) Equity → preference → debt  B) Debt → preference → equity  C) Preference → debt → equity  D) All simultaneously\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Lenders first**, then preference shareholders, then ordinary shareholders (the residual).\nA) and C) invert the seniority. D) Claims are ranked, not simultaneous.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Art and collectibles are characterised by:\nA) Regular cash flow  B) No cash flow, illiquidity and subjective valuation  C) Daily price discovery  D) Strong regulation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — They pay nothing, trade rarely at wide spreads, and are valued subjectively.\nA) They generate no income. C) There is no continuous market. D) They are **unregulated**.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which of these has the lowest credit risk?\nA) A high-yield corporate bond  B) A government security  C) An unrated debenture  D) A distressed bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sovereign debt carries **effectively nil** credit risk in domestic currency.\nA), C) and D) all carry material or extreme default risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** The main advantage of investing directly rather than through a managed vehicle is:\nA) Guaranteed returns  B) Lowest cost, with no management fee  C) Automatic diversification  D) No effort required\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Direct investing avoids management fees entirely.\nA) No route guarantees returns. C) Diversification is **harder** directly with a small corpus. D) Direct investing requires the **most** effort.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which asset class is the traditional long-run inflation-beater?\nA) Cash  B) Equity  C) Government bonds  D) Bank deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity's returns have historically outpaced inflation over long periods.\nA), C) and D) typically deliver low or negative **real** returns after tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The AIF minimum investment for employees and directors of the AIF is:\nA) ₹1 crore  B) ₹50 lakh  C) ₹25 lakh  D) ₹5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A concession to **₹25 lakh** applies to employees and directors of the AIF or its manager.\nA) Is the general minimum. B) Is the PMS minimum. D) Is not a prescribed threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which statement about commodities is correct?\nA) They pay regular interest  B) They often carry storage and insurance costs  C) They are risk-free  D) They are highly correlated with equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Physical commodities incur **storage and insurance** costs — a negative carry.\nA) They pay nothing. C) Commodity prices are highly volatile. D) Their **low** correlation with equity is precisely why they diversify.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Distressed debt resolution in India operates primarily under:\nA) The Companies Act, 1956  B) The Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016  C) FEMA  D) The SEBI Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **IBC, 2016** provides the resolution framework.\nA) Has largely been superseded. C) Governs foreign exchange. D) Governs securities markets.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Which vehicle allows a customised portfolio for an individual client?\nA) A mutual fund  B) A PMS, especially non-discretionary  C) An index fund  D) An ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because PMS holds securities individually rather than pooling them, the portfolio **can be customised**.\nA), C) and D) all give every investor the same pooled portfolio.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations are from the year:\nA) 1996  B) 2012  C) 2020  D) 1992\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The current **SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020**.\nA) Mutual Funds. B) AIFs. D) The SEBI Act itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Higher expected return is associated with:\nA) Lower risk  B) Higher risk — a wider range of outcomes, including worse ones  C) No risk  D) Guaranteed outcomes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk means **dispersion of outcomes**, not a promise of more.\nA) and C) invert the relationship. D) If risk guaranteed return, it would not be risk.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A client with ₹40 lakh wants a professionally managed portfolio of individually held securities. What can you tell her?\nA) She qualifies for a PMS  B) She falls below the ₹50 lakh PMS minimum, so a mutual fund is the appropriate route  C) She qualifies for an AIF  D) She must invest directly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹40 lakh is below the ₹50 lakh PMS minimum**, so PMS is not available. Mutual funds have no such barrier and offer professional management.\nA) She is ₹10 lakh short. C) AIFs need ₹1 crore — even further out of reach. D) Direct investing is possible but not the only alternative.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A client wants exposure to commercial real estate but has only ₹2 lakh and needs liquidity. The best route is:\nA) Buying a shop  B) A listed REIT  C) An unlisted property fund  D) Physical gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **REIT** provides commercial property exposure at a **small ticket size** with **listed liquidity**.\nA) ₹2 lakh cannot buy commercial property, and it would be illiquid. C) Unlisted funds have high minimums and lock-ins. D) Gold is a different asset class entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Which pairing correctly matches vehicle to minimum investment?\nA) MF ₹50 lakh; PMS ₹1 crore; AIF ₹500  B) MF ₹500; PMS ₹50 lakh; AIF ₹1 crore  C) MF ₹1 crore; PMS ₹500; AIF ₹50 lakh  D) All three ₹50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The ladder is **₹500 → ₹50 lakh → ₹1 crore**.\nA) and C) scramble the order. D) The minimums differ sharply, which is the whole point of the ladder.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An investor holds a BBB− rated bond that is downgraded to BB+. What is the likely consequence?\nA) The price rises  B) The bond falls below investment grade, potentially triggering forced selling by mandate-constrained institutions, so the price falls  C) Nothing changes  D) The coupon rises automatically\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Crossing **below BBB−** makes it high yield. Many institutional mandates prohibit sub-investment-grade holdings, so the technical selling pressure often exceeds the credit deterioration alone.\nA) Downgrades push prices down. C) The rating boundary matters enormously. D) The coupon is contractually fixed.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A client asks why a \"capital protected\" structured product is not risk-free. The best answer is:\nA) It is risk-free  B) The protection is only the issuer's promise, so the client bears the issuer's credit risk — and protection typically applies only at maturity  C) SEBI does not allow such products  D) The stock market guarantees it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two points: **issuer credit risk**, and the fact that protection generally applies **only if held to maturity**.\nA) It carries real risk. C) Such products are permitted. D) No market guarantees principal.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Which client is best suited to distressed securities?\nA) A 25-year-old starting their first SIP  B) A retiree needing monthly income  C) A sophisticated HNI with specialist advice, investing a small satellite allocation via a Category II or III AIF  D) A client with a 6-month goal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Distressed investing needs **legal and restructuring expertise**, tolerance for total loss, and a long, illiquid horizon.\nA) A beginner should not start here. B) Distressed securities pay nothing reliably. D) They are entirely illiquid over 6 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A client says \"equity is risky, so I'll put my 20-year retirement money in fixed deposits\". The best response is:\nA) Agreed — FDs are always safer  B) Over 20 years the greater risk is inflation eroding purchasing power; FDs often deliver a negative post-tax real return  C) Equity guarantees higher returns  D) She should use only gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Over a long horizon, **shortfall/inflation risk dominates volatility risk**. An FD at 7% with 6% inflation and 30% tax delivers roughly **−1% real**.\nA) \"Safe\" nominally is not safe in real terms. C) Nothing guarantees equity returns. D) Gold is a diversifier, not a retirement solution.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which of these best describes the regulatory logic of the MF/PMS/AIF ladder?\nA) Higher minimums attract more regulation  B) As the minimum investment rises, regulation and disclosure fall while strategy freedom rises  C) All three are regulated identically  D) AIFs are the most regulated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regulators protect **small investors** most tightly and assume large investors can protect themselves.\nA) Reverses the relationship. C) They differ substantially. D) AIFs are the **least** disclosure-intensive of the three.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A company is liquidated. Its assets cover the bank loan and debentures fully, with a small surplus. Who receives the surplus?\nA) The debenture holders again  B) Preference shareholders, then ordinary shareholders  C) Only the government  D) Nobody\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — After lenders are paid in full, **preference shareholders** rank next, and any remainder goes to **ordinary shareholders** as the residual claim.\nA) Lenders are paid only their contractual entitlement. C) The government's claims rank as a specified creditor, not as a residual owner. D) The surplus belongs to the owners.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A client wants a portfolio that can use leverage and invest in unlisted companies. Which vehicle is appropriate?\nA) A mutual fund  B) An AIF  C) A bank deposit  D) An index fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **AIFs** have the widest strategy freedom, including leverage, derivatives and unlisted assets.\nA) Mutual funds face tight restrictions on leverage and unlisted exposure. C) A deposit has no strategy. D) An index fund merely tracks a benchmark.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Which of these correctly distinguishes a mutual fund from a PMS?\nA) Both pool investor money  B) A mutual fund pools money into units; a PMS holds securities in the client's own name  C) A PMS is cheaper  D) A mutual fund has a ₹50 lakh minimum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the defining structural difference.\nA) A PMS does **not** pool. C) PMS fees are generally **higher**, often with performance components. D) ₹50 lakh is the **PMS** minimum.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** An investor holds unlisted shares in a start-up and wants to exit next month. The likely outcome is:\nA) An easy sale on the exchange  B) Difficulty — unlisted equity has no ready market, so exit depends on finding a buyer or a corporate event  C) SEBI will buy the shares  D) Guaranteed exit at fair value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Unlisted equity is **highly illiquid**; exit typically requires a secondary sale, a funding round or an IPO.\nA) Unlisted shares do not trade on an exchange. C) SEBI does not buy securities. D) There is no guaranteed exit or price.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Gold's main portfolio role is:\nA) Generating regular income  B) Diversification and inflation protection as a small satellite allocation  C) Providing guaranteed returns  D) Serving as an emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gold's **low correlation** with equity and debt and its inflation-hedging history make it a diversifier — typically a modest allocation.\nA) Gold pays no income. C) Gold prices are volatile. D) Physical gold is not an ideal emergency asset, though gold ETFs are more liquid.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A client's adviser recommends only products from one fund house and earns commission on each. This conflicts most directly with:\nA) The definition of equity  B) The fiduciary duty and the segregation of advice from distribution  C) Bond pricing rules  D) The AIF minimum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An RIA must place the **client's interest first** and keep advice **separate from distribution**.\nA), C) and D) are unrelated to the conflict described.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Which asset would you expect to fall most in a sharp equity market crash?\nA) A government security  B) A high-yield corporate bond  C) A liquid fund  D) A bank fixed deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **High-yield debt behaves more like equity** in a crisis, because default risk rises exactly when the economy weakens.\nA) Government securities typically **rally** in a flight to safety. C) and D) are stable, capital-preserving instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** An investor wants ₹10,000 a month invested with professional management. The realistic option is:\nA) A PMS  B) An AIF  C) A mutual fund SIP  D) Distressed securities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only **mutual funds** accept such small amounts, via SIPs from as little as ₹100–₹500.\nA) Requires ₹50 lakh. B) Requires ₹1 crore. D) Requires sophistication and a large corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Which of these best captures why a self-occupied house should not count toward goal funding?\nA) It has no market value  B) It produces no income and cannot be partly sold, so it cannot fund a goal without the family losing their home  C) It is a financial asset  D) It is fully liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Its value is real but **inaccessible** without disrupting the family's housing.\nA) It certainly has market value. C) It is a **physical** asset. D) Property is highly illiquid.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A client compares a 9% government bond with an 11% corporate bond of the same maturity. The 2% difference represents:\nA) A tax benefit  B) The credit spread — compensation for default risk  C) A brokerage fee  D) Inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **credit spread** compensates the lender for the corporate issuer's **default risk**.\nA) Tax treatment does not create the spread. C) Brokerage is a transaction cost, not a yield difference. D) Inflation affects both bonds equally.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which statement about PMS fees is correct?\nA) They are capped like a mutual fund's TER  B) They can be fixed and/or performance-linked  C) There are no fees  D) They are paid by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PMS commonly charges a **fixed fee, a performance fee, or both** (with high watermark and hurdle rate mechanics — see Chapter 12).\nA) The TER cap applies to mutual funds. C) PMS is generally the more expensive route. D) SEBI does not pay fees.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** An investor is told a bond fund \"cannot lose money because bonds are safe\". The correct clarification is:\nA) The statement is accurate  B) Bond prices fall when interest rates rise, and issuers can default — so bond funds can and do lose value  C) Only equity funds lose money  D) Bond funds are guaranteed by the RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Debt carries **interest rate risk** and **credit risk**; both can produce losses.\nA) It is a common and costly misconception. C) Debt funds have delivered negative returns in rate-rising and credit-event periods. D) No fund is RBI-guaranteed.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which combination correctly describes equity?\nA) Contractual claim, defined maturity, senior in liquidation  B) Residual claim, no maturity, last in liquidation, voting rights  C) Fixed interest, no voting rights  D) Guaranteed dividend, first claim\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All four are defining equity features.\nA) Describes debt. C) Describes debt or preference shares. D) No such instrument exists — dividends are never guaranteed and equity ranks last.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A client wants to invest ₹1.5 crore in a strategy using derivatives and short positions. The appropriate vehicle is:\nA) A mutual fund  B) A Category III AIF  C) A bank deposit  D) A REIT\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Category III AIFs** employ diverse or complex trading strategies including leverage and derivatives, and ₹1.5 crore clears the ₹1 crore minimum.\nA) Mutual funds face tight derivative restrictions. C) A deposit has no strategy. D) A REIT is a property vehicle.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which of these has the widest bid–ask spread and least price transparency?\nA) A Nifty 50 stock  B) A government security  C) An artwork  D) A liquid fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Art has **no continuous market**, is valued subjectively, and can carry very large spreads and long selling periods.\nA), B) and D) all have active markets and observable prices.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** An investor asks whether they should hold equity for a goal 18 months away. The answer is:\nA) Yes, equity always outperforms  B) No — 18 months is too short for equity's volatility; use debt or liquid instruments  C) Yes, if they are young  D) Yes, because of tax benefits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Horizon governs allocation.** With 18 months there is no time to recover from a drawdown.\nA) Equity's outperformance is a long-run tendency, not a short-run certainty. C) Age drives capacity, but the **goal's** horizon is what binds here. D) Tax never overrides the horizon match.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which is the most accurate statement about listed versus unlisted equity returns?\nA) Unlisted always returns more  B) Unlisted may offer higher returns but carries illiquidity, valuation and information risk  C) Listed always returns more  D) They are identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Any return premium comes with **real, compensating risks**.\nA) and C) state certainties that do not exist. D) The risk profiles differ sharply.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which vehicle would you recommend to a first-time investor with ₹5,000 a month?\nA) A PMS  B) An AIF  C) A diversified equity mutual fund SIP  D) Unlisted start-up equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Low minimum, **instant diversification**, professional management, daily liquidity and the highest disclosure standards.\nA) and B) are far beyond the amount. D) Unlisted equity is illiquid and unsuitable for a beginner.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** The main reason commodities improve a portfolio is:\nA) They pay high interest  B) Their low correlation with equity and debt improves diversification  C) They are risk-free  D) They are highly liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Assets that do not move together reduce overall portfolio volatility.\nA) Commodities pay nothing. C) They are volatile. D) Liquidity varies, and physical holdings are not especially liquid.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A client holds a \"capital protected\" note maturing in 5 years but needs the money in year 2. What should they expect?\nA) Full capital back  B) The protection applies only at maturity, so an early exit may realise less than the principal — and there may be no secondary market  C) A guaranteed profit  D) SEBI compensation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Structured products are typically **not principal-protected before maturity** and are often illiquid.\nA) Protection is a maturity feature. C) There is no guaranteed profit. D) SEBI does not compensate market outcomes.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which statement about AIF disclosure is correct?\nA) AIFs publish daily NAV  B) AIFs disclose primarily to their own investors and have the lowest public disclosure of the three vehicles  C) AIFs disclose more than mutual funds  D) AIFs have no reporting at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — AIFs report to investors under their fund documents, but public disclosure is minimal.\nA) Daily NAV is a **mutual fund** feature. C) Mutual funds disclose far more. D) AIFs do report — to investors and SEBI.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** An adviser recommends 100% equity to a 62-year-old retiree drawing monthly income. This is unsuitable primarily because:\nA) Equity returns are low  B) The retiree needs stability and predictable income, and a drawdown early in withdrawal would be very damaging  C) Equity is illegal for retirees  D) Equity has no dividends\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Withdrawing from a falling portfolio locks in losses (sequence-of-returns risk). A retiree needs a substantial **debt/income** component.\nA) Equity's long-run returns are strong — the problem is the horizon and drawdown need. C) There is no such prohibition. D) Equity can pay dividends, but they are not dependable income.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Which of these correctly orders the vehicles by liquidity, highest first?\nA) AIF → PMS → open-ended mutual fund  B) Open-ended mutual fund → PMS → AIF  C) PMS → AIF → mutual fund  D) All equal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Open-ended mutual funds redeem on **any business day**; PMS is moderate; AIFs are usually **close-ended with lock-ins**.\nA) Reverses the order. C) Misplaces the mutual fund. D) They differ substantially.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A high-yield bond fund and a government bond fund both fall in value. The most likely differing cause is:\nA) Both fell only from rate rises  B) The government fund fell mainly from interest rate risk; the high-yield fund also suffered credit spread widening  C) Neither is affected by rates  D) High-yield funds have no credit risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Government bonds carry essentially **only interest rate risk**; high-yield carries **both rate and credit risk**, and spreads typically widen in stress.\nA) Ignores the credit dimension. C) Both are rate-sensitive. D) Credit risk is high-yield's defining feature.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which is TRUE of the relationship between an RIA and the client's assets?\nA) The RIA holds the client's securities  B) The client continues to hold the assets; the RIA supplies strategy, discipline and review  C) The RIA guarantees returns  D) The RIA must be paid by the fund house\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An adviser **advises**; custody stays with the client (unlike a discretionary PMS, where the manager transacts on the client's behalf).\nA) RIAs do not custody client assets. C) No adviser may guarantee returns. D) An RIA is paid by the **client**, not the manufacturer.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which asset class would you expect to perform best during a period of unexpected high inflation?\nA) Long-duration government bonds  B) Commodities and, over longer periods, equity  C) Cash  D) Fixed deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Commodities** respond directly to price rises; **equity** can pass costs on over time.\nA) Long bonds are hurt most by the rate rises that accompany inflation. C) and D) lose real value as inflation rises.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which combination of features belongs to a mutual fund rather than a PMS?\nA) ₹50 lakh minimum, securities in own name  B) Units of a pooled scheme, daily NAV, capped TER, low minimum  C) Performance fees with a hurdle rate  D) Customised individual portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All four are mutual fund features.\nA), C) and D) all describe a **PMS**.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Scenario & Discrimination (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client has ₹1.2 crore and wants: (i) individually held securities, (ii) some customisation, and (iii) full transparency on holdings. Which vehicle fits best and why?\nA) A mutual fund, since it discloses most  B) A PMS — it holds securities in her own name, allows customisation, and she comfortably exceeds the ₹50 lakh minimum  C) A Category III AIF  D) Direct equity only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three requirements point to **PMS**: securities are **not pooled** (held in her own demat account), customisation is possible especially in non-discretionary mandates, and she can see every holding directly.\nA) A mutual fund discloses well publicly but gives **pooled units**, not individually held securities, and no customisation. C) An AIF pools money into units and discloses least. D) Direct investing gives control but no professional management. **Note she also qualifies for an AIF (₹1 crore), but an AIF fails requirements (i) and (ii).**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Rank these by seniority of claim in liquidation, highest first: ordinary equity, secured debenture, preference share, unsecured bank loan.\nA) Ordinary equity → preference → unsecured loan → secured debenture  B) Secured debenture → unsecured bank loan → preference share → ordinary equity  C) Preference → ordinary → secured → unsecured  D) All rank equally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Secured creditors** rank first (they have a charge on assets), then **unsecured creditors**, then **preference shareholders**, then **ordinary shareholders** as the residual.\nA) Exactly inverts it. C) Places equity above debt. D) Seniority is the entire point of capital structure. **The general rule: security beats no security; debt beats equity; preference beats ordinary.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A client compares two 5-year bonds: Bond A (AAA, 7.5%) and Bond B (BB, 12%). What does the 4.5% difference represent, and what should the client consider?\nA) A tax difference  B) The credit spread — compensation for Bond B's materially higher default risk; the client should ask whether 4.5% adequately compensates for possible total loss  C) An error in pricing  D) Bond B is simply better\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The spread is the market's **price for default risk**. The right question is not \"which yields more\" but **\"is the extra 4.5% enough for the probability and severity of default?\"** — a BB bond that defaults may return only a fraction of principal, wiping out many years of extra yield.\nA) Both are taxed similarly. C) The spread is deliberate market pricing. D) A higher yield is compensation for risk, not a free gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** An investor's portfolio is: 40% self-occupied house, 25% gold jewellery, 20% bank FDs, 10% equity funds, 5% cash. What is the principal weakness?\nA) Too much equity  B) Too little productive, goal-funding financial assets — 65% sits in a self-occupied house and jewellery, which generate no income  C) Too much cash  D) The portfolio is ideal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The house is **consumption** and jewellery is a **personal-use/physical** asset; neither produces income nor can be partly liquidated. Only **35%** (FDs, equity, cash) is genuinely available to fund goals — and just 10% has real growth potential.\nA) 10% equity is **too little**, not too much, for most long-horizon goals. C) 5% cash is modest. D) The concentration in unproductive assets is a serious weakness.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Which scenario correctly illustrates why higher risk does NOT guarantee higher return?\nA) A high-yield bond that pays every coupon and matures at par  B) A high-yield bond portfolio where several issuers default, producing a return below that of government securities despite the higher promised yield  C) Equity that rises 20%  D) A fixed deposit that matures\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk means a **distribution of outcomes**. The higher promised yield is only realised if defaults stay below expectations; when they do not, the risky portfolio **underperforms the safe one**.\nA) Shows the risk not materialising — the favourable case. C) Shows a good equity outcome, not the principle. D) A deposit is the low-risk baseline. **This is precisely why \"higher risk = higher return\" must be stated as higher *expected* return with wider dispersion.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client asks why a REIT is not simply \"the same as owning property\". Give the two most important differences.\nA) There are no differences  B) A REIT is a listed security offering daily liquidity, small ticket size, professional management and diversification across many properties — but the investor owns units, not a specific property, and bears market price volatility  C) REITs guarantee rent  D) REITs are unregulated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — REITs convert an illiquid, indivisible, concentrated asset into a **liquid, divisible, diversified** one — at the cost of **market price volatility** and no control over any specific property.\nA) The differences are substantial. C) Distributions depend on rental performance and occupancy. D) REITs are **SEBI-regulated**.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** An adviser is comparing routes for a ₹75 lakh corpus. Which statement about the regulatory trade-off is MOST accurate?\nA) A PMS at this level offers more disclosure than a mutual fund  B) The client qualifies for PMS but not AIF; moving from a mutual fund to a PMS gains customisation and direct holding, but loses the mutual fund's daily NAV, capped TER and highest disclosure standards  C) All three vehicles are equally regulated  D) An AIF is available at ₹75 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹75 lakh clears the **₹50 lakh PMS** minimum but not the **₹1 crore AIF** minimum. The trade-off is real: PMS gains customisation and direct ownership but gives up the mutual fund's **cost cap and disclosure regime**.\nA) Mutual funds disclose more. C) They differ markedly. D) ₹75 lakh is below the AIF threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A structured product promises \"100% capital protection plus 60% of Nifty upside over 5 years\", issued by an NBFC rated A. Identify the two main risks.\nA) There are none  B) Issuer credit risk (an A-rated NBFC could default, voiding the \"protection\") and liquidity risk (likely no secondary market, and protection applies only at maturity)  C) Only market risk  D) Only currency risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Credit risk** is the headline point: capital protection is only the **issuer's promise**, and an A rating is investment grade but not risk-free. **Liquidity risk** follows: an early exit forgoes the protection and may find no buyer.\nA) Both risks are material. C) Market risk is partly what the structure addresses. D) There is no currency exposure in a rupee Nifty-linked note.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A 30-year-old wants to fund a house down payment in 3 years and retirement in 30 years from one ₹20 lakh corpus. What is the correct approach?\nA) Put everything in equity  B) Split by goal — the 3-year down payment in debt/liquid instruments; the 30-year retirement portion in equity-oriented investments  C) Put everything in FDs  D) Put everything in gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Goal bucketing**: each goal gets an allocation matched to its **own horizon**. The 3-year money cannot bear equity volatility; the 30-year money must not sit in low-real-return debt.\nA) Risks a shortfall on the near goal. C) Would likely fail the 30-year goal against inflation. D) Gold is a diversifier, not a whole strategy.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Which characteristic is shared by commodities, art and unlisted equity but NOT by listed equity?\nA) They are all regulated by SEBI  B) None generates contractual cash flow AND all lack continuous public price discovery  C) They all pay dividends  D) They are all highly liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Commodities and art produce **no cash flow**, unlisted equity produces none reliably, and **none has a continuous public price** — so valuation is estimated rather than observed.\nA) Art and physical commodities are not SEBI-regulated. C) None reliably pays dividends. D) All three are **illiquid** relative to listed equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** An investor holds a portfolio of 20 stocks, all in banking. Which risk is she most exposed to, and what does the asset-class framework suggest?\nA) No particular risk — 20 stocks is diversified  B) Sector-specific (concentration) risk — holding many securities within one sector does not diversify away the sector's common drivers  C) Currency risk  D) Interest rate risk only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Diversification requires low correlation.** Twenty banking stocks share exposure to rates, credit cycles and regulation, so they move together. Number of holdings ≠ diversification.\nA) Count alone does not diversify. C) There is no direct currency exposure in domestic banks. D) Rate risk is one driver, but the broader issue is **sector concentration**.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Which statement best explains why AIFs have a ₹1 crore minimum?\nA) To raise more money  B) To restrict participation to investors presumed able to assess complex, illiquid, lightly disclosed strategies and absorb losses  C) Because AIFs are the safest vehicle  D) SEBI sets it arbitrarily\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The minimum acts as a **suitability filter**: AIFs carry the widest strategy freedom and the lowest disclosure, so access is limited to investors presumed capable of evaluating and bearing that.\nA) The purpose is investor protection, not fundraising. C) AIFs are generally the **riskiest** of the three vehicles. D) The threshold reflects a deliberate regulatory philosophy.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A client is offered an unlisted company's shares at \"a 40% discount to fair value\". What should the adviser check first?\nA) Nothing — a discount is always good  B) Who determined \"fair value\" and on what basis, since unlisted equity has no market price; plus the exit route, information rights and lock-in  C) The Nifty level  D) The gold price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **no observable market price**, \"fair value\" is an **estimate**, potentially by an interested party. The decisive questions are valuation basis, **how and when the investor can exit**, and what information they will receive.\nA) A discount to an unverifiable number means nothing. C) and D) are irrelevant to a specific unlisted holding.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Which pair of vehicles both give the investor UNITS rather than directly held securities?\nA) PMS and AIF  B) Mutual fund and AIF  C) PMS and mutual fund  D) Direct equity and PMS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both **mutual funds and AIFs are pooled** and issue **units**. **PMS is not pooled** — securities sit in the client's own name.\nA) and C) both wrongly include PMS. D) Direct equity means owning shares outright.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** An investor asks whether she should buy a bond fund now that interest rates are expected to fall sharply. What is the reasoning?\nA) Falling rates hurt bond prices  B) Falling rates raise bond prices, so a bond fund — especially a longer-duration one — should benefit, though the expectation may already be priced in  C) Rates do not affect bonds  D) She should buy equity instead\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Prices move **inversely** to rates, and **longer duration means greater sensitivity**. The caveat matters: widely expected rate cuts are often **already reflected** in current prices.\nA) Inverts the relationship. C) Rates are the primary driver of bond prices. D) A different asset class does not answer the question asked.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A client's adviser earns both a client fee and manufacturer commission on the same recommendation. Which principle is breached, and what is the regulatory position?\nA) None  B) The fiduciary duty and the requirement to segregate advice from distribution — an individual RIA cannot provide both advisory and distribution services to the same client  C) Only a disclosure formality  D) It is required\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SEBI's framework requires **segregation of advisory and distribution**; an individual investment adviser cannot do both for the same client, precisely because dual compensation corrupts the recommendation.\nA) A material conflict exists. C) It goes well beyond disclosure — the activity itself is restricted. D) Exactly the opposite.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Which asset class combination would provide the LEAST diversification benefit?\nA) Indian equity and Indian government bonds  B) Indian large-cap equity and Indian mid-cap equity  C) Equity and gold  D) Equity and real estate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Large-cap and mid-cap **equity are the same asset class** and are highly correlated; combining them provides little diversification.\nA), C) and D) all pair genuinely different asset classes with lower correlations. **Diversification comes from low correlation, not from the number of holdings.**\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A retiree needs ₹50,000 a month and holds ₹1.5 crore. Which allocation is most appropriate in principle?\nA) 100% equity for maximum growth  B) A majority in debt/income instruments for stability and predictable cash flow, with a meaningful equity allocation to protect against inflation over a long retirement  C) 100% in a single corporate bond  D) 100% in gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A retiree faces **two** risks: **volatility/sequence risk** (argues for debt) and **longevity/inflation risk** over a possibly 25-year retirement (argues for some equity). The answer is a **balance**, not a corner.\nA) Exposes essential income to drawdowns. C) Concentrates all credit risk in one issuer. D) Gold pays no income at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Which of these correctly identifies what an investor gives up by choosing a mutual fund over a PMS?\nA) Liquidity and disclosure  B) Customisation and direct ownership of the underlying securities  C) Professional management  D) Regulation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A mutual fund gives everyone the **same pooled portfolio** in **units**; a PMS allows tailoring and holds securities **in the client's own name**.\nA) A mutual fund **gains** on both counts. C) Both are professionally managed. D) A mutual fund is the **more** regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Two clients each invest ₹1 crore: one in a Category III AIF, one in an equity mutual fund. Which faces more uncertainty about what they hold at any moment, and why?\nA) The mutual fund investor  B) The AIF investor — AIFs disclose to investors under fund documents rather than publishing daily NAV and monthly portfolios, and may use leverage, derivatives and unlisted assets  C) Both are identical  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutual funds publish **daily NAV and monthly portfolio disclosures**; AIFs do not. Add wide strategy freedom and the AIF investor has materially less visibility.\nA) Reverses it. C) The disclosure regimes differ sharply. D) The difference is real and is the basis for the higher minimum.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Which is the strongest argument against holding a large allocation in art or collectibles?\nA) They never rise in value  B) No cash flow, subjective and expertise-dependent valuation, very wide spreads, authenticity and storage risk, and no regulatory investor protection  C) They are illegal  D) They are too liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each factor independently argues for a small allocation at most; together they make art a **passion allocation**, not a core holding.\nA) They can appreciate substantially — that is not the objection. C) They are entirely legal. D) They are extremely **illiquid**.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** An investor holds a 10-year corporate bond and rates rise 2%. What happens to the bond and to her income if she holds to maturity?\nA) She loses both price and income  B) The market price falls, but if she holds to maturity she still receives the contracted coupons and principal — the loss is opportunity cost, not realised loss  C) The coupon rises  D) The bond defaults\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Price and yield move inversely, so the **market price falls**. But the coupon is **contractual**: holding to maturity delivers the promised cash flows. What she loses is the chance to earn the new, higher rate.\nA) Income is unaffected if there is no default. C) Coupons on fixed-rate bonds do not change. D) A rate rise is not a credit event. **This distinction — mark-to-market loss vs realised loss — is heavily tested.**\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client wants exposure to Indian infrastructure with listed liquidity and periodic income. The best-fitting instrument is:\nA) A REIT  B) An InvIT  C) A gold ETF  D) A liquid fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **InvIT** holds infrastructure assets (roads, transmission, pipelines) and is listed and SEBI-regulated, distributing cash flows periodically.\nA) A REIT holds **real estate**, not infrastructure. C) and D) are entirely different asset classes.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which statement about the relationship between minimum investment and regulation is correct across MF, PMS and AIF?\nA) Higher minimums bring stricter disclosure  B) Higher minimums bring lighter disclosure and wider strategy freedom, on the assumption that larger investors can protect themselves  C) Minimums are unrelated to regulation  D) All three have identical minimums\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The ladder runs **₹500 (heaviest regulation) → ₹50 lakh → ₹1 crore (lightest disclosure, widest freedom)**.\nA) Inverts the relationship. C) The link is deliberate regulatory design. D) The minimums differ by orders of magnitude.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An investor's entire ₹80 lakh corpus is in one AAA corporate bond. Identify the risks.\nA) None — AAA is safe  B) Concentration/issuer risk (a single downgrade or default hits everything), interest rate risk, and reinvestment risk — a rating is not a guarantee and can change  C) Only currency risk  D) Only equity risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **AAA is the highest rating, not a guarantee** — ratings migrate, and India has seen highly rated issuers downgraded sharply. Holding a single issuer means **zero diversification** of credit risk, plus rate and reinvestment risk.\nA) Complacency about AAA is precisely the trap. C) A rupee bond has no currency exposure. D) There is no equity here.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Which describes the correct sequence when matching a product to a client?\nA) Pick the highest-returning product, then find a client  B) Establish the goal, horizon and risk profile, then select the asset class, then the vehicle, then the specific product  C) Pick the vehicle first  D) Pick the product with the highest commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Suitability flows **from the client outward**: goal → horizon and risk profile → asset class → vehicle → product.\nA) Is product-push in reverse. C) The vehicle depends on the asset class and corpus size. D) A direct breach of fiduciary duty.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Which pair of asset classes would a client typically use for a 2-year goal and a 20-year goal respectively?\nA) Equity and cash  B) Debt/liquid instruments and equity  C) Art and gold  D) Distressed securities and cash\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short horizon → **capital stability** (debt/liquid). Long horizon → **growth** (equity), where volatility has time to average out.\nA) Exactly reverses the correct matching. C) Neither is a core holding for a defined goal. D) Distressed securities are unsuitable for almost any retail goal.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client claims that because a PMS holds securities in her own name, it must be \"safer\" than a mutual fund. Evaluate.\nA) Correct — direct holding removes market risk  B) Direct holding removes pooling and gives transparency of ownership, but market risk is identical; and the mutual fund actually offers a capped TER, daily NAV and stricter diversification norms  C) Correct — PMS is regulated more heavily  D) PMS carries no risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Ownership structure and market risk are different things.** Holding securities directly does not make them fall less. On several protective dimensions — **cost cap, disclosure frequency and diversification limits** — the mutual fund is the tighter regime.\nA) Market risk attaches to the securities, not the wrapper. C) Mutual funds are the **more** regulated. D) All market investments carry risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Which single factor most determines whether equity is appropriate for a given goal?\nA) The client's age  B) The goal's time horizon  C) The current market level  D) The client's income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Horizon** is the binding constraint: even a young, wealthy, risk-tolerant client should not fund a 12-month goal with equity.\nA) Age influences risk **capacity** but the goal's own horizon governs. C) Timing the market is unreliable and not the basis for allocation. D) Income affects capacity, not the horizon match.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which statement best summarises this chapter?\nA) All investments are equally risky  B) Every investment is defined by what you own (asset class), how you own it (direct, MF, PMS, AIF), and whether its risk, liquidity and horizon match the goal it funds  C) Equity always beats debt  D) Managed portfolios always beat direct investing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The chapter's whole structure is **asset class × access route × suitability**.\nA) Risk varies enormously across asset classes. C) Equity outperforms over long periods but not over every period, and is unsuitable for short goals. D) Managed vehicles add professional management **and cost**; direct investing is cheaper but demands skill.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.**\n\n### Case: Structuring Ms Rao's ₹1.4 Crore\n\nMs Sunita Rao, **46**, has just received **₹1.4 crore** from the sale of a commercial property. She has:\n\n- An existing emergency fund of **₹9,00,000** (6 months of expenses)\n- **No outstanding debt**\n- Two goals: her daughter's postgraduate education abroad in **4 years**, costing **₹45,00,000** in today's terms, and her own retirement at **60** (14 years away)\n- A **moderate** risk profile: she is uncomfortable with sharp drawdowns but understands she needs growth\n\nShe has been approached with three proposals: a **Category III AIF** with a long–short strategy (₹1 crore minimum), a **PMS** with a concentrated 15-stock portfolio (₹50 lakh minimum), and a **structured note** from an A-rated NBFC promising \"100% capital protection plus 55% of Nifty upside over 6 years\".\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** Which of the three proposals does Ms Rao technically qualify for on minimum-investment grounds?\nA) Only the PMS  B) All three — she has ₹1.4 crore, clearing both the ₹50 lakh PMS and ₹1 crore AIF minimums  C) Only the AIF  D) None\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹1.4 crore clears the **₹50 lakh PMS** minimum and the **₹1 crore AIF** minimum, and structured notes have no such regulatory floor.\nA) and C) each overlook one qualifying vehicle. D) She comfortably exceeds both thresholds. *(Note carefully: **qualifying is not the same as being suitable** — that is what the remaining questions test.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Her daughter's education is 4 years away and costs ₹45,00,000 today. At 6% education inflation, what will it cost, and which asset class should fund it?\nA) ₹45,00,000; equity  B) About ₹56,80,000; predominantly debt/liquid instruments given the 4-year horizon  C) About ₹90,00,000; the AIF  D) ₹45,00,000; the structured note\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = 45,00,000 × (1.06)⁴ = 45,00,000 × **1.26248** = **₹56,81,160**. With only **4 years** and a non-negotiable date, the priority is **capital certainty**, so this bucket belongs predominantly in debt/liquid instruments with perhaps a small equity component.\nA) Ignores inflation and misallocates to equity. C) Overstates the inflation effect badly. D) The structured note runs **6 years** — it would still be locked up when the fees fall due.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** What is the most serious problem with the structured note for Ms Rao?\nA) The returns are too high  B) A 6-year term against a 4-year goal, plus issuer credit risk from an A-rated NBFC — \"capital protection\" is only that issuer's promise, and there is likely no secondary market  C) It is illegal  D) It has no market exposure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Three compounding problems. **Tenor mismatch:** 6 years versus a 4-year goal. **Credit risk:** the \"protection\" is only as good as the **A-rated NBFC** behind it — investment grade, but not risk-free. **Liquidity:** structured notes typically have no secondary market, and protection generally applies **only at maturity**.\nA) The upside participation is capped at 55%, not excessive. C) Such products are permitted. D) It does have Nifty-linked exposure — that is the point of the option leg.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Is the concentrated 15-stock PMS suitable for her retirement bucket?\nA) Yes — concentrated portfolios always outperform  B) It is a legitimate option for a long-horizon bucket, but a 15-stock portfolio carries high concentration risk that sits awkwardly with her stated discomfort with sharp drawdowns  C) No — PMS is prohibited for her  D) Yes, she should put all ₹1.4 crore into it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — She **qualifies** and a 14-year horizon can bear equity. But a **15-stock concentrated portfolio** has materially higher stock-specific risk than a diversified fund, and she has explicitly said she is **uncomfortable with sharp drawdowns**. The mismatch is with her **risk tolerance**, not her capacity.\nA) Concentration raises dispersion of outcomes in **both** directions. C) She meets the minimum, so it is not prohibited. D) Allocating everything to one concentrated equity strategy ignores the 4-year goal entirely.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** What is the most appropriate overall structure for Ms Rao's ₹1.4 crore?\nA) All ₹1.4 crore into the Category III AIF  B) Bucket by goal — roughly ₹45–50 lakh in debt/liquid instruments for the 4-year education goal, the remaining ~₹90 lakh in diversified equity-oriented investments for the 14-year retirement goal, matched to a moderate risk profile  C) All into the structured note  D) Leave it all in a savings account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Goal bucketing** is the answer. The education bucket needs about **₹56.8 lakh at maturity**; funding it with roughly **₹45–50 lakh** in debt today gets there safely over 4 years. The remaining **~₹90 lakh** has a **14-year** horizon and belongs in diversified equity-oriented investments — a **diversified** approach fits her moderate tolerance better than a 15-stock PMS or a long–short AIF.\nA) A Category III AIF's leverage and derivative strategies are the least suitable fit for a moderate-risk investor, and it would strand the education money. C) Fails on tenor, credit and liquidity as QC3 established. D) Guarantees a negative real return over 14 years.\n\n> ⚠️ **The lesson:** Ms Rao **qualifies** for every product offered to her — and that is exactly the danger. **Eligibility is not suitability.** The adviser's job is to start from her goals, horizons and tolerance, and only then choose the vehicle.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "08", "title": "Investing in Stocks", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "# Chapter 8: Investing in Stocks — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 3 · Investment Products (30 marks).**\n> ⭐ The **valuation ratios** in 8.5.4 are guaranteed exam marks — learn each formula, what it means, and when it fails. Fundamental vs technical analysis is the other reliable question source.\n\n---\n\n## 8.1 Equity as an Investment\n\nBuying a share makes you a **part-owner** of a business. Your return comes from two sources:\n\n$$\\textbf{Total Return} = \\textbf{Capital Appreciation} + \\textbf{Dividend Yield}$$\n\n**Why equity works over the long run:** a company's earnings tend to grow with the economy and with inflation, so equity is a claim on a **growing, inflation-linked stream of profits** — unlike a bond, whose coupon is fixed in nominal terms.\n\n**The cost:** ownership is a **residual** claim. Shareholders are paid last, dividends are discretionary, and the capital can be lost entirely.\n\n---\n\n## 8.2 Diversification of Risk\n\nDiversification reduces risk **without necessarily reducing expected return** — the closest thing to a free lunch in investing. It works because assets do not move in perfect lock-step.\n\n### Two dimensions\n\n| | **Cross-sectional diversification** | **Time-series diversification** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Meaning** | Spreading across **many securities, sectors and asset classes at one point in time** | Spreading **entry across time** |\n| **Reduces** | Company- and sector-specific risk | The risk of investing everything at a bad price |\n| **In practice** | A diversified portfolio | **SIP / rupee-cost averaging** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Critical limit:** diversification eliminates **unsystematic** (company/sector-specific) risk but **cannot eliminate systematic (market) risk**. When the whole market falls, diversification does not save you. Beyond roughly 15–25 well-chosen, low-correlation stocks, additional names add little.\n>\n> And remember Chapter 7's trap: **20 banking stocks are not diversified.** Diversification requires **low correlation**, not merely a high count.\n\n---\n\n## 8.3 Risks of Equity Investment\n\n| Risk | What it is | Diversifiable? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Market risk** | The whole market falls — recessions, rate shocks, geopolitics | ❌ **No** (systematic) |\n| **Sector-specific risk** | A sector suffers — regulation, commodity prices, technology shifts | ✅ Yes, across sectors |\n| **Company-specific risk** | One firm fails — fraud, product failure, management error | ✅ Yes, across companies |\n| **Transactional risk** | Execution and settlement problems, bad fills, operational error | Partly, via good practice |\n| **Liquidity risk** | Cannot exit at a fair price; wide spreads in small-caps | Partly, via avoiding illiquid names |\n\n> 🧠 **Systematic (market) risk is compensated** — you are paid a risk premium for bearing it because you cannot avoid it. **Unsystematic risk is not compensated**, because you could have diversified it away for free. This idea underpins CAPM and beta in Chapters 14 and 16.\n\n---\n\n## 8.4 Overview of the Equity Market\n\n- **Primary market** — IPOs, FPOs, rights issues (Chapter 6)\n- **Secondary market** — NSE, BSE, T+1 settlement\n- **Segments by size** — large-cap, mid-cap, small-cap (SEBI defines these by market-cap rank: **1–100 large, 101–250 mid, 251 onwards small**)\n- **Indices** — Nifty 50, Sensex; free-float market-cap weighted\n\n---\n\n## 8.5 Equity Research and Stock Selection\n\n### 8.5.1 Fundamental Analysis\n\nEstimating a security's **intrinsic value** from the underlying business — earnings, assets, growth, management, industry — and comparing it with the market price.\n\n$$\\text{If Intrinsic Value} > \\text{Market Price} \\Rightarrow \\text{potentially undervalued (buy)}$$\n\n#### Top-down vs bottom-up\n\n| | **Top-down** | **Bottom-up** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Starts with** | The **economy** | The **company** |\n| **Then** | Economy → industry → company | Company merits, largely regardless of macro |\n| **Suits** | Cyclical and macro-driven sectors | Stock-pickers seeking mispriced individual firms |\n\n#### Buy-side vs sell-side research\n\n| | **Sell-side** | **Buy-side** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Works for** | Brokers, investment banks | Mutual funds, insurers, PMS, pension funds |\n| **Research is** | **Published** to clients, often with buy/hold/sell recommendations | **Internal and proprietary** |\n| **Paid via** | Brokerage/commissions | The employer's own returns |\n| **Conflict** | May face pressure from banking relationships | Fewer external conflicts |\n\n#### Sector classification\nGrouping companies by business activity (banking, IT, FMCG, pharma, auto) so that peers can be compared and sector exposures measured.\n\n### 8.5.2 The Stock Analysis Process — E → I → C\n\n**1. Economy analysis** — GDP growth, inflation, interest rates, fiscal and monetary policy, currency, commodity prices, the global cycle.\n\n**2. Industry/sector analysis** — industry life cycle (pioneering → expansion → maturity → decline), competitive intensity, regulation, entry barriers, pricing power, input costs.\n\n**3. Company analysis** — two halves:\n\n- **Quantitative:** revenue and profit growth, margins, return on equity, debt levels, cash flow quality, working capital.\n- **Qualitative:** management quality and integrity, business model, competitive advantage (\"moat\"), corporate governance, related-party transactions, promoter pledging.\n\n### 8.5.3 Fundamentals-Driven Models (absolute valuation)\n\n#### Discounted Cash Flow (DCF)\n\nThe value of a business is the **present value of its future cash flows**:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Value} = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\frac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t} + \\frac{\\text{Terminal Value}}{(1+r)^n}}$$\n\n**Terminal value** (Gordon growth): $TV = \\dfrac{CF_{n+1}}{r-g}$, requiring **g < r**.\n\n- ✅ Theoretically the soundest method — it values the business on its own merits.\n- ❌ **Extremely sensitive** to assumptions about growth (g) and discount rate (r). Small changes swing the answer wildly, and the terminal value often dominates. *\"Garbage in, gospel out.\"*\n\n#### Dividend Discount Model\nA DCF variant using dividends: $P_0 = \\dfrac{D_1}{r-g}$. Works only for stable dividend payers.\n\n#### Asset-based valuation\nValue = **net assets** (assets − liabilities), i.e. book value. Useful for asset-heavy or liquidating businesses; poor for service or brand-driven companies whose value is intangible.\n\n### 8.5.4 ⭐ Market-Driven Models — Relative Valuation\n\nRelative valuation compares a company with **peers or its own history** using multiples. Faster and more intuitive than DCF, but it only tells you whether a stock is cheap **relative to something else** — if the whole sector is overvalued, relative valuation will not reveal it.\n\n| Ratio | Formula | What it says | Watch out for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **P/E** | Price ÷ Earnings per share | What you pay per ₹1 of earnings | **Meaningless if earnings are negative**; distorted by one-off items |\n| **P/B** | Price ÷ Book value per share | Price relative to net assets | Best for **banks and financials**; poor for asset-light firms |\n| **P/S** | Price ÷ Sales per share | Price per ₹1 of revenue | Useful for **loss-making** or early-stage firms; **ignores profitability entirely** |\n| **PEG** | P/E ÷ Earnings growth rate (%) | P/E adjusted for growth | **PEG < 1** often suggests good value; depends on a reliable growth estimate |\n| **EV/EBITDA** | Enterprise Value ÷ EBITDA | Whole-firm value vs operating cash earnings | **Capital-structure neutral**, so it compares differently-leveraged firms fairly |\n| **EBIT/EV** | EBIT ÷ Enterprise Value | The \"earnings yield\" of the whole firm | The inverse of an EV multiple |\n| **EV/Sales** | Enterprise Value ÷ Sales | Whole-firm value per ₹1 of revenue | For loss-makers; ignores margins |\n| **Dividend yield** | Dividend per share ÷ Price | Cash income return | A **high yield can signal a falling price**, not generosity |\n| **Earnings yield** | EPS ÷ Price (the inverse of P/E) | Earnings return on the price paid | Comparable with bond yields |\n\n**Enterprise Value:** $EV = \\text{Market Cap} + \\text{Debt} - \\text{Cash}$ — what it would cost to buy the whole business including its debts.\n\n#### EVA and MVA\n\n- **EVA (Economic Value Added)** = Net Operating Profit After Tax − (Capital × Cost of Capital). **Positive EVA means the firm earned more than its cost of capital** — genuine value creation, not just accounting profit.\n- **MVA (Market Value Added)** = Market Value of the firm − Capital Invested. The market's verdict on cumulative value creation.\n\n#### Industry-specific metrics\nBecause standard ratios do not fit every sector: **banks** — Net Interest Margin, Gross/Net NPA, CASA ratio, capital adequacy; **telecom** — ARPU, subscriber count; **retail** — same-store sales growth, sales per square foot; **hotels** — occupancy, average room rate; **IT** — utilisation, attrition, billing rates.\n\n### 8.6 Combining Relative and DCF Approaches\nBest practice uses **both**: DCF anchors value in the business's own economics; relative multiples sanity-check that answer against what the market pays for comparable firms. When the two diverge sharply, the assumptions deserve re-examination.\n\n---\n\n## 8.7 Technical Analysis\n\n**Technical analysis studies price and volume history to forecast future price movements** — it ignores intrinsic value entirely.\n\n### 8.7.1 Assumptions\n1. **The market discounts everything** — all information is already in the price.\n2. **Prices move in trends** — and a trend is more likely to continue than to reverse.\n3. **History repeats itself** — because human psychology is consistent, price patterns recur.\n\n### 8.7.2 Technical vs Fundamental Analysis\n\n| | **Fundamental** | **Technical** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Studies** | Financial statements, economy, industry, management | **Price and volume charts** |\n| **Answers** | **What** to buy | **When** to buy or sell |\n| **Horizon** | Long term | Short to medium term |\n| **Basis** | Intrinsic value | Market psychology and trends |\n| **Data** | Fundamentals, released periodically | Price/volume, continuous |\n\n> 🧠 **The clean way to remember it: fundamental analysis tells you WHAT to buy; technical analysis tells you WHEN.** Many practitioners use both.\n\n### 8.7.3 Advantages of technical analysis\nApplicable to any traded instrument; timing-focused; provides clear entry, exit and stop-loss levels; works when fundamental data is unavailable or stale; captures sentiment that fundamentals miss.\n\n### 8.7.4 Technical rules and indicators\n\n| Tool | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Support** | A price level where buying has historically emerged, halting a fall |\n| **Resistance** | A level where selling has historically emerged, capping a rise |\n| **Trendline** | A line joining successive highs or lows, defining the trend |\n| **Moving average** | Smooths price to reveal the trend; crossovers signal changes |\n| **RSI** | Relative Strength Index (0–100); conventionally **>70 overbought, <30 oversold** |\n| **MACD** | Moving Average Convergence Divergence — momentum via the relationship between two moving averages |\n| **Bollinger Bands** | Bands set a number of standard deviations around a moving average, showing relative volatility |\n| **Volume** | Confirms a move — a price break on **high volume** is more credible |\n| **Chart patterns** | Head-and-shoulders, double top/bottom, triangles, flags |\n| **Candlesticks** | Doji, hammer, engulfing patterns signalling potential reversals |\n\n> ⚠️ **Once support is broken it often becomes resistance** (and vice versa) — a classic exam point.\n\n### 8.7.5 Fixed income and technical analysis\nTechnical analysis can be applied to **bond yields and prices**, and to interest-rate futures, though fundamental drivers (monetary policy, inflation, credit) dominate more than in equity.\n\n---\n\n## 8.8 Qualitative Evaluation and Corporate Governance\n\nNumbers describe the past; **quality of management and governance determines whether the future resembles it.**\n\n**What a stock picker examines:**\n\n- **Board composition** — genuinely independent directors, relevant expertise\n- **Promoter holding and pledging** — high **pledged shares** are a serious red flag (forced selling risk)\n- **Related-party transactions** — potential siphoning of value\n- **Auditor quality** — and any **auditor resignation**, a major warning sign\n- **Disclosure quality** — clear, consistent, timely reporting\n- **Capital allocation record** — has management reinvested wisely, or built empires?\n- **Minority shareholder treatment** — dividend policy, fairness in group restructurings\n- **Accounting aggressiveness** — revenue recognition, related-party receivables, frequent \"exceptional items\"\n\n> ⚠️ **Governance red flags to memorise:** frequent **auditor changes or resignations**, high **promoter pledging**, large **related-party transactions**, repeated restatements, opaque group structures, and a persistent gap between **reported profit and operating cash flow**.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Equity total return | Capital appreciation + dividend yield |\n| Cross-sectional diversification | Across **securities/sectors** at one time |\n| Time-series diversification | Across **time** — i.e. a SIP |\n| Diversification eliminates | **Unsystematic** risk only |\n| Systematic risk | Market-wide, **undiversifiable**, and **compensated** |\n| Unsystematic risk | Company/sector-specific, diversifiable, **not compensated** |\n| SEBI cap definitions | 1–100 large, 101–250 mid, 251+ small |\n| Fundamental analysis | Estimates **intrinsic value** |\n| Technical analysis | Studies **price and volume** |\n| The clean split | Fundamental = **what** to buy; technical = **when** |\n| Top-down | Economy → industry → company |\n| Bottom-up | Company first |\n| Sell-side research | Brokers/banks, **published** |\n| Buy-side research | Funds/institutions, **internal** |\n| E → I → C | Economy → Industry → Company |\n| DCF | PV of future cash flows + terminal value |\n| DCF's weakness | Extreme sensitivity to **g** and **r** |\n| Terminal value | CF ÷ (r − g), needs **g < r** |\n| P/E | Price ÷ EPS; **useless if earnings are negative** |\n| P/B | Price ÷ book; best for **banks/financials** |\n| P/S | For **loss-making** firms; ignores profitability |\n| PEG | P/E ÷ growth; **< 1** often signals value |\n| EV | Market cap + debt − cash |\n| EV/EBITDA | **Capital-structure neutral** comparison |\n| Earnings yield | EPS ÷ Price — the inverse of P/E |\n| High dividend yield warning | May reflect a **falling price**, not generosity |\n| EVA | NOPAT − (Capital × Cost of Capital); **positive = real value creation** |\n| MVA | Market value − capital invested |\n| Bank metrics | NIM, Gross/Net NPA, CASA, capital adequacy |\n| Technical assumptions | Market discounts everything; prices trend; history repeats |\n| RSI thresholds | **>70 overbought, <30 oversold** |\n| Broken support | Often becomes **resistance** |\n| Governance red flags | Auditor resignation, **promoter pledging**, related-party deals, profit–cash flow gap |\n\n> **Exam tip:** for any valuation-ratio question, ask **\"what is in the denominator, and can it be negative or distorted?\"** That single question explains why P/E fails for loss-makers, why P/B suits banks, and why P/S is used for early-stage companies.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 8: Investing in Stocks — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The valuation ratios are the highest-yield items — know each formula AND when it breaks.**\n\n**Q1.** What are the two components of equity total return?\n> **A:** **Capital appreciation + dividend yield.**\n\n**Q2.** Why does equity beat inflation over the long run, in principle?\n> **A:** Company earnings tend to grow with the economy and with inflation, so equity is a claim on a **growing, inflation-linked stream of profits** — unlike a bond, whose coupon is fixed in nominal terms.\n\n**Q3.** Distinguish cross-sectional from time-series diversification.\n> **A:** **Cross-sectional** = across many securities, sectors and asset classes **at one point in time**. **Time-series** = spreading **entry across time**, i.e. a **SIP / rupee-cost averaging**.\n\n**Q4.** What is diversification's fundamental limit?\n> **A:** It eliminates **unsystematic (company/sector-specific) risk** but **cannot eliminate systematic (market) risk**. When the whole market falls, diversification does not save you.\n\n**Q5.** Roughly how many stocks are needed for effective diversification, and what is the catch?\n> **A:** Around **15–25** well-chosen stocks capture most of the benefit. The catch: they must have **low correlation** — 20 banking stocks are *not* diversified.\n\n**Q6.** Which type of risk is compensated by the market, and why?\n> **A:** **Systematic (market) risk** — you are paid a risk premium because it **cannot be avoided**. **Unsystematic risk is NOT compensated**, because you could have diversified it away for free.\n\n**Q7.** Name the five risks of equity investing.\n> **A:** **Market** risk, **sector-specific** risk, **company-specific** risk, **transactional** risk and **liquidity** risk.\n\n**Q8.** State SEBI's market-cap definitions for large, mid and small cap.\n> **A:** By market-capitalisation rank: **1–100 large cap**, **101–250 mid cap**, **251 onwards small cap**.\n\n**Q9.** What does fundamental analysis try to establish?\n> **A:** A security's **intrinsic value** from the underlying business, to compare against the market price. If intrinsic value > market price, the stock is potentially **undervalued**.\n\n**Q10.** Distinguish top-down from bottom-up analysis.\n> **A:** **Top-down** starts with the **economy** → industry → company. **Bottom-up** starts with the **company** itself, largely regardless of the macro picture.\n\n**Q11.** Distinguish sell-side from buy-side research.\n> **A:** **Sell-side** works for brokers/investment banks and **publishes** research to clients (paid via brokerage). **Buy-side** works for funds/insurers/PMS and keeps research **internal and proprietary**.\n\n**Q12.** State the E → I → C process.\n> **A:** **Economy** analysis → **Industry/sector** analysis → **Company** analysis.\n\n**Q13.** What are the two halves of company analysis?\n> **A:** **Quantitative** (growth, margins, return on equity, debt, cash flow) and **Qualitative** (management quality, business model, moat, corporate governance).\n\n**Q14.** State the DCF formula and its terminal-value component.\n> **A:** **Value = Σ [CFₜ ÷ (1+r)ᵗ] + Terminal Value ÷ (1+r)ⁿ**, where **TV = CF₍ₙ₊₁₎ ÷ (r − g)**, valid only when **g < r**.\n\n**Q15.** What is DCF's greatest weakness?\n> **A:** **Extreme sensitivity to the growth rate (g) and discount rate (r)** — small changes swing the answer wildly, and terminal value often dominates. \"Garbage in, gospel out.\"\n\n**Q16.** When is asset-based valuation appropriate, and when does it fail?\n> **A:** Appropriate for **asset-heavy or liquidating** businesses. It fails for **service and brand-driven** companies whose value is intangible and off the balance sheet.\n\n**Q17.** State the P/E ratio and its main limitation.\n> **A:** **Price ÷ Earnings per share.** It becomes **meaningless when earnings are negative**, and is distorted by one-off items.\n\n**Q18.** For which sector is P/B most useful, and why?\n> **A:** **Banks and financials** — their assets are largely financial and marked close to realisable value, so book value is meaningful. It is poor for asset-light firms.\n\n**Q19.** When would you use P/S rather than P/E?\n> **A:** For **loss-making or early-stage** companies, where earnings are negative or meaningless. Its weakness: it **ignores profitability entirely**.\n\n**Q20.** What is the PEG ratio, and what value suggests good value?\n> **A:** **P/E ÷ earnings growth rate (%)**. A **PEG below 1** often suggests good value — but it depends entirely on a reliable growth estimate.\n\n**Q21.** State the Enterprise Value formula.\n> **A:** **EV = Market Capitalisation + Debt − Cash** — what it would cost to buy the whole business including its debts.\n\n**Q22.** Why is EV/EBITDA preferred for comparing companies with different debt levels?\n> **A:** Because it is **capital-structure neutral** — EV includes debt and EBITDA is measured before interest, so differently-leveraged firms compare fairly. P/E is distorted by leverage.\n\n**Q23.** What is earnings yield, and what is it useful for?\n> **A:** **EPS ÷ Price** — the **inverse of P/E**. It expresses the return as a percentage, making it directly comparable with **bond yields**.\n\n**Q24.** Why can a high dividend yield be a warning sign?\n> **A:** Yield = dividend ÷ **price**. A **falling price** mechanically raises the yield, so a high figure may signal distress rather than generosity.\n\n**Q25.** State the EVA formula and what a positive value means.\n> **A:** **EVA = NOPAT − (Capital × Cost of Capital).** Positive EVA means the firm earned **more than its cost of capital** — genuine value creation, not merely accounting profit.\n\n**Q26.** What is MVA?\n> **A:** **Market Value Added = Market value of the firm − Capital invested** — the market's verdict on cumulative value creation.\n\n**Q27.** Name the industry-specific metrics for banks, telecom and retail.\n> **A:** **Banks** — NIM, Gross/Net NPA, CASA ratio, capital adequacy. **Telecom** — ARPU, subscriber count. **Retail** — same-store sales growth, sales per square foot.\n\n**Q28.** State the three assumptions of technical analysis.\n> **A:** 1) **The market discounts everything** — all information is in the price. 2) **Prices move in trends.** 3) **History repeats itself**, because human psychology is consistent.\n\n**Q29.** Give the clean one-line split between fundamental and technical analysis.\n> **A:** **Fundamental analysis tells you WHAT to buy; technical analysis tells you WHEN.**\n\n**Q30.** Define support and resistance, and state what happens when support breaks.\n> **A:** **Support** is a level where buying has historically halted a fall; **resistance** is where selling has capped a rise. **Once support is broken it often becomes resistance** (and vice versa).\n\n**Q31.** What are the conventional RSI thresholds?\n> **A:** RSI runs 0–100; conventionally **above 70 is overbought** and **below 30 is oversold**.\n\n**Q32.** What does volume add to a technical signal?\n> **A:** **Confirmation.** A price breakout on **high volume** is far more credible than the same move on thin volume.\n\n**Q33.** Name four technical indicators besides support/resistance.\n> **A:** **Moving averages** (and crossovers), **RSI**, **MACD**, **Bollinger Bands** — plus chart patterns (head-and-shoulders, double top/bottom, triangles) and candlestick patterns.\n\n**Q34.** Can technical analysis be applied to fixed income?\n> **A:** **Yes** — to bond yields, prices and interest-rate futures — though fundamental drivers (monetary policy, inflation, credit) dominate more than in equity.\n\n**Q35.** Name six corporate governance red flags.\n> **A:** **Auditor resignation or frequent auditor changes**, high **promoter share pledging**, large **related-party transactions**, repeated **restatements**, opaque group structures, and a persistent gap between **reported profit and operating cash flow**.\n\n**Q36.** Why is high promoter pledging a serious concern?\n> **A:** If the share price falls, lenders can **invoke the pledge and dump the shares**, creating forced selling that drives the price down further — a self-reinforcing spiral.\n\n**Q37.** What is the fundamental limitation of relative valuation?\n> **A:** It only tells you whether a stock is cheap **relative to something else**. If the entire sector or market is overvalued, relative valuation will **not reveal it**.\n\n**Q38.** Why should DCF and relative valuation be used together?\n> **A:** DCF anchors value in the **business's own economics**; relative multiples **sanity-check** that against what the market pays for comparable firms. Sharp divergence means the assumptions need re-examination.\n\n**Q39.** What is the industry life cycle, and why does it matter?\n> **A:** **Pioneering → expansion → maturity → decline.** The stage determines expected growth, margins, competitive intensity and the appropriate valuation approach.\n\n**Q40.** What single question resolves most valuation-ratio problems?\n> **A:** **\"What is in the denominator, and can it be negative or distorted?\"** That explains why P/E fails for loss-makers, why P/B suits banks, and why P/S is used for early-stage firms.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 8: Investing in Stocks — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Valuation ratio arithmetic appears in every tier.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The total return from an equity investment comes from:\nA) Interest and principal  B) Capital appreciation and dividend yield  C) Rent and appreciation  D) Coupon payments only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity returns come from **price appreciation** plus **dividends**.\nA) Describes a bond. C) Describes real estate. D) Bonds pay coupons; equity does not.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Diversification eliminates:\nA) All risk  B) Unsystematic (company- and sector-specific) risk  C) Systematic risk  D) Inflation risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Diversification removes **unsystematic** risk only.\nA) Some risk always remains. C) **Systematic (market) risk cannot be diversified away** — that is its defining property. D) Inflation affects all assets and is not diversified away by holding more stocks.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which type of risk is compensated with a risk premium?\nA) Unsystematic risk  B) Systematic risk  C) Both equally  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You are paid only for risk you **cannot avoid**. Unsystematic risk is uncompensated because diversification removes it at no cost.\nA) Reverses the principle. C) They are treated very differently. D) Systematic risk is certainly compensated — that is the equity risk premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A SIP is an example of:\nA) Cross-sectional diversification  B) Time-series diversification  C) Leverage  D) Technical analysis\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Spreading **entry across time** is time-series diversification (rupee-cost averaging).\nA) That means spreading across securities at one point in time. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The P/E ratio is:\nA) Price ÷ Book value  B) Price ÷ Earnings per share  C) Price ÷ Sales  D) Earnings ÷ Price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — P/E = **Price ÷ EPS**, i.e. what you pay per ₹1 of earnings.\nA) Is P/B. C) Is P/S. D) Is the **earnings yield**, the inverse of P/E.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The P/E ratio becomes meaningless when:\nA) The company pays no dividend  B) Earnings are negative  C) The stock is large-cap  D) The company has debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A negative denominator makes the ratio uninterpretable.\nA) Dividends do not enter the P/E. C) and D) affect valuation but do not break the ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Enterprise Value is calculated as:\nA) Market cap + debt − cash  B) Market cap − debt  C) Market cap + cash  D) Total assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **EV = Market cap + debt − cash** — what it costs to buy the whole business including its debts.\nB) and C) get the signs wrong. D) Total assets is an accounting measure, not EV.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** EV/EBITDA is preferred over P/E when comparing companies because it is:\nA) Easier to compute  B) Capital-structure neutral  C) Always lower  D) Regulated by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EV includes debt and EBITDA is before interest, so **differently-leveraged firms compare fairly**.\nA) It requires more inputs, not fewer. C) It is not systematically lower. D) Ratios are not regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The PEG ratio is:\nA) Price ÷ Earnings  B) P/E ÷ earnings growth rate  C) Price ÷ Growth  D) Earnings ÷ Growth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PEG adjusts the P/E for growth; **below 1** often suggests good value.\nA) Is the P/E itself. C) and D) are not standard definitions.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which ratio is most useful for valuing banks?\nA) P/S  B) P/B  C) EV/Sales  D) PEG\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Banks' assets are largely financial and carried near realisable value, so **book value is meaningful**.\nA) and C) are for loss-making or early-stage firms. D) PEG is growth-focused and not bank-specific.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Technical analysis is based on:\nA) Financial statements  B) Price and volume history  C) Management interviews  D) Economic policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Technical analysis studies **price and volume charts**, ignoring intrinsic value.\nA), C) and D) are all fundamental analysis inputs.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which statement captures the split between the two schools?\nA) Fundamental tells you when; technical tells you what  B) Fundamental tells you what to buy; technical tells you when  C) They are identical  D) Only technical analysis works\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The standard formulation: **fundamental = what, technical = when**.\nA) Reverses it. C) They differ in data, horizon and philosophy. D) Both have adherents and limitations.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** RSI readings above 70 conventionally indicate:\nA) Oversold  B) Overbought  C) A buy signal  D) Bankruptcy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Above **70 = overbought**; below **30 = oversold**.\nA) Is below 30. C) Overbought is generally treated as a caution, not a buy. D) RSI says nothing about solvency.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Support is a price level where:\nA) Selling pressure caps a rise  B) Buying has historically emerged, halting a fall  C) Trading is suspended  D) The company issues shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Support is a floor formed by historical buying interest.\nA) Describes **resistance**. C) and D) are unrelated to chart levels.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** When support is broken, it often becomes:\nA) Stronger support  B) Resistance  C) Irrelevant  D) A dividend level\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A broken support level commonly acts as **resistance** on any subsequent rally.\nA) Once breached it has failed as support. C) It remains a significant reference level. D) Meaningless.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** The three assumptions of technical analysis are:\nA) Markets are inefficient, prices are random, history is irrelevant  B) The market discounts everything, prices move in trends, history repeats  C) Only fundamentals matter  D) Volume is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — These three underpin all technical work.\nA) Contradicts each assumption. C) Technical analysis explicitly sets fundamentals aside. D) Volume is a key confirming indicator.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** In the DCF model, terminal value is calculated as:\nA) CF ÷ r  B) CF ÷ (r − g), requiring g < r  C) CF × (1 + g)  D) CF × r\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Gordon growth formula, valid only when **g < r**.\nA) Assumes zero growth. C) and D) are not present-value formulas.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The main weakness of the DCF model is:\nA) It is too simple  B) Extreme sensitivity to the growth and discount rate assumptions  C) It cannot be applied to companies  D) It ignores cash flows\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Small changes in **g** or **r** swing the result dramatically, and terminal value often dominates.\nA) It is the most complex of the common methods. C) It is designed for companies. D) Cash flows are its entire basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Sell-side research is:\nA) Internal and proprietary  B) Published to clients, typically by brokers and investment banks  C) Produced by mutual funds for their own use  D) Prohibited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sell-side research is **published**, and paid for through brokerage.\nA) and C) describe **buy-side** research. D) It is a legitimate, regulated activity.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The E → I → C sequence stands for:\nA) Equity → Investment → Capital  B) Economy → Industry → Company  C) Earnings → Income → Cash  D) Entry → Investment → Closure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The standard **top-down** stock analysis sequence.\nA), C) and D) are invented expansions.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** EVA is calculated as:\nA) NOPAT − (Capital × Cost of Capital)  B) Revenue − Expenses  C) Market cap − Book value  D) EBITDA − Depreciation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Positive EVA means the firm earned more than its cost of capital** — real value creation.\nB) Is accounting profit. C) Is closer to MVA. D) Is roughly EBIT.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Earnings yield is:\nA) P/E  B) EPS ÷ Price  C) Dividend ÷ Price  D) Price ÷ Sales\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **inverse of P/E**, expressed as a percentage so it compares with bond yields.\nA) Is its reciprocal. C) Is dividend yield. D) Is P/S.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which is a company-specific (unsystematic) risk?\nA) A recession  B) A rise in interest rates  C) A product recall at one firm  D) A geopolitical crisis\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A single firm's problem is **diversifiable**.\nA), B) and D) affect the whole market and are **systematic**.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Under SEBI's classification, companies ranked 101–250 by market capitalisation are:\nA) Large cap  B) Mid cap  C) Small cap  D) Micro cap\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **1–100 large, 101–250 mid, 251 onwards small.**\nA) Is ranks 1–100. C) Is 251 onwards. D) Is not a SEBI category for this purpose.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A high dividend yield may indicate:\nA) Always a strong company  B) Possibly a falling share price rather than generosity  C) Guaranteed income  D) Low risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Yield = dividend ÷ **price**; a falling price mechanically raises the yield.\nA) It can equally signal distress. C) Dividends are discretionary. D) High yield often accompanies **higher** risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which valuation ratio would you use for a loss-making start-up?\nA) P/E  B) P/S or EV/Sales  C) PEG  D) Dividend yield\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With negative earnings, revenue-based multiples are the workable option.\nA) Breaks with negative earnings. C) Requires meaningful earnings and growth. D) A loss-maker pays no dividend.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Bottom-up analysis begins with:\nA) GDP forecasts  B) The individual company  C) Monetary policy  D) Sector rotation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bottom-up starts with company merits, largely regardless of the macro view.\nA), C) and D) are all **top-down** starting points.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Which is a governance red flag?\nA) An independent board  B) Auditor resignation  C) Consistent disclosure  D) Low promoter pledging\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **auditor resignation** is one of the strongest warning signals available to an outside investor.\nA), C) and D) are all positive governance indicators.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** High promoter pledging is a concern because:\nA) It raises dividends  B) A price fall can trigger invocation and forced selling, driving the price down further  C) It reduces debt  D) It is illegal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **self-reinforcing spiral** of forced selling is the danger.\nA) Pledging has no bearing on dividends. C) It typically reflects promoter borrowing. D) Pledging is legal but must be disclosed.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Asset-based valuation is least suitable for:\nA) A steel plant  B) A property company  C) A software services firm  D) A shipping company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **software services** firm's value lies in people, brand and contracts — intangibles largely absent from the balance sheet.\nA), B) and D) are all asset-heavy businesses where book value is meaningful.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** MVA is:\nA) NOPAT − capital charge  B) Market value of the firm − capital invested  C) Market cap ÷ book value  D) Sales − costs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Market Value Added** is the market's verdict on cumulative value creation.\nA) Is EVA. C) Is P/B. D) Is profit.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Which metric is specific to the banking sector?\nA) ARPU  B) Net Interest Margin  C) Same-store sales growth  D) Occupancy rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **NIM** (along with NPAs, CASA and capital adequacy) is a core banking metric.\nA) Telecom. C) Retail. D) Hotels.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Volume in technical analysis is used primarily to:\nA) Calculate P/E  B) Confirm the credibility of a price move  C) Measure dividends  D) Set the coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A breakout on **high volume** is far more credible than one on thin volume.\nA), C) and D) are unrelated to volume.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The industry life cycle stages are:\nA) Birth → death  B) Pioneering → expansion → maturity → decline  C) Bull → bear  D) Primary → secondary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The stage determines expected growth, margins and competitive intensity.\nA) Is too crude. C) Describes market phases, not industries. D) Describes market segments.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Fundamental analysis compares intrinsic value with:\nA) The book value  B) The market price  C) The face value  D) The dividend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If intrinsic value exceeds the **market price**, the stock may be undervalued.\nA) Book value is one input to some methods. C) Face value is an accounting artefact. D) Dividends are one input to the DDM.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A company's share price is ₹1,200 and its EPS is ₹48. What is the P/E, and the earnings yield?\nA) P/E 25; earnings yield 4%  B) P/E 4%; earnings yield 25  C) P/E 25; earnings yield 25%  D) P/E 48; earnings yield 2%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — P/E = 1,200 ÷ 48 = **25**. Earnings yield = EPS ÷ Price = 48 ÷ 1,200 = **4%** (the reciprocal of 25).\nB) Swaps them. C) Miscomputes the yield. D) Confuses EPS with the P/E. **Useful check: earnings yield = 1 ÷ P/E, so 1 ÷ 25 = 4% ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A stock trades at ₹600 with a book value per share of ₹250. What is the P/B?\nA) 0.42  B) 2.4  C) 350  D) 150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — P/B = 600 ÷ 250 = **2.4**, i.e. the market pays 2.4× the net asset value.\nA) Inverts the ratio. C) Is the difference, not the ratio. D) Has no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A company has a market cap of ₹8,000 crore, debt of ₹2,500 crore and cash of ₹700 crore. What is its Enterprise Value?\nA) ₹9,800 crore  B) ₹11,200 crore  C) ₹5,500 crore  D) ₹8,000 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — EV = market cap + debt − cash = 8,000 + 2,500 − 700 = **₹9,800 crore**.\nB) Adds cash instead of subtracting. C) Subtracts debt instead of adding. D) Ignores the capital structure entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Using Q38's EV and EBITDA of ₹1,400 crore, what is EV/EBITDA?\nA) 5.7  B) 7.0  C) 8.0  D) 3.9\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EV/EBITDA = 9,800 ÷ 1,400 = **7.0**.\nA) Uses ₹8,000 crore market cap instead of EV. C) Uses a different EV. D) Miscomputes.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A stock has a P/E of 30 and expected earnings growth of 25% a year. What is its PEG, and what does it suggest?\nA) 1.2 — modestly expensive relative to growth  B) 0.83 — good value  C) 750 — meaningless  D) 5 — very cheap\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PEG = 30 ÷ 25 = **1.2**. Since **PEG below 1** is the conventional value threshold, 1.2 suggests the stock is **modestly expensive** relative to its growth.\nB) Inverts the calculation. C) Multiplies instead of dividing. D) Has no basis. **Caveat: PEG is only as reliable as the growth forecast.**\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An investor holds 25 stocks, all in the IT sector. What has she achieved?\nA) Full diversification  B) Company-specific risk is reduced, but sector risk remains concentrated  C) Elimination of market risk  D) Nothing at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Holding many names within one sector diversifies **company-specific** risk but leaves the portfolio fully exposed to IT sector drivers (currency, global tech spending, visa policy).\nA) Diversification requires **low correlation** across sectors. C) Market risk is never diversifiable. D) Company-specific risk genuinely was reduced.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A company reports EPS of ₹40, up from ₹25, but the increase came entirely from selling a factory. How should an analyst treat the P/E?\nA) Use it as reported  B) Adjust for the one-off gain, since P/E based on non-recurring earnings is misleading  C) Ignore earnings entirely  D) Use P/B instead always\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A one-off asset sale is **not recurring operating earnings**, so a P/E built on it understates the true multiple.\nA) Would give a flatteringly low P/E. C) Earnings still matter — they just need adjusting. D) Switching ratios is not required; adjusting the input is.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Two firms have identical operations, but Firm A is debt-free and Firm B is heavily leveraged. Which ratio compares them most fairly?\nA) P/E  B) EV/EBITDA  C) Dividend yield  D) P/B\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **EV/EBITDA is capital-structure neutral**: EV adds debt and EBITDA is measured before interest.\nA) P/E is distorted by leverage, since interest reduces earnings. C) Dividend policy differs for reasons unrelated to operations. D) P/B reflects accounting equity, which leverage also distorts.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A stock's price breaks below a long-standing support level at ₹450 on heavy volume. A technical analyst would conclude:\nA) A strong buy signal  B) A bearish signal — with heavy volume confirming, and ₹450 likely to act as resistance on any rally  C) The company is bankrupt  D) The P/E has fallen\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **break of support on high volume** is a classic bearish confirmation, and the broken support typically becomes **resistance**.\nA) Breaking support is bearish, not bullish. C) A chart break says nothing about solvency. D) A price fall does lower the P/E, but that is not the technical conclusion.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Which analyst is more likely to face a conflict of interest arising from investment banking relationships?\nA) A buy-side analyst at a pension fund  B) A sell-side analyst at an investment bank  C) A retail investor  D) An auditor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sell-side research sits within firms that also earn from **underwriting and advisory** work for the companies covered.\nA) Buy-side analysts serve their own fund's returns, with fewer external conflicts. C) and D) have different roles and conflicts.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A DCF model gives a value of ₹1,800 with g = 5% and r = 11%. If g is raised to 7%, what happens?\nA) Value falls  B) Value rises substantially, because the terminal value denominator (r − g) shrinks from 6% to 4%  C) No change  D) Value becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The terminal value denominator falls from **0.06 to 0.04**, a one-third reduction that raises terminal value by roughly **50%**. This is precisely DCF's sensitivity problem.\nA) A higher growth rate raises value. C) The effect is large. D) Value would only break down if g ≥ r.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A company's operating cash flow is consistently far below its reported net profit. This suggests:\nA) Excellent earnings quality  B) A possible earnings-quality problem — profits may be driven by accruals rather than cash  C) The company is debt-free  D) Nothing of concern\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A persistent **profit–cash flow gap** is a classic warning of aggressive revenue recognition or mounting receivables.\nA) It indicates the opposite. C) The gap says nothing about debt. D) It is one of the strongest red flags available.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Which stock would you expect to have the highest liquidity risk?\nA) A Nifty 50 constituent  B) A small-cap stock with thin daily volumes  C) A large-cap bank  D) An index ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Thin volumes mean **wide spreads and difficulty exiting** without moving the price.\nA), C) and D) are all highly liquid.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** An investor argues that adding a 60th stock will meaningfully reduce her portfolio's risk. The correct response is:\nA) Correct — more is always better  B) Most diversification benefit is captured by roughly 15–25 low-correlation stocks; beyond that the marginal benefit is small  C) She should hold only one stock  D) Diversification eliminates market risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The benefit curve flattens quickly, and over-diversification simply approximates the index while adding monitoring cost.\nA) The marginal benefit approaches zero. C) One stock is maximum unsystematic risk. D) Market risk is never diversifiable.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A company with a P/E of 12 operates in a sector where the average P/E is 25. This means:\nA) The stock is definitely undervalued  B) It is cheap relative to peers, but the discount may be justified by lower growth, higher risk or governance concerns  C) The stock is overvalued  D) The sector average is wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A discount is a **question, not an answer**. Cheap stocks are often cheap for a reason — the analyst's job is to find out which.\nA) Relative cheapness is not proof of undervaluation. C) It is cheaper, not dearer, than peers. D) The average is simply an observation.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which risk remains after a portfolio holds 40 stocks across 12 sectors?\nA) Company-specific risk  B) Systematic (market) risk  C) No risk at all  D) Only liquidity risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Broad diversification removes most unsystematic risk, leaving **market risk**, which cannot be diversified away.\nA) It has largely been diversified. C) Market risk always remains. D) Liquidity risk is manageable via stock selection.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A technical analyst notes that a stock's 50-day moving average has crossed above its 200-day moving average. This is generally read as:\nA) A bearish signal  B) A bullish signal (a \"golden cross\")  C) A dividend announcement  D) An accounting change\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A shorter average crossing **above** a longer one signals strengthening momentum.\nA) The reverse crossing (a \"death cross\") is bearish. C) and D) are corporate events, not chart signals.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** An investor uses only technical analysis to select a stock for a 15-year retirement goal. The main criticism is:\nA) Technical analysis is illegal  B) Technical analysis is oriented to short- and medium-term timing, not to the long-run business quality that drives a 15-year outcome  C) Charts are unavailable  D) It requires a demat account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Over 15 years, **business fundamentals** dominate; chart patterns say nothing about whether the company will still be earning in 2040.\nA) It is entirely legal. C) Charts are freely available. D) Irrelevant to the criticism.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A firm reports NOPAT of ₹900 crore, capital employed of ₹6,000 crore and a cost of capital of 12%. What is its EVA?\nA) ₹180 crore  B) ₹720 crore  C) ₹900 crore  D) −₹180 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Capital charge = 6,000 × 12% = **₹720 crore**. EVA = 900 − 720 = **₹180 crore**, so the firm created genuine value.\nB) Is the capital charge itself. C) Is NOPAT before the charge. D) Gets the sign wrong. **Positive EVA means the firm earned more than its cost of capital.**\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Which combination correctly describes buy-side research?\nA) Published to clients, paid via brokerage  B) Internal and proprietary, produced for the employer's own portfolios  C) Produced by auditors  D) Prohibited by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Buy-side analysts work for asset managers and keep their work **internal**.\nA) Describes sell-side. C) Auditors perform a statutory role. D) It is entirely legitimate.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A company's promoter has pledged 65% of his holding. An analyst should regard this as:\nA) A positive sign of confidence  B) A significant risk — a price fall could trigger invocation and forced selling  C) Irrelevant  D) A guarantee of dividends\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — High pledging creates a **forced-selling spiral** risk if the price falls.\nA) It usually reflects promoter leverage, not confidence. C) It is among the more predictive red flags. D) There is no link to dividends.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which valuation approach is theoretically soundest but most assumption-sensitive?\nA) P/E multiple  B) DCF  C) Dividend yield  D) P/S\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **DCF** values the business on its own economics, but the answer swings on **g** and **r**.\nA), C) and D) are relative or partial measures — quicker, but they only tell you about value relative to something else.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Two companies both have a P/E of 20. Company X grows earnings at 30%, Company Y at 5%. Which appears better value?\nA) Company Y  B) Company X, with a PEG of 0.67 versus Y's 4.0  C) They are identical  D) Cannot be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PEG(X) = 20 ÷ 30 = **0.67** (below 1, attractive); PEG(Y) = 20 ÷ 5 = **4.0** (expensive for its growth).\nA) Y is far more expensive relative to growth. C) The same P/E means very different things at different growth rates — that is precisely PEG's purpose. D) PEG makes them directly comparable.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A stock's dividend yield rises from 2% to 6% while the dividend per share is unchanged. What happened?\nA) The company tripled its dividend  B) The share price fell by about two-thirds  C) Earnings rose  D) The face value changed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With the dividend constant, yield can only rise if the **price falls**. Tripling the yield implies the price fell to roughly one-third of its former level.\nA) The dividend is stated as unchanged. C) Earnings do not enter dividend yield. D) Face value is irrelevant to yield. **This is exactly why a high yield can be a distress signal.**\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which is the correct order of the top-down process?\nA) Company → industry → economy  B) Economy → industry → company  C) Industry → economy → company  D) Company → economy → industry\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Top-down narrows from the macro picture to the specific firm.\nA) Describes bottom-up. C) and D) scramble the sequence.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A company's ROE is 22% while its cost of equity is 13%. This suggests:\nA) The company is destroying value  B) The company is earning well above its cost of equity — a sign of value creation  C) ROE is irrelevant  D) The company should shut down\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Earning a **return above the cost of capital** is the essence of value creation (the same idea EVA formalises).\nA) That would require ROE below the cost of equity. C) ROE is a central profitability measure. D) The opposite conclusion.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which pair of analyses would a disciplined long-term investor most sensibly combine?\nA) Technical analysis alone  B) Fundamental analysis to choose the stock, with technical analysis to help time the entry  C) Astrology and charts  D) Only dividend yield\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **What to buy** (fundamental) plus **when to buy** (technical) is the standard combination.\nA) Ignores business quality over a long horizon. C) Not an analytical method. D) A single ratio is far too narrow.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A company's P/B is 0.6 — well below 1. What does this mean, and what should be checked?\nA) The stock is certainly a bargain  B) The market values the company below its book value, which may signal distress, poor returns on assets, or an overstated book value  C) The company has no assets  D) The P/E must also be low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Trading below book means the market doubts the assets will earn an adequate return — or doubts the **book value itself** (e.g. unprovisioned bad loans at a bank).\nA) Deep-value opportunities exist, but so do value traps. C) A low P/B means assets are cheaply valued, not absent. D) P/E and P/B can diverge sharply.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** An investor buys equally into 10 stocks each month for three years. She is practising:\nA) Cross-sectional diversification only  B) Both cross-sectional (10 stocks) and time-series (monthly entry) diversification  C) Leverage  D) Arbitrage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Ten holdings give **cross-sectional** spread; monthly investing gives **time-series** spread.\nA) Ignores the timing dimension. C) No borrowing is involved. D) No risk-free price difference is being exploited.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which sector metric would you use to assess a telecom company's revenue quality?\nA) NIM  B) ARPU (average revenue per user)  C) Same-store sales  D) Occupancy rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **ARPU** and subscriber numbers are the core telecom metrics.\nA) Banking. C) Retail. D) Hotels.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A DCF assumes a terminal growth rate of 12% and a discount rate of 10%. What is wrong?\nA) Nothing  B) g ≥ r, so the terminal value formula breaks down and implies infinite value  C) The growth rate is too low  D) The discount rate is too high\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **g (12%) > r (10%)**, the denominator (r − g) is negative and the formula is meaningless. In principle no company can grow faster than the discount rate forever.\nA) It is a fatal modelling error. C) 12% perpetual growth is implausibly **high**, not low. D) The discount rate is not the problem — the growth assumption is.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which of these best explains why unsystematic risk carries no risk premium?\nA) It is larger than systematic risk  B) It can be eliminated at no cost through diversification, so the market will not pay you to bear it  C) It is illegal to bear  D) It does not exist\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Markets compensate only **unavoidable** risk. Bearing avoidable risk is a choice, not a service.\nA) Size is not the criterion. C) There is nothing illegal about a concentrated portfolio. D) It certainly exists — it is simply diversifiable.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A company frequently changes auditors and reports large related-party transactions. An analyst should:\nA) Ignore this — it is routine  B) Treat these as serious governance red flags warranting deep scrutiny or avoidance  C) Increase the valuation multiple  D) Assume higher growth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both are **classic red flags** associated with value siphoning and accounting manipulation.\nA) These are precisely the signals a careful analyst weights heavily. C) and D) reward the very behaviour that should be penalised.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A stock trades at ₹900 with sales per share of ₹300 and no profit. What is the P/S, and why use it here?\nA) 3.0 — used because P/E is meaningless with no profit  B) 0.33 — used to measure profitability  C) 600 — the difference  D) P/S cannot be used\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — P/S = 900 ÷ 300 = **3.0**. With no profit the P/E is undefined, so a **revenue** multiple is the workable alternative.\nB) Inverts the ratio and misstates its purpose — P/S **ignores** profitability. C) Is the difference, not a ratio. D) P/S is designed for exactly this situation.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which statement about relative valuation is correct?\nA) It reveals whether a whole market is overvalued  B) It only shows value relative to peers or history, so a uniformly overvalued sector will still look \"fairly valued\" internally  C) It is more precise than DCF  D) It requires no assumptions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is relative valuation's **fundamental blind spot**.\nA) It cannot detect market-wide mispricing. C) It is quicker but not more precise. D) It assumes the peer set is comparable and correctly priced.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A company has 12 crore shares at ₹850, debt ₹3,200 crore, cash ₹900 crore, EBITDA ₹1,600 crore and net profit ₹640 crore. Compute the P/E and EV/EBITDA.\nA) P/E 15.9; EV/EBITDA 7.8  B) P/E 15.9; EV/EBITDA 6.4  C) P/E 12.0; EV/EBITDA 7.8  D) P/E 20.0; EV/EBITDA 8.0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Market cap** = 12 crore × ₹850 = **₹10,200 crore**. **EPS** = 640 ÷ 12 = ₹53.33, so **P/E** = 850 ÷ 53.33 = **15.9** (equivalently 10,200 ÷ 640 = 15.9). **EV** = 10,200 + 3,200 − 900 = **₹12,500 crore**. **EV/EBITDA** = 12,500 ÷ 1,600 = **7.8**.\nB) 6.4 divides by **market cap** instead of EV, ignoring the net debt an acquirer must assume. C) miscomputes the P/E. D) is wrong on both. **Note EV/EBITDA (7.8) exceeds market cap/EBITDA (6.4) precisely because the company carries ₹2,300 crore of net debt.**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Two companies in the same sector: X has P/E 18 and growth 20%; Y has P/E 32 and growth 40%. Which is better value on PEG, and what caveat applies?\nA) X, PEG 0.90 vs Y's 0.80  B) Y, PEG 0.80 vs X's 0.90 — but a 40% growth forecast is far less reliable than 20%  C) They are identical  D) X, because its P/E is lower\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PEG(X) = 18 ÷ 20 = **0.90**; PEG(Y) = 32 ÷ 40 = **0.80**. On PEG, **Y is cheaper**.\nA) Gets the numbers right but the conclusion backwards. C) They differ. D) A lower P/E alone ignores growth — the whole reason PEG exists. **The caveat is the real lesson: PEG is only as good as the growth estimate, and high growth rates are the hardest to sustain and the easiest to over-forecast.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A DCF values a firm at ₹2,400 crore using r = 12% and terminal g = 4%. If r rises to 14%, what happens to the terminal value component, roughly?\nA) It rises  B) It falls sharply — the denominator (r − g) widens from 8% to 10%, cutting terminal value by about 20%  C) It is unchanged  D) It becomes negative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Terminal value ∝ 1 ÷ (r − g). Moving from **0.08 to 0.10** multiplies TV by 0.08 ÷ 0.10 = **0.80**, a **20% fall**.\nA) A higher discount rate lowers present value. C) The effect is substantial. D) It falls but stays positive while r > g. **This is DCF's sensitivity in one calculation: a 2-point change in r cut a fifth off the dominant value component.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** An investor's portfolio is 30 stocks: 12 banks, 10 NBFCs, 8 insurers. Assess the diversification.\nA) Excellent — 30 stocks across 3 sectors  B) Poor — all 30 are financials, sharing exposure to interest rates, credit cycles and regulation, so correlations are high  C) Perfect  D) Only market risk remains\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Banks, NBFCs and insurers are **all financial-sector businesses** driven by the same rate and credit cycle. The apparent three-sector spread is illusory.\nA) Counting names and nominal sectors misses correlation. C) It is heavily concentrated. D) Substantial **sector-specific** risk remains on top of market risk. **Diversification is about low correlation, never about count.**\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A bank trades at a P/B of 0.5 with reported book value ₹200 per share. Its gross NPAs are 14% and rising, with low provision coverage. What is the most likely explanation?\nA) The market is irrational  B) The market doubts the stated book value — unprovisioned bad loans mean the true book is lower than ₹200  C) The bank is a certain bargain  D) P/B is inapplicable to banks\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a bank, **book value is only as good as its loan provisioning**. With **14% gross NPAs and low coverage**, the market is signalling that writing down bad loans would materially reduce the ₹200 book value.\nA) The discount has a clear rationale. C) This is a textbook **value trap**. D) P/B is the *most* relevant ratio for banks — which is exactly why its quality matters so much here.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A company reports rising profits for three years, but receivables have grown from 45 to 140 days of sales and operating cash flow is negative. What should an analyst conclude?\nA) The company is performing excellently  B) Earnings quality is poor — revenue may be recognised on sales that are not converting to cash, a classic accounting red flag  C) The company has too much cash  D) The P/E is too high\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Receivables tripling in days-of-sales while operating cash flow is **negative** points to **aggressive revenue recognition** or to sales made to weak customers who cannot pay.\nA) Profit growth without cash generation is a warning, not a strength. C) The company is consuming cash. D) The P/E level is a separate question — the issue is whether the E is real.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A stock has beta 1.4, the risk-free rate is 7% and the equity risk premium is 6%. What return does CAPM require, and what does beta measure?\nA) 13.0%; total risk  B) 15.4%; systematic (market) risk  C) 8.4%; unsystematic risk  D) 7.0%; no risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAPM: **Required return = Rf + β × ERP** = 7% + (1.4 × 6%) = 7% + 8.4% = **15.4%**. Beta measures **systematic (market) risk** — sensitivity to market movements — not total risk.\nA) Uses beta 1.0. C) Adds only the premium component and misidentifies the risk type. D) Ignores beta entirely. **Only systematic risk is priced, which is why beta and not standard deviation appears in CAPM.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A company's EVA is negative despite reporting a healthy accounting profit. What does this mean?\nA) The accounts are fraudulent  B) The firm earned a profit but LESS than its cost of capital, so it destroyed economic value  C) The firm has no capital  D) EVA cannot be negative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Accounting profit ignores the **cost of equity capital**. Negative EVA means the return on capital fell short of what investors require — value destruction despite a positive bottom line.\nA) Negative EVA is an economic judgement, not evidence of fraud. C) The capital charge presupposes capital. D) Negative EVA is common.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A stock at ₹720 has support at ₹700 and resistance at ₹780. It breaks above ₹780 on triple its average volume. What is the technical reading?\nA) Bearish — it has risen too far  B) Bullish breakout confirmed by volume; ₹780 would now be expected to act as support  C) Neutral  D) The company has issued a bonus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A break **above resistance** on **heavy volume** is a bullish confirmation, and the broken resistance typically flips to **support**.\nA) Technical analysis reads a confirmed breakout as continuation, not exhaustion. C) Volume confirmation makes it a definite signal. D) A corporate action would be reflected differently.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A company with EPS of ₹60 trades at ₹1,500. The bond yield is 7.5%. Compare the earnings yield with the bond yield.\nA) Earnings yield 4%, below the bond yield of 7.5% — equity looks expensive on this measure  B) Earnings yield 25%, well above  C) Earnings yield 7.5%, identical  D) They cannot be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Earnings yield = 60 ÷ 1,500 = **4%**, versus a **7.5%** bond yield. On this simple comparison, the equity offers a lower earnings return than risk-free-ish debt — a caution flag.\nB) Inverts the calculation (that is the P/E of 25). C) They differ. D) The earnings yield exists precisely to enable this comparison. **The caveat: equity earnings can grow, while a bond coupon is fixed — so a lower starting yield can still be justified.**\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** An investor holds one stock worth ₹50 lakh, her entire portfolio. Which risks does she bear, and which could she remove for free?\nA) Only market risk  B) Both systematic and unsystematic risk — the unsystematic (company-specific) portion could be diversified away at no cost, and she is not compensated for bearing it  C) No risk  D) Only liquidity risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Concentration means she bears **maximum company-specific risk** on top of unavoidable market risk. The market pays her **nothing extra** for the company-specific portion, because it is removable for free.\nA) She bears far more than market risk. C) Single-stock concentration is the riskiest equity position possible. D) Liquidity risk is one component, not the whole picture.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Which combination would justify a company trading at a persistent premium P/E to its sector?\nA) Falling market share and rising debt  B) Superior and durable return on capital, consistent cash conversion, strong governance and a defensible competitive moat  C) Frequent auditor changes  D) High promoter pledging\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A premium multiple is justified by **higher, more durable and more certain future cash flows** — which is what a moat and clean governance deliver.\nA), C) and D) are all reasons a stock should trade at a **discount**.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A company sells a division for a large one-off gain, tripling reported EPS. Its P/E falls from 30 to 10. An analyst should:\nA) Buy immediately — the P/E is now cheap  B) Recompute the P/E on continuing operations only, since the low multiple is an artefact of non-recurring earnings  C) Use P/B instead  D) Ignore the sale\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A P/E built on **one-off** earnings is misleading. Stripping the gain would restore a multiple close to the original 30 — and the company now also has **one fewer division** generating future earnings.\nA) Precisely the trap the question sets. C) Switching ratios does not fix a distorted input. D) The sale is central to understanding both the earnings and the reduced future earnings base.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Which pairing of company type and valuation approach is MOST appropriate?\nA) Loss-making start-up → P/E  B) Mature bank → P/B; early-stage e-commerce firm → EV/Sales; stable dividend payer → dividend discount model  C) Software firm → asset-based valuation  D) Every company → P/E\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each method is matched to what is measurable and meaningful for that business.\nA) P/E fails with negative earnings. C) Asset-based valuation is worst for asset-light, intangible-heavy firms. D) No single ratio suits all businesses — that is the chapter's central lesson.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A portfolio manager finds a stock with intrinsic value ₹1,100 (via DCF) trading at ₹1,450, while peers trade at a similar EV/EBITDA. What are the possible interpretations?\nA) The stock is definitely a buy  B) Either the stock (and possibly the whole sector) is overvalued, or the DCF assumptions are too conservative — the divergence is a signal to re-examine both  C) DCF is always right  D) Relative valuation is always right\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — DCF says overvalued; relative valuation says in line with peers. Both can be true if the **entire sector** is expensive — relative valuation cannot detect market-wide mispricing. Alternatively the DCF's g or r may be too pessimistic.\nA) The DCF indicates it is expensive, not cheap. C) and D) treat one method as infallible; the right response is to interrogate the assumptions behind both.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A stock's RSI reads 82 while the price is at an all-time high on declining volume. A technical analyst would note:\nA) A strong buy signal  B) Overbought conditions (RSI > 70) with declining volume failing to confirm the advance — a caution signal  C) The company is undervalued  D) A bonus issue is due\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **RSI above 70 is overbought**, and a rise on **falling volume** lacks confirmation — often described as a weakening advance.\nA) Overbought plus unconfirmed volume is the opposite of a strong buy. C) Technical analysis makes no valuation claim. D) Unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Two firms have identical EBITDA of ₹500 crore. Firm A: market cap ₹4,000 crore, debt ₹1,000 crore, cash ₹200 crore. Firm B: market cap ₹4,500 crore, no debt, cash ₹300 crore. Which is cheaper on EV/EBITDA?\nA) Firm A at 9.6× vs Firm B at 8.4×  B) Firm B at 8.4× vs Firm A at 9.6×  C) They are identical  D) Firm A, because its market cap is lower\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Firm A EV** = 4,000 + 1,000 − 200 = **₹4,800 crore** → EV/EBITDA = 4,800 ÷ 500 = **9.6×**. **Firm B EV** = 4,500 + 0 − 300 = **₹4,200 crore** → 4,200 ÷ 500 = **8.4×**. **Firm B is cheaper.**\nA) Has the numbers but the conclusion inverted. C) They differ. D) **This is the exact trap:** Firm A has the lower market cap but the **higher** enterprise value, because its debt must be assumed by any acquirer.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Which scenario best illustrates that relative valuation cannot detect a bubble?\nA) A stock at a P/E of 8 in a sector averaging 8  B) Every stock in a sector trading at a P/E of 90, so each looks \"fairly valued\" against its peers even though all are extremely expensive on cash flow  C) A stock at a P/E of 15  D) A loss-making firm\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Relative valuation measures against the **peer set**. If the entire peer set is mispriced, every member looks fine relative to the others — the blind spot only an absolute method like DCF can expose.\nA) Shows consistency at a low multiple, not a bubble. C) Is unremarkable. D) Raises a different issue (which ratio to use).\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A company's ROE is 25%, but it achieves this with a debt-to-equity ratio of 4:1. What should an analyst conclude?\nA) It is a superb business  B) The high ROE is substantially leverage-driven, so it carries higher financial risk and is less durable than the same ROE achieved with low debt  C) ROE is meaningless  D) The company has no debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Leverage **mechanically amplifies ROE** by shrinking the equity denominator. The same 25% from an unlevered business is far higher quality, because it does not depend on continued access to cheap debt and is not magnified into losses in a downturn.\nA) The headline number flatters the reality. C) ROE is useful — it just needs decomposing (DuPont: margin × turnover × leverage). D) 4:1 is high leverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An analyst forecasts a company will grow at 25% for 10 years, then 8% forever, with a discount rate of 11%. What is the flaw?\nA) None  B) A perpetual 8% growth rate is aggressive relative to an 11% discount rate, and 25% for a decade is very rarely sustained — the terminal value will dominate and the valuation will be inflated  C) The discount rate is too low  D) Growth should be higher\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two compounding optimisms. Perpetual growth should not exceed long-run nominal GDP growth; at **8% against r = 11%**, the terminal denominator is only 3%, so terminal value **dominates** the result. And sustaining **25% for 10 years** is exceptionally rare.\nA) These are material modelling problems. C) 11% is a reasonable equity discount rate. D) The growth assumptions are already too high.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A stock's price falls 40% but its business fundamentals are unchanged. On a DCF basis, what has happened?\nA) Intrinsic value fell 40%  B) Intrinsic value is unchanged, so the stock is now potentially undervalued relative to it — assuming the fundamentals genuinely have not changed  C) The DCF is invalid  D) The company must have issued shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — DCF value depends on **future cash flows and the discount rate**, not the market price. If neither changed, intrinsic value is unchanged and the gap has widened in the buyer's favour.\nA) Price and intrinsic value are different things — the whole premise of fundamental analysis. C) The method still applies. D) A price fall does not imply an issuance. **The essential caveat: verify that fundamentals really are unchanged — a 40% fall usually means the market believes something has.**\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Which combination of technical signals would a chartist regard as most bearish?\nA) Price above the 200-day moving average, RSI 45, rising volume on up-days  B) Price breaking below support on heavy volume, 50-day MA crossing below 200-day MA, RSI falling through 30  C) A golden cross with high volume  D) RSI at 50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Three bearish confirmations together: a **support break on heavy volume**, a **death cross**, and RSI breaking down through the oversold threshold indicating strong downward momentum.\nA) Describes a healthy uptrend. C) A golden cross is bullish. D) RSI 50 is neutral.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A company's shares trade at ₹380 with 8 crore shares outstanding. Net profit is ₹190 crore, of which ₹60 crore came from a one-time tax refund. What is the adjusted P/E?\nA) 16.0  B) 23.4  C) 12.0  D) 30.4\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Market cap** = 8 crore × ₹380 = **₹3,040 crore**. **Reported P/E** = 3,040 ÷ 190 = 16.0. But recurring profit = 190 − 60 = **₹130 crore**, so the **adjusted P/E** = 3,040 ÷ 130 = **23.4**.\nA) Is the flattering reported figure. C) and D) do not follow. **The stock is materially more expensive than the headline P/E suggests — always strip one-offs.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which describes the correct relationship between number of holdings, correlation and portfolio risk?\nA) More holdings always means less risk regardless of correlation  B) Risk falls as holdings increase only to the extent the additions have low correlation with existing holdings; systematic risk remains regardless  C) Correlation is irrelevant  D) One stock is optimal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Diversification works **through low correlation**, and it has a floor: **systematic risk**.\nA) Twenty highly correlated stocks barely diversify. C) Correlation is the entire mechanism. D) One stock maximises unsystematic risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An investor asks why she should care about corporate governance when the financial numbers look excellent. The best answer is:\nA) Governance is irrelevant to returns  B) Reported numbers describe the past and depend on management's integrity to be accurate; weak governance means the numbers may be unreliable and minority shareholders may not receive their share of future value  C) Governance only matters for banks  D) SEBI guarantees governance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two distinct risks: the numbers may not be **true**, and even if true, value may be **diverted** from minority shareholders through related-party transactions or unfair restructurings.\nA) Governance failures have destroyed shareholder value repeatedly. C) It matters in every sector. D) SEBI sets rules but cannot guarantee conduct.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A stock has a P/E of 45 in a sector averaging 20. Which single question best determines whether this is justified?\nA) Is the share price high?  B) Does the company have materially superior and durable growth, returns on capital and earnings quality to justify the premium — or is the multiple driven by sentiment?  C) Is it a large-cap?  D) Does it pay dividends?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A premium multiple must be **earned by superior economics**. If growth, returns and earnings quality do not justify it, the premium is sentiment and will mean-revert.\nA) Price level alone says nothing about value. C) Size does not justify a multiple. D) Dividend policy is largely separate from the growth premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A DCF, a P/E comparison and a P/B comparison give values of ₹800, ₹1,050 and ₹640 for the same stock trading at ₹900. What should the analyst do?\nA) Pick the highest and buy  B) Treat the range as the useful output — understand why the methods diverge (growth assumptions, asset intensity, peer comparability) and form a judgement, rather than trusting any single figure  C) Pick the lowest and sell  D) Discard all three\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Valuation produces a **range, not a point**. Divergence is informative: it tells you which assumptions are driving which answer.\nA) and C) cherry-pick the number that supports a pre-formed view. D) Three imperfect estimates are far more useful than none. **The stock at ₹900 sits inside the ₹640–₹1,050 range — the honest conclusion is \"roughly fairly valued, with wide uncertainty\".**\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Which statement about beta and standard deviation is correct?\nA) They measure the same thing  B) Standard deviation measures TOTAL risk; beta measures only SYSTEMATIC risk — which is why only beta is priced in CAPM  C) Beta measures total risk  D) Neither is used in practice\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A stock can have high standard deviation (much of it company-specific, hence diversifiable and unpriced) yet a low beta.\nA) They measure different things. C) Reverses the definitions. D) Both are standard measures — see Chapter 16.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A company in a declining industry trades at a P/E of 6 with a 9% dividend yield. Assess.\nA) An obvious bargain  B) Possibly a value trap — a declining industry means shrinking future earnings, and a 9% yield may be unsustainable if profits fall; the low multiple may correctly reflect the outlook  C) Certain to double  D) The dividend is guaranteed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Low multiples and high yields in **structurally declining** industries frequently signal that the market has correctly priced falling earnings — the classic **value trap**.\nA) Cheapness relative to the past is not cheapness relative to the future. C) Nothing is certain. D) Dividends are discretionary and are usually cut when earnings fall.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single principle best summarises equity analysis in this chapter?\nA) Buy whatever has the lowest P/E  B) Estimate what the business is worth from its economics, cross-check against what the market pays for comparable firms, verify that the numbers and the people behind them are trustworthy, and diversify across low-correlation holdings  C) Follow chart patterns only  D) Buy the highest dividend yield\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It captures all four strands: **absolute valuation** (DCF), **relative valuation** (multiples), **qualitative/governance** assessment, and **diversification**.\nA) The lowest P/E is often the most troubled company. C) Charts address timing, not business quality. D) The highest yield is frequently the most distressed company.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.**\n\n### Case: Analysing Vantage Chemicals Ltd\n\nAn analyst is evaluating **Vantage Chemicals Ltd**:\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Shares outstanding | **15 crore** |\n| Market price | **₹640** |\n| Net profit (FY25) | **₹720 crore** |\n| — of which, one-time land sale gain | **₹180 crore** |\n| EBITDA | **₹1,350 crore** |\n| Total debt | **₹2,800 crore** |\n| Cash and equivalents | **₹500 crore** |\n| Book value per share | **₹290** |\n| Expected earnings growth (recurring) | **14% p.a.** |\n| Sector average P/E | **22** |\n\nThe analyst also notes: the promoter has **pledged 48%** of his holding, and the company's **statutory auditor resigned** last quarter citing \"pre-occupation\".\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is Vantage's market capitalisation and Enterprise Value?\nA) Market cap ₹9,600 crore; EV ₹11,900 crore  B) Market cap ₹9,600 crore; EV ₹7,300 crore  C) Market cap ₹640 crore; EV ₹2,940 crore  D) Market cap ₹11,900 crore; EV ₹9,600 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Market cap** = 15 crore × ₹640 = **₹9,600 crore**. **EV** = market cap + debt − cash = 9,600 + 2,800 − 500 = **₹11,900 crore**.\nB) Subtracts debt instead of adding it. C) Confuses the share price with market cap. D) Swaps the two figures. *(EV exceeds market cap because Vantage carries net debt of ₹2,300 crore.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What is the reported P/E, and what is the P/E adjusted for the one-time gain?\nA) Reported 13.3; adjusted 17.8  B) Reported 17.8; adjusted 13.3  C) Reported 13.3; adjusted 13.3  D) Reported 22.0; adjusted 14.0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Reported P/E** = 9,600 ÷ 720 = **13.3**. **Recurring profit** = 720 − 180 = **₹540 crore**, so the **adjusted P/E** = 9,600 ÷ 540 = **17.8**.\nB) Inverts the two. C) Fails to strip the one-off. D) Uses the sector average as the reported figure. **The headline P/E of 13.3 flatters the stock; on recurring earnings it is 17.8 — a third more expensive.**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Compute EV/EBITDA and P/B for Vantage.\nA) EV/EBITDA 8.8; P/B 2.21  B) EV/EBITDA 7.1; P/B 2.21  C) EV/EBITDA 8.8; P/B 0.45  D) EV/EBITDA 11.9; P/B 1.35\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **EV/EBITDA** = 11,900 ÷ 1,350 = **8.81**. **P/B** = 640 ÷ 290 = **2.21**.\nB) Uses market cap instead of EV. C) Inverts the P/B. D) Miscomputes both. **Note how the debt matters: market cap/EBITDA would be only 7.1, but any acquirer must also assume ₹2,300 crore of net debt — hence 8.8.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Using the adjusted P/E and recurring growth, what is the PEG, and how does the stock compare with its sector?\nA) PEG 1.27; the stock trades below the sector P/E of 22 on an adjusted basis  B) PEG 0.95; expensive  C) PEG 2.5; very cheap  D) PEG 1.27; the stock trades above the sector P/E\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **PEG** = adjusted P/E ÷ growth = 17.8 ÷ 14 = **1.27**. Against the **sector average P/E of 22**, Vantage's adjusted 17.8 is still a **discount**.\nB) Uses the reported P/E. C) Inverts the calculation. D) 17.8 is below 22, not above. **So on the numbers alone Vantage looks moderately cheap versus its sector — but PEG above 1 says it is not cheap relative to its own growth. The discount needs explaining, which QC5 addresses.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Given the full picture, what is the analyst's most defensible conclusion?\nA) Buy — the stock trades at a discount to the sector  B) The sector discount is likely explained by governance risk: an auditor resigning citing \"pre-occupation\" and 48% promoter pledging are serious red flags that raise doubts about earnings reliability and create forced-selling risk — further investigation or avoidance is warranted  C) Sell short immediately  D) Governance is irrelevant given the low P/E\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The valuation work says \"moderately cheap versus sector\". The **qualitative** work says why. **An auditor resigning with a vague reason** is one of the strongest warning signals available to an outside investor — auditors rarely leave a clean client. Combined with **48% promoter pledging**, which creates a forced-selling spiral if the price falls, the market's discount looks **rational rather than an opportunity**.\nA) Treats a discount as automatically an opportunity, ignoring why it exists. C) The evidence justifies caution and investigation, not a confident short position. D) Governance is precisely what determines whether the reported earnings — and therefore the low multiple — can be trusted.\n\n> ⚠️ **The lesson this caselet is built to teach:** valuation ratios tell you *what the market is paying*; they never tell you *whether the numbers are real*. Vantage's headline P/E of 13.3 becomes 17.8 once a one-off is stripped, and the remaining sector discount is best explained by governance risk — not by mispricing. **Always finish the qualitative work before acting on the quantitative.**\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "09", "title": "Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "# Chapter 9: Investing in Fixed Income Securities — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 3 · Investment Products (30 marks).**\n> ⭐ The hardest maths in the paper sits here: **bond pricing, yield measures and duration**. Chapter 2's TVM formulas are the foundation — a bond is simply an annuity (the coupons) plus a lump sum (the redemption).\n\n---\n\n## 9.1 The Debt Market and Its Role\n\nDebt lets **governments and corporates borrow** from many lenders at once.\n\n| Borrower | Why they issue debt |\n|---|---|\n| **Government** | Fund the fiscal deficit, infrastructure, and manage liquidity |\n| **Corporates** | Fund expansion without diluting ownership; interest is **tax-deductible**, unlike dividends |\n\n**Why debt rather than equity, from the issuer's side:** no dilution of control, a **known** cost, and interest is deductible for tax. The cost is a **fixed obligation** that must be met regardless of profitability.\n\n---\n\n## 9.2 The Bond Market Ecosystem\n\n| Participant | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Issuers** | Government, PSUs, corporates, banks, NBFCs |\n| **Investors** | Banks, insurers, pension funds, mutual funds, FPIs, retail |\n| **Intermediaries** | Merchant bankers, brokers, primary dealers |\n| **Credit rating agencies** | Assess default risk (CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, India Ratings) |\n| **Debenture trustees** | Protect bondholders' interests; enforce security |\n| **Regulators** | **RBI** — G-secs and money market; **SEBI** — corporate bonds and listing |\n\n> ⚠️ **Split jurisdiction:** government securities and the money market sit with the **RBI**; **corporate bonds** and their listing sit with **SEBI**.\n\n---\n\n## 9.3 ⭐ Risks in Fixed Income\n\n| Risk | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Interest rate risk** | Bond prices fall when rates rise. The **biggest** risk for a high-quality bond |\n| **Call risk** | The issuer redeems early (when rates fall), forcing reinvestment at lower rates |\n| **Reinvestment risk** | Coupons must be reinvested at whatever rate then prevails — a risk that **rates fall** |\n| **Credit risk** | The issuer may not pay. Three sub-types: **downgrade risk** (rating cut), **spread/basis risk** (credit spreads widen), **default risk** (non-payment) |\n| **Liquidity risk** | Cannot sell at a fair price; wide spreads, especially in corporate bonds |\n| **Exchange rate risk** | For foreign-currency bonds, adverse currency movement |\n| **Inflation (purchasing power) risk** | Fixed coupons buy less as prices rise |\n| **Volatility risk** | Relevant to bonds with embedded options — higher volatility raises option value |\n| **Political / legal risk** | Changes in law, tax or regulation affecting the bond |\n| **Event risk** | A specific event — merger, restructuring, disaster — impairing the issuer |\n\n> 🧠 **Interest rate risk and reinvestment risk pull in opposite directions.** If rates rise, your bond's *price* falls (bad) but you reinvest coupons at *higher* rates (good). This offsetting is exactly what **duration** captures — see 9.7.\n\n---\n\n## 9.4 ⭐ Bond Pricing\n\n### 9.4.1 Par value\nThe **face value** repaid at maturity — typically ₹100 or ₹1,000 for corporate bonds and ₹100 for G-secs. **The coupon is always calculated on the face value, never on the market price.**\n\n### 9.4.2 The pricing principle\n\n**A bond's price is the present value of all its future cash flows**, discounted at the required yield:\n\n$$\\boxed{P = \\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\frac{C}{(1+y)^t} + \\frac{F}{(1+y)^n}}$$\n\nEquivalently, using the annuity formula from Chapter 2:\n\n$$\\boxed{P = C \\times \\frac{1-(1+y)^{-n}}{y} + \\frac{F}{(1+y)^n}}$$\n\nwhere **C** = coupon per period, **F** = face value, **y** = required yield per period, **n** = number of periods.\n\n**Worked example:** a ₹1,000 face value bond, 8% annual coupon, 5 years to maturity, required yield 10%.\n- Coupon = ₹80 a year; annuity factor = [1 − (1.10)⁻⁵] ÷ 0.10 = (1 − 0.62092) ÷ 0.10 = **3.7908**\n- PV of coupons = 80 × 3.7908 = **₹303.26**\n- PV of redemption = 1,000 ÷ (1.10)⁵ = 1,000 ÷ 1.61051 = **₹620.92**\n- **Price = 303.26 + 620.92 = ₹924.18**\n\n### 9.4.3 Pricing variants\n\n| Bond type | Adjustment |\n|---|---|\n| **Annual coupon** | As above |\n| **Semi-annual coupon** | **Halve the coupon and the yield; double the periods.** A 10% coupon, 6 years, 12% yield → C = 5% of face, y = 6%, n = 12 |\n| **Zero-coupon bond** | No coupons: **P = F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ**. Always issued/trades at a **discount** |\n| **Between coupon dates** | Price includes **accrued interest**. **Dirty price = clean price + accrued interest** |\n| **Perpetual bond** | No maturity: **P = C ÷ y** — a perpetuity |\n\n### 9.4.4 ⭐ The Price–Yield Relationship\n\n$$\\textbf{Price and yield move in OPPOSITE directions.}$$\n\n| Condition | Bond trades at |\n|---|---|\n| **Coupon rate > required yield** | **Premium** (price > face value) |\n| **Coupon rate = required yield** | **Par** (price = face value) |\n| **Coupon rate < required yield** | **Discount** (price < face value) |\n\n> 🧠 **The intuition:** if a bond pays 8% while the market demands 10%, nobody will pay full price for it. Its price must fall until the *total* return to a buyer equals 10%. In the example above, ₹924.18 is exactly the price at which an 8% coupon delivers a 10% yield.\n\n**The relationship is convex, not a straight line** — prices rise more when yields fall than they fall when yields rise by the same amount. This is **convexity**, and it is favourable to the bondholder.\n\n**Pricing matrix:** a grid of bond prices across different yields and maturities, used to value illiquid bonds by reference to comparable traded ones.\n\n### 9.4.5 Perpetual bonds\nNo maturity date; the price is simply **C ÷ y**. In India these are often **AT-1 bonds** issued by banks — which carry **write-down risk** and are considerably riskier than ordinary bonds despite the steady coupon.\n\n---\n\n## 9.5 ⭐ Yield Measures\n\n| Measure | Formula / meaning | Limitation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Coupon rate** | Coupon ÷ **Face value** | Fixed; ignores the price you paid |\n| **Current yield** | **Annual coupon ÷ Market price** | Ignores capital gain/loss at maturity and the timing of cash flows |\n| **Yield to Maturity (YTM)** | The single discount rate that makes the PV of all cash flows equal the market price | Assumes **all coupons are reinvested at the YTM** and the bond is **held to maturity** |\n| **Effective yield** | Yield adjusted for **compounding within the year** | — |\n| **Yield to Call (YTC)** | YTM calculated to the **call date and call price** | Relevant only for callable bonds |\n| **Yield to Put (YTP)** | YTM calculated to the **put date and put price** | Relevant only for puttable bonds |\n\n### The relationships — a favourite exam question\n\n| Bond trading at | Relationship |\n|---|---|\n| **Discount** | Coupon rate **<** Current yield **<** YTM |\n| **Par** | Coupon rate **=** Current yield **=** YTM |\n| **Premium** | Coupon rate **>** Current yield **>** YTM |\n\n> 🧠 **Why:** a discount bond gives you a **capital gain** at maturity (you paid less than face value), which YTM counts but current yield ignores — so YTM is highest. A premium bond delivers a **capital loss** at maturity, so YTM is lowest.\n\n**YTM's two big assumptions:** (1) every coupon is reinvested at the YTM itself — rarely true, which is **reinvestment risk**; and (2) the bond is held to maturity.\n\n**Yield to Worst:** for a bond with multiple call dates, the lowest of all possible yields — the conservative measure.\n\n---\n\n## 9.6 The Yield Curve\n\nThe **yield curve** plots yield against maturity for bonds of the same credit quality (usually government securities).\n\n| Shape | Appearance | Typical interpretation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Normal (upward sloping)** | Long yields > short yields | Healthy expansion; investors demand a **term premium** for locking money away longer |\n| **Flat** | Similar across maturities | Transition or uncertainty |\n| **Inverted** | Short yields > long yields | Market expects **rate cuts / slowdown**; historically a recession signal |\n| **Humped** | Peaks in the middle | Mixed expectations |\n\n**Theories explaining the shape:**\n- **Expectations theory** — long rates reflect expected future short rates.\n- **Liquidity preference theory** — investors demand a premium for longer maturities, so the curve is normally upward sloping.\n- **Market segmentation theory** — different investors have fixed maturity preferences (insurers want long, banks want short), so supply and demand at each segment set that segment's rate.\n\n**Uses:** benchmark pricing for corporate bonds (which trade at a spread over the curve), signalling the economic outlook, and informing portfolio positioning.\n\n---\n\n## 9.7 ⭐ Duration\n\n**Duration measures a bond's price sensitivity to interest rate changes** — and, equivalently, the weighted-average time to receive its cash flows.\n\n### Macaulay duration\nThe weighted average time (in years) to receive the bond's cash flows, weighted by the present value of each.\n\n### Modified duration\nThe practical measure:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Modified Duration} = \\frac{\\text{Macaulay Duration}}{1 + y}}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{\\%\\ \\Delta P \\approx -\\text{Modified Duration} \\times \\Delta y}$$\n\n**Example:** a bond with modified duration **6.2** when yields rise **0.75%** (75 basis points):\n% change ≈ −6.2 × 0.0075 = **−4.65%**. A ₹10,00,000 holding loses about **₹46,500**.\n\n### What drives duration\n\n| Factor | Effect on duration |\n|---|---|\n| **Longer maturity** | **Higher** duration (more sensitive) |\n| **Higher coupon** | **Lower** duration (more value arrives sooner) |\n| **Higher yield** | Lower duration |\n| **Zero-coupon bond** | Duration = **maturity exactly** (the only cash flow is at the end) |\n\n> 🧠 **Two facts to memorise:**\n> 1. **A zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration equals its maturity** — the single most-tested duration fact.\n> 2. **Longer duration = more interest rate risk.** If you expect rates to **fall**, go **long** duration; if you expect rates to **rise**, go **short** duration.\n\n**Convexity** is the second-order effect — the curvature of the price–yield relationship. Duration alone underestimates the price rise when yields fall and overestimates the fall when yields rise; convexity corrects for this, and **positive convexity benefits the bondholder**.\n\n---\n\n## 9.8 The Money Market\n\nShort-term debt: **maturity up to one year**, regulated by the **RBI**.\n\n### 9.8.1 Participants\n**Demand side:** banks (managing reserves), corporates (short-term funding). **Supply side:** banks with surplus, mutual funds (liquid schemes), insurers, the RBI. **Intermediaries:** primary dealers, brokers.\n\n### 9.8.2 Instruments\n\n| Instrument | Issuer | Tenor | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Treasury bills (T-bills)** | Government of India | **91, 182, 364 days** | **Zero-coupon**, issued at a discount, redeemed at face value. Risk-free |\n| **Cash Management Bills** | Government | Under 91 days | For temporary cash mismatches |\n| **Certificates of Deposit (CDs)** | Banks and select FIs | 7 days–1 year | Negotiable term deposits |\n| **Commercial Paper (CP)** | Corporates, PDs, FIs | 7 days–1 year | **Unsecured** promissory note; needs a credit rating |\n| **Call money** | Banks | **Overnight** | Inter-bank; rate is the \"call rate\" |\n| **Notice money** | Banks | 2–14 days | — |\n| **Term money** | Banks | 15 days–1 year | — |\n| **Repo / Reverse repo** | Banks, RBI | Short | Sale with an agreement to repurchase — effectively collateralised borrowing |\n| **TREPS / CBLO** | Market participants | Short | Tri-party repo, centrally cleared |\n\n> 🧠 **Repo vs reverse repo, from the RBI's perspective:** in a **repo**, banks borrow from the RBI against securities (the **repo rate**). In a **reverse repo**, banks lend to the RBI (the **reverse repo rate**). The repo rate is the RBI's main policy rate.\n\n---\n\n## 9.9 The Government Debt Market\n\n**Participants:** the **RBI** (as the government's debt manager and banker), **primary dealers** (obliged to bid at auctions), banks (holding G-secs for SLR), insurers, pension funds, mutual funds, FPIs and retail (via **RBI Retail Direct**).\n\n### Instruments\n\n| Instrument | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **Dated G-secs** | Fixed-coupon bonds, typically 5–40 years; **coupon paid half-yearly** |\n| **Treasury bills** | 91/182/364 days, zero-coupon |\n| **State Development Loans (SDLs)** | Issued by state governments; slightly higher yield than central G-secs |\n| **Floating Rate Bonds** | Coupon resets against a benchmark |\n| **Inflation Indexed Bonds** | Principal or coupon linked to an inflation index |\n| **Sovereign Gold Bonds** | Gold-linked, with an additional fixed interest rate |\n| **Savings (Taxable) Bonds** | Retail-oriented government savings instruments |\n| **STRIPS** | Coupons and principal separated and traded as individual zero-coupon securities |\n\nG-secs are sold through **RBI auctions** and carry **no credit risk** in domestic currency.\n\n---\n\n## 9.10 The Corporate Debt Market\n\n**Participants:** issuers (corporates, NBFCs, banks), investors (mutual funds, insurers, pension funds, FPIs, HNIs), and intermediaries (merchant bankers, arrangers, rating agencies, **debenture trustees**).\n\n### Instruments\n\n| Instrument | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **Company/corporate deposits** | Unsecured deposits with a company; **not DICGC-insured** — credit risk sits with the company |\n| **Bonds and debentures** | Secured or unsecured; convertible or non-convertible (NCDs) |\n| **Infrastructure bonds** | Issued to fund infrastructure |\n| **Inflation-indexed bonds** | Return linked to inflation |\n| **Perpetual/AT-1 bonds** | No maturity; bank capital instruments with **write-down risk** |\n| **Masala bonds** | **Rupee-denominated** bonds issued **outside India** — the issuer bears no currency risk; the investor does |\n\n**Debentures may be:** secured/unsecured, convertible (fully or partly) / non-convertible, redeemable/perpetual.\n\n---\n\n## 9.11 Small Savings and Bank Deposits\n\n### Bank deposits\n- **Savings, current, fixed (FD) and recurring (RD)** deposits.\n- **Interest rates on FDs** are set by each bank and vary with tenor; senior citizens typically receive an additional 0.25–0.75%.\n- **DICGC insurance covers up to ₹5 lakh** per depositor per bank (principal + interest), across all branches.\n- Premature withdrawal usually carries a penalty.\n\n### Floating Rate Savings Bonds, 2020 (Taxable)\n- Issued by the Government of India; **7-year** tenor.\n- Interest **resets half-yearly**, pegged at **NSC rate + 0.35%**.\n- Interest is **fully taxable**; not tradable.\n\n### ⭐ Small savings schemes — the comparison table\n\n| Scheme | Tenor | Key features | Tax |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **PPF** | **15 years** (extendable in 5-year blocks) | Min ₹500, **max ₹1.5 lakh a year**; loan and partial withdrawal facilities | **EEE** — exempt at all three stages |\n| **Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme (SCSS)** | **5 years** (+3 extension) | Age **60+** (or 55+ on superannuation); **max ₹30 lakh**; **quarterly** interest | Interest **taxable**; 80C on deposit |\n| **National Savings Certificate (NSC)** | **5 years** | Interest **compounded annually but payable at maturity**; reinvested interest also qualifies for 80C | Interest taxable |\n| **Kisan Vikas Patra (KVP)** | Until the amount **doubles** (period set by the prevailing rate) | No maximum limit; **no 80C benefit** | Interest taxable |\n| **Sukanya Samriddhi Account (SSA)** | Until the girl turns 21 | For a **girl child under 10**; deposits for 15 years; **max ₹1.5 lakh a year** | **EEE** |\n| **Post Office MIS** | 5 years | **Monthly** interest payout | Interest taxable |\n| **Post Office Time/Recurring Deposits** | 1–5 years | Similar to bank deposits | Taxable |\n\n> ⚠️ **Most-tested facts:** **PPF and SSA are EEE.** **PPF is 15 years with a ₹1.5 lakh annual cap.** **SCSS is for 60+, capped at ₹30 lakh, paying quarterly.** **KVP doubles your money and gets NO 80C benefit.**\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Bond price | **PV of all future cash flows** at the required yield |\n| Bond price formula | C × [(1 − (1+y)⁻ⁿ) ÷ y] + F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ |\n| Semi-annual bonds | **Halve** C and y, **double** n |\n| Zero-coupon bond | P = F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ; always at a discount |\n| Perpetual bond | P = C ÷ y |\n| Dirty price | Clean price **+ accrued interest** |\n| Price and yield | Move **inversely**; the curve is **convex** |\n| Coupon > yield | Trades at a **premium** |\n| Coupon < yield | Trades at a **discount** |\n| Current yield | Coupon ÷ **market price** |\n| YTM | The rate equating PV of cash flows to price |\n| YTM's assumptions | Coupons reinvested **at the YTM**; held to maturity |\n| Discount bond ordering | Coupon < Current yield < **YTM** |\n| Premium bond ordering | Coupon > Current yield > **YTM** |\n| Modified duration | Macaulay ÷ (1 + y) |\n| Price change | ≈ **−Modified duration × Δy** |\n| Zero-coupon duration | **= its maturity** |\n| Higher coupon | **Lower** duration |\n| Longer maturity | **Higher** duration |\n| Expect rates to fall | Go **long** duration |\n| Convexity | Second-order effect; **benefits the bondholder** |\n| Normal yield curve | Upward sloping — term premium |\n| Inverted curve | Short > long; signals expected **rate cuts / slowdown** |\n| T-bill tenors | **91, 182, 364 days**; zero-coupon |\n| Commercial paper | **Unsecured** corporate short-term note; needs a rating |\n| Call money | **Overnight** inter-bank |\n| Repo rate | Rate at which **banks borrow from the RBI** |\n| G-sec coupon frequency | **Half-yearly** |\n| DICGC cover | **₹5 lakh** per depositor per bank |\n| PPF | **15 years**, ₹1.5 lakh cap, **EEE** |\n| SCSS | Age **60+**, ₹30 lakh cap, **quarterly** interest |\n| NSC | 5 years; interest compounded but paid at maturity |\n| KVP | Doubles the money; **no 80C** |\n| SSA | Girl under 10; **EEE**; ₹1.5 lakh cap |\n| Floating Rate Savings Bond | 7 years; **NSC + 0.35%**, reset half-yearly; taxable |\n| Masala bond | **Rupee-denominated**, issued **outside India** |\n| Regulator split | **RBI** — G-secs & money market; **SEBI** — corporate bonds |\n\n> **Exam tip:** almost every bond question reduces to one of three tasks — **price it** (discount the cash flows), **compare coupon with yield** (premium/par/discount), or **apply duration** (% price change ≈ −MD × Δy). Identify which one is being asked before computing.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 9: Investing in Fixed Income Securities — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **Bond pricing, the yield orderings and duration are the highest-yield items.**\n\n**Q1.** State the bond pricing principle and formula.\n> **A:** A bond's price is the **present value of all its future cash flows**: **P = C × [(1 − (1+y)⁻ⁿ) ÷ y] + F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ** — an annuity (coupons) plus a lump sum (redemption).\n\n**Q2.** How do you adjust the formula for a semi-annual bond?\n> **A:** **Halve the coupon and the yield; double the number of periods.** A 10% coupon over 6 years at a 12% yield becomes C = 5%, y = 6%, n = 12.\n\n**Q3.** How is a zero-coupon bond priced?\n> **A:** **P = F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ** — there are no coupons, so it always trades/issues at a **discount** to face value.\n\n**Q4.** How is a perpetual bond priced?\n> **A:** **P = C ÷ y** — a perpetuity, since there is no redemption.\n\n**Q5.** What is the difference between clean and dirty price?\n> **A:** **Dirty price = clean price + accrued interest.** Between coupon dates the buyer must compensate the seller for interest earned but not yet paid.\n\n**Q6.** State the relationship between price and yield.\n> **A:** They move **inversely**, and the relationship is **convex** (curved), not linear.\n\n**Q7.** When does a bond trade at a premium, at par, and at a discount?\n> **A:** **Coupon > yield → premium.** **Coupon = yield → par.** **Coupon < yield → discount.**\n\n**Q8.** Explain intuitively why a bond paying 8% trades below par when the market demands 10%.\n> **A:** Nobody will pay full price for a below-market coupon. The **price must fall** until the buyer's total return — coupons **plus the capital gain at redemption** — equals the 10% the market demands.\n\n**Q9.** Define current yield and state its limitation.\n> **A:** **Current yield = annual coupon ÷ market price.** It **ignores the capital gain or loss at maturity** and the timing of cash flows.\n\n**Q10.** What is YTM, and what two assumptions does it make?\n> **A:** The single discount rate that makes the PV of all cash flows equal the market price. It assumes **(1) all coupons are reinvested at the YTM** and **(2) the bond is held to maturity**.\n\n**Q11.** State the ordering of coupon rate, current yield and YTM for a DISCOUNT bond.\n> **A:** **Coupon rate < Current yield < YTM.** (For a **premium** bond the ordering reverses; at **par** all three are equal.)\n\n**Q12.** Why is YTM highest for a discount bond?\n> **A:** Because the investor also receives a **capital gain at maturity** (paid less than face value), which YTM captures but current yield ignores.\n\n**Q13.** What are Yield to Call and Yield to Worst?\n> **A:** **YTC** is the YTM computed to the **call date and call price**. **Yield to Worst** is the lowest of all possible yields across call dates — the conservative measure.\n\n**Q14.** Name the ten risks in fixed income.\n> **A:** **Interest rate, call, reinvestment, credit** (downgrade / spread / default), **liquidity, exchange rate, inflation, volatility, political-legal** and **event** risk.\n\n**Q15.** How do interest rate risk and reinvestment risk offset each other?\n> **A:** If rates **rise**, the bond's **price falls** (bad) but **coupons are reinvested at higher rates** (good). This offsetting is exactly what **duration** captures.\n\n**Q16.** What is duration, in two senses?\n> **A:** A measure of a bond's **price sensitivity to interest rate changes**, and equivalently the **weighted-average time** to receive its cash flows.\n\n**Q17.** State the modified duration formula and the price-change approximation.\n> **A:** **Modified Duration = Macaulay Duration ÷ (1 + y)**, and **% change in price ≈ −Modified Duration × Δy**.\n\n**Q18.** A bond has modified duration 6.2 and yields rise 75 basis points. What happens to the price?\n> **A:** % change ≈ −6.2 × 0.0075 = **−4.65%**. On a ₹10,00,000 holding that is a loss of about **₹46,500**.\n\n**Q19.** What is a zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration?\n> **A:** **Exactly equal to its maturity** — because the only cash flow arrives at the end. This is the single most-tested duration fact.\n\n**Q20.** How do maturity, coupon and yield each affect duration?\n> **A:** **Longer maturity → higher** duration. **Higher coupon → lower** duration (more value arrives sooner). **Higher yield → lower** duration.\n\n**Q21.** If you expect interest rates to fall, how should you position duration?\n> **A:** Go **LONG duration** — longer-duration bonds gain most when yields fall. If you expect rates to **rise**, go **short duration**.\n\n**Q22.** What is convexity, and who does it benefit?\n> **A:** The **curvature** of the price–yield relationship — the second-order effect duration alone misses. **Positive convexity benefits the bondholder**: prices rise more when yields fall than they fall when yields rise equally.\n\n**Q23.** Describe the four yield curve shapes and what each signals.\n> **A:** **Normal (upward)** — healthy expansion, term premium. **Flat** — transition/uncertainty. **Inverted** (short > long) — expected **rate cuts/slowdown**, historically a recession signal. **Humped** — mixed expectations.\n\n**Q24.** Name the three theories explaining the yield curve's shape.\n> **A:** **Expectations theory** (long rates reflect expected future short rates), **liquidity preference theory** (a premium is demanded for longer maturities), and **market segmentation theory** (different investors have fixed maturity preferences).\n\n**Q25.** Which regulator covers government securities, and which covers corporate bonds?\n> **A:** **RBI** — government securities and the money market. **SEBI** — corporate bonds and their listing.\n\n**Q26.** State the T-bill tenors and their key feature.\n> **A:** **91, 182 and 364 days.** They are **zero-coupon**, issued at a discount and redeemed at face value, and are **risk-free**.\n\n**Q27.** What is commercial paper?\n> **A:** An **unsecured** short-term promissory note issued by corporates, PDs and FIs, with a tenor of **7 days to 1 year**, requiring a **credit rating**.\n\n**Q28.** What is a certificate of deposit?\n> **A:** A **negotiable term deposit** issued by banks and select financial institutions, with a tenor of **7 days to 1 year**.\n\n**Q29.** Distinguish call, notice and term money.\n> **A:** **Call money** — **overnight** inter-bank. **Notice money** — 2–14 days. **Term money** — 15 days to 1 year.\n\n**Q30.** Explain repo and reverse repo from the RBI's perspective.\n> **A:** In a **repo**, **banks borrow from the RBI** against securities (the **repo rate** — the main policy rate). In a **reverse repo**, **banks lend to the RBI**.\n\n**Q31.** How often do dated government securities pay coupons?\n> **A:** **Half-yearly.**\n\n**Q32.** What are SDLs and STRIPS?\n> **A:** **SDLs** are State Development Loans — bonds issued by state governments, yielding slightly more than central G-secs. **STRIPS** separate a bond's coupons and principal into individually traded **zero-coupon** securities.\n\n**Q33.** What is a Masala bond?\n> **A:** A **rupee-denominated bond issued OUTSIDE India**. Because it is denominated in rupees, the **issuer bears no currency risk — the investor does**.\n\n**Q34.** What is an AT-1 (perpetual) bond, and what makes it risky?\n> **A:** A bank capital instrument with **no maturity**. Despite the steady coupon it carries **write-down risk** — it can be written off entirely if the bank is in trouble.\n\n**Q35.** How much does DICGC deposit insurance cover?\n> **A:** **₹5 lakh per depositor per bank** (principal + interest), across all branches of that bank.\n\n**Q36.** State PPF's tenor, annual limit and tax status.\n> **A:** **15 years** (extendable in 5-year blocks), **maximum ₹1.5 lakh a year**, and **EEE** — exempt at contribution, accrual and withdrawal.\n\n**Q37.** State SCSS's eligibility, limit, tenor and payout frequency.\n> **A:** Age **60+** (or 55+ on superannuation), maximum **₹30 lakh**, **5 years** (extendable by 3), with **quarterly** interest. The interest is **taxable**.\n\n**Q38.** What is distinctive about NSC's interest?\n> **A:** It is **compounded annually but paid only at maturity**, and the **reinvested interest also qualifies for Section 80C** in the intervening years.\n\n**Q39.** What is Kisan Vikas Patra's distinguishing feature — and its tax drawback?\n> **A:** It runs until the **amount doubles**, with **no maximum limit** — but it carries **no 80C benefit** and the interest is taxable.\n\n**Q40.** Who is Sukanya Samriddhi for, and what is its tax status?\n> **A:** A **girl child under 10**; deposits for 15 years, maturing when she turns 21. Maximum **₹1.5 lakh a year**, and it is **EEE**.\n\n**Q41.** Describe the Floating Rate Savings Bonds, 2020.\n> **A:** A **7-year** Government of India bond whose interest **resets half-yearly at the NSC rate + 0.35%**. The interest is **fully taxable** and the bond is **not tradable**.\n\n**Q42.** Are company deposits covered by deposit insurance?\n> **A:** **No.** DICGC covers **bank** deposits only. A company deposit carries the **company's own credit risk**, which is why it pays more.\n\n**Q43.** Why do corporates issue debt rather than equity?\n> **A:** **No dilution of ownership**, a **known** cost, and **interest is tax-deductible** (unlike dividends). The trade-off is a **fixed obligation** payable regardless of profits.\n\n**Q44.** What does a debenture trustee do?\n> **A:** **Protects bondholders' interests** and enforces the security on their behalf — a mandatory appointment for public debt issues.\n\n**Q45.** What single question resolves most bond exam problems?\n> **A:** Which of three tasks is being asked — **price it** (discount the cash flows), **compare coupon with yield** (premium/par/discount), or **apply duration** (% change ≈ −MD × Δy)?\n", "questions": "# Chapter 9: Investing in Fixed Income Securities — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Bond pricing, yield orderings and duration dominate.** Keep a spreadsheet handy. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** A bond's price is:\nA) Its face value always  B) The present value of all its future cash flows  C) The coupon rate  D) The sum of all coupons\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A bond is valued by **discounting its coupons and redemption** at the required yield.\nA) Price equals face value only when the coupon equals the yield. C) The coupon is one input. D) Undiscounted coupons ignore the time value of money and the redemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Bond prices and yields move:\nA) In the same direction  B) In opposite directions  C) Independently  D) Only upward\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The single most important relationship in fixed income.\nA) and C) are incorrect. D) Bond prices fall as well as rise.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A bond trades at a premium when:\nA) Coupon rate < required yield  B) Coupon rate = required yield  C) Coupon rate > required yield  D) It is a zero-coupon bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An above-market coupon is worth paying extra for, so the price exceeds face value.\nA) Gives a **discount**. B) Gives **par**. D) Zero-coupon bonds always trade at a discount.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** For a bond trading at a discount, the correct ordering is:\nA) Coupon > Current yield > YTM  B) Coupon < Current yield < YTM  C) All three equal  D) YTM < Coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A discount bond delivers a **capital gain at maturity**, which YTM counts and current yield ignores — so YTM is highest.\nA) Is the **premium** bond ordering. C) Applies only at par. D) Contradicts the discount case.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The current yield is:\nA) Coupon ÷ Face value  B) Coupon ÷ Market price  C) The YTM  D) Coupon × Years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Current yield relates the coupon to what you **actually paid**.\nA) Is the **coupon rate**. C) YTM also accounts for capital gain/loss and timing. D) Is meaningless.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** YTM assumes that:\nA) Coupons are spent  B) All coupons are reinvested at the YTM and the bond is held to maturity  C) The bond is sold immediately  D) Rates never change\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — These are YTM's two standing assumptions, and the first is the source of **reinvestment risk**.\nA) YTM assumes reinvestment, not spending. C) Selling early gives a realised yield, not YTM. D) YTM makes no such claim.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration equals:\nA) Zero  B) Its maturity  C) Half its maturity  D) One year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With a **single cash flow at the end**, the weighted-average time to receive cash is exactly the maturity.\nA), C) and D) are incorrect. This is the most-tested duration fact in the syllabus.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Modified duration is calculated as:\nA) Macaulay duration × (1 + y)  B) Macaulay duration ÷ (1 + y)  C) Maturity ÷ 2  D) Coupon ÷ price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Dividing by (1 + y) converts Macaulay duration into a direct price-sensitivity measure.\nA) Multiplies instead of dividing. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The approximate percentage price change of a bond is:\nA) +Modified duration × Δy  B) −Modified duration × Δy  C) Duration ÷ Δy  D) Δy ÷ duration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **negative sign** reflects the inverse price–yield relationship.\nA) Gets the sign wrong. C) and D) are not the formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Treasury bills in India are issued for tenors of:\nA) 91, 182 and 364 days  B) 1, 2 and 3 years  C) 30, 60 and 90 days  D) 5 and 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **91, 182 and 364 days**, all zero-coupon.\nB) and D) are dated securities, not T-bills. C) Is not the Indian tenor set.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Treasury bills are:\nA) Coupon-bearing  B) Zero-coupon, issued at a discount  C) Perpetual  D) Convertible\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — They are issued **below face value** and redeemed at par, the difference being the return.\nA) T-bills pay no coupon. C) They have a defined short maturity. D) They are not convertible.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Commercial paper is:\nA) A secured government bond  B) An unsecured short-term promissory note issued by corporates  C) A bank deposit  D) An equity share\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CP is **unsecured**, requires a credit rating, and runs 7 days to 1 year.\nA) Government short-term paper is the T-bill. C) That is a certificate of deposit. D) CP is debt, not equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Call money refers to:\nA) Overnight inter-bank borrowing  B) A callable bond  C) A 5-year loan  D) A telephone service\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Overnight** inter-bank funds; the rate is the \"call rate\".\nB) A callable bond is a different concept (call risk). C) Term money runs 15 days–1 year. D) Irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The repo rate is the rate at which:\nA) Banks lend to the RBI  B) Banks borrow from the RBI against securities  C) Companies borrow from banks  D) Individuals borrow\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In a **repo**, banks borrow from the RBI against collateral. It is the RBI's main policy rate.\nA) That is the **reverse repo**. C) and D) are commercial lending, not policy rates.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Dated government securities pay coupons:\nA) Monthly  B) Quarterly  C) Half-yearly  D) Annually\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Indian dated G-secs pay **half-yearly** coupons.\nA), B) and D) are not the standard G-sec convention.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** DICGC deposit insurance covers up to:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹2 lakh  C) ₹5 lakh  D) ₹10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹5 lakh per depositor per bank**, covering principal and interest across all branches.\nA) Was the historical limit. B) and D) are not the current cover.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** PPF has a tenor of:\nA) 5 years  B) 10 years  C) 15 years, extendable in 5-year blocks  D) 21 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **15 years**, extendable in blocks of 5.\nA) Is NSC/SCSS. B) Is not a PPF term. D) Is Sukanya Samriddhi's maturity, tied to the girl's age.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The maximum annual contribution to PPF is:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹1.5 lakh  C) ₹2 lakh  D) Unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹1.5 lakh** per financial year.\nA), C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** PPF's tax treatment is:\nA) EET  B) ETE  C) EEE  D) TEE\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **EEE**: exempt on contribution, on accrual and on withdrawal.\nA), B) and D) describe other tax regimes; PPF is one of the few genuinely EEE instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme is available to those aged:\nA) 50 and above  B) 55 and above only  C) 60 and above (or 55+ on superannuation)  D) 65 and above\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **60+**, with an exception for those retiring on superannuation at 55+.\nA), B) and D) are incorrect thresholds.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The maximum investment in SCSS is:\nA) ₹15 lakh  B) ₹30 lakh  C) ₹50 lakh  D) ₹1.5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹30 lakh**.\nA) Was the earlier limit. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** SCSS pays interest:\nA) Monthly  B) Quarterly  C) Annually  D) At maturity only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Quarterly** payouts, which suits retirees needing regular income.\nA) Post Office MIS pays monthly. C) and D) are incorrect for SCSS.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Kisan Vikas Patra:\nA) Offers Section 80C benefit  B) Runs until the invested amount doubles, with no 80C benefit  C) Has a ₹1.5 lakh cap  D) Is tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — KVP doubles the money over a period set by the prevailing rate, has **no maximum limit**, and carries **no 80C deduction**.\nA) It specifically does not. C) There is no cap. D) Interest is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Sukanya Samriddhi Account can be opened for:\nA) Any child  B) A girl child below 10 years  C) A boy child  D) Anyone above 18\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a **girl child under 10**, maturing when she turns 21.\nA), C) and D) are outside the scheme's eligibility.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A Masala bond is:\nA) A foreign-currency bond issued in India  B) A rupee-denominated bond issued outside India  C) A commodity bond  D) A government bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Rupee-denominated, issued offshore** — so the **investor**, not the issuer, bears the currency risk.\nA) Reverses it. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which risk arises when an issuer redeems a bond early?\nA) Inflation risk  B) Call risk  C) Liquidity risk  D) Event risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Call risk**: the issuer calls the bond when rates fall, forcing the investor to reinvest at lower rates.\nA), C) and D) are different risks.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Reinvestment risk is the risk that:\nA) Rates rise  B) Coupons must be reinvested at lower rates  C) The issuer defaults  D) The bond is illiquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is specifically the risk that **rates fall**, so intermediate cash flows earn less.\nA) Rising rates hurt the **price** but help reinvestment. C) Is credit risk. D) Is liquidity risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Which of these is a sub-type of credit risk?\nA) Downgrade risk  B) Interest rate risk  C) Inflation risk  D) Call risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Credit risk covers **downgrade risk, spread/basis risk and default risk**.\nB), C) and D) are separate risk categories.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** A normal (upward-sloping) yield curve indicates:\nA) Expected recession  B) Longer maturities yield more, reflecting a term premium  C) Short rates above long rates  D) Flat expectations\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investors demand extra compensation for locking money away longer.\nA) That is signalled by an **inverted** curve. C) Describes inversion. D) Describes a flat curve.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** An inverted yield curve typically signals:\nA) Strong growth ahead  B) Expected rate cuts and possible slowdown  C) Rising inflation  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short yields above long yields imply the market expects **rates to fall**, historically a recession signal.\nA) Is signalled by a steep normal curve. C) Rising inflation usually steepens the curve. D) Inversion is one of the most-watched macro signals.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** For a bond with a higher coupon, all else equal, duration is:\nA) Higher  B) Lower  C) Unchanged  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A higher coupon delivers **more value sooner**, shortening the weighted-average time to receive cash.\nA) Reverses it. C) The coupon materially affects duration. D) Only a zero-price bond would have zero duration.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Which regulator oversees corporate bonds and their listing?\nA) The RBI  B) SEBI  C) IRDAI  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SEBI** covers corporate bonds; the **RBI** covers G-secs and the money market.\nA) Handles government securities. C) and D) have no jurisdiction here.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A debenture trustee's role is to:\nA) Rate the bond  B) Protect bondholders' interests and enforce the security  C) Issue the bond  D) Set the coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A mandatory appointment for public debt issues, acting **for the bondholders**.\nA) Is a credit rating agency. C) Is the issuer. D) Is set at issuance by the issuer and market.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The dirty price of a bond is:\nA) The clean price minus accrued interest  B) The clean price plus accrued interest  C) The face value  D) The coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Between coupon dates the buyer compensates the seller for **interest accrued but unpaid**.\nA) Reverses the sign. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** The Floating Rate Savings Bonds, 2020 pay interest linked to:\nA) The repo rate  B) The NSC rate + 0.35%  C) The Nifty  D) Gold prices\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The coupon **resets half-yearly at NSC + 0.35%**, over a 7-year tenor, and is **fully taxable**.\nA), C) and D) are not the benchmark.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A ₹1,000 face value bond pays an 8% annual coupon, has 5 years to maturity, and the required yield is 10%. What is its price?\nA) ₹1,000  B) ₹924.18  C) ₹1,075.82  D) ₹800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Coupon = ₹80. Annuity factor = [1 − (1.10)⁻⁵] ÷ 0.10 = (1 − 0.62092) ÷ 0.10 = **3.7908**. PV of coupons = 80 × 3.7908 = **₹303.26**. PV of redemption = 1,000 ÷ 1.61051 = **₹620.92**. Price = **₹924.18**.\nA) Would apply only if coupon = yield. C) Is the premium case (coupon above yield). D) Over-discounts. **Coupon (8%) < yield (10%), so a discount is expected ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A ₹1,000 bond pays a 12% annual coupon with 4 years remaining and a required yield of 9%. Will it trade at a premium or discount?\nA) Discount, because 12% > 9%  B) Premium, because the coupon exceeds the required yield  C) At par  D) Cannot say\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Coupon (12%) > yield (9%)** means an above-market income stream, so buyers bid the price **above face value**.\nA) Gets the direction backwards. C) Par applies only when coupon = yield. D) The rule is deterministic.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A zero-coupon bond with ₹1,00,000 face value matures in 6 years. At an 8% yield, what is its price?\nA) ₹63,017  B) ₹1,00,000  C) ₹52,000  D) ₹1,58,687\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — P = F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ = 1,00,000 ÷ (1.08)⁶ = 1,00,000 ÷ **1.58687** = **₹63,017**.\nB) Zero-coupon bonds never trade at par before maturity. C) Over-discounts. D) Is the future value, not the price.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A bond with a ₹1,000 face value and 9% coupon trades at ₹900. What is the current yield?\nA) 9.0%  B) 10.0%  C) 8.1%  D) 11.1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Current yield = annual coupon ÷ market price = ₹90 ÷ ₹900 = **10.0%**.\nA) Is the coupon **rate** (based on face value). C) Divides by the wrong figure. D) Is 1,000 ÷ 900. **Since it trades at a discount, YTM will be higher still than 10%.**\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A bond has modified duration of 5.5. If yields fall by 50 basis points, the price will approximately:\nA) Fall 2.75%  B) Rise 2.75%  C) Rise 5.5%  D) Fall 0.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — % change ≈ −MD × Δy = −5.5 × (−0.005) = **+2.75%**. Falling yields **raise** prices.\nA) Gets the direction wrong. C) Ignores the size of the yield move. D) Ignores duration entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A semi-annual bond has a 10% coupon, 6 years to maturity and a 12% required yield. What inputs go into the pricing formula?\nA) C = 100, y = 12%, n = 6  B) C = 5% of face, y = 6%, n = 12  C) C = 10, y = 12%, n = 12  D) C = 5, y = 12%, n = 6\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Halve the coupon and yield, double the periods**: coupon = 5% of face per half-year, y = 6% per half-year, n = 12 half-years.\nA) Uses annual conventions throughout. C) Halves only the periods. D) Halves only the coupon. **This period conversion is the most common source of error.**\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** An investor holds a bond with modified duration 8.0 and ₹25,00,000 invested. Yields rise 1%. What is the approximate loss?\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — % change ≈ −8.0 × 0.01 = **−8%**. Loss = 8% × ₹25,00,000 = **₹2,00,000**.\nB) Uses 0.1%. C) Applies 8 as a multiple rather than a percentage sensitivity. D) Understates it. **A duration of 8 means a 1% rate move costs 8% of capital — a big number many investors underestimate.**\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which bond has the greatest interest rate sensitivity?\nA) 2-year, 10% coupon  B) 10-year, 10% coupon  C) 10-year zero-coupon  D) 2-year zero-coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Longest maturity plus no coupon** gives the highest duration. A 10-year zero has a Macaulay duration of exactly **10 years**, higher than a 10-year coupon bond.\nA) and D) are short-dated. B) Has coupons that shorten its duration below 10. **Longer maturity ↑ duration; higher coupon ↓ duration.**\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** An investor expects the RBI to cut rates sharply. How should she position her bond portfolio?\nA) Shorten duration  B) Lengthen duration to maximise price gains  C) Hold only T-bills  D) Sell all bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Falling yields raise prices, and **longer duration amplifies the gain**.\nA) Would minimise the benefit. C) T-bills have almost no duration, so they gain least. D) Selling forgoes the anticipated gain entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A ₹1,000 bond with a 7% coupon trades at ₹1,050. What is the relationship between coupon, current yield and YTM?\nA) Coupon < Current yield < YTM  B) Coupon > Current yield > YTM  C) All equal  D) YTM > Coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It trades at a **premium** (₹1,050 > ₹1,000), so the ordering reverses: **coupon 7% > current yield (70 ÷ 1,050 = 6.67%) > YTM**.\nA) Is the discount ordering. C) Applies only at par. D) A premium bond's YTM is below its coupon, because of the capital loss at redemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A perpetual bond pays ₹90 a year. At a required yield of 12%, what is its price?\nA) ₹750  B) ₹1,080  C) ₹90  D) ₹1,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — P = C ÷ y = 90 ÷ 0.12 = **₹750**.\nB) Multiplies instead of dividing. C) Is one year's coupon. D) Assumes par regardless of yield.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A client asks why her \"safe\" bond fund lost money last quarter. The most likely explanation is:\nA) The issuers all defaulted  B) Interest rates rose, so bond prices fell — mark-to-market losses, not defaults  C) The fund was fraudulent  D) Bonds cannot lose money\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Interest rate risk** is the dominant risk for high-quality bond funds; rising rates reduce NAV even with no credit event.\nA) Widespread default is rare and would be reported. C) An unwarranted conclusion. D) A common and costly misconception.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Which instrument would a corporate treasurer use to borrow for 90 days?\nA) A 10-year debenture  B) Commercial paper  C) Equity  D) A perpetual bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **CP** is the standard short-term corporate borrowing instrument (7 days–1 year).\nA) and D) are long-term or perpetual. C) Equity is permanent capital, not borrowing.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A ₹1,00,000 T-bill with 91 days to maturity is bought at ₹98,200. What is the return?\nA) ₹1,800, being the discount realised at redemption  B) A 9% coupon  C) Nothing  D) ₹98,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — T-bills are **zero-coupon**: the return is the **difference between purchase price and face value**, here ₹1,00,000 − ₹98,200 = **₹1,800**.\nB) T-bills pay no coupon. C) The discount is the return. D) Is the purchase price.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** An investor in the 30% tax bracket compares PPF at 7.1% with a bank FD at 7.5%. Which gives the better post-tax return?\nA) The FD, since 7.5% > 7.1%  B) PPF — it is EEE and therefore tax-free, while the FD's post-tax return is about 5.25%  C) They are identical  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PPF's **EEE** status means 7.1% is received in full. The FD's post-tax return = 7.5% × (1 − 0.30) = **5.25%**. PPF wins by nearly 2 percentage points.\nA) Compares pre-tax with tax-free — the classic error. C) They differ substantially. D) PPF is clearly better here. **Always compare post-tax returns.**\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which of these bonds carries no credit risk in domestic currency?\nA) A AAA corporate bond  B) A central government security  C) An AT-1 bank bond  D) A company deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A sovereign borrowing in its **own currency** is effectively free of default risk.\nA) AAA is the highest **corporate** rating, not risk-free. C) AT-1 bonds carry write-down risk. D) Company deposits carry the company's credit risk and are uninsured.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A callable bond is called by the issuer. When is this most likely, and why is it bad for the investor?\nA) When rates rise; the investor gains  B) When rates fall — the issuer refinances cheaply, and the investor must reinvest at lower rates  C) At random  D) When the issuer defaults\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Issuers call when they can **refinance more cheaply**, which is exactly when the investor's reinvestment options are worst.\nA) Calling is unattractive to the issuer when rates rise. C) Calls are economically driven. D) A call is a repayment, not a default.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** The yield curve inverts. A bond investor should interpret this as:\nA) Strong growth expected  B) The market expecting rate cuts and possibly a slowdown  C) Rising credit risk  D) Nothing meaningful\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short yields exceeding long yields imply expected **rate reductions**.\nA) Would produce a steep normal curve. C) The curve reflects rates, not credit (though spreads may widen separately). D) Inversion is a closely watched signal.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** An investor buys a bond between coupon dates. What must she pay in addition to the clean price?\nA) Nothing  B) Accrued interest, giving the dirty price  C) The full next coupon  D) A penalty\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — She compensates the seller for interest earned since the last coupon date: **dirty = clean + accrued**.\nA) The seller is entitled to interest earned. C) She pays only the accrued portion, not the whole coupon. D) There is no penalty.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A ₹1,000 bond with a 10% coupon has 3 years remaining and a required yield of 10%. What is its price?\nA) ₹1,000  B) ₹1,100  C) ₹900  D) ₹1,300\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — When **coupon = required yield**, the bond trades **exactly at par** — no computation needed.\nB) Would be a premium. C) Would be a discount. D) Is the undiscounted total of coupons plus principal. **Recognising the coupon = yield shortcut saves time in the exam.**\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which small savings scheme allows the largest single deposit?\nA) PPF (₹1.5 lakh)  B) SCSS (₹30 lakh)  C) Sukanya Samriddhi (₹1.5 lakh)  D) NSC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SCSS permits up to **₹30 lakh**, far above PPF's and SSA's ₹1.5 lakh annual limits.\nA) and C) share the ₹1.5 lakh cap. D) NSC has no cap for investment but no comparable single-scheme limit is specified; SCSS's ₹30 lakh is the prescribed maximum among these.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A retiree wants regular quarterly income with government backing. The most suitable scheme is:\nA) PPF  B) SCSS  C) KVP  D) Sukanya Samriddhi\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SCSS** is designed for the 60+ cohort and pays **quarterly** interest, up to ₹30 lakh.\nA) PPF locks money for 15 years with no interim income. C) KVP pays only at maturity. D) SSA is for a girl child.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Two bonds are identical except that Bond A matures in 3 years and Bond B in 15 years. Rates rise 1%. Which falls more?\nA) Bond A  B) Bond B, because longer maturity means higher duration  C) Both equally  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Longer maturity → higher duration → greater price sensitivity.**\nA) The short bond is far less sensitive. C) Duration differs sharply. D) Both fall, but by very different amounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A corporate deposit pays 2.5% more than a bank FD. What is the trade-off?\nA) There is none  B) Company deposits are not DICGC-insured, so the extra yield compensates for the company's credit risk  C) The company deposit is safer  D) The FD is riskier\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **No deposit insurance** and the issuer's own credit risk explain the premium.\nA) The difference in safety is material. C) and D) reverse the risk ranking.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which statement about NSC is correct?\nA) Interest is paid annually in cash  B) Interest is compounded annually but paid at maturity, and reinvested interest also qualifies for 80C  C) It is EEE  D) It has a 15-year tenor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This dual feature — deemed reinvestment qualifying for 80C — is NSC's distinguishing characteristic.\nA) No annual payout occurs. C) The interest is **taxable**, so it is not EEE. D) NSC runs **5 years**.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** An AT-1 perpetual bond issued by a bank differs from an ordinary bond because:\nA) It matures in 5 years  B) It has no maturity and carries write-down risk — it can be written off if the bank is in trouble  C) It is government-guaranteed  D) It is risk-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — AT-1 instruments are **loss-absorbing bank capital**, far riskier than the steady coupon suggests.\nA) They are perpetual. C) and D) There is no guarantee and substantial risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which of these is the biggest risk for a portfolio of AAA-rated government bonds?\nA) Default risk  B) Interest rate risk  C) Credit risk  D) Downgrade risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With credit risk essentially absent, **interest rate risk dominates** for high-quality bonds.\nA), C) and D) are all negligible for domestic government securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** In a repo transaction, the borrower:\nA) Sells securities with an agreement to repurchase them, effectively borrowing against collateral  B) Gifts securities  C) Issues equity  D) Defaults\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A repo is **collateralised borrowing** dressed as a sale-and-repurchase.\nB), C) and D) do not describe a repo.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A bond's YTM is 9% but the investor can only reinvest coupons at 6%. What is the consequence?\nA) The realised return will exceed 9%  B) The realised return will be below the 9% YTM — this is reinvestment risk  C) No effect  D) The bond defaults\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — YTM **assumes reinvestment at the YTM itself**. If actual reinvestment rates are lower, the realised return falls short.\nA) Lower reinvestment rates reduce, not raise, the outcome. C) It has a direct effect on the realised return. D) Reinvestment risk is unrelated to default.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which of these bonds would have a Macaulay duration exactly equal to 7?\nA) A 7-year, 8% coupon bond  B) A 7-year zero-coupon bond  C) A 10-year, 5% coupon bond  D) A perpetual bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Only a **zero-coupon bond** has duration equal to its maturity, because the sole cash flow arrives at the end.\nA) Its coupons pull duration **below** 7. C) A 10-year bond with coupons has a duration below 10 but not necessarily 7. D) Perpetual bonds have a duration derived from the yield, not a maturity.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Under market segmentation theory, the yield curve's shape is determined by:\nA) Expected future short rates  B) Supply and demand within each maturity segment, as different investors prefer different maturities  C) A liquidity premium alone  D) Government decree\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Insurers want long maturities, banks want short ones; each segment clears independently.\nA) Is expectations theory. C) Is liquidity preference theory. D) Rates are market-determined.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A ₹5,00,000 investment in a bond fund with modified duration 3.2 experiences a 150 bp rate rise. What is the approximate loss?\nA) ₹24,000  B) ₹16,000  C) ₹7,500  D) ₹1,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — % change ≈ −3.2 × 0.015 = **−4.8%**. Loss = 4.8% × ₹5,00,000 = **₹24,000**.\nB) Uses 1%. C) Ignores duration. D) Is ten times too large. **A short-duration fund limits the damage — the reason liquid and short-duration funds are used for near-term goals.**\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which combination correctly describes a Sovereign Gold Bond?\nA) A corporate bond linked to gold  B) A government security linked to the gold price, paying an additional fixed interest rate  C) Physical gold  D) A gold ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SGBs give **gold price exposure plus a fixed interest coupon**, with sovereign backing.\nA) They are government-issued. C) There is no physical delivery during the term. D) An ETF is a mutual fund product without a coupon.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Convexity means that:\nA) Price changes are linear in yield  B) Prices rise more when yields fall than they fall when yields rise by the same amount — which benefits the bondholder  C) Duration is always zero  D) Bonds have no risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The price–yield curve is **curved**, and positive convexity is favourable to the holder.\nA) Duration alone assumes linearity — convexity is the correction. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** An investor wants to lock in a known sum on a known future date with no reinvestment risk. Which bond suits best?\nA) A high-coupon bond  B) A zero-coupon bond maturing on that date  C) A perpetual bond  D) A floating rate bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **no intermediate cash flows**, a zero-coupon bond has **no reinvestment risk** and delivers a certain amount on a certain date.\nA) Its coupons must be reinvested at unknown rates. C) It never matures. D) Its coupon varies with the benchmark.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A ₹1,000 bond pays a 9% annual coupon with 6 years to maturity. The required yield is 7%. What is the price?\nA) ₹1,095.33  B) ₹1,000  C) ₹904.67  D) ₹1,540\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Coupon = ₹90. (1.07)⁶ = **1.50073**, so (1.07)⁻⁶ = **0.66634**. Annuity factor = (1 − 0.66634) ÷ 0.07 = 0.33366 ÷ 0.07 = **4.7665**. PV of coupons = 90 × 4.7665 = **₹428.99**. PV of principal = 1,000 × 0.66634 = **₹666.34**. **Price = ₹1,095.33**.\nB) Would need coupon = yield. C) Is the discount case, wrong direction. D) Is the undiscounted total. **Coupon (9%) > yield (7%) → premium ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Using Q71's bond, what is the current yield, and how does it compare with the coupon rate and YTM?\nA) 8.22%; coupon 9% > current yield 8.22% > YTM 7%  B) 9%; all equal  C) 8.22%; YTM is highest  D) 7%; coupon is lowest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Current yield = 90 ÷ 1,095.33 = **8.22%**. It is a **premium** bond, so the ordering is **coupon (9%) > current yield (8.22%) > YTM (7%)**.\nB) They differ because the price is above par. C) YTM is **lowest** for a premium bond, because of the capital loss at redemption. D) Misassigns the values.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A ₹1,000 bond pays a 10% coupon **semi-annually**, with 4 years to maturity and a required yield of 8%. What is the price?\nA) ₹1,067.33  B) ₹1,000  C) ₹934  D) ₹1,400\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Convert to half-years: **C = ₹50**, **y = 4%**, **n = 8**. (1.04)⁸ = **1.36857**, inverse = **0.73069**. Annuity factor = (1 − 0.73069) ÷ 0.04 = 0.26931 ÷ 0.04 = **6.7327**. PV of coupons = 50 × 6.7327 = **₹336.64**. PV of principal = 1,000 × 0.73069 = **₹730.69**. **Price = ₹1,067.33**.\nB) Requires coupon = yield. C) Wrong direction — coupon (10%) exceeds yield (8%), so a premium. D) Undiscounted total.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A bond has Macaulay duration 7.5 years and a yield of 8%. What is its modified duration, and the approximate price change if yields rise 1.2%?\nA) MD 6.94; price falls about 8.33%  B) MD 8.10; price falls 9.72%  C) MD 7.50; price falls 9.0%  D) MD 6.94; price rises 8.33%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **MD = 7.5 ÷ 1.08 = 6.944**. % change ≈ −6.944 × 0.012 = **−8.33%**.\nB) Multiplies by (1+y) instead of dividing. C) Fails to convert Macaulay into modified duration. D) Gets the direction wrong — rising yields **lower** prices.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Two bonds both yield 8%: Bond X is a 10-year zero-coupon; Bond Y is a 10-year 12% coupon bond. Which has higher duration and by roughly how much?\nA) Bond Y, because of its high coupon  B) Bond X, with duration exactly 10 years versus Bond Y's roughly 6.5–7 years  C) They are equal at 10  D) Bond X at 5 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration equals its maturity**, so Bond X is exactly **10**. Bond Y's large coupons return value early, pulling its duration well below 10 — typically around **6.5–7 years** at these parameters.\nA) A **higher coupon reduces** duration. C) The coupon bond's duration is necessarily below its maturity. D) Bond X's duration is 10, not 5. **Bond X is therefore substantially more rate-sensitive.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** An investor holds ₹40,00,000 in a bond fund with modified duration 6.5. She expects a 100 bp rate cut. What is her expected gain, and what is the main risk to this view?\nA) Gain about ₹2,60,000; the risk is that rates rise instead, producing an equal loss  B) Gain ₹40,000; no risk  C) Loss ₹2,60,000  D) Gain ₹6,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — % change ≈ −6.5 × (−0.01) = **+6.5%**. Gain = 6.5% × ₹40,00,000 = **₹2,60,000**. The symmetry is the risk: if rates instead **rise** 100 bp, she loses roughly the same amount (slightly less, thanks to positive convexity).\nB) Uses the wrong scale. C) Wrong direction for a rate cut. D) Overstates by a factor of 2.5. **Duration is a two-way street — it is a measure of risk, not of expected return.**\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A ₹1,00,000 face value T-bill with 182 days to maturity is purchased at ₹96,500. What is the annualised return (simple, 365-day basis)?\nA) 3.63%  B) 7.28%  C) 3.50%  D) 14.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Discount = 1,00,000 − 96,500 = **₹3,500**. Return over 182 days = 3,500 ÷ 96,500 = **3.627%**. Annualised = 3.627% × (365 ÷ 182) = 3.627% × 2.005 = **7.27%**.\nA) Is the un-annualised 182-day return. C) Divides by face value rather than price. D) Roughly doubles the correct figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A client says \"I'll hold my bond to maturity, so interest rate risk doesn't affect me.\" Evaluate.\nA) Completely correct  B) Partly right — she avoids realising the mark-to-market loss, but she still bears opportunity cost and reinvestment risk, and the point fails if she needs to sell early  C) Completely wrong  D) Interest rate risk does not exist\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Holding to maturity does secure the **contracted cash flows**, so no capital loss is realised. But she is **locked into a below-market yield** (opportunity cost), her **coupons are reinvested at whatever rates prevail**, and any need to sell early crystallises the loss.\nA) It overlooks opportunity and reinvestment effects. C) Her core point about not realising a loss is valid. D) It certainly exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A bond portfolio manager expects rates to rise. Which set of actions is consistent?\nA) Buy long-dated zero-coupon bonds  B) Shorten duration — shift into shorter maturities, higher coupons, floating rate bonds and liquid funds  C) Buy perpetual bonds  D) Increase duration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Every element **reduces duration**: shorter maturity, higher coupon, floating coupons that reset upward, and near-zero-duration liquid instruments.\nA) Long zeros have the **highest** duration — maximum damage. C) Perpetuals are highly rate-sensitive. D) The opposite of what the view implies.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A ₹1,000 bond with an 11% coupon and 5 years remaining trades at ₹1,080. Roughly what is its YTM, and how do you know it is below the coupon rate?\nA) About 9.0%; the premium price implies YTM < coupon  B) About 12%; YTM > coupon  C) Exactly 11%  D) About 6%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A price **above par** means the market's required yield is **below** the coupon rate. Approximating: annual coupon ₹110, plus an amortised capital loss of (1,000 − 1,080) ÷ 5 = −₹16 a year, over an average price of about ₹1,040 → (110 − 16) ÷ 1,040 ≈ **9.0%**.\nB) YTM above the coupon would imply a discount price. C) Would require the price to be exactly par. D) Too low for these parameters. **The shortcut: premium ⇒ YTM < current yield < coupon.**\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A 15-year bond has modified duration 9.8 and convexity that adds roughly 0.6% to the price when yields move 1%. If yields fall 1%, what is the total estimated price change?\nA) +9.8%  B) About +10.4% (duration effect +9.8% plus convexity +0.6%)  C) +9.2%  D) −10.4%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Duration effect** = −9.8 × (−0.01) = **+9.8%**. **Convexity** adds a further **+0.6%**, giving about **+10.4%**.\nA) Ignores convexity. C) Subtracts convexity — but positive convexity always **adds** to the gain when yields fall (and **cushions** the loss when yields rise). D) Wrong direction.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An investor compares: PPF at 7.1% (EEE), SCSS at 8.2% (taxable), and an FD at 7.6% (taxable). She is in the 30% bracket. Rank them by post-tax return.\nA) SCSS > FD > PPF  B) PPF 7.10% > SCSS 5.74% > FD 5.32%  C) FD > PPF > SCSS  D) All equal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **PPF** is EEE, so **7.10%** is received in full. **SCSS** post-tax = 8.2% × 0.70 = **5.74%**. **FD** post-tax = 7.6% × 0.70 = **5.32%**.\nA) Ranks on pre-tax figures. C) Miscomputes. D) They differ by nearly 2 percentage points. **The headline-rate ranking (SCSS > FD > PPF) completely inverts once tax is applied — the central lesson of this comparison.** *(SCSS still has a role: it provides quarterly income, which PPF does not.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Which sequence correctly ranks these by interest rate sensitivity, highest first?\nA) 91-day T-bill, 5-year 10% bond, 20-year zero-coupon  B) 20-year zero-coupon, 5-year 10% bond, 91-day T-bill  C) 5-year bond, T-bill, 20-year zero  D) All equal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sensitivity tracks **duration**: the **20-year zero** has duration 20 (highest), the **5-year coupon bond** roughly 4, and the **91-day T-bill** about 0.25 (lowest).\nA) Exactly inverts the ranking. C) Scrambles it. D) They differ by roughly eighty-fold between the extremes.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A corporate bond yielding 10.5% is downgraded from AA to BBB. What happens, and why does the effect exceed the pure default-probability change?\nA) The price rises  B) The price falls as the credit spread widens; the effect is amplified because mandate-constrained institutional investors may be forced to sell  C) Nothing changes  D) The coupon increases\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A downgrade widens the **credit spread**, raising the required yield and cutting the price. **Forced selling** by investors whose mandates restrict lower-rated holdings amplifies the move beyond what the default-probability change alone implies.\nA) Downgrades lower prices. C) The effect is material. D) The coupon is contractually fixed.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** An investor buys a ₹1,000 bond with a 12% semi-annual coupon at par, planning to hold 5 years. Rates then fall to 8%. What are the two opposing effects?\nA) Both effects are negative  B) The bond's price rises (favourable if she sells), but coupons must be reinvested at 8% rather than 12% (unfavourable if she holds)  C) Both effects are positive  D) No effect\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the **duration trade-off** made concrete: falling rates raise the **price** but worsen **reinvestment**.\nA) The price effect is positive. C) The reinvestment effect is negative. D) Both effects are real. **Duration is precisely the horizon at which these two effects offset.**\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A ₹1,000 zero-coupon bond maturing in 8 years is priced at ₹540. What is its yield?\nA) 7.98%  B) 8.53%  C) 5.40%  D) 12.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — y = (F ÷ P)^(1/n) − 1 = (1,000 ÷ 540)^(1/8) − 1 = (1.85185)^0.125 − 1. ln(1.85185) = 0.61619; ÷ 8 = 0.077024; e^0.077024 = **1.08007**. Yield = **8.01%**, closest to **7.98%**.\nB) Slightly overstates. C) Is the price as a fraction. D) Far too high. **Note this is exactly the CAGR formula from Chapter 2.**\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Which statement about the money market is INCORRECT?\nA) Maturities are up to one year  B) It is regulated by the RBI  C) Commercial paper is secured by collateral  D) T-bills are zero-coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Commercial paper is UNSECURED** — that is its defining feature, which is why it requires a credit rating.\nA), B) and D) are all correct statements about the money market.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client holds ₹50 lakh across three banks: ₹20 lakh in Bank A, ₹20 lakh in Bank B, ₹10 lakh in Bank C. How much is DICGC-insured?\nA) ₹5 lakh in total  B) ₹15 lakh — ₹5 lakh per depositor per bank, across three banks  C) ₹50 lakh  D) ₹10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cover is **₹5 lakh per depositor per bank**, so ₹5 lakh × 3 banks = **₹15 lakh** insured; the remaining ₹35 lakh is uninsured.\nA) Treats the cover as a single overall cap. C) Far exceeds the cover. D) Counts only one bank. **Spreading deposits across banks is the practical way to extend insured cover.**\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A 10-year bond and a 10-year zero-coupon bond both yield 8%. Rates rise to 9%. Which loses more, and roughly by how much?\nA) The coupon bond, about 12%  B) The zero-coupon bond, roughly 8.5–9% versus about 6.5–7% for the coupon bond  C) They lose equally  D) Neither loses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **zero** has Macaulay duration 10, so modified duration ≈ 10 ÷ 1.08 = **9.26**; a 1% rise costs roughly **9.3%**. The coupon bond's duration is around 7, so modified duration ≈ 6.5, costing about **6.5%**.\nA) The coupon bond loses **less**. C) Their durations differ substantially. D) Both fall when yields rise.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An investor wants to fund a child's education costing ₹20,00,000 in exactly 7 years, with certainty. Which is the most precise instrument?\nA) An equity fund  B) A zero-coupon bond (or G-sec STRIP) maturing in 7 years, sized to redeem at ₹20,00,000 — no reinvestment risk and no price risk if held to maturity  C) A perpetual bond  D) A liquid fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **zero-coupon instrument matching the goal date** eliminates **reinvestment risk** (no interim cash flows) and, if held to maturity, delivers a **known amount on a known date** — the textbook immunisation solution.\nA) Equity cannot guarantee a specific sum on a specific date. C) A perpetual bond never matures. D) A liquid fund's returns over 7 years are unknown and would need continual reinvestment.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A bond fund's NAV falls 7% in a quarter with no defaults in the portfolio. Its modified duration is 7.2. What most likely happened?\nA) The fund manager committed fraud  B) Yields rose by roughly 1% (7% ÷ 7.2 ≈ 0.97%), producing mark-to-market losses  C) The issuers defaulted  D) The fund charged high fees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Working backwards: Δy ≈ −(% change) ÷ MD = 7% ÷ 7.2 ≈ **0.97%**. A roughly **1% rise in yields** fully explains the fall.\nA) An unwarranted conclusion where the arithmetic explains it. C) The question states there were no defaults. D) Fees of that magnitude in one quarter are implausible.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Which bond would you expect to have the LOWEST yield, and why?\nA) A BBB corporate bond  B) A central government security, because it carries no domestic credit risk  C) An AT-1 bank bond  D) A high-yield corporate bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Yield compensates for risk. With **no credit risk**, the sovereign pays least; everything else trades at a **spread over** it.\nA), C) and D) all carry credit risk — and AT-1 additionally carries write-down risk — so all yield more.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A ₹1,000 bond with a 6% coupon and 4 years remaining trades at ₹930. Calculate the approximate YTM.\nA) 6.00%  B) About 8.06%  C) 6.45%  D) 10.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Approximate YTM = [coupon + (F − P) ÷ n] ÷ [(F + P) ÷ 2] = [60 + (1,000 − 930) ÷ 4] ÷ [(1,000 + 930) ÷ 2] = [60 + 17.5] ÷ 965 = 77.5 ÷ 965 = **8.03%**.\nA) Is the coupon rate. C) Is the current yield (60 ÷ 930 = 6.45%). D) Far too high. **Ordering check for a discount bond: coupon 6% < current yield 6.45% < YTM 8.03% ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** An investor is told a perpetual AT-1 bond is \"just like an FD but with a better rate\". Identify the three errors.\nA) There are none  B) It has no maturity, it carries write-down risk (it can be extinguished if the bank is in trouble), and it has no deposit insurance — none of which applies to an FD  C) Only the rate differs  D) AT-1 bonds are safer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Three material differences: **no maturity** (you cannot simply wait to get your money back), **write-down risk** (the principal can be wiped out while the bank continues operating), and **no DICGC insurance**.\nA) The differences are fundamental. C) The risk profile differs enormously. D) AT-1 bonds are substantially **riskier** than deposits.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A yield curve steepens sharply (long yields rise much more than short yields). Which portfolio suffers most?\nA) A portfolio of 91-day T-bills  B) A portfolio of 20-year government bonds, because long duration meets the largest yield rise  C) A liquid fund  D) A floating rate bond portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The damage is **duration × yield change**, and long bonds have **both** the highest duration **and** the biggest yield move in a steepening.\nA) and C) have almost no duration. D) Floating coupons reset upward, largely insulating the price.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Which statement about the relationship between YTM and realised return is correct?\nA) They are always identical  B) They coincide only if all coupons are reinvested at the YTM and the bond is held to maturity — otherwise the realised return differs  C) YTM is always higher  D) Realised return is always higher\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — YTM is a **promised** yield conditional on two assumptions; the realised return depends on actual reinvestment rates and the actual holding period.\nA) They coincide only under those specific conditions. C) and D) Either can exceed the other depending on the rate path.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A company deposit offers 9.5% while a bank FD offers 7.2% and a AAA corporate bond yields 8.1%. What does the ordering tell you?\nA) The company deposit is the best investment  B) The yield ladder reflects a risk ladder — the uninsured company deposit carries the most credit risk, the AAA bond less, the insured bank deposit least  C) All three are identical in risk  D) The FD is riskiest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Yield is the price of risk.** The 2.3-point gap between the company deposit and the FD is compensation for the absence of insurance and the company's own default risk.\nA) Highest yield ≠ best investment; it must be judged against the risk taken and the client's need. C) Their risk profiles differ substantially. D) The insured bank deposit is the safest of the three.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** An investor holds a bond portfolio with duration 5 and a goal exactly 5 years away. What is the significance?\nA) Nothing  B) The portfolio is approximately immunised — the price effect and reinvestment effect of a rate change roughly offset over that horizon  C) She should sell immediately  D) Duration is irrelevant to goals\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Immunisation**: when duration equals the investment horizon, a rate rise's **capital loss** is roughly offset by **higher reinvestment income**, and vice versa — so the terminal value is largely protected.\nA) It is a deliberate and powerful technique. C) The alignment is exactly what she wants. D) Matching duration to horizon is one of duration's main practical uses.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Which combination of features would produce the **highest** duration?\nA) Short maturity, high coupon, high yield  B) Long maturity, zero coupon, low yield  C) Short maturity, zero coupon  D) Long maturity, high coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three factors push duration **up**: **long maturity** (↑), **zero coupon** (↑, since no value returns early), and **low yield** (↑).\nA) All three push duration down. C) Short maturity caps it. D) The high coupon pulls duration down.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single framework best organises fixed income analysis?\nA) Buy the highest yield available  B) Identify the cash flows and discount them (price), compare the coupon with the required yield (premium/par/discount), measure rate sensitivity (duration), and assess who might not pay (credit)  C) Ignore duration  D) Only buy government bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Four questions cover essentially every bond problem: **what are the cash flows, what is it worth, how sensitive is it to rates, and how likely is repayment?**\nA) The highest yield reflects the highest risk. C) Duration is the central risk measure. D) Government bonds eliminate credit risk but not interest rate risk, and forgo the credit spread entirely.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Convert to per-period figures before pricing.\n\n### Case: Mr Bhatia's Fixed Income Decision\n\nMr Bhatia, 58, is restructuring ₹60,00,000 of his portfolio into fixed income ahead of retirement at 60. He is considering:\n\n- **Bond P:** a ₹1,000 face value corporate bond, **10% annual coupon**, **5 years** to maturity, rated AA, currently priced at **₹1,079**\n- **Bond Q:** a **10-year zero-coupon** government security, ₹1,000 face value, priced at **₹508**\n- **SCSS:** at **8.2%**, paid quarterly, taxable\n- **PPF:** at **7.1%**, EEE (he already has an account opened 12 years ago)\n\nMr Bhatia is in the **30%** tax bracket. He expects to need **regular income** from age 60 and believes **interest rates will fall** over the next two years.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is Bond P's current yield, and how does it rank against its coupon rate and YTM?\nA) 9.27%; coupon 10% > current yield 9.27% > YTM  B) 10%; all equal  C) 9.27%; YTM is highest  D) 8.0%; coupon is lowest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Current yield = annual coupon ÷ price = ₹100 ÷ ₹1,079 = **9.27%**. Bond P trades at a **premium** (₹1,079 > ₹1,000), so the ordering is **coupon (10%) > current yield (9.27%) > YTM**.\nB) They differ because the price is above par. C) For a **premium** bond, YTM is the **lowest** of the three — the buyer suffers a capital loss of ₹79 at redemption. D) Misassigns the figures.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What yield does Bond Q offer?\nA) 5.08%  B) About 7.02%  C) 10.0%  D) 49.2%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a zero-coupon bond, y = (F ÷ P)^(1/n) − 1 = (1,000 ÷ 508)^(1/10) − 1 = (1.96850)^0.1 − 1. ln(1.96850) = 0.67717; ÷ 10 = 0.067717; e^0.067717 = **1.07006**. **Yield ≈ 7.01%**.\nA) Is the price expressed as a fraction. C) Is Bond P's coupon rate. D) Is the total gain over ten years, not the annual rate. *(Same formula as CAGR in Chapter 2.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Given Mr Bhatia's expectation that rates will fall, which instrument benefits MOST, and why?\nA) SCSS, because it pays the highest rate  B) Bond Q — a 10-year zero-coupon bond has Macaulay duration of exactly 10, the highest of the options, so its price gains most when yields fall  C) PPF, because it is EEE  D) Bond P, because of its high coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **zero-coupon bond's duration equals its maturity**, so Bond Q's duration is **10** — far above Bond P's (roughly 4.2, since its coupons return value early). With **% price change ≈ −MD × Δy**, Bond Q gains most from falling yields.\nA) SCSS pays a **fixed** administered rate; its value does not rise when market yields fall. C) PPF's rate is administered, not market-priced. D) Bond P's **high coupon shortens** its duration, reducing the gain.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Comparing SCSS at 8.2% (taxable) with PPF at 7.1% (EEE) for a 30% taxpayer, which delivers the higher post-tax return — and what is the countervailing consideration?\nA) SCSS, at 8.2%  B) PPF at 7.10% beats SCSS's post-tax 5.74% — but SCSS pays quarterly income, which Mr Bhatia needs from 60, while PPF pays nothing until maturity  C) They are equal  D) Neither is suitable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SCSS post-tax** = 8.2% × (1 − 0.30) = **5.74%**. **PPF** is EEE, so its **7.10%** is received in full — a **1.36 percentage point** advantage.\nBut return is not the only criterion: Mr Bhatia needs **regular income from 60**, and **SCSS pays quarterly** while **PPF pays nothing until maturity**. Note also that his PPF account, opened 12 years ago, matures in about 3 years — conveniently close to his retirement.\nA) Ranks on the pre-tax headline. C) They differ materially. D) Both have a legitimate role.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** What is the most appropriate overall structure for Mr Bhatia?\nA) Put all ₹60,00,000 into Bond Q to maximise the rate-fall gain  B) Blend: SCSS (up to ₹30 lakh) for quarterly income from 60, continue PPF for tax-free accumulation, and hold a moderate allocation to Bond P/Q for the rate view — while recognising that a single AA corporate bond concentrates credit risk  C) Put everything into SCSS  D) Hold only cash\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Three objectives must be served at once. **Income:** SCSS is purpose-built, capped at **₹30 lakh**, paying **quarterly**. **Tax efficiency:** PPF's EEE status makes it the best post-tax rate available to him, and it matures near his retirement. **The rate view:** some duration exposure captures a rate fall — but it is a *view*, not a certainty, and Bond Q's duration of 10 cuts both ways.\n\nTwo risk points a good adviser would raise: **Bond P is a single AA corporate issuer**, so concentrating there means undiversified credit risk (a fund would spread it); and Mr Bhatia is **2 years from retirement**, so the portion funding near-term income should not sit in a 10-year duration instrument.\n\nA) Concentrating everything in a 10-year zero at age 58 is a large, undiversified bet on one macro view. C) SCSS is capped at ₹30 lakh and would leave half the corpus unallocated. D) Cash guarantees a negative real return.\n\n> ⚠️ **The lesson:** the highest headline rate (SCSS at 8.2%) is not the highest post-tax return (PPF at 7.1%), and the instrument that gains most from his rate view (Bond Q) is the least suited to his income need. **Match each rupee to the job it must do.**\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "10", "title": "Understanding Derivatives", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "# Chapter 10: Understanding Derivatives — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 3 · Investment Products (30 marks).**\n> Conceptually the trickiest chapter, but the exam questions are mostly **definitional and payoff-based**. Master the **four rights-and-obligations combinations** for options and the **forward vs futures** table, and most questions answer themselves.\n\n---\n\n## 10.1 Basics of Derivatives\n\n**A derivative is a contract whose value is DERIVED from an underlying asset.**\n\nThe derivative has no independent value — it is a claim whose worth depends entirely on what happens to something else.\n\n**Underlyings can be:** equity shares, indices, interest rates, currencies, commodities, bonds — even weather or credit events.\n\n### Why derivatives exist\n1. **Risk transfer** — those who don't want a risk can pass it to those willing to bear it.\n2. **Price discovery** — futures prices reveal market expectations.\n3. **Leverage** — a large exposure for a small outlay (margin).\n4. **Lower transaction costs** than trading the underlying.\n5. **Market completion** — payoffs otherwise unavailable.\n\n---\n\n## 10.2 Underlying Concepts\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Underlying** | The asset from which value is derived |\n| **Spot price** | Today's price of the underlying |\n| **Futures/forward price** | The agreed price for a future transaction |\n| **Contract size / lot** | The standardised quantity per contract |\n| **Expiry** | The date the contract ends (Indian equity derivatives: monthly, on a specified weekday) |\n| **Margin** | Collateral deposited to cover potential loss |\n| **Mark to market (MTM)** | Daily settlement of gains/losses to margin accounts |\n| **Open interest** | Total number of contracts outstanding — a gauge of participation |\n| **Basis** | **Spot price − Futures price** |\n| **Cost of carry** | Interest, storage and insurance costs less income (e.g. dividends) from holding the underlying |\n\n### The cost-of-carry relationship\n\n$$\\boxed{F = S \\times e^{(r-q)T}} \\quad \\text{or approximately} \\quad F = S \\times (1 + r - q)^T$$\n\nwhere **S** = spot, **r** = risk-free rate, **q** = income yield (dividends), **T** = time.\n\n> 🧠 **Normally the futures price exceeds spot** (called **contango**) because holding the asset costs interest. When the futures price is **below** spot, the market is in **backwardation**. **At expiry, the futures price converges to the spot price** — this convergence is guaranteed by arbitrage.\n\n---\n\n## 10.3 ⭐ Types of Derivative Products\n\n### 10.3.1 Forwards\nA **customised, bilateral (OTC)** agreement to buy or sell an asset at a set price on a future date.\n\n- ✅ Fully customisable in size, date and terms\n- ❌ **Counterparty risk** — no guarantee the other side performs\n- ❌ Illiquid; hard to exit before maturity\n- Settlement occurs **only at maturity**\n\n### 10.3.2 Futures\nA **standardised, exchange-traded** forward.\n\n| | **Forward** | **Futures** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Traded** | OTC, bilateral | **Exchange** |\n| **Terms** | Customised | **Standardised** (lot size, expiry) |\n| **Counterparty risk** | **High** — rests with the other party | **Eliminated** — the clearing corporation is the CCP |\n| **Margin** | Usually none | **Mandatory** (initial + MTM) |\n| **Settlement** | At maturity only | **Daily mark to market** |\n| **Liquidity** | Low | High |\n| **Regulation** | Light | Fully regulated |\n\n> ⚠️ **The single biggest difference: futures are guaranteed by the clearing corporation and settled daily; forwards are not.**\n\n### 10.3.3 ⭐ Options\n\n**An option gives the BUYER a RIGHT without an OBLIGATION. The SELLER (writer) has an OBLIGATION without a right.**\n\nThat asymmetry is the whole concept — and the source of most exam questions.\n\n| | **Call option** | **Put option** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Buyer has the right to** | **BUY** the underlying | **SELL** the underlying |\n| **Buyer's view** | **Bullish** | **Bearish** |\n| **Buyer pays** | Premium | Premium |\n| **Buyer's maximum loss** | **The premium** | **The premium** |\n| **Buyer's maximum gain** | **Unlimited** | Large but capped (the price can only fall to zero) |\n| **Seller's view** | Neutral-to-bearish | Neutral-to-bullish |\n| **Seller's maximum gain** | **The premium** | **The premium** |\n| **Seller's maximum loss** | **Unlimited** | Large but capped |\n\n> 🧠 **The four positions to know cold:**\n> - **Long call** — bullish · pay premium · loss capped at premium · **unlimited** gain\n> - **Long put** — bearish · pay premium · loss capped at premium · large gain\n> - **Short call** — receive premium · gain capped at premium · **UNLIMITED loss** ⚠️\n> - **Short put** — receive premium · gain capped at premium · large loss\n>\n> **Option sellers face far greater risk than buyers.** A buyer can lose only the premium; a **short call has theoretically unlimited loss**. This is why sellers must post margin and buyers need not.\n\n#### Key option terms\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Strike (exercise) price** | The price at which the option can be exercised |\n| **Premium** | The price paid by the buyer to the seller |\n| **European option** | Exercisable **only at expiry** — the Indian standard for index and stock options |\n| **American option** | Exercisable **any time up to expiry** |\n| **Intrinsic value** | The immediate exercise value — **never negative** |\n| **Time value** | Premium − intrinsic value; **decays to zero at expiry** |\n\n#### Moneyness\n\n| | **Call** | **Put** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **In the money (ITM)** | Spot > Strike | Spot < Strike |\n| **At the money (ATM)** | Spot = Strike | Spot = Strike |\n| **Out of the money (OTM)** | Spot < Strike | Spot > Strike |\n\n**Intrinsic value:** Call = **max(Spot − Strike, 0)**; Put = **max(Strike − Spot, 0)**.\n\n#### Payoffs at expiry\n\n- **Long call:** Payoff = max(S − K, 0) − Premium. **Break-even = Strike + Premium.**\n- **Long put:** Payoff = max(K − S, 0) − Premium. **Break-even = Strike − Premium.**\n- **Short call:** Premium − max(S − K, 0)\n- **Short put:** Premium − max(K − S, 0)\n\n**Worked example.** Buy a call, strike ₹1,000, premium ₹40.\n- Break-even = 1,000 + 40 = **₹1,040**\n- Spot at expiry ₹1,090 → intrinsic 90, profit = 90 − 40 = **+₹50**\n- Spot ₹1,020 → intrinsic 20, profit = 20 − 40 = **−₹20** (a loss, though the option is ITM)\n- Spot ₹950 → intrinsic 0, loss = **−₹40** (the full premium)\n\n> ⚠️ **Classic trap:** an option can be **in the money and still lose money**, because the premium must be recovered first. ITM ≠ profitable.\n\n### 10.3.4 Swaps\nAn agreement to **exchange cash flows** on specified dates.\n\n- **Interest rate swap** — the most common: one party pays **fixed** and receives **floating**, the other the reverse. Used to convert floating-rate borrowing into fixed, or vice versa.\n- **Currency swap** — exchange principal and interest in different currencies.\n- Swaps are **OTC** instruments.\n\n---\n\n## 10.4 Structure of Derivative Markets\n\n| | **OTC** | **Exchange traded** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Contracts** | Customised | Standardised |\n| **Counterparty risk** | Present | Eliminated by the CCP |\n| **Transparency** | Low | High |\n| **Regulation** | Lighter | Full |\n| **Examples** | Forwards, swaps, exotic options | Futures, listed options |\n\n---\n\n## 10.5 ⭐ Purpose of Derivatives\n\n### Hedging\n**Reducing or eliminating an existing risk.** The hedger already has an exposure and uses a derivative to offset it.\n\n- A farmer with a standing crop **sells futures** to lock in a price.\n- An investor holding equity **buys index puts** to protect against a fall (a \"protective put\").\n- An importer with a dollar payable **buys dollar futures** to fix the cost.\n\n> 🧠 **A hedge is not designed to make money.** It converts an uncertain outcome into a certain one — and if the market moves favourably, the hedge gives up that gain. That is the price of certainty.\n\n### Speculation\n**Taking on risk to profit from an expected price move**, with no underlying exposure. Leverage magnifies both gains and losses. Speculators supply the liquidity hedgers need.\n\n### Arbitrage\n**Simultaneously buying and selling in two markets to lock in a risk-free profit** from a price difference. Example: if futures are priced above their fair cost-of-carry value, buy the spot and sell the future. Arbitrage keeps prices aligned and, by its own action, eliminates the mispricing it exploits.\n\n> 🧠 **The three participants form an ecosystem:** hedgers **transfer** risk, speculators **absorb** it and supply liquidity, and arbitrageurs **keep prices correct**.\n\n---\n\n## 10.6 Costs, Benefits and Risks\n\n**Benefits:** risk management, price discovery, leverage/capital efficiency, lower transaction costs, and the ability to take a view in either direction (including going short).\n\n**Costs and risks:**\n\n| Risk | Explanation |\n|---|---|\n| **Leverage risk** | A small margin controls a large exposure — losses can **exceed the initial outlay** |\n| **Counterparty risk** | In OTC contracts (eliminated on exchange) |\n| **Liquidity risk** | Far-month or deep OTM contracts may be thinly traded |\n| **Basis risk** | The hedge and the exposure do not move perfectly together, so the hedge is imperfect |\n| **Model/valuation risk** | Complex instruments may be mispriced |\n| **Operational risk** | Margin calls, settlement failures, errors |\n\n> ⚠️ **The defining danger:** unlike equity, where the maximum loss is the amount invested, a **derivative position can lose more than the margin deposited**. Daily MTM means losses are demanded in cash immediately.\n\n---\n\n## 10.7 Equity, Currency and Commodity Derivatives\n\n| Segment | Underlying | Regulator | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Equity derivatives** | Indices and individual stocks | **SEBI** | Nifty and Bank Nifty futures & options, stock futures & options |\n| **Currency derivatives** | Currency pairs | **SEBI and RBI jointly** | USD-INR, EUR-INR, GBP-INR, JPY-INR futures & options |\n| **Commodity derivatives** | Metals, energy, agri | **SEBI** (since 2015, when FMC merged into SEBI) | Gold, silver, crude, cotton, guar |\n| **Interest rate derivatives** | Government securities | SEBI / RBI | Interest rate futures |\n\n**Indian equity derivatives:** European-style options, **monthly expiry**, **cash-settled** for indices; stock derivatives moved to **physical settlement**.\n\n---\n\n## 10.8 Derivative Strategies (introductory)\n\n| Strategy | Construction | View |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Protective put** | Hold the stock + buy a put | Bullish but wants **downside insurance** |\n| **Covered call** | Hold the stock + sell a call | Neutral to mildly bullish; earns premium, **caps upside** |\n| **Long straddle** | Buy a call and a put at the **same strike** | Expects a **big move**, direction unknown |\n| **Long strangle** | Buy an OTM call and an OTM put | Same view, cheaper, needs a bigger move |\n| **Bull call spread** | Buy a lower-strike call, sell a higher-strike call | Moderately bullish; caps both cost and gain |\n| **Bear put spread** | Buy a higher-strike put, sell a lower-strike put | Moderately bearish |\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Derivative | A contract whose value is **derived** from an underlying |\n| Forward | **OTC, customised**, counterparty risk, settled at maturity |\n| Futures | **Exchange-traded, standardised**, CCP-guaranteed, **daily MTM** |\n| Option buyer | Has a **right, no obligation**; pays premium; **loss capped at premium** |\n| Option seller | Has an **obligation, no right**; receives premium; **risk far greater** |\n| Long call | Bullish; **unlimited** gain; loss = premium |\n| Long put | Bearish; large gain; loss = premium |\n| Short call | **UNLIMITED loss** ⚠️; gain = premium |\n| Short put | Large loss; gain = premium |\n| Call break-even | **Strike + Premium** |\n| Put break-even | **Strike − Premium** |\n| Call intrinsic value | max(Spot − Strike, 0) |\n| Put intrinsic value | max(Strike − Spot, 0) |\n| Time value | Premium − intrinsic; **decays to zero at expiry** |\n| ITM call | Spot **>** Strike |\n| ITM put | Spot **<** Strike |\n| European option | Exercisable **only at expiry** (the Indian standard) |\n| American option | Exercisable **any time** |\n| Cost of carry | F = S × (1 + r − q)^T |\n| Contango | Futures **above** spot (normal) |\n| Backwardation | Futures **below** spot |\n| At expiry | Futures price **converges to spot** |\n| Basis | Spot − Futures |\n| Hedging | **Reducing an existing** risk; gives up favourable moves |\n| Speculation | Taking risk for profit, **no underlying exposure** |\n| Arbitrage | **Risk-free** profit from a price difference in two markets |\n| Swap | Exchange of **cash flows**; OTC |\n| Interest rate swap | Fixed for floating |\n| Key derivative danger | Losses can **exceed the margin deposited** |\n| Basis risk | The hedge doesn't move perfectly with the exposure |\n| Currency derivatives regulator | **SEBI and RBI jointly** |\n| Commodity derivatives regulator | **SEBI** (FMC merged in 2015) |\n| Protective put | Stock + long put = downside insurance |\n| Covered call | Stock + short call = premium income, capped upside |\n| Long straddle | Same-strike call + put; expects a **big move either way** |\n\n> **Exam tip:** for any option question, first write down **(1) is it a call or a put, (2) am I the buyer or the seller?** Those two answers fix the rights, obligations, maximum gain and maximum loss immediately. Then apply the break-even formula.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 10: Understanding Derivatives — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The four option positions and the forward-vs-futures table carry the most marks.**\n\n**Q1.** Define a derivative.\n> **A:** A **contract whose value is derived from an underlying asset** — it has no independent value of its own.\n\n**Q2.** Name five purposes derivatives serve.\n> **A:** **Risk transfer**, **price discovery**, **leverage**, **lower transaction costs** than trading the underlying, and **market completion** (payoffs otherwise unavailable).\n\n**Q3.** Give five differences between a forward and a future.\n> **A:** **Forward** — OTC, customised, counterparty risk, no margin, settled only at maturity. **Future** — exchange-traded, standardised, **CCP-guaranteed**, margined, **daily mark to market**.\n\n**Q4.** What is the single biggest difference between forwards and futures?\n> **A:** **Futures are guaranteed by the clearing corporation and settled daily; forwards are not** — a forward leaves you exposed to the other party's default.\n\n**Q5.** State the fundamental asymmetry of options.\n> **A:** The **buyer has a RIGHT without an OBLIGATION**; the **seller (writer) has an OBLIGATION without a right**. That asymmetry is the whole concept.\n\n**Q6.** What does a call buyer have the right to do, and what is their view?\n> **A:** The right to **BUY** the underlying at the strike price. The view is **bullish**.\n\n**Q7.** What does a put buyer have the right to do, and what is their view?\n> **A:** The right to **SELL** the underlying at the strike price. The view is **bearish**.\n\n**Q8.** State maximum gain and loss for a LONG CALL.\n> **A:** Maximum loss = **the premium**. Maximum gain = **unlimited** (the price can rise without limit).\n\n**Q9.** State maximum gain and loss for a SHORT CALL.\n> **A:** Maximum gain = **the premium**. Maximum loss = **UNLIMITED** ⚠️ — the riskiest of the four basic positions.\n\n**Q10.** State maximum gain and loss for a LONG PUT.\n> **A:** Maximum loss = **the premium**. Maximum gain is large but **capped**, because the underlying can only fall to zero.\n\n**Q11.** State maximum gain and loss for a SHORT PUT.\n> **A:** Maximum gain = **the premium**. Maximum loss is large but capped (underlying falling to zero).\n\n**Q12.** Why must option sellers post margin while buyers need not?\n> **A:** Because a **buyer's loss is capped at the premium already paid**, while a **seller's loss can be unlimited** (short call) or very large (short put). The margin secures that open-ended obligation.\n\n**Q13.** State the break-even for a long call and a long put.\n> **A:** **Long call break-even = Strike + Premium.** **Long put break-even = Strike − Premium.**\n\n**Q14.** State the intrinsic value formulas.\n> **A:** **Call = max(Spot − Strike, 0).** **Put = max(Strike − Spot, 0).** Intrinsic value is **never negative**.\n\n**Q15.** What is time value, and what happens to it?\n> **A:** **Time value = Premium − Intrinsic value.** It **decays to zero at expiry**, when the premium equals intrinsic value exactly.\n\n**Q16.** Define moneyness for calls and puts.\n> **A:** **Call:** ITM when Spot > Strike; OTM when Spot < Strike. **Put:** ITM when Spot < Strike; OTM when Spot > Strike. **ATM** when Spot = Strike for both.\n\n**Q17.** Can an option be in the money and still lose money? Explain.\n> **A:** **Yes.** A call with strike ₹1,000 bought for ₹40 with spot at ₹1,020 has intrinsic value ₹20 but cost ₹40 — a **₹20 loss** despite being ITM. **The premium must be recovered first: ITM ≠ profitable.**\n\n**Q18.** Distinguish European from American options, and state the Indian standard.\n> **A:** **European** — exercisable **only at expiry**. **American** — exercisable **any time up to expiry**. Indian index and stock options are **European**.\n\n**Q19.** State the cost-of-carry relationship.\n> **A:** **F = S × (1 + r − q)^T** (or F = S × e^((r−q)T)), where r is the risk-free rate and q the income yield (e.g. dividends).\n\n**Q20.** What are contango and backwardation?\n> **A:** **Contango** — futures price **above** spot (the normal state, reflecting carrying cost). **Backwardation** — futures price **below** spot.\n\n**Q21.** What happens to the futures price at expiry, and why?\n> **A:** It **converges to the spot price** — enforced by **arbitrage**, since any gap could be captured risk-free at that moment.\n\n**Q22.** Define basis.\n> **A:** **Basis = Spot price − Futures price.**\n\n**Q23.** What is a swap, and what is the most common type?\n> **A:** An agreement to **exchange cash flows** on specified dates. The most common is the **interest rate swap** — one party pays **fixed** and receives **floating**, the other the reverse. Swaps are **OTC**.\n\n**Q24.** Define hedging, and state what it costs.\n> **A:** **Reducing or eliminating an EXISTING risk** using a derivative. Its cost: it **converts uncertainty into certainty**, so a favourable market move is given up. A hedge is not designed to make money.\n\n**Q25.** Give three examples of hedging.\n> **A:** A **farmer sells futures** to lock in a crop price; an **investor buys index puts** to protect a portfolio; an **importer buys currency futures** to fix a payable.\n\n**Q26.** Define speculation and arbitrage.\n> **A:** **Speculation** — taking on risk to profit from an expected move, with **no underlying exposure**. **Arbitrage** — simultaneously buying and selling in two markets to lock in a **risk-free** profit from a price difference.\n\n**Q27.** How do the three participant types form an ecosystem?\n> **A:** **Hedgers transfer** risk, **speculators absorb** it and supply liquidity, and **arbitrageurs keep prices correct** — and by acting, eliminate the mispricing they exploit.\n\n**Q28.** What is the defining danger of derivatives compared with equity?\n> **A:** With equity your maximum loss is the amount invested. With a derivative, **losses can EXCEED the margin deposited** — and daily MTM demands those losses in cash immediately.\n\n**Q29.** What is basis risk?\n> **A:** The risk that the **hedge and the exposure do not move perfectly together**, leaving the hedge imperfect.\n\n**Q30.** Name the main risks of derivatives.\n> **A:** **Leverage risk**, **counterparty risk** (OTC), **liquidity risk**, **basis risk**, **model/valuation risk** and **operational risk**.\n\n**Q31.** Who regulates equity, currency and commodity derivatives in India?\n> **A:** **Equity — SEBI.** **Currency — SEBI and RBI jointly.** **Commodity — SEBI** (since the FMC merged into SEBI in **2015**).\n\n**Q32.** What is mark to market, and open interest?\n> **A:** **MTM** is the **daily settlement** of gains and losses to margin accounts. **Open interest** is the total number of contracts outstanding — a gauge of participation.\n\n**Q33.** Describe a protective put and a covered call.\n> **A:** **Protective put** = hold the stock **+ buy a put** — bullish but wants **downside insurance**. **Covered call** = hold the stock **+ sell a call** — earns premium but **caps the upside**.\n\n**Q34.** Describe a long straddle and what view it expresses.\n> **A:** **Buy a call AND a put at the same strike.** It profits from a **big move in either direction** — the view is on **volatility**, not direction.\n\n**Q35.** A call with strike ₹1,000 costs ₹40. Give the profit at spot prices of ₹1,090, ₹1,020 and ₹950.\n> **A:** **₹1,090:** intrinsic 90 − premium 40 = **+₹50**. **₹1,020:** intrinsic 20 − 40 = **−₹20**. **₹950:** intrinsic 0 − 40 = **−₹40** (the full premium). Break-even is **₹1,040**.\n\n**Q36.** What two questions should you ask first in any option problem?\n> **A:** **(1) Is it a call or a put? (2) Am I the buyer or the seller?** Those two answers fix the rights, obligations, maximum gain and maximum loss immediately.\n\n**Q37.** What are the settlement conventions for Indian equity derivatives?\n> **A:** **European-style** options, **monthly expiry**, **cash-settled** for indices, with **physical settlement** for stock derivatives.\n\n**Q38.** Why does leverage make derivatives dangerous for retail investors?\n> **A:** A **small margin controls a large exposure**, so a modest adverse move in the underlying can wipe out — and exceed — the entire amount deposited, with **margin calls demanded in cash the same day**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 10: Understanding Derivatives — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Option payoffs and break-evens dominate the numerical questions.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** A derivative is:\nA) A share issued by a company  B) A contract whose value is derived from an underlying asset  C) A type of bank deposit  D) A government bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A derivative has **no independent value**; its worth depends entirely on the underlying.\nA), C) and D) are all primary instruments with intrinsic value of their own.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** An option BUYER has:\nA) An obligation but no right  B) A right but no obligation  C) Both a right and an obligation  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The buyer pays a premium for the **right to choose** whether to exercise.\nA) Describes the **seller/writer**. C) and D) misstate the asymmetry that defines options.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** An option SELLER (writer) has:\nA) A right but no obligation  B) An obligation but no right  C) No exposure  D) Unlimited rights\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The writer receives the premium and **must perform** if the buyer exercises.\nA) Describes the buyer. C) The writer's exposure can be unlimited. D) The writer has no rights at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A call option gives the buyer the right to:\nA) Sell the underlying  B) Buy the underlying  C) Borrow the underlying  D) Cancel the contract\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Call = right to BUY**, taken by a **bullish** investor.\nA) Describes a put. C) and D) are not option rights.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A put option buyer is:\nA) Bullish  B) Bearish  C) Neutral  D) Arbitraging\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A put gives the **right to SELL**, which gains value as the price falls.\nA) Describes a call buyer. C) and D) describe different motives.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The maximum loss for a call option BUYER is:\nA) Unlimited  B) The premium paid  C) The strike price  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The worst case is that the option expires worthless and the **premium** is lost.\nA) Describes the **short call**. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The maximum loss for a call option SELLER is:\nA) The premium received  B) Unlimited  C) Zero  D) The strike price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because the underlying can rise without limit, a **naked short call has theoretically unlimited loss** — the riskiest basic position.\nA) Is the maximum **gain**. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The break-even for a long call is:\nA) Strike − Premium  B) Strike + Premium  C) The premium alone  D) The strike alone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The buyer must recover the premium before profiting, so break-even = **Strike + Premium**.\nA) Is the **long put** break-even. C) and D) ignore one of the two components.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The break-even for a long put is:\nA) Strike + Premium  B) Strike − Premium  C) Premium ÷ Strike  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The price must fall below the strike by at least the premium: **Strike − Premium**.\nA) Is the long call break-even. C) and D) are meaningless here.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The intrinsic value of a call option is:\nA) max(Strike − Spot, 0)  B) max(Spot − Strike, 0)  C) Spot − Strike, which can be negative  D) The premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **max(Spot − Strike, 0)** — intrinsic value can never be negative, because the buyer would simply not exercise.\nA) Is the **put** formula. C) Omits the floor at zero. D) Premium = intrinsic + time value.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A call option is in the money when:\nA) Spot < Strike  B) Spot > Strike  C) Spot = Strike  D) The premium is high\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A call to buy below the market price has value: **Spot > Strike**.\nA) Is out of the money for a call (and ITM for a put). C) Is at the money. D) Premium level does not define moneyness.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A put option is in the money when:\nA) Spot > Strike  B) Spot < Strike  C) Spot = Strike  D) Always\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A right to sell above the market price has value: **Spot < Strike**.\nA) Is OTM for a put. C) Is ATM. D) Options are frequently OTM.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Time value of an option:\nA) Increases as expiry approaches  B) Decays to zero at expiry  C) Is always zero  D) Equals the strike\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At expiry the premium equals **intrinsic value only** — all time value has decayed.\nA) Reverses the effect. C) Time value is positive before expiry. D) Unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** European options can be exercised:\nA) Any time before expiry  B) Only at expiry  C) Only in Europe  D) Never\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Only at expiry** — and this is the Indian standard for index and stock options.\nA) Describes an **American** option. C) The name is conventional, not geographic. D) They can certainly be exercised.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Which is exchange-traded and standardised?\nA) A forward  B) A future  C) A swap  D) An OTC option\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Futures** are standardised and exchange-traded with a CCP.\nA), C) and D) are all OTC instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Counterparty risk in a futures contract is:\nA) High  B) Eliminated by the clearing corporation acting as central counterparty  C) Borne by SEBI  D) Unregulated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **CCP** guarantees settlement, backed by margins and a guarantee fund.\nA) Describes a **forward**. C) SEBI regulates but is not a counterparty. D) Futures are fully regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Mark to market means:\nA) Settlement only at maturity  B) Daily settlement of gains and losses to margin accounts  C) Marketing the contract  D) Setting the strike price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Daily MTM** is a defining feature of exchange-traded futures.\nA) Describes a forward. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Hedging means:\nA) Taking risk to profit  B) Reducing or eliminating an existing risk  C) Risk-free arbitrage  D) Borrowing money\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A hedger **already has an exposure** and uses a derivative to offset it.\nA) Is speculation. C) Is arbitrage. D) Is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Speculation in derivatives involves:\nA) Offsetting an existing exposure  B) Taking on risk to profit from an expected move, with no underlying exposure  C) Risk-free profit  D) Insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The speculator has **no position to protect** — the derivative is the position.\nA) Is hedging. C) Is arbitrage. D) A hedge, not speculation, resembles insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Arbitrage means:\nA) Taking directional risk  B) Simultaneously buying and selling in two markets to lock in a risk-free profit  C) Hedging an exposure  D) Writing options\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Arbitrage exploits a **price difference** with no net risk.\nA) Is speculation. C) Is hedging. D) Is a specific option position, not a strategy category.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A swap is an agreement to:\nA) Exchange cash flows on specified dates  B) Buy a share  C) Sell a bond  D) Borrow from a bank\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Most commonly an **interest rate swap** exchanging fixed for floating.\nB), C) and D) are not swaps.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** In an interest rate swap, typically:\nA) Both parties pay fixed  B) One party pays fixed and receives floating, the other the reverse  C) Both pay floating  D) No payments occur\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the standard \"plain vanilla\" structure.\nA) and C) would have no purpose. D) Cash flows are the whole point.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Contango describes a market where:\nA) Futures price is below spot  B) Futures price is above spot  C) They are equal  D) No futures exist\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **normal** state, reflecting the cost of carrying the asset.\nA) Is **backwardation**. C) Happens only at expiry. D) Irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** At expiry, the futures price:\nA) Diverges from spot  B) Converges to the spot price  C) Doubles  D) Becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Convergence** is enforced by arbitrage — any gap at expiry could be captured risk-free.\nA), C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Basis is defined as:\nA) Futures − Spot  B) Spot − Futures  C) Strike − Spot  D) Premium ÷ Strike\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Basis = Spot − Futures**.\nA) Reverses the convention. C) and D) relate to options, not basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which regulator oversees commodity derivatives in India?\nA) The RBI  B) SEBI  C) IRDAI  D) The FMC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SEBI**, since the Forward Markets Commission merged into it in **2015**.\nA) Regulates banking and money markets. C) Insurance. D) The FMC no longer exists as a separate regulator.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Currency derivatives in India are regulated by:\nA) SEBI alone  B) SEBI and the RBI jointly  C) IRDAI  D) The MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Currency derivatives sit at the intersection of securities markets (SEBI) and foreign exchange (RBI).\nA) Omits the RBI's role. C) and D) have no jurisdiction.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A protective put involves:\nA) Selling a stock and buying a call  B) Holding a stock and buying a put  C) Selling a put  D) Buying two calls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Stock **plus a long put** provides **downside insurance** while retaining upside.\nA), C) and D) describe different positions.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** A covered call involves:\nA) Holding a stock and selling a call  B) Buying a call with no stock  C) Selling a put  D) Buying a straddle\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The stock \"covers\" the short call, earning premium but **capping the upside**.\nB) Is a naked long call. C) and D) are different strategies.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** A long straddle involves:\nA) Buying a call and a put at the same strike  B) Selling a call and a put  C) Buying two calls  D) Buying a stock\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — It profits from a **large move in either direction** — a view on **volatility**, not direction.\nB) Is a short straddle. C) and D) are directional positions.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** The key risk unique to derivatives compared with equity is:\nA) Market risk  B) Losses can exceed the amount deposited as margin  C) Inflation risk  D) Liquidity risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With equity your loss is capped at what you invested; a **leveraged derivative position can lose more**.\nA), C) and D) affect both equity and derivatives.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Open interest measures:\nA) The interest rate  B) The total number of contracts outstanding  C) The premium  D) The strike price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A gauge of **participation** in a contract.\nA), C) and D) are unrelated concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Basis risk is the risk that:\nA) The hedge and the exposure do not move perfectly together  B) The counterparty defaults  C) Rates rise  D) Volume falls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — An imperfect correlation makes the hedge incomplete.\nB) Is counterparty risk. C) and D) are different risks.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Which statement about a hedge is correct?\nA) It is designed to make a profit  B) It converts an uncertain outcome into a certain one, giving up favourable moves  C) It eliminates all risk permanently  D) It requires no cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A hedge buys **certainty**, and the price is forgoing the upside.\nA) Profit is not the purpose. C) Basis risk usually remains. D) Hedging costs premium or margin.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Indian index options are:\nA) American style  B) European style, exercisable only at expiry  C) Perpetual  D) Never exercised\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Indian index and stock options are **European**.\nA) Is the US convention for stock options. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** An investor buys a call with strike ₹800 for a premium of ₹25. What is the break-even?\nA) ₹775  B) ₹825  C) ₹800  D) ₹25\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Break-even = Strike + Premium = 800 + 25 = **₹825**.\nA) Is the **put** break-even formula. C) Ignores the premium. D) Is the premium alone.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Using Q36's option, what is the profit if the spot at expiry is ₹870?\nA) ₹70  B) ₹45  C) ₹95  D) −₹25\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic = 870 − 800 = **₹70**. Profit = 70 − 25 = **₹45**.\nA) Is the intrinsic value, ignoring the premium paid. C) Adds the premium instead of subtracting. D) Is the loss if the option expires worthless.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Using Q36's option, what is the outcome if the spot at expiry is ₹790?\nA) Profit ₹10  B) Loss of the full ₹25 premium — the option expires worthless  C) Loss ₹10  D) Break-even\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At ₹790 the call is **out of the money** (spot < strike), so intrinsic = 0 and the entire **₹25 premium** is lost.\nA) There is no profit. C) The loss is the full premium, not ₹10. D) Break-even is ₹825.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An investor buys a put with strike ₹500 for ₹18. What is the break-even and the profit at a spot of ₹450?\nA) Break-even ₹518; profit ₹32  B) Break-even ₹482; profit ₹32  C) Break-even ₹482; profit ₹50  D) Break-even ₹500; profit ₹18\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Break-even = Strike − Premium = 500 − 18 = **₹482**. At ₹450, intrinsic = 500 − 450 = **₹50**; profit = 50 − 18 = **₹32**.\nA) Uses the call break-even formula. C) Gives intrinsic value, not profit. D) Ignores the premium in both.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A call with strike ₹1,200 has a premium of ₹65 when spot is ₹1,230. What are its intrinsic and time values?\nA) Intrinsic ₹30; time value ₹35  B) Intrinsic ₹65; time value ₹0  C) Intrinsic ₹35; time value ₹30  D) Intrinsic ₹0; time value ₹65\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Intrinsic = max(1,230 − 1,200, 0) = **₹30**. Time value = premium − intrinsic = 65 − 30 = **₹35**.\nB) Would apply only at expiry. C) Swaps the two. D) The option is ITM, so intrinsic is not zero.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An investor writes (sells) a call with strike ₹950 for ₹30. The spot at expiry is ₹1,040. What is her result?\nA) Profit ₹30  B) Loss ₹60  C) Loss ₹90  D) Profit ₹90\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The buyer exercises: the writer pays intrinsic = 1,040 − 950 = **₹90**, but keeps the **₹30 premium**. Net = 30 − 90 = **−₹60**.\nA) Would apply only if the option expired worthless. C) Forgets the premium received. D) Gets the direction wrong — the writer **loses** when the price rises above the strike.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A farmer expecting to harvest wheat in 3 months wants to fix his selling price. He should:\nA) Buy wheat futures  B) Sell wheat futures  C) Buy a call option  D) Do nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He is **long the physical crop**, so he **sells futures** to lock in a price and offset a fall.\nA) Buying futures would **double** his exposure to falling prices. C) A call profits from rising prices — the wrong direction for his risk. D) Leaves him fully exposed.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** An importer must pay USD 1,00,000 in 3 months and fears the rupee weakening. He should:\nA) Sell dollar futures  B) Buy dollar futures to fix his cost  C) Buy rupee puts  D) Do nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He is **short dollars** (he must buy them later), so **buying dollar futures** fixes the rate.\nA) Selling would worsen the exposure. C) Confuses the direction. D) Leaves him exposed to rupee depreciation.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which position carries theoretically unlimited loss?\nA) Long call  B) Long put  C) Naked short call  D) Buying a stock\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The underlying can rise without limit, so the writer's obligation is unbounded.\nA) and B) cap the loss at the premium. D) A stock's loss is capped at the amount invested (price can fall only to zero).\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** An investor holds shares worth ₹10,00,000 and fears a market fall but does not want to sell. The appropriate strategy is:\nA) Sell a call  B) Buy index puts (a protective put)  C) Buy more shares  D) Sell the shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **protective put** insures the downside while keeping the shares and the upside.\nA) A covered call earns premium but provides only limited downside cushion. C) Increases the exposure. D) Contradicts the stated wish not to sell.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Spot is ₹500, the risk-free rate 8% and there are 6 months to expiry with no dividend. What is the approximate fair futures price?\nA) ₹480  B) ₹520  C) ₹540  D) ₹500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — F = S × (1 + r)^T = 500 × (1.08)^0.5 = 500 × **1.03923** = **₹519.62 ≈ ₹520**.\nA) Would imply backwardation with no reason. C) Applies a full year's interest at double the rate. D) Ignores the cost of carry. **Futures normally trade above spot — contango.**\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A trader observes futures trading well above their fair cost-of-carry value. The arbitrage is to:\nA) Buy futures and sell spot  B) Buy the spot and sell the futures  C) Buy both  D) Sell both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sell the **overpriced** leg (futures) and buy the **underpriced** leg (spot), locking in the difference risk-free.\nA) Reverses it, buying the expensive leg. C) and D) leave a directional position, not an arbitrage.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** An investor sells a put with strike ₹600 for ₹22. The spot at expiry is ₹640. What is the result?\nA) Loss ₹40  B) Profit ₹22 — the put expires worthless  C) Profit ₹40  D) Loss ₹18\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At ₹640 the put is **out of the money** (spot > strike), so the buyer does not exercise and the writer **keeps the full ₹22 premium**.\nA) and D) would apply if the price had fallen below the strike. C) The maximum gain for a writer is the premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which combination correctly describes a covered call?\nA) Long stock + long call  B) Long stock + short call — premium income with capped upside  C) Short stock + short call  D) Long put + short put\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The shares \"cover\" the obligation if the call is exercised.\nA) Doubles the bullish exposure. C) and D) are different structures.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A trader expects a large move in a stock ahead of results but does not know the direction. The suitable strategy is:\nA) Buy a call only  B) A long straddle — buy a call and a put at the same strike  C) Sell a straddle  D) Buy the stock\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **long straddle** profits from a big move **either way**.\nA) Profits only if the price rises. C) A **short** straddle profits from the price staying still — the opposite view. D) Profits only from a rise.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which statement about margin is correct?\nA) Only option buyers post margin  B) Option sellers and futures traders post margin; option buyers pay only the premium  C) Nobody posts margin  D) Margin equals the contract value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **buyer's loss is capped at the premium already paid**, so no further security is needed. **Sellers and futures traders** carry open-ended obligations and must post margin.\nA) Reverses it. C) Margin is fundamental to exchange-traded derivatives. D) Margin is a fraction of contract value — that is what creates leverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** An option is described as \"deep in the money with little time value\". This most likely means:\nA) It is far out of the money  B) The spot is far beyond the strike and expiry is near, so the premium is almost entirely intrinsic value  C) The premium is zero  D) It is a forward\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Deep ITM plus near expiry means **premium ≈ intrinsic value**.\nA) Contradicts \"in the money\". C) A deep ITM option has substantial premium. D) A forward is not an option.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A hedger complains that his hedge \"cost him money\" because prices moved favourably. The correct response is:\nA) The hedge failed  B) A hedge converts uncertainty into certainty — giving up a favourable move is the price of that protection, not a failure  C) He should never hedge  D) He should have speculated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Hedging is **insurance**, not a profit strategy. Forgoing the upside is exactly what buying certainty costs.\nA) The hedge worked as designed. C) Hedging remains appropriate where the exposure is genuine and unwanted. D) Speculation adds risk rather than removing it.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which is TRUE of a forward contract?\nA) It is exchange-traded  B) It is customised, OTC, and carries counterparty risk  C) It has daily MTM  D) It is guaranteed by a clearing corporation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — These three are the defining features of a forward.\nA), C) and D) all describe **futures**.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A call option with strike ₹700 is trading when spot is ₹680. The option is:\nA) In the money  B) Out of the money  C) At the money  D) Deep in the money\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a **call**, ITM requires **spot > strike**. Here 680 < 700, so it is **out of the money**.\nA) and D) require the spot above the strike. C) ATM requires spot = strike.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A put option with strike ₹700 when spot is ₹680 is:\nA) Out of the money  B) In the money, with intrinsic value ₹20  C) At the money  D) Worthless\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a **put**, ITM requires **spot < strike**. Intrinsic = 700 − 680 = **₹20**.\nA) Reverses the put rule. C) ATM requires equality. D) An ITM option has positive value. **Note the same spot/strike pair makes the call OTM and the put ITM — they are mirror images.**\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Leverage in derivatives arises because:\nA) Contracts are free  B) A small margin controls a much larger exposure  C) There is no risk  D) Premiums are refunded\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Paying a fraction of the contract value magnifies both gains and losses.\nA) Margin and premium are real costs. C) Leverage increases risk. D) Premiums are not refundable.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which participant keeps futures and spot prices aligned?\nA) The hedger  B) The arbitrageur  C) The speculator  D) The regulator\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Arbitrageurs exploit — and thereby **eliminate** — any deviation from fair value.\nA) Hedgers transfer risk. C) Speculators supply liquidity and absorb risk. D) Regulators set rules but do not price contracts.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** An investor buys a call for ₹50 with strike ₹2,000. At expiry the spot is ₹2,030. What is the outcome?\nA) Profit ₹30  B) Loss ₹20 — the option is ITM but the premium is not recovered  C) Profit ₹80  D) Loss ₹50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic = 2,030 − 2,000 = **₹30**; profit = 30 − 50 = **−₹20**. She should still exercise (recovering ₹30 beats abandoning it for nothing), but the trade loses money.\nA) Ignores the premium. C) Adds instead of subtracting. D) Would apply only if the option expired worthless. **ITM does not mean profitable.**\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which risk is eliminated by trading futures on an exchange rather than forwards OTC?\nA) Market risk  B) Counterparty (default) risk  C) Basis risk  D) Leverage risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **CCP** guarantees performance.\nA) Price movements affect both equally. C) Basis risk arises from imperfect hedging, not venue. D) Leverage exists in both.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** An interest rate swap would be used by a company that:\nA) Wants to buy shares  B) Has floating-rate borrowing and wants the certainty of fixed payments  C) Wants to sell a bond  D) Needs a loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The company **pays fixed and receives floating**, converting its floating liability into a fixed one.\nA), C) and D) do not involve swaps.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A covered call writer's position when the stock rises sharply above the strike is:\nA) Unlimited profit  B) Profit capped at the strike plus the premium received — the upside beyond the strike is forgone  C) Unlimited loss  D) No effect\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The shares are effectively sold at the strike, so gains beyond it are given up. The premium partly compensates.\nA) The short call caps the upside. C) The stock holding **covers** the obligation, so there is no unlimited loss. D) The position is materially affected.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which of these is NOT a benefit of derivatives?\nA) Risk transfer  B) Price discovery  C) Elimination of all investment risk  D) Lower transaction costs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Derivatives **transfer and reshape** risk; they never eliminate it from the system.\nA), B) and D) are genuine benefits.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A trader sells a naked call on a stock at strike ₹1,500 for ₹40. The stock is acquired at ₹2,200. What is the loss?\nA) ₹40 profit  B) Loss ₹660  C) Loss ₹700  D) Loss ₹2,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic at ₹2,200 = 2,200 − 1,500 = **₹700**. The writer keeps the ₹40 premium, so net loss = 700 − 40 = **₹660**.\nA) Would apply only if the option expired worthless. C) Ignores the premium received. D) Is the stock price, not the loss. **This illustrates why naked call writing is so dangerous.**\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which statement about open interest is correct?\nA) It measures the premium  B) It is the total number of contracts outstanding, indicating participation  C) It is the strike price  D) It equals volume\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Open interest counts **positions still open**, unlike volume which counts trades in a period.\nA) and C) are different concepts. D) Volume and open interest are distinct measures.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A stock trades at ₹1,000 with a 3-month future at ₹975. This is:\nA) Contango  B) Backwardation — the futures price is below spot  C) Impossible  D) Arbitrage-free by definition\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Futures **below** spot is **backwardation**, which can arise from high dividend expectations or supply/demand pressures.\nA) Contango is futures **above** spot. C) It occurs regularly. D) It may or may not present an arbitrage, depending on the carry.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A protective put is best described as:\nA) A speculative bet  B) Portfolio insurance — paying a premium to cap the downside while keeping the upside  C) A way to earn income  D) Arbitrage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Structurally identical to an insurance policy: a premium buys protection against a loss.\nA) It reduces rather than adds risk. C) A **covered call** earns income; a protective put **costs** premium. D) There is no risk-free profit involved.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which pair correctly matches strategy to view?\nA) Long straddle → expects no movement  B) Covered call → neutral to mildly bullish; long straddle → expects a large move either way  C) Protective put → bearish only  D) Short call → bullish\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both pairings are correct.\nA) A **long** straddle needs a big move; a **short** straddle wants stillness. C) A protective put is held by someone **bullish** who wants insurance. D) A short call is neutral-to-**bearish**.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A speculator with ₹1,00,000 takes a futures position worth ₹10,00,000. The underlying falls 12%. What happens?\nA) She loses ₹12,000  B) She loses ₹1,20,000 — more than her entire ₹1,00,000 deposit  C) She loses nothing  D) She gains\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The loss is on the **full contract value**: 12% × ₹10,00,000 = **₹1,20,000**, which exceeds the ₹1,00,000 margin. She faces a margin call for the shortfall.\nA) Applies 12% to the margin rather than the exposure. C) The position is fully exposed. D) A fall hurts a long position. **This is the defining derivative danger.**\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which of these correctly states the Indian equity derivatives convention?\nA) American options, weekly expiry, physically settled indices  B) European options, monthly expiry, cash-settled indices and physically settled stock derivatives  C) American options, no expiry  D) European options, physically settled indices\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three elements are correct for the Indian market.\nA) and C) misstate the exercise style. D) Index derivatives are **cash-settled** — you cannot deliver an index.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** An investor buys a call, strike ₹1,500, premium ₹60, lot size 500. At expiry the spot is ₹1,610. What is the net profit on the lot?\nA) ₹55,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹30,000  D) −₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic per share = 1,610 − 1,500 = **₹110**. Net per share = 110 − 60 = **₹50**. On a lot of 500: 50 × 500 = **₹25,000**.\nA) Applies intrinsic without deducting the premium (110 × 500). C) Uses the premium alone (60 × 500). D) Wrong direction. **Break-even was ₹1,560; the spot exceeded it by ₹50.**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Using Q71's contract, what would the result be if the spot at expiry were ₹1,530?\nA) Profit ₹15,000  B) Loss ₹15,000 — the option is ITM by ₹30 but cost ₹60  C) Loss ₹30,000  D) Break-even\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic = ₹30; net per share = 30 − 60 = **−₹30**; on 500 shares = **−₹15,000**. She still exercises (recovering ₹15,000 beats abandoning it), but the trade loses.\nA) Wrong direction. C) Would be the loss if it expired fully worthless (60 × 500 = ₹30,000). D) Break-even is ₹1,560.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** An investor writes a put, strike ₹800, premium ₹35, lot 1,000. The spot at expiry is ₹720. What is the result?\nA) Profit ₹35,000  B) Loss ₹45,000  C) Loss ₹80,000  D) Profit ₹45,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The put is **ITM** (spot < strike), so the buyer exercises. Intrinsic = 800 − 720 = **₹80** per share. The writer keeps ₹35, so net = 35 − 80 = **−₹45** per share; on 1,000 = **−₹45,000**.\nA) Would apply only if the put expired worthless. C) Ignores the premium received. D) Wrong direction — the writer of a put **loses when the price falls**.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A stock trades at ₹2,000. A 3-month future trades at ₹2,050, the risk-free rate is 8% and no dividend is expected. Is there an arbitrage?\nA) No, the future is fairly priced  B) Yes — fair value is about ₹2,039, so the future is overpriced; buy spot and sell the future  C) Yes — buy the future and sell spot  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Fair value = 2,000 × (1.08)^0.25 = 2,000 × **1.01943** = **₹2,038.86**. The future at ₹2,050 is **overpriced by about ₹11**, so the arbitrage is to **buy the cheap leg (spot) and sell the expensive leg (future)**.\nA) There is a gap beyond the fair carry. C) Reverses the legs, buying the expensive one. D) With S, r and T given it is computable.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** An investor holds 2,000 shares at ₹950 and buys 2,000 puts at strike ₹900 for a premium of ₹25. The stock falls to ₹780. What is the net position value?\nA) ₹15,60,000  B) ₹17,50,000  C) ₹19,00,000  D) ₹18,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Shares are now worth 2,000 × ₹780 = **₹15,60,000**. The puts are ITM by 900 − 780 = **₹120**, worth 2,000 × 120 = **₹2,40,000**. Premium paid = 2,000 × 25 = **₹50,000**. Net = 15,60,000 + 2,40,000 − 50,000 = **₹17,50,000**.\nA) Counts only the shares, ignoring the insurance payout. C) Is the original value (2,000 × 950 = ₹19,00,000). D) Miscomputes the put payoff. **Unhedged, the position would be worth just ₹15,60,000 — so the protective put preserved ₹1,90,000 net of premium.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A trader sells a covered call: holds 500 shares bought at ₹600, sells calls at strike ₹650 for ₹20. At expiry the spot is ₹720. What is the total profit?\nA) ₹60,000  B) ₹35,000  C) ₹85,000  D) ₹10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The call is exercised, so shares are effectively sold at the **strike of ₹650**. Gain on shares = (650 − 600) × 500 = **₹25,000**. Premium retained = 20 × 500 = **₹10,000**. Total = **₹35,000**.\nA) Is the gain if he had simply held the shares to ₹720 ((720 − 600) × 500 = ₹60,000). C) Adds both incorrectly. D) Counts the premium alone. **The covered call earned ₹10,000 of premium but gave up ₹35,000 of upside above ₹650 — the trade-off in one number.**\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Which position would you take to profit if a stock stays exactly where it is?\nA) Long straddle  B) Short straddle — selling a call and a put, collecting both premiums as they decay  C) Long call  D) Long put\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **short straddle** profits when the underlying stays near the strike and both options decay to zero. ⚠️ Note it carries **unlimited risk** from the short call leg.\nA) A long straddle needs a **big move**. C) and D) are directional positions.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** An investor buys a straddle: call and put both at strike ₹1,000, premiums ₹45 and ₹38. What are the two break-even points?\nA) ₹1,045 and ₹962  B) ₹1,083 and ₹917  C) ₹1,000 and ₹1,083  D) ₹955 and ₹1,045\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total premium = 45 + 38 = **₹83**. **Upper break-even = Strike + total premium = 1,000 + 83 = ₹1,083.** **Lower break-even = Strike − total premium = 1,000 − 83 = ₹917.**\nA) Uses each premium separately rather than the combined cost. C) Omits the lower break-even. D) Uses the wrong premium. **The stock must move more than ₹83 in either direction for the straddle to profit.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A speculator deposits ₹2,00,000 margin for a futures position worth ₹16,00,000. The underlying rises 5%. What is the return on her margin?\nA) 5%  B) 40% — the ₹80,000 gain on a ₹2,00,000 deposit  C) 8%  D) 12.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gain = 5% × ₹16,00,000 = **₹80,000**. Return on margin = 80,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **40%**.\nA) Is the move in the underlying, not the leveraged return. C) and D) do not follow. **The leverage is 8× (₹16,00,000 ÷ ₹2,00,000), so a 5% move becomes 40% — and a 5% adverse move would lose 40% of her capital.**\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Which sequence correctly ranks these by maximum possible loss, largest first?\nA) Long call, long put, naked short call  B) Naked short call (unlimited), short put (large, capped at strike × lot), long call (premium only)  C) Long put, long call, short call  D) All identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **naked short call is unbounded**; the **short put** is large but capped (the underlying can only fall to zero); the **long call** risks only the premium.\nA) Places the unlimited-loss position last. C) Inverts the ranking. D) The exposures differ enormously.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A company with a ₹50 crore floating-rate loan enters a swap to pay fixed at 9% and receive floating. Rates then rise to 12%. What is the outcome?\nA) The company loses  B) The company benefits — it pays 9% fixed while receiving 12% floating, which offsets its higher loan cost  C) No effect  D) The swap is cancelled\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Its loan cost rose to 12%, but the swap **receives** 12% floating and **pays** 9% fixed, so its **net cost stays around 9%** — exactly the certainty the swap was bought for.\nA) The swap protected it. C) The swap has a clear effect. D) Swaps do not auto-cancel. **Had rates fallen, the company would have been locked into 9% while the market paid less — the symmetrical cost of certainty.**\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An option premium is ₹85 with intrinsic value ₹85. What can you conclude?\nA) The option is OTM  B) Time value is zero, so the option is at or very near expiry  C) The option is worthless  D) The premium is wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Time value = premium − intrinsic = 0**, which happens **at expiry** (or for a deep-ITM option with negligible time remaining).\nA) An OTM option has **zero** intrinsic value. C) It has ₹85 of value. D) The pricing is internally consistent.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A hedger sells futures against a physical holding but the futures contract's underlying is a broad index while his holding is a single stock. What risk remains?\nA) None  B) Basis risk — the stock and the index will not move identically, so the hedge is imperfect  C) Counterparty risk  D) Credit risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **cross-hedge** leaves **basis risk**: company-specific moves are not offset by an index hedge.\nA) The hedge is imperfect. C) Exchange-traded futures eliminate counterparty risk. D) No credit exposure arises here.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Which statement about option buyers and sellers is MOST accurate?\nA) Buyers face greater risk than sellers  B) Sellers face far greater risk — a buyer's loss is capped at the premium while a naked seller's can be unlimited — which is why only sellers post margin  C) Both face identical risk  D) Neither faces risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The asymmetry of rights and obligations produces an asymmetry of risk, and the margin regime reflects it exactly.\nA) Reverses the reality. C) Their exposures are fundamentally different. D) Both face risk, but of very different magnitudes.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A trader observes: spot ₹400, 1-month future ₹396, no dividends, risk-free rate 7%. What is the situation?\nA) Normal contango  B) Backwardation — the future is below spot, and below fair value of about ₹402, suggesting either an arbitrage or expected dividends/supply factors  C) Fair value  D) Impossible\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Fair value = 400 × (1.07)^(1/12) = 400 × **1.00565** = **₹402.26**. The future at ₹396 is in **backwardation** and below fair value.\nA) Contango means the future trades **above** spot. C) It is ₹6 below fair value. D) Backwardation occurs regularly, often when a dividend is expected or in tight physical markets.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** An investor writes a naked put on a stock at strike ₹1,200 for ₹50 with lot size 400. The company collapses and the stock falls to ₹150. What is the loss?\nA) ₹20,000 profit  B) Loss ₹4,00,000  C) Loss ₹4,80,000  D) Loss ₹60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic = 1,200 − 150 = **₹1,050** per share. Net per share = 50 − 1,050 = **−₹1,000**. On 400 shares = **−₹4,00,000**.\nA) Would apply only if the put expired worthless. C) Ignores the ₹50 premium received (1,050 × 400 = ₹4,20,000; and ₹4,80,000 corresponds to the full strike value). D) Understates it by a factor of nearly seven. **The premium of ₹20,000 collected proved trivial against a ₹4 lakh loss — the asymmetry of option writing.**\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Which combination of features belongs to a FORWARD and not a future?\nA) Standardised, exchange-traded, CCP-guaranteed  B) Customised terms, OTC, counterparty risk, settled only at maturity  C) Daily mark to market  D) Mandatory margin\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All four are forward characteristics.\nA), C) and D) all describe **futures**.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A portfolio manager holds ₹5 crore of equity and buys index puts costing 1.5% of portfolio value. The market falls 20%. Roughly what has she achieved?\nA) Nothing  B) She has paid ₹7.5 lakh for insurance that offsets much of the ₹1 crore paper loss — converting an uncertain outcome into a bounded one  C) A profit of ₹1 crore  D) A loss of ₹1.075 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Premium = 1.5% × ₹5 crore = **₹7.5 lakh**. A 20% fall would cost **₹1 crore** unhedged; the puts offset much of that (the exact amount depends on the strike). The **cost of the insurance is known in advance; the loss avoided is not**.\nA) The hedge clearly worked. C) A protective put does not turn a falling market into a profit. D) Ignores the put payoff entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Which is the correct interpretation of \"the futures price converges to spot at expiry\"?\nA) It is a coincidence  B) Arbitrage enforces it — at expiry the contract IS the underlying, so any gap could be captured risk-free  C) Regulators set it  D) It only applies to commodities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At expiry the time to carry is zero, so cost of carry vanishes and **F = S**. Any deviation is an instant riskless profit and is arbitraged away.\nA) It is structural, not coincidental. C) It is a market mechanism, not a rule. D) It applies to all futures.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A trader buys a call for ₹30 and simultaneously sells a higher-strike call for ₹12 (a bull call spread). What are the net cost and the maximum loss?\nA) Net cost ₹42; max loss ₹42  B) Net cost ₹18; max loss ₹18  C) Net cost ₹18; max loss unlimited  D) Net cost ₹12; max loss ₹30\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net premium paid = 30 − 12 = **₹18**, which is also the **maximum loss** (if both expire worthless).\nA) Adds the premiums instead of netting them. C) The **long call caps the short call's risk**, so the loss is bounded. D) Miscomputes the net. **A spread reduces both the cost and the maximum gain compared with a naked long call.**\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** An investor is told that \"selling options is like earning rent — you collect premium every month\". What is the flaw?\nA) There is none  B) The premium is small and capped, while the potential loss is large or unlimited — one adverse move can erase many months of collected premium  C) Options cannot be sold  D) Premiums are refunded\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Option selling has a **positive-most-of-the-time, catastrophic-occasionally** payoff. Q86 illustrated it: ₹20,000 of premium against a ₹4 lakh loss.\nA) The asymmetry is fundamental. C) Selling options is routine. D) Premiums are not refunded — that is precisely the seller's income.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Which set of actions is consistent for a jeweller who has agreed to deliver gold jewellery in 3 months at a fixed rupee price?\nA) Sell gold futures  B) Buy gold futures — he must acquire gold later, so he is effectively short gold and locks in the purchase cost  C) Buy gold puts  D) Do nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He has committed to a **selling price** but has **not yet bought** the gold, so a price **rise** hurts him. **Buying futures** fixes his input cost.\nA) Selling futures would suit someone who **owns** gold. C) Puts protect against a **fall**, which is not his risk. D) Leaves him exposed to rising gold prices.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A stock is at ₹1,000. Calls: strike 950 premium ₹80; strike 1,000 premium ₹45; strike 1,050 premium ₹22. Which has the highest intrinsic value and which the highest time value?\nA) Highest intrinsic: 1,050 strike; highest time value: 950 strike  B) Highest intrinsic: 950 strike (₹50); highest time value: 1,000 strike (₹45)  C) All have the same intrinsic value  D) Highest intrinsic: 1,000 strike\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Intrinsic values:** 950 strike = max(1,000 − 950, 0) = **₹50**; 1,000 strike = **₹0** (ATM); 1,050 strike = **₹0** (OTM). **Time values:** 80 − 50 = **₹30**; 45 − 0 = **₹45**; 22 − 0 = **₹22**.\nA) Reverses both. C) They differ. D) The ATM option has zero intrinsic value. **Time value peaks at the money — a classic exam point.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which statement about leverage risk is MOST accurate?\nA) Leverage only magnifies gains  B) Leverage magnifies both gains and losses, and losses can exceed the margin deposited, with margin calls demanded in cash immediately  C) Leverage eliminates risk  D) Leverage applies only to options\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three elements matter: **symmetry**, **loss beyond capital**, and the **immediacy of the cash demand** through daily MTM.\nA) Losses are magnified equally. C) It increases risk. D) Futures are the most leveraged instruments of all.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An arbitrageur finds a mispricing and executes the trade. What happens to the mispricing?\nA) It widens  B) It narrows and eventually disappears, because the arbitrageur's own buying and selling pushes the two prices together  C) It stays constant  D) It becomes permanent\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Arbitrage is **self-eliminating**: buying the cheap leg raises its price and selling the expensive leg lowers its price.\nA) The trade pushes prices together. C) and D) would imply markets never correct — the opposite of what arbitrage does.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A retail investor asks whether he should sell naked options to generate income on a ₹5 lakh portfolio. The best advice is:\nA) Yes — it is easy income  B) No — naked option writing carries unlimited or very large loss potential and margin obligations far beyond a ₹5 lakh portfolio's capacity to absorb  C) Yes, if he sells only calls  D) Yes, with no margin needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The risk is **structurally unsuited** to a small portfolio: one adverse move can exceed the entire capital, and margin calls must be met in cash the same day.\nA) The \"easy income\" framing hides the tail risk. C) **Naked calls are the most dangerous** of all — unlimited loss. D) Writers must post margin precisely because of this exposure.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Which correctly matches participant to function in the derivatives ecosystem?\nA) Hedgers absorb risk; speculators transfer it  B) Hedgers transfer risk; speculators absorb it and supply liquidity; arbitrageurs keep prices correct  C) All three speculate  D) Only arbitrageurs matter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each plays a distinct and necessary role.\nA) Reverses hedgers and speculators. C) Hedgers reduce risk; arbitrageurs take none. D) Without speculators there would be no counterparty for hedgers.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** An option's premium is ₹120, of which ₹90 is intrinsic. Expiry is 2 months away. What happens to the premium if the spot is unchanged at expiry?\nA) It rises to ₹150  B) It falls to about ₹90 as the ₹30 of time value decays to zero  C) It stays ₹120  D) It falls to zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Time value decays to zero at expiry**, leaving only intrinsic value: **₹90**.\nA) Premiums do not rise with an unchanged spot. C) Time decay is certain. D) The option retains its ₹90 intrinsic value. **Time decay is the option buyer's persistent headwind and the seller's tailwind.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client wants exposure to a stock's upside with a strictly limited maximum loss. Which position fits?\nA) A futures long  B) A long call — the maximum loss is the premium, while the upside is unlimited  C) A naked short put  D) A covered call\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **long call** is the only one of the four with a **capped, known loss** and uncapped upside.\nA) A futures long has losses limited only by the price falling to zero, and can exceed the margin. C) A short put has large downside and capped upside. D) A covered call caps the upside.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which two questions should always be asked first in an option problem?\nA) What is the strike and what is the expiry?  B) Is it a call or a put, and am I the buyer or the seller — those two answers fix the rights, obligations, maximum gain and maximum loss  C) What is the lot size?  D) Who is the broker?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Those two answers determine **everything structural** about the position; strike, premium and lot size then supply the arithmetic.\nA) Necessary for computation but not for identifying the position's nature. C) Affects scale only. D) Irrelevant to the payoff.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** For each position, first identify call/put and buyer/seller.\n\n### Case: Ms Verma's Derivative Positions\n\nMs Verma holds **4,000 shares** of Zenith Ltd, bought at **₹850**. The stock now trades at **₹1,000**. She is worried about a correction over the next month but does not want to sell.\n\nHer broker suggests three alternatives (lot size **1,000 shares**, one-month expiry):\n\n1. **Buy 4 put contracts**, strike **₹960**, premium **₹28**\n2. **Sell 4 call contracts**, strike **₹1,060**, premium **₹22**\n3. **Do nothing**\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is Ms Verma's current unrealised gain, and what is her risk?\nA) Gain ₹6,00,000; her risk is that the stock falls, eroding the gain  B) Gain ₹1,50,000; no risk  C) Gain ₹4,00,000  D) No gain\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gain = (1,000 − 850) × 4,000 shares = ₹150 × 4,000 = **₹6,00,000** unrealised. Her risk is straightforward: she is **long 4,000 shares**, so a fall erodes that gain and eventually her capital.\nB) ₹1,50,000 uses only 1,000 shares. C) Uses a ₹100 gain. D) The stock has risen ₹150 from her cost.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Under Option 1 (protective put), what does she pay, and what is her worst-case position value at expiry?\nA) Pays ₹1,12,000; floor value about ₹38,40,000 − ₹1,12,000 = ₹37,28,000  B) Pays ₹28,000; no floor  C) Pays ₹1,12,000; no protection  D) Pays nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Premium** = ₹28 × 4,000 shares = **₹1,12,000**. The puts guarantee she can sell at **₹960**, so her floor on the shares is 960 × 4,000 = **₹38,40,000**. Net of premium, her worst case is about **₹37,28,000** — regardless of how far the stock falls.\nB) ₹28,000 counts only one lot. C) The whole point of the put is protection. D) Options are not free. **She has bought insurance with a known cost and a known floor.**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Under Option 2 (covered call), what does she receive, and what happens if the stock rises to ₹1,150?\nA) Receives ₹88,000; shares effectively sold at ₹1,060, so she forgoes the gain above that strike  B) Receives ₹22,000; unlimited upside retained  C) Receives ₹88,000; unlimited upside retained  D) Receives nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Premium received** = ₹22 × 4,000 = **₹88,000**. If the stock reaches ₹1,150 the calls are exercised, so her shares are effectively sold at **₹1,060**. She forgoes the ₹90 per share above the strike — **₹3,60,000** of upside — partly offset by the ₹88,000 premium.\nB) ₹22,000 counts one lot. C) A short call **caps** the upside; that is its defining feature. D) The premium is real income. **The covered call earns income but is the wrong tool if she genuinely fears a fall — it provides only ₹22 per share of cushion.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** The stock falls to ₹880 at expiry. Compare the outcomes of all three options.\nA) All three are identical  B) Protective put ≈ ₹37,28,000; covered call ≈ ₹36,08,000; do nothing = ₹35,20,000  C) The covered call is best  D) Doing nothing is best\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Do nothing:** 880 × 4,000 = **₹35,20,000**. **Protective put:** the put is ITM by 960 − 880 = ₹80, worth ₹3,20,000; so 35,20,000 + 3,20,000 − 1,12,000 premium = **₹37,28,000**. **Covered call:** the call expires worthless (880 < 1,060), so she keeps the ₹88,000 premium: 35,20,000 + 88,000 = **₹36,08,000**.\nA) They differ by over ₹2 lakh. C) The covered call is better than nothing but ₹1.2 lakh worse than the put. D) Doing nothing is the worst outcome in a falling market. **The protective put wins precisely because that is the scenario it was bought for.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** The stock instead rises to ₹1,120. Which strategy performs best, and what is the general lesson?\nA) The covered call  B) Doing nothing (₹44,80,000), then the protective put (₹43,68,000), then the covered call (₹43,28,000) — protection always costs something when the feared event does not occur  C) The protective put  D) All identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Do nothing:** 1,120 × 4,000 = **₹44,80,000**. **Protective put:** the put expires worthless, so 44,80,000 − 1,12,000 = **₹43,68,000**. **Covered call:** shares called away at ₹1,060 → 1,060 × 4,000 = 42,40,000, plus the ₹88,000 premium = **₹43,28,000**.\nA) The covered call is the **worst** here, because the upside above ₹1,060 is surrendered. C) The put costs its premium for protection that proved unnecessary. D) They differ by over ₹1.5 lakh.\n\n> ⚠️ **The lesson this caselet is built to teach:** there is **no strategy that wins in every scenario**. The protective put wins when the market falls and costs a known premium when it rises; the covered call earns income in flat markets and surrenders the upside in strong ones; doing nothing wins in a rally and is worst in a fall. **Choose based on which risk you actually want to eliminate — and accept that certainty always has a price.** Given Ms Verma explicitly wants downside protection while keeping the shares, **Option 1 (the protective put) matches her stated objective.**\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "11", "title": "Mutual Funds", "module": "Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "12", "title": "Portfolio Manager", "module": "Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "13", "title": "Overview of Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs)", "module": "Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "14", "title": "Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "module": "Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "15", "title": "Portfolio Construction Process", "module": "Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "16", "title": "Portfolio Performance Measurement and Evaluation", "module": "Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "17", "title": "Operational Aspects of Investment Management", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "18", "title": "Key Regulations", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "19", "title": "Ethical Issues", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}, {"num": "20", "title": "Grievance Redress Mechanism", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "", "flashcards": "", "questions": ""}], "papers": [], "primer": "", "excel": ""};
